window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2024-101", year: 2024, no: 101, subject: "computer", topic: "Characteristics of a CISC processor",
q: `Consider the following characteristics of Complex Instruction Set Computer (CISC) processor: 1. It is having a small number of instructions. 2. It is having less addressing modes. 3. Most instructions can manipulate operands in the memory. 4. Control unit is microprogrammed. Which of the above characteristics is/are not correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "1 and 2", "1 and 4", "3 and 4"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A CISC processor has a large number of instructions (so 1 is not correct), many addressing modes (so 2 is not correct), most instructions that can work on operands in memory (3 is correct) and a microprogrammed control unit (4 is correct). The incorrect statements are 1 and 2.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> CISC: many instructions, many modes, microprogrammed.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-102", year: 2024, no: 102, subject: "computer", topic: "Adding instructions to a processor",
q: `In which of the following processors, the designer can add easily new instruction without changing the architecture of the processor?`,
opts: ["CISC processor only", "RISC processor only", "Both CISC and RISC processors", "Neither CISC processor nor RISC processor"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) CISC processor only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a microprogrammed CISC control unit a new instruction is added by writing a new microprogram in the control store, without changing the hardware. A RISC processor uses hard-wired control, so a new instruction needs a change in the hardware.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> RISC control is hard-wired.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Only the microprogrammed processor can do this.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The CISC processor can.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Microprogram: add instructions by software.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-103", year: 2024, no: 103, subject: "computer", topic: "Performance of a CPU",
q: `Which one of the following is used to increase the performance of CPU, that means executing more instructions in less time?`,
opts: ["Sequencing", "Pipelining", "Scheduling", "Spooling"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Pipelining</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Pipelining overlaps the fetch, decode and execute stages of successive instructions, so the throughput rises to about one instruction per clock cycle.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Sequencing is the ordering of control signals.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Scheduling decides which process runs.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Spooling buffers I/O.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Pipeline: overlap of stages.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-104", year: 2024, no: 104, subject: "computer", topic: "Bus designed for bandwidth",
q: `Which one of the following buses was designed to improve bandwidth and decrease latency in computer systems?`,
opts: ["PCI bus", "VESA bus", "EISA bus", "ISA bus"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) PCI bus</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Peripheral Component Interconnect bus, developed by Intel, was designed with a 32/64-bit width and a 33/66 MHz clock to raise the bandwidth and cut the latency compared with the ISA and EISA buses.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The VESA bus was a video-oriented local bus.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The EISA bus extended ISA to 32 bits at a low speed.</li><li><b>(d)</b> ISA is the oldest and slowest of these.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> PCI replaced ISA, EISA and VESA.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-105", year: 2024, no: 105, subject: "computer", topic: "Functions of a USB root hub", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following functions of Root Hub: 1. It performs power distribution to the devices. 2. It enables and disables the ports. 3. It reports status of each port to the user.`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 2 only</h3><p class="note">Self-corrected: our solution was (d); statement 3 says the status is reported to the user, whereas it is reported to the host, so the key (c) is accepted.</p><p><b>Concept:</b> The root hub distributes power to the attached devices (1) and enables and disables the ports (2). It reports the port status to the host controller and the host software, not to the user, so statement 3 is not accepted.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Root hub: power, port control; status to the host.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-106", year: 2024, no: 106, subject: "computer", topic: "DDR3 transfer rate",
q: `In Double Data Rate SDRAM, if 100 MHz clock rate and 64 bits data bus transfers data, then what is the approximate transfer rate for DDR3?`,
opts: ["1600 MB/s", "6400 MB/s", "3200 MB/s", "800 MB/s"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 6400 MB/s</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> DDR3 prefetches 8 bits per pin per clock cycle: the data rate is 8 × the clock rate.</p><div class="formula">Rate = transfers/s × bytes per transfer</div><div class="formula">DDR3: 8 transfers per clock</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Transfers per second = 8 × 100 MHz = 800 MT/s.</li><li>Rate = 800×10⁶ × 8 bytes (64 bits) = 6400 MB/s.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1600 MB/s is the DDR1 value (2 × 100 MHz × 8 bytes).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 3200 MB/s is DDR2 (4× rate).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 800 MB/s is single data rate.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> DDR 2×, DDR2 4×, DDR3 8× the clock.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-107", year: 2024, no: 107, subject: "computer", topic: "Rotational delay of a disk",
q: `What is the approximate rotational delay if the disk drive has 8 surfaces, each surface has 1024 tracks, each track has 64 sectors, each sector can hold 512 bytes and rotation speed of 6000 r.p.m.? (It is assumed that the sector is away from head half of the track)`,
opts: ["0·005 s", "0·050 s", "0·500 s", "0·505 s"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·005 s</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Rotational delay of a disk.</p><div class="formula">T_rev = 60/N</div><div class="formula">Delay = fraction × T_rev</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>One revolution = 60/6000 = 10 ms.</li><li>The sector is half a track away, so the delay is half a revolution = 5 ms = 0.005 s.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0.05 s is ten times too large.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.5 s is a hundred times too large.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.505 s is not a rotational delay.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Delay = fraction of a revolution × 60/rpm.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-108", year: 2024, no: 108, subject: "computer", topic: "Pages in a virtual disk",
q: `How many pages are in the disk, if the capacity of a virtual disk is 2 MB and each page is 2 kB in a byte-addressable system?`,
opts: ["2048", "1024", "100", "500"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1024</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Virtual memory divides the address space into fixed-size pages. The number of pages is the total capacity divided by the page size; with powers of two this is a subtraction of exponents.</p><div class="formula">Pages = capacity/page size</div><div class="formula">2 MB = 2²¹ B, 2 kB = 2¹¹ B</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Pages = 2²¹/2¹¹ = 2¹⁰ = <b>1024</b>.</li><li>A page number therefore needs 10 bits, and the offset within a page 11 bits.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2048 would be for 1 kB pages.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 1024.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 100 is not a power of two; page counts are.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 500 is not a power of two.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Address bits = page-number bits + offset bits (here 10 + 11 = 21).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-109", year: 2024, no: 109, subject: "computer", topic: "Logging",
q: `Which one of the following is used to keep the track of program statistics that may be a valuable tool for system administrators who wish to reconfigure the system to improve computing services?`,
opts: ["Programming table", "Spooling", "Logging", "Making file"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Logging</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> An operating system keeps logs of program usage and statistics, which the administrator can study to reconfigure the system for better service.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A programming table is not an OS facility.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Spooling buffers I/O.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Making a file is not a tracking tool.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Logging: record of events and statistics.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-110", year: 2024, no: 110, subject: "computer", topic: "Message queue with zero capacity",
q: `If communicating processes reside in a temporary queue and the queue has a maximum length of zero, then the link cannot have any messages waiting in it. The sender must block until the recipient receives the message. In what way can such queues be implemented?`,
opts: ["Bounded capacity", "Unbounded capacity", "Non-zero capacity", "Zero capacity"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Zero capacity</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A queue with length zero is a zero-capacity queue (no buffering); the sender must wait for the receiver (a rendezvous). Bounded capacity has a finite length n, and unbounded capacity has a potentially infinite length.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A bounded queue holds up to n messages.</li><li><b>(b)</b> An unbounded queue never makes the sender wait.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A non-zero queue can hold messages.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Capacity 0 → sender blocks.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-111", year: 2024, no: 111, subject: "computer", topic: "Round-robin average waiting time",
q: `Consider the following set of processes that arrive at time 0 with the length of the CPU burst given in milliseconds: P₁ 24, P₂ 3, P₃ 3. What is the average waiting time under the Round-Robin Scheduling, if we use a time quantum of 4 milliseconds?`,
opts: ["5·66 milliseconds", "3·50 milliseconds", "7·00 milliseconds", "6·55 milliseconds"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 5·66 milliseconds</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Round-robin scheduling gives each process at most one time quantum in turn (FCFS order), pre-empting it at the end of the quantum and placing it at the back of the queue. Waiting time = completion time − burst time − arrival time.</p><div class="formula">Waiting = completion − burst − arrival</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Quantum 4 ms. Gantt chart: P₁ 0–4, P₂ 4–7, P₃ 7–10, P₁ 10–30.</li><li>P₁ waits 30 − 24 = 6 ms; P₂ waits 7 − 3 = 4 ms; P₃ waits 10 − 3 = 7 ms.</li><li>Average = (6 + 4 + 7)/3 = 17/3 = <b>5.66 ms</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 17/3 = 5.66 ms.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 3.50 ms would need a total wait of 10.5 ms.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 7.00 ms is P₃'s wait alone.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 6.55 ms would need a total wait of 19.65 ms.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> FCFS for the same set gives (0 + 24 + 27)/3 = 17 ms – RR is far fairer to short jobs.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-112", year: 2024, no: 112, subject: "computer", topic: "Priority scheduling",
q: `Consider the following set of processes, assumed to have arrived at time 0 in the order P₁, …, P₅, with the length of the CPU burst given in milliseconds: P₁ 10 (priority 3), P₂ 1 (1), P₃ 2 (4), P₄ 1 (5), P₅ 5 (2). Assuming that low numbers represent high priority, what is the average waiting time under the priority scheduling?`,
opts: ["5·66 milliseconds", "4·50 milliseconds", "8·20 milliseconds", "6·55 milliseconds"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 8·20 milliseconds</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Non-pre-emptive priority scheduling runs the ready process with the highest priority (here the lowest number) to completion. With all processes arriving at t = 0, each waits for the sum of the bursts of all higher-priority processes.</p><div class="formula">Waiting time = start time (arrival 0)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Order: P₂(1), P₅(2), P₁(3), P₃(4), P₄(5).</li><li>Start times: P₂ 0, P₅ 1, P₁ 6, P₃ 16, P₄ 18.</li><li>Average = (0 + 1 + 6 + 16 + 18)/5 = 41/5 = <b>8.2 ms</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 5.66 ms is the RR answer of the previous question.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 4.50 ms would need a total wait of 22.5 ms.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 8.2 ms.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 6.55 ms would need a total wait of 32.75 ms.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Priority scheduling can starve low-priority jobs; ageing fixes it.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-113", year: 2024, no: 113, subject: "power", topic: "Superheaters in steam power plants", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct regarding superheaters in steam power plants?`,
opts: ["In modern utility high-pressure boilers, more than 40% of the total heat absorbed in the generation of steam takes place in the superheaters.", "In modern utility high-pressure boilers, less than 40% of the total heat absorbed in the generation of steam takes place in the superheaters.", "In modern utility high-pressure boilers, less than 20% of the total heat absorbed in the generation of steam takes place in the superheaters.", "In modern utility high-pressure boilers, less than 30% of the total heat absorbed in the generation of steam takes place in the superheaters."], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) More than 40% of the total heat absorbed (UPSC key)</h3><p class="note">Self-corrected: our answer (b) was a recall guess; the key (a) is accepted (no source for the exact percentage was checked).</p><p><b>Concept:</b> In modern high-pressure utility boilers the latent heat of vaporisation is small and the sensible heating of the steam is large, so superheating (with reheating) takes a large share of the heat absorbed, which the key puts at more than 40 %. (Recall item.)</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Contradicts the key.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Contradicts the key.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Contradicts the key.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Higher pressure → smaller latent heat → bigger superheat share.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-114", year: 2024, no: 114, subject: "power", topic: "Bundle conductors",
q: `What are the advantages of bundle conductors?`,
opts: ["Reactance is reduced, GMR is increased and voltage gradient is reduced", "Surge impedance is reduced, GMR is decreased and voltage gradient is increased", "Reactance is increased, GMR is increased and voltage gradient is reduced", "Corona loss is reduced, GMR is increased and voltage gradient is increased"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Reactance is reduced, GMR is increased and voltage gradient is reduced</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Bundling several sub-conductors per phase raises the equivalent radius and the geometric mean radius (GMR). The inductance and the reactance fall, and the surface voltage gradient falls, so there is less corona and less radio interference.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The GMR increases and the voltage gradient falls.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The reactance falls, not rises.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The voltage gradient falls, not rises.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Bundling: bigger GMR, lower X, lower gradient.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-115", year: 2024, no: 115, subject: "power", topic: "Loading below surge impedance loading",
q: `If the loading of the line is less than the surge impedance loading, then which one of the following statements is correct?`,
opts: ["The absorbed reactive power is greater than the generated reactive power and receiving-end voltage is greater than sending-end voltage.", "The absorbed reactive power is less than the generated reactive power and receiving-end voltage is greater than sending-end voltage.", "The absorbed reactive power is greater than the generated reactive power and receiving-end voltage is less than sending-end voltage.", "The absorbed reactive power is less than the generated reactive power and receiving-end voltage is less than sending-end voltage."], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Absorbed reactive power is less than the generated; receiving-end voltage is higher</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Below the SIL the line charging (generated) reactive power exceeds the I²X absorbed by the line, so the surplus VARs raise the voltage: the receiving-end voltage is greater than the sending-end voltage (the Ferranti effect).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The generated VARs exceed the absorbed.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Both statements are reversed.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The receiving-end voltage is higher.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> P &lt; SIL: line generates VARs, V_r &gt; V_s.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-116", year: 2024, no: 116, subject: "power", topic: "Economic size of a cable",
q: `What are the overall diameter and dia of metal sheath of a single-core cable respectively for working voltage of 80 kV, the dielectric strength of the insulating material being 60 kV/cm?`,
opts: ["2·66 cm and 2·66e cm", "3·66 cm and 3·66e cm", "4·66 cm and 4·66e cm", "3·66 cm and 2·66e cm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 2·66 cm and 2·66e cm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a single-core cable, the maximum stress is at the conductor surface: g_max = 2V/[d ln(D/d)]. For a fixed g_max, the overall diameter D is smallest when ln(D/d) = 1, i.e. D/d = e; then g_max = 2V/d. (Here V is taken as the peak working voltage, as in the key.)</p><div class="formula">g_max = 2V/[d ln(D/d)]</div><div class="formula">Economic: D/d = e ⇒ d = 2V/g_max, D = e·d</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>d = 2 × 80/60 = <b>2.66 cm</b> (conductor diameter).</li><li>D = e × 2.66 = <b>2.66e cm ≈ 7.24 cm</b> (inner diameter of the sheath).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 2.66 cm and 2.66e cm.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 3.66 cm would need g_max = 43.7 kV/cm.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 4.66 cm would need g_max = 34.3 kV/cm.</li><li><b>(d)</b> D must equal e × d; 3.66 and 2.66e break that.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Same condition d/r = e appears in corona (max V_c) and cable design (min D).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-117", year: 2024, no: 117, subject: "power", topic: "Reducing corona loss",
q: `Which one of the following is correct regarding reduction of corona loss?`,
opts: ["Corona losses can be reduced by using large dia conductors, hollow conductors and bundled conductors", "Corona losses can be reduced by using small dia conductors, hollow conductors and bundled conductors", "Corona losses can be reduced by using large dia conductors, hollow conductors and single conductors", "Corona losses can be reduced by using large dia conductors, solid conductors and single conductors"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Large-diameter, hollow and bundled conductors</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A larger effective radius lowers the surface voltage gradient, so the corona loss falls: large-diameter conductors, hollow conductors (a large diameter with little metal) and bundled conductors.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Small conductors raise the gradient.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A single conductor does not raise the effective radius.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Solid single conductors of a smaller radius raise the gradient.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Lower gradient = lower corona.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-118", year: 2024, no: 118, subject: "power", topic: "Directional feature for a reactance relay",
q: `Which one of the following types of relay is used to give directional feature to reactance relay?`,
opts: ["IDMT relay", "Impedance relay", "Mho relay", "Non-directional reactance relay"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Mho relay</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A reactance relay is not directional by itself. A mho (admittance) relay, which is inherently directional, is used as the starting unit to supply the directional feature.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> An IDMT relay is an overcurrent relay.</li><li><b>(b)</b> An impedance relay is non-directional.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A non-directional relay cannot give a direction.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Reactance relay + mho unit.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-119", year: 2024, no: 119, subject: "power", topic: "Breaking and making current of a circuit breaker",
q: `What are the values of breaking current and making current of a circuit breaker respectively, rated at 1000 A, 2000√3 MVA, 20 kV, 3 s, oil circuit breaker?`,
opts: ["100 kA and 255 kA", "200 kA and 255 kA", "100 kA and 200 kA", "200 kA and 200 kA"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 100 kA and 255 kA</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Circuit-breaker ratings: symmetrical breaking current = rated MVA/(√3 × rated kV). The making current is the peak of the first major loop of an asymmetrical fault current: 1.8 (doubling factor) × √2 × I_break ≈ 2.55 × I_break.</p><div class="formula">I_break = MVA/(√3 kV)</div><div class="formula">I_make = 1.8 × √2 × I_break = 2.55 I_break</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I_break = 2000√3 × 10⁶/(√3 × 20 × 10³) = <b>100 kA</b>.</li><li>I_make = 2.55 × 100 = <b>255 kA</b> (peak).</li><li>Short-time rating: 100 kA for 3 s.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 100 kA and 255 kA.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Breaking current is 100 kA, not 200 kA.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Making current is 2.55 × breaking, not 2×.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Both wrong.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Making current is a peak value; breaking current is rms.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-120", year: 2024, no: 120, subject: "power", topic: "Nodal admittance formulation", selfCorrected: true,
q: `The nodal admittance formulation, using the nodal voltages as the independent variables, is the most economic`,
opts: ["in the view of computer time only", "in the view of computer memory only", "in the view of both computer time and memory", "in the view of stability"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) in the view of computer memory only</h3><p class="note">Self-corrected: our answer (c) was a recall guess; the key (b) is accepted.</p><p><b>Concept:</b> The bus admittance matrix is sparse and symmetric, so it needs little computer memory (only the non-zero elements need be stored). The key takes the economy as being in memory only.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Time is not the only benefit measured.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The key limits the economy to memory.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Stability is not a computer-economy issue.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Y-bus: sparse and symmetric.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-121", year: 2024, no: 121, subject: "power", topic: "Kinetic energy from the inertia constant",
q: `A 50 Hz, four-pole turbo-generator rated at 30 MVA, 13·2 kV has an inertia constant of H = 10 kW-s/kVA. What is the value of kinetic energy stored in the rotor at synchronous speed?`,
opts: ["180 megajoules", "200 megajoules", "300 megajoules", "400 megajoules"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 300 megajoules</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The inertia constant H is the kinetic energy stored in the rotor at synchronous speed per unit of machine rating: H = KE/S (MJ/MVA, or kW·s/kVA, which is the same unit).</p><div class="formula">KE = H × S</div><div class="formula">M = HS/(πf) (angular momentum)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>KE = 10 MJ/MVA × 30 MVA = <b>300 MJ</b>.</li><li>M = 300/(π × 50) = 1.91 MJ·s/rad.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 180 MJ would need H = 6.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 200 MJ would need H = 6.67.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 300 MJ.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 400 MJ would need H = 13.3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Typical H: turbo-alternators 4–10, hydro 2–4 MJ/MVA.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-122", year: 2024, no: 122, subject: "power", topic: "Advantages of d.c. transmission",
q: `Which of the following is correct regarding the advantages of DC transmission over AC transmission?`,
opts: ["Power per conductor is more, more corona loss and possibility of higher operating voltages", "Power per conductor is more, less corona loss and possibility of higher operating voltages", "Power per conductor is more, no skin effect and possibility of lower operating voltages", "Power per conductor is more, there is charging current and possibility of higher operating voltages"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) More power per conductor, less corona loss, higher operating voltages</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For the same insulation level d.c. carries more power per conductor, has less corona loss and no skin effect, and permits higher operating voltages (no charging current).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The corona loss is less.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The operating voltage can be higher.</li><li><b>(d)</b> There is no charging current in d.c.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> DC: no skin effect, no charging current.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-123", year: 2024, no: 123, subject: "power", topic: "Shunt and series capacitors",
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct regarding shunt/series capacitor?`,
opts: ["Shunt capacitor improves the power factor whereas series capacitor improves the stability of transmission line.", "Series capacitor improves the power factor whereas shunt capacitor improves the stability of transmission line.", "Shunt capacitor improves both the power factor and the stability of transmission line.", "Series capacitor improves both the power factor and the stability of transmission line."], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Shunt capacitor improves the power factor; series capacitor improves the stability</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A shunt capacitor supplies the lagging reactive power of the load and so raises the power factor. A series capacitor cancels part of the line reactance and so raises the power-transfer limit and the stability.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The roles are reversed.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A shunt capacitor does not reduce the line reactance.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A series capacitor mainly cuts the line reactance.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Shunt: pf. Series: reactance and stability.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-124", year: 2024, no: 124, subject: "power", topic: "Smart grid versus conventional grid",
q: `In smart grid, the model load, FACTS devices and control, protection platform as compared to conventional grid are respectively`,
opts: ["dynamic, specified and adaptive nature", "dynamic, adaptive and adaptive nature", "dynamic, adaptive and defined nature", "static, adaptive and adaptive nature"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) dynamic, adaptive and adaptive nature</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a smart grid the load model is dynamic (not static), the FACTS devices and their control are adaptive and the protection platform is adaptive, in contrast with the fixed settings of a conventional grid. (Recall item.)</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The FACTS control is adaptive, not specified.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The protection is adaptive, not defined.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The load model is dynamic.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Smart grid: dynamic and adaptive.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-125", year: 2024, no: 125, subject: "power", topic: "Hydraulic power",
q: `A large hydropower station has a head of 324 m and an average flow of 1370 m³/s. The reservoir of water behind the dams and dikes is composed of series of lakes covering an area of 6400 km². What is the available hydraulic power?`,
opts: ["4350 kW", "4350 MW", "435 MW", "435 kW"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 4350 MW</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Hydraulic power available from a flow Q falling through a head H is the rate of loss of potential energy: P = ρgQH. The reservoir area affects storage (energy over time), not instantaneous power.</p><div class="formula">P = ρ g Q H (W)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>P = 1000 × 9.81 × 1370 × 324.</li><li>= 4.354 × 10⁹ W ≈ <b>4350 MW</b>.</li><li>With turbine-generator efficiency ~90 %, electrical output ≈ 3900 MW.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> kW instead of MW: 1000 times too small.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 4350 MW.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Ten times too small.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Ten thousand times too small.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Quick estimate: P(kW) ≈ 9.81 Q H.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2024f.js ----
// ESE 2024 Prelims Paper II, Electrical Engineering, Series A (PNGE-D-ETE), Q126–150
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; 3 dropped: Q2, 68, 136), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2024-126", year: 2024, no: 126, subject: "basic", topic: "Semiconductor diode regions", doubt: true,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding semiconductor diode: 1. In the non-conducting region (when the p-n junction is reverse biased), the diode current is exactly zero. 2. The diode requires a small positive voltage to be applied before it enters the conducting region. 3. For large input voltages and/or currents, the diode enters breakdown regions in the forward direction.`,
opts: ["1 and 2", "2 only", "1 only", "2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 2 and 3 (UPSC key)</h3><p class="note">Doubt: breakdown of a diode occurs in the reverse direction (the large forward current region is limited by heating, not breakdown), so statement 3 is not correct; our solution was (b). Followed the UPSC key (d).</p><p><b>Concept:</b> (2) is correct: the diode conducts appreciably only after the cut-in voltage (about 0.7 V for silicon). (1) is not exact: a reverse-biased diode carries the small reverse saturation current, not exactly zero. (3) as printed says breakdown occurs in the forward direction, whereas breakdown occurs in the reverse direction; the key nevertheless counts it as correct.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Statement 1 is not exactly true (there is a leakage current).</li><li><b>(b)</b> This is our answer; the key also accepts statement 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Statement 1 is not exactly true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Breakdown: reverse; cut-in: forward.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-127", year: 2024, no: 127, subject: "materials", topic: "Intrinsic conductivity of silicon",
q: `The mobilities of free electrons and holes in pure silicon are 1300 cm²/V-s and 500 cm²/V-s respectively. What is the value of intrinsic conductivity for silicon? (Assume nᵢ = 1·5 × 10¹⁰ cm⁻³ for silicon at room temperature)`,
opts: ["2·69 × 10¹³ S/cm", "4·32 × 10⁻⁶ S/cm", "4·32 × 10¹³ S/cm", "2·69 × 10⁻⁶ S/cm"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 4·32 × 10⁻⁶ S/cm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula:</b> σᵢ = q nᵢ(μₙ + μₚ).</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>σᵢ = 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ × 1.5×10¹⁰ × (1300 + 500) = 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ × 1.5×10¹⁰ × 1800.</li><li>σᵢ = 4.32×10⁻⁶ S/cm.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The power of ten is wrong (the exponent cannot be +13).</li><li><b>(c)</b> The power of ten is wrong.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2.69×10⁻⁶ would result from using only one of the mobilities incorrectly.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> σ = q n (μₙ + μₚ).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-128", year: 2024, no: 128, subject: "basic", topic: "Depletion region under reverse bias",
q: `What happens to the depletion region and the depletion region capacitance of a p-n junction diode with increased reverse-biased voltage?`,
opts: ["The depletion region shrinks and the depletion region capacitance decreases", "The depletion region shrinks and the depletion region capacitance increases", "The depletion region widens and the depletion region capacitance decreases", "The depletion region widens and the depletion region capacitance increases"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) The depletion region widens and its capacitance decreases</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A larger reverse bias widens the depletion layer, W ∝ √(V₀ + V_R). The junction capacitance C = εA/W is therefore smaller.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The region widens under reverse bias.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The region widens and the capacitance decreases.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A wider layer means a smaller capacitance.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> C_j ∝ 1/√(V₀ + V_R).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-129", year: 2024, no: 129, subject: "basic", topic: "Voltage-divider bias",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding voltage-divider bias in a transistor: 1. The voltage-divider bias configuration uses two DC bias sources to provide forward-reverse bias to the transistor. 2. The voltage-divider bias provides a very small base current to the transistor compared to the bias current. 3. Two resistors R₁ and R₂ form a voltage divider that provides the base bias voltage to the transistor.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> (1) is false: voltage-divider bias uses a single supply V_CC and two resistors, not two sources. (2) is true: the base current is small compared with the current through the divider. (3) is true: R₁ and R₂ form the divider that sets the base voltage.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> One supply, two resistors.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-130", year: 2024, no: 130, subject: "materials", topic: "Fermi level at a higher temperature",
q: `In an n-type semiconductor, the Fermi level is 0·3 eV below the conduction level at a room temperature of 300 K. If the temperature is increased to 360 K, what is the new position of the Fermi level?`,
opts: ["Remains unchanged", "0·26 eV above the conduction level", "0·36 eV above the conduction level", "0·36 eV below the conduction level"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·36 eV below the conduction level</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a constant donor density n = N_c exp[−(E_c − E_F)/kT], so E_c − E_F = kT ln(N_c/n), which grows in proportion to T in the key's simple treatment.</p><div class="formula">E_c − E_F = kT ln(N_c/n) ∝ T (key's approximation)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>E_c − E_F = 0.3 × 360/300 = 0.36 eV.</li><li>The Fermi level stays below the conduction band, at 0.36 eV.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> It changes with temperature.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The Fermi level of an n-type material remains below E_c.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The Fermi level of an n-type material remains below E_c.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> (E_c − E_F) ∝ T for fixed n/N_c.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-131", year: 2024, no: 131, subject: "basic", topic: "Common-base amplifier regions",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding common-base transistor amplifier: 1. In the active region, the collector-base junction is forward biased while the base-emitter junction is reverse biased. 2. In the cutoff region, the collector-base and base-emitter junctions of a transistor are both reverse biased. 3. In the saturation region, the collector-base and base-emitter junctions are both forward biased.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Active region: the emitter junction is forward biased and the collector junction reverse biased, so (1) states the junctions the wrong way round. Cut-off: both reverse (2 is correct). Saturation: both forward (3 is correct).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Active: BE forward, CB reverse.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-132", year: 2024, no: 132, subject: "basic", topic: "Active region of a CE amplifier",
q: `What are the biasing states of collector-base junction and base-emitter junction in the active region of a common-emitter transistor amplifier?`,
opts: ["Both collector-base junction and base-emitter junction are forward biased", "Both collector-base junction and base-emitter junction are reverse biased", "The collector-base junction is forward biased and the base-emitter junction is reverse biased", "The collector-base junction is reverse biased and the base-emitter junction is forward biased"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Collector-base reverse biased, base-emitter forward biased</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the active (amplifying) region the emitter junction is forward biased, injecting carriers, and the collector junction is reverse biased, collecting them.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Both forward is saturation.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Both reverse is cut-off.</li><li><b>(c)</b> This is the inverse active mode.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Active: BE forward, BC reverse.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-133", year: 2024, no: 133, subject: "basic", topic: "Operating regions of a BJT",
q: `Match the following Lists: <br><b>List-I</b> (n-p-n BJT operating regions): P. Cutoff region, Q. Saturation region, R. Forward active region. <br><b>List-II</b> (characteristics): 1. I_C = β_F I_B, I_B &gt; 0, V_BE &gt; V_BE(on), V_CE &gt; V_CE(sat); 2. I_C = I_B = I_E = 0, V_BE &lt; V_BE(on), V_BC &lt; V_BC(on); 3. I_B &gt; 0, I_C &gt; 0, I_C &lt; β_F I_B, V_BE &gt; V_BE(on).`,
opts: ["P-1, Q-2, R-3", "P-2, Q-3, R-1", "P-1, Q-3, R-2", "P-2, Q-1, R-3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) P-2, Q-3, R-1</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Operating regions of a BJT.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Cut-off: all currents zero and both junctions below turn-on: 2.</li><li>Saturation: both junctions on, the collector current is below β_F I_B: 3.</li><li>Forward active: I_C = β_F I_B with V_CE above V_CE(sat): 1.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> P is cut-off (2), not 1.</li><li><b>(c)</b> P is cut-off (2), not 1.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Q is saturation (3) and R is active (1).</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Active: I_C = βI_B; saturation: I_C &lt; βI_B.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-134", year: 2024, no: 134, subject: "basic", topic: "Negative feedback",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding negative feedback in amplifier circuits: 1. It has reduction in overall voltage gain. 2. It has enhanced frequency response. 3. It has higher output impedance.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Negative feedback reduces the gain by (1 + Aβ) (1), extends the bandwidth, i.e. enhances the frequency response (2), and, for the common voltage-sampling types, lowers the output impedance; it is not higher, so (3) is not accepted.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Negative feedback: lower gain, wider bandwidth.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-135", year: 2024, no: 135, subject: "basic", topic: "Hartley oscillator",
q: `In the Hartley oscillator, L₂ = 0·4 mH and C = 0·004 μF. If the frequency of the oscillator is 120 kHz, what is the value of L₁? (Neglect the mutual inductance)`,
opts: ["4 mH", "0·04 mH", "0·4 mH", "40 mH"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 0·04 mH</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula:</b> f = 1/[2π√((L₁ + L₂)C)].</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>L₁ + L₂ = 1/[(2π × 120×10³)² × 4×10⁻⁹] = 1/(5.685×10¹¹ × 4×10⁻⁹) = 0.4398 mH.</li><li>L₁ = 0.4398 − 0.4 = 0.04 mH.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 4 mH is a hundred times too large.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.4 mH would give f = 112 kHz.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 40 mH is a thousand times too large.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> L_total = 1/(ω²C).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-136", year: 2024, no: 136, subject: "basic", topic: "Wien bridge oscillator", dropped: true,
q: `In a Wien bridge oscillator, if the value of R is 10 kΩ and the frequency of oscillation is 10 kHz, what is the value of capacitor C?`,
opts: ["0·159 pF", "15·9 pF", "159 pF", "1·59 pF"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Dropped by UPSC (not scored)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> UPSC dropped this item. For a Wien bridge f = 1/(2πRC), so C = 1/(2π × 10⁴ × 10⁴) = 1.59 nF = 1590 pF, which is not among the options (the options are 0.159, 15.9, 159 and 1.59 pF).</p><p>Check: 2π × 10 kΩ × 10 kHz = 6.283×10⁸ per farad, so C = 1.59×10⁻⁹ F. The options differ from this by a factor of 10 or 1000 (159 pF would be correct for 100 kHz), so the printed data and the options do not agree and the item was dropped. In the standard text the Wien-bridge condition R₁ = R₂ = R, C₁ = C₂ = C gives f₀ = 1/(2πRC), and the gain of the non-inverting amplifier must be at least 3 to start the oscillation, but neither fact changes the value of C computed here.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> f = 1/(2πRC).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-137", year: 2024, no: 137, subject: "basic", topic: "Frequency stability of an oscillator",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding frequency stability oscillator: 1. Due to change in temperature, the values of the frequency-determining components, viz., resistor, inductor and capacitor are changed. 2. Frequency can affect due to variation in biasing conditions and loading conditions. 3. The effective resistance of the tank circuit is unchanged when the load is connected.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> (1) True: temperature changes the component values. (2) True: the biasing and the loading change the active-device capacitances and the tank. (3) False: connecting a load changes the effective resistance (Q) of the tank.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Temperature, bias and load all shift the frequency.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-138", year: 2024, no: 138, subject: "pe", topic: "Power diode as a switch",
q: `A power diode can be used as a switch because its resistance can be controlled with`,
opts: ["applied current", "small current", "higher current", "applied voltage"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) applied voltage</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The diode resistance is very low when a forward voltage is applied (on) and very high when the voltage is reversed (off), so the applied voltage controls it as a switch.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The current is the result of the voltage.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The current is the result of the voltage.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The current is the result of the voltage.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Forward bias: on; reverse bias: off.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-139", year: 2024, no: 139, subject: "pe", topic: "Turn-off of a power diode", selfCorrected: true,
q: `The reverse current reduces and the voltage across the power diode grows more negative during the turn-off process of the power diode. This time is called`,
opts: ["fall time", "recovery time", "reverse recovery time", "rise time"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) fall time</h3><p class="note">Self-corrected: our answer (b) was a guess; the key (a) is accepted.</p><p><b>Concept:</b> The turn-off has two parts: first the stored charge is removed while the current falls through zero to its negative peak (t_a); then the reverse current decays and the reverse voltage builds up (t_b). The key names the second stage, where the current falls back towards zero and the voltage grows more negative, the fall time. The whole of t_a + t_b is the reverse recovery time.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Recovery time is the term for the whole period.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Reverse recovery time covers both stages.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The rise time belongs to turn-on.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> t_rr = t_a + t_b.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-140", year: 2024, no: 140, subject: "pe", topic: "MOS power transistor",
q: `What are the significant advantages of MOS power transistor over bipolar power transistor in the pulse power supplies?`,
opts: ["Very high input resistance and the input currents are of the order of nA", "Very low input resistance and the input currents are of the order of kA", "Very high input resistance and the input currents are of the order of kA", "Very low input resistance and the input currents are of the order of nA"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Very high input resistance and input currents of the order of nA</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The MOS gate is insulated by an oxide layer, so the input resistance is very high and the gate current only nanoamperes, which makes the drive circuit simple.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The input resistance is high and the current is tiny.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The input current is nA, not kA.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The input resistance is high, not low.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> MOSFET: voltage-driven.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-141", year: 2024, no: 141, subject: "pe", topic: "Turn-off of a GTO",
q: `In a gate turnoff thyristor, the turning-off is achieved by`,
opts: ["latching current at gate", "holding current at gate", "positive current at gate", "negative current at gate"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) negative current at gate</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A GTO is turned on by a positive gate pulse and turned off by a large negative gate current pulse which extracts the charge from the p-base.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Latching current is an anode quantity.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Holding current is an anode quantity.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A positive gate current turns it on.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> GTO: gate pulse + on, − off.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-142", year: 2024, no: 142, subject: "pe", topic: "Capacitive filter of a bridge rectifier",
q: `The function of a capacitive filter in a Graetz diode bridge rectifier is to`,
opts: ["remove small load current ripples from the rectified output signal", "minimize voltage variations in AC input signal", "reduce harmonics in the rectified output signal", "introduce more ripples into the rectified output signal"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) remove small ripples from the rectified output signal</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The shunt capacitor charges at the voltage peaks and discharges into the load between them, smoothing the pulsating output and removing the ripple.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> It does not act on the a.c. input.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Harmonic reduction is the job of an LC filter on the a.c. side.</li><li><b>(d)</b> It reduces the ripple.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> C filter: smooths the d.c. output.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-143", year: 2024, no: 143, subject: "pe", topic: "Inductive filter",
q: `The use of an inductive filter in a rectifier circuit provides satisfactory performance only when`,
opts: ["the load current is high", "the load voltage is high", "the load current is low", "the load voltage is low"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) the load current is high</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> An inductive (series-L) filter opposes changes in current. Its smoothing improves as the load current grows (the choke then carries a large current), and with a light load the output approaches the peak voltage. It is therefore used for heavy-current loads.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The voltage level is not the criterion.</li><li><b>(c)</b> At a low current the inductive reactance is too small to be effective.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The voltage level is not the criterion.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> L filter: high current loads; C filter: low current loads.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-144", year: 2024, no: 144, subject: "pe", topic: "First-quadrant drive",
q: `If a separately excited DC motor is to be operated in the first quadrant only, the converter is used in`,
opts: ["single-phase half-controlled rectifier", "single-phase full-controlled rectifier", "single-phase dual-controlled rectifier", "four-quadrant chopper"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) single-phase half-controlled rectifier</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A half-controlled (semi-) converter gives only a positive voltage and a positive current: single-quadrant operation. The full converter works in two quadrants, and the dual converter and the four-quadrant chopper in four.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> A full converter has two-quadrant operation.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A dual converter has four-quadrant operation.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A four-quadrant chopper has four-quadrant operation.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Semiconverter: 1 quadrant.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-145", year: 2024, no: 145, subject: "pe", topic: "Static Scherbius drive", selfCorrected: true,
q: `The static Scherbius drive can able to provide`,
opts: ["variable torque control", "constant torque control", "braking operation", "variable speed"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) constant torque control</h3><p class="note">Self-corrected: our answer (d), variable speed, is also a feature of the drive but a web search (an EEE text on slip-power recovery) names constant-torque control as the characteristic of the static Scherbius drive; the key (b) is accepted.</p><p><b>Concept:</b> In the static Scherbius drive the slip power of a wound-rotor induction motor is returned to the supply through a converter. Its speed is controlled below (and above) synchronous speed with a constant-torque characteristic. A static Kramer drive, in contrast, gives constant-power control.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The torque capability is constant, not variable.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Braking is not the purpose of the drive.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Speed is varied, but the characteristic named in this question is the constant torque.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Scherbius: constant torque; Kramer: constant power.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-146", year: 2024, no: 146, subject: "pe", topic: "Series resonant inverter",
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct regarding series resonant inverter?`,
opts: ["The load current is a square waveform.", "The output voltage waveform depends on the damping factor of load impedance.", "The trigger frequency is higher than the damped resonant frequency.", "The input voltage waveform depends on the damping factor of load impedance."], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) The output voltage waveform depends on the damping factor of the load impedance</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The current of a series resonant inverter is a (part-)sinusoid set by the series RLC circuit, and the shape of the output voltage depends on the damping factor.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The load current is oscillatory, not square.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The trigger frequency is chosen lower than the damped resonant frequency for self-commutation.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The input is a fixed d.c. voltage.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Series resonant: damping decides the shape.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-147", year: 2024, no: 147, subject: "pe", topic: "Single-phase bridge inverter with R load",
q: `If a single-phase full-bridge voltage source inverter operates with R load, the nature of output current is`,
opts: ["square wave", "sine wave", "triangular wave", "pulse wave"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) square wave</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The output voltage of a full-bridge inverter is a square wave. With a resistive load the current is v/R, with the same shape: a square wave.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> A sine wave needs filtering.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A triangular wave results from an inductive load.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A pulse train is a different switching pattern.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> R load: i has the shape of v.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-148", year: 2024, no: 148, subject: "pe", topic: "Cores for high-frequency transformers",
q: `Which one of the following cores is used for large mains transformers to reduce eddy current loss of high-frequency operation in power electronic circuit?`,
opts: ["Laminated iron core", "Laminated steel core", "Compressed ferromagnetic alloy core", "Ferromagnetic alloy core"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Compressed ferromagnetic alloy core</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At high frequency the eddy-current loss of laminated steel becomes large. Cores of compressed (pressed-powder) ferromagnetic alloy, whose particles are insulated from one another, keep the eddy currents small and are used at high frequency.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Laminations are not enough at high frequency.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Laminations are not enough at high frequency.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A solid alloy core would have a large eddy loss.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Powder cores for high frequency.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-149", year: 2024, no: 149, subject: "pe", topic: "Phase-controlled a.c. voltage controller",
q: `When an induction motor and a heater are supplied from a phase-controlled single-phase AC voltage controllers, then`,
opts: ["only fundamental component of output voltage and current is useful in the induction motor but fundamental and harmonics are useful in the heater", "fundamental and harmonics are useful in the induction motor but only fundamental component of output voltage and current is useful in the heater", "both fundamental and harmonics are useful in the induction motor and heater", "only harmonics are useful in the induction motor and heater"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Induction motor: fundamental only; heater: fundamental and harmonics</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The motor develops torque only from the fundamental component; the harmonics only cause losses and heating. A resistive heater turns the power of all the components into heat, so the harmonics are as useful as the fundamental.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The roles are reversed.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The harmonics are not useful in the motor.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The fundamental is the useful component in the motor.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Heater: heat from all harmonics; motor: torque from the fundamental.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-150", year: 2024, no: 150, subject: "pe", topic: "IGBT",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding IGBT: 1. It combines into it the advantages of both MOSFET and BJT. 2. It is free from second breakdown problem present in BJT. 3. It has low input impedance and high power loss.`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> An IGBT has the MOS gate drive (a high input impedance) and the low conduction loss of a bipolar device (1 is true). It is free from the second breakdown of the BJT (2 is true). Statement 3 is false: the input impedance is high and the power loss is low.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> IGBT: MOS gate + bipolar conduction.</p>` }
);

