// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; none dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2023-101", year: 2023, no: 101, subject: "power", topic: "Overcurrent relay characteristics", selfCorrected: true,
q: `The overcurrent relays are categorized by obtaining their characteristics as under: (figure of operating time versus multiple of plug setting with curves (a), (b), (c), (d)). The curve (b) in the characteristics is pertaining to which category of current relay?<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2023-101.png" alt="Figure for question 101">`,
opts: ["Extremely inverse relay", "Very inverse relay", "Inverse time", "Inverse definite minimum time"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Inverse definite minimum time</h3><p class="note">Self-corrected: our solution was (c), inverse time; the flattening of curve (b) at high current makes it the IDMT type, and the key (d) is accepted.</p><p><b>Concept:</b> The four curves are, from the flattest to the steepest: (a) definite time, (b) inverse definite minimum time (IDMT), (c) very inverse and (d) extremely inverse. Curve (b) falls with increasing current but flattens to a constant minimum operating time at high multiples of the plug setting: this is the IDMT characteristic.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The extremely inverse relay is the steepest curve (d).</li><li><b>(b)</b> The very inverse relay is curve (c).</li><li><b>(c)</b> A plain inverse-time curve keeps falling without a flat minimum; the flattening of curve (b) marks it as IDMT.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> IDMT: inverse, then definite minimum.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-102", year: 2023, no: 102, subject: "power", topic: "Circuit breaker statements",
q: `Consider the following statements: 1. The arcing time of arc controlled circuit breaker varies considerably depending upon the breaking current. 2. Except for a certain medium range of voltages, air circuit breakers are widely used for the low voltage circuits as well as the highest transmission voltages. 3. The resistance switching reduces help to distribute the transient recovery voltage more uniformly across the several gaps. 4. With the use of better insulating materials for the arcing chambers, the oil C.Bs. are able to meet the increased fault levels of the systems easily. 5. The resistance switching reduces transient voltages during switching out inductive load but enhances for capacitive loads. Which of the above statements is/are not correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "4 only", "5 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 5 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Resistance switching damps the transient recovery voltage and distributes it uniformly across the gaps (3). It reduces the transient voltages both for inductive-current chopping and for capacitive-current interruption, so statement 5, which says it enhances them for capacitive loads, is not correct. Statements 1, 2 and 4 are standard descriptions of the arc-controlled, air and oil breakers.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Resistance switching reduces transients in both cases.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-103", year: 2023, no: 103, subject: "power", topic: "Bus types in load flow",
q: `Consider the table below for different types of buses where each set may consist of any parameters ‘V’ is voltage magnitude, ‘P’ is real power injection, ‘Q’ is reactive power and ‘δ’ is phase angle. Load bus: specified Set A, obtained Set D; Slack bus: specified Set B, obtained Set E; Generator bus: specified Set C, obtained Set F. Select the correct answer using the above table:`,
opts: ["Set A and E are same, but Set C and D are different", "Set B and F are same, but Set C and E are different", "Set C and D are same, but Set A and F are different", "Set C and E are same, but Set D and B are different"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Set A and E are same, but Set C and D are different</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Specified/obtained quantities:</b></p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Load bus (PQ): specified P, Q (Set A); obtained V, δ (Set D).</li><li>Slack bus: specified V, δ (Set B); obtained P, Q (Set E).</li><li>Generator bus (PV): specified P, V (Set C); obtained Q, δ (Set F).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> B = {V, δ} and F = {Q, δ} are not the same.</li><li><b>(c)</b> C = {P, V} and D = {V, δ} are different, and A = {P, Q} differs from F = {Q, δ}.</li><li><b>(d)</b> C = {P, V} and E = {P, Q} are different, and D and B are the same, not different.</li></ul><p>So Set A = Set E = {P, Q} and Set B = Set D = {V, δ}; Set C = {P, V} differs from Set D = {V, δ}. Option (a) is therefore right.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> PQ: P,Q given. Slack: V,δ given. PV: P,V given.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-104", year: 2023, no: 104, subject: "power", topic: "Reactive power limits at PV buses",
q: `While the load flow equations are nonlinear and they are solved by using Gauss-Seidel Iterative Method by including PV buses, what would be the reason for the violation of reactive power limit?`,
opts: ["Too low specified voltage level only", "Too high specified voltage level only", "Either too low or too high specified voltage level", "Neither too low nor too high specified voltage level"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Either too low or too high specified voltage level</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At a PV bus the voltage magnitude is held at a specified value and the reactive power Q is computed. If the specified voltage is too high, the generator would have to supply more VAR than its upper limit; if it is too low, the generator would have to absorb more VAR than its lower limit. So the limits can be violated by a specified voltage that is either too high or too low.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A too-high voltage also causes a violation.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A too-low voltage also causes a violation.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The violation is caused by the specified voltage.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Q at a PV bus follows from the specified |V|.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-105", year: 2023, no: 105, subject: "power", topic: "Equal area criterion",
q: `Consider a system consisting of a synchronous machine connected to an infinite bus bar. The below power angle curve is obtained using equal area criterion when sudden increase in mechanical load on that motor occurs: What is the condition of power at point ‘b’ if P_e is electromagnetic power and P_s is shaft power?<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2023-105.png" alt="Figure for question 105">`,
opts: ["P_e = P_s", "P_e < P_s", "P_e > P_s", "P_m = P_s"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) P_e = P_s</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Point b is at δ_s, where the P_e curve crosses the horizontal line of the shaft power P_s. At that point the electromagnetic power equals the shaft power: this is the new equilibrium (accelerating area a = decelerating area c about it).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> P_e &lt; P_s holds only at angles below δ_s.</li><li><b>(c)</b> P_e &gt; P_s holds only at angles beyond δ_s.</li><li><b>(d)</b> P_m is the peak of the curve, not the shaft power.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Equilibrium: P_e = P_s.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-106", year: 2023, no: 106, subject: "power", topic: "Accelerating torque",
q: `If T_s represents the shaft torque and T_e the electromagnetic torque and if these are assumed positive for a generator, the net torque causing acceleration T_a is`,
opts: ["T_a = T_s − T_e", "T_a = T_s + T_e", "T_a = 2T_s − T_e", "T_a = T_s + 2T_e"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) T_a = T_s − T_e</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a generator the shaft (prime mover) torque drives the rotor and the electromagnetic torque opposes it. The net accelerating torque is T_a = T_s − T_e, which enters the swing equation J dω/dt = T_a.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Both torques would add.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2T_s is not meaningful.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2T_e is not meaningful.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Net torque = driving − opposing.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-107", year: 2023, no: 107, subject: "power", topic: "High power factor in HVDC",
q: `In HVDC transmission, high power factor of the system is maintained for the following reason(s): 1. For a given current and voltage of the thyristor and transformers, the power rating of the converters is equal. 2. The stresses on the thyristors and damping circuits are reduced. 3. For the same power to be transmitted the current rating of the system is reduced and also the copper losses in the ac lines are reduced.`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A high power factor lowers the reactive power the converter absorbs. Then (2) the stresses on the thyristors and damping (snubber) circuits fall, and (3) for the same real power the current rating is smaller and the copper losses in the a.c. lines are lower. Statement 1 is not a reason: the power rating of the converter is not simply equal for a given current and voltage; it depends on the power factor.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> High pf: less VAR, less current, less stress.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-108", year: 2023, no: 108, subject: "power", topic: "Hydro turbines",
q: `Which type of hydro turbine is totally embedded in the fluid and powered from the pressure drop across the device?`,
opts: ["Impulse turbine only", "Reaction turbine only", "Squirrel cage turbine only", "Both impulse turbine and reaction turbine"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Reaction turbine only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A reaction turbine (Francis, Kaplan) is completely immersed in the water, and the runner is turned by the pressure drop across it. An impulse turbine (Pelton) is driven by a jet at atmospheric pressure and is not submerged.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> An impulse turbine works with a free jet at atmospheric pressure.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A squirrel-cage turbine is not a hydro turbine type.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Only the reaction type is submerged and driven by the pressure drop.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Reaction: submerged, pressure drop.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-109", year: 2023, no: 109, subject: "pe", topic: "Reverse recovery time of a diode",
q: `By considering the standard notations, reverse recovery time (t_rr) of a power diode is`,
opts: ["t_rr = √(2Q_R/(di/dt))", "t_rr = √(2Q_R/(dv/dt))", "t_rr = √(2(dv/dt)/Q_R)", "t_rr = √(2(dI/dt)/Q_R)"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) t_rr = √(2Q_R/(di/dt))</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The stored charge Q_R is the area of the triangle formed by the reverse current: Q_R = ½ I_RR t_rr, with I_RR = (di/dt) t_rr in the idealised case. So Q_R = ½ (di/dt) t_rr², giving t_rr = √(2Q_R/(di/dt)).</p><div class="formula">Q_R = ½I_RRt_rr, I_RR = (di/dt)t_rr</div><div class="formula">t_rr = √(2Q_R/(di/dt))</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Q_R = ½(di/dt)t_rr².</li><li>t_rr = <b>√(2Q_R/(di/dt))</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The rate of change of current, not voltage, appears.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Inverted and with dv/dt.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Inverted.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Q_R = ½ (di/dt) t_rr².</p>` },

{ id: "2023-110", year: 2023, no: 110, subject: "pe", topic: "Overdrive factor of a transistor",
q: `The overdrive factor (ODF) of a power transistor can be expressed as`,
opts: ["base current required to operate in saturation (I_BS) / base current (I_B)", "base current (I_B) / base current required to operate in saturation (I_BS)", "base current required to operate in saturation (I_BS) / collector current (I_C)", "collector current (I_C) / base current (I_B)"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) I_B / I_BS</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The overdrive factor is the ratio of the actual base current to the base current that is just enough for saturation: ODF = I_B/I_BS, with a value above 1 for the transistor to be driven well into saturation.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> This is the inverse.</li><li><b>(c)</b> It mixes the base and collector currents.</li><li><b>(d)</b> This is the forced beta or the DC gain.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> ODF = I_B/I_BS.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-111", year: 2023, no: 111, subject: "pe", topic: "Gate pulse amplitude and turn-on time",
q: `When the amplitude of the gate pulse to thyristor is increased,`,
opts: ["the delay time would decrease, but the rise time would increase.", "both delay time and rise time would increase.", "the delay time would decrease, but the rise time remains unaffected.", "the delay time would increase, but the rise time would decrease."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) the delay time would decrease, but the rise time remains unaffected</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A larger gate current injects charge faster, so the delay time t_d, which is the time the anode current takes to reach 10 % of its final value, decreases. The rise time depends mainly on the anode circuit (the load inductance and the supply voltage), so the key takes it as unaffected.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The rise time does not increase.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The delay time decreases.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The delay time does not increase.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> More gate current: shorter delay.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-112", year: 2023, no: 112, subject: "pe", topic: "Average output of a single-phase bridge rectifier",
q: `A single-phase full-wave bridge rectifier circuit is fed from a 220 V, 50 Hz supply. It consists of four diodes, a load resistance 20 Ω and a very large inductance so that the load current is constant. What is the average or dc output voltage?`,
opts: ["78·135 V", "140·125 V", "198·165 V", "311·025 V"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 198·165 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Given:</b> V_rms = 220 V, so V_m = 220√2 = 311.1 V.</p><div class="formula">V_m = √2V_rms</div><div class="formula">Full-wave: V_dc = 2V_m/π</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>V_dc = 2 × 311.1/π = 198.1 V (option 198.165 V, the slight difference comes from the rounded π and √2).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 78.1 V ≈ 0.355 × 220, no rectifier formula gives it.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 140.1 V = 0.637 × 220 uses V_rms in place of V_m.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 311 V is the peak, not the average.</li></ul><p><b>Formula:</b> for a full-wave bridge with a constant load current the output voltage is the rectified sine wave: V_dc = 2V_m/π.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> V_dc = 0.9 V_rms.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-113", year: 2023, no: 113, subject: "pe", topic: "Overlap in a controlled rectifier",
q: `In a single-phase full-wave controlled bridge rectifier with source inductance and R-L load, the output voltage during overlap is equal to`,
opts: ["source voltage", "difference of source voltage and voltage drop in inductance", "zero", "addition of source voltage and voltage drop in inductance"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) zero</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> During the overlap (commutation) interval all four thyristors conduct, so the output terminals are short-circuited through the source inductance: the output voltage is zero while the load current is carried by the freewheeling action of the bridge.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The source voltage appears across the inductance, not the load.</li><li><b>(b)</b> This is the voltage across the other branches, not the output.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The drop is across the inductance, which does not add to the output.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Overlap: all four SCRs on, v_o = 0.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-114", year: 2023, no: 114, subject: "pe", topic: "Ripple factor of a step-down chopper",
q: `If a step-down chopper operates in the continuous conduction mode with a duty cycle D, the ripple factor is`,
opts: ["√(D − D²)/D", "√(D² + D)/D", "D/√(D² − D)", "D/√(D + D²)"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) √(D − D²)/D</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a chopper output of V_s for DT and 0 for (1 − D)T: V_dc = DV_s and V_rms = √D V_s.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>(V_rms/V_dc)² = D/D² = 1/D.</li><li>RF = √(1/D − 1) = √((1 − D)/D) = √(D − D²)/D.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> √(D² + D) would follow from a sign error.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Inverted and with the wrong sign.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Inverted.</li></ul><p><b>Formula:</b> ripple factor = √[(V_rms/V_dc)² − 1].</p><p><b>Remember:</b> RF = √(V_rms²/V_dc² − 1).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-115", year: 2023, no: 115, subject: "pe", topic: "First-quadrant drive converter", doubt: true,
q: `When a separately excited dc motor is to be controlled from a 3-phase source for operation in the first quadrant only, which one of the following converters is used?`,
opts: ["Three-phase dual converter", "Three-phase semi converter", "Three-phase full converter", "Three-phase half-wave converter"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Three-phase half-wave converter</h3><p class="note">Doubt: a three-phase semiconverter is also a one-quadrant converter, and UPSC’s own 2022 key (ESE 2022 Q81) took the semiconverter for the same question wording. Our solution was (b); followed the UPSC key (d) here. Web check: sources call the three-phase semiconverter the usual answer and the half-wave converter only a theoretical one-quadrant option (d.c. in the supply current), so the two UPSC keys are not consistent.</p><p><b>Concept:</b> Both the three-phase half-wave converter and the three-phase semiconverter operate in the first quadrant only (positive voltage and current); the full converter gives two quadrants and the dual converter four. The key names the half-wave converter, the simplest one-quadrant three-phase converter.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A dual converter gives four-quadrant operation.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A semiconverter is also first-quadrant only; our solution took it as the usual choice.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A full converter gives two-quadrant operation.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Dual: 4 quadrants; full: 2; half-wave, semi: 1.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-116", year: 2023, no: 116, subject: "machines", topic: "Speed control of a slip-ring motor",
q: `A wound rotor or slip-ring asynchronous motor is commonly used as drive in overhead cranes and load equalization. Which of the following speed controls is adopted?`,
opts: ["Stator current control", "Static rotor resistance control", "Stator voltage control", "Stator voltage and frequency control"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Static rotor resistance control</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Slip-ring motors allow external resistance in the rotor circuit; varying it gives a high starting torque and a wide speed range, which suits cranes. In drives it is done with a static (chopper-controlled) rotor resistance.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Stator current control is not a standard method.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Stator voltage control gives a narrow speed range.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Voltage and frequency control is used with squirrel-cage motors.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Slip ring: rotor resistance control.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-117", year: 2023, no: 117, subject: "pe", topic: "Series resonant converter",
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct in a series resonant converter?`,
opts: ["The load current is a square waveform.", "The output voltage waveform depends on the damping factor of load impedance.", "The trigger frequency is higher than the damped resonant frequency.", "The output voltage waveform does not depend on the damping factor of load impedance."], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) The output voltage waveform depends on the damping factor of load impedance</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a series resonant converter the load current is quasi-sinusoidal, determined by the L-C-R resonant circuit. The shape of the output voltage depends on the damping factor of the load impedance (underdamped or overdamped).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The load current is a resonant sine pulse, not a square wave.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The trigger frequency is chosen below the damped resonant frequency for the self-commutation of the thyristors.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The output voltage does depend on the damping factor.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Series resonant: sinusoidal current, damping decides the shape.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-118", year: 2023, no: 118, subject: "pe", topic: "Resonant converter switching",
q: `Which of the following switching techniques is commonly opted in resonant converters?`,
opts: ["Frequency switching", "Voltage switching", "Zero voltage switching or zero current switching", "Current switching"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Zero voltage switching or zero current switching</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Resonant converters make the switch voltage or current pass through zero by resonance, so that the switch changes state at zero voltage (ZVS) or zero current (ZCS). This removes the switching loss and the stresses.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Frequency switching is not a switching technique.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Hard voltage switching causes losses.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Hard current switching causes losses.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> ZVS / ZCS: soft switching.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-119", year: 2023, no: 119, subject: "pe", topic: "Static transfer switch in a UPS",
q: `The function of static transfer switch in a multimodular Uninterruptible Power Supply (UPS) system is to change over`,
opts: ["the power supply to the critical load from the UPS to power line", "the battery bank to the critical load", "the power supply to UPS", "the battery bank to the UPS from the power line"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) the power supply to the critical load from the UPS to power line</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The static transfer switch moves the critical load from the UPS inverter to the bypass (mains) supply without interruption if the inverter fails or is overloaded.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The battery is connected to the inverter, not through the transfer switch.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The supply to the UPS is not switched by it.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The battery bank is not changed over by it.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> STS: UPS ↔ bypass line.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-120", year: 2023, no: 120, subject: "power", topic: "Operating point of an SVC", doubt: true,
q: `Static VAR Compensator (SVC) is usually designed to operate at`,
opts: ["slightly lagging power factor", "leading power factor", "unity power factor", "zero power factor lagging"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) slightly lagging power factor</h3><p class="note">Doubt: general descriptions of SVCs say they bring the system towards unity power factor and operate on either side of it (a web search found no source that gives a slightly lagging design point). Our solution was (c); followed the UPSC key (a).</p><p><b>Concept:</b> An SVC is built from thyristor-controlled reactors with fixed capacitor banks, and is usually designed to operate near the lagging side of unity so that the inductive range is available for absorbing VARs when needed, and the capacitive range to supply them. The key lists a slightly lagging power factor.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> A leading design would remove the margin for inductive operation.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Unity is only one point in the control range.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Zero power factor lagging means a purely inductive current.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Key: slightly lagging.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-121", year: 2023, no: 121, subject: "ad", topic: "Spectral efficiency of 16-QAM",
q: `What is the spectral efficiency of 16-QAM?`,
opts: ["1 bps/Hz", "2 bps/Hz", "3 bps/Hz", "4 bps/Hz"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 4 bps/Hz</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> M-ary QAM carries log₂M bits per symbol. With ideal Nyquist filtering the symbol rate equals the bandwidth, so the spectral efficiency is log₂M bit/s/Hz.</p><div class="formula">η = log₂M (bps/Hz)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>16-QAM: log₂16 = <b>4 bps/Hz</b>.</li><li>(With raised-cosine roll-off α, η = log₂M/(1 + α).)</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1 bps/Hz is BPSK.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 2 bps/Hz is QPSK/4-QAM.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 3 bps/Hz is 8-PSK.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 4 bps/Hz.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Higher M gives more bits per Hz but needs higher SNR.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-122", year: 2023, no: 122, subject: "ad", topic: "Minimum sampling rate",
q: `A continuous-time signal is given below: x(t) = 8 cos 200πt. What is the minimum sampling rate required to avoid aliasing?`,
opts: ["100 Hz", "200 Hz", "50 Hz", "150 Hz"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 200 Hz</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Nyquist sampling theorem: a band-limited signal must be sampled at more than twice its highest frequency to avoid aliasing. Convert the angular frequency to Hz first.</p><div class="formula">f = ω/2π</div><div class="formula">f_s ≥ 2f_max</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ω = 200π ⇒ f = 100 Hz.</li><li>f_s(min) = 2 × 100 = <b>200 Hz</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 100 Hz is the signal frequency itself.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 200 Hz.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 50 Hz is far below Nyquist.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 150 Hz is below the Nyquist rate.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> The amplitude 8 does not affect the sampling rate.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-123", year: 2023, no: 123, subject: "ad", topic: "Glitch in a DAC",
q: `By considering the standard notations, in DACs, a glitch due to the switch associated with the most significant bit can have an amplitude almost equal to`,
opts: ["2V_ref", "½ V_ref", "3V_ref", "⅓ V_ref"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) ½ V_ref</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The most significant bit carries a weight of V_ref/2. When the code changes from 0111… to 1000…, the MSB switch and the other switches do not change at exactly the same instant, and the output can momentarily swing by almost the MSB weight, ½ V_ref.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A glitch cannot exceed the full-scale range by a factor of 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 3V_ref is above the full scale.</li><li><b>(d)</b> ⅓ V_ref is not a binary weight.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> MSB weight = V_ref/2.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-124", year: 2023, no: 124, subject: "ad", topic: "Q factor of a band-pass filter",
q: `What is the value of Q factor if the band pass filter is having frequencies from 800 Hz to 1200 Hz?`,
opts: ["1·30", "2·45", "3·40", "4·35"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2·45</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Given:</b> f_L = 800 Hz, f_H = 1200 Hz.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Bandwidth B = 400 Hz; centre frequency f₀ = √(f_L f_H) = √(960 000) = 979.8 Hz.</li><li>Q = f₀/B = 979.8/400 = 2.45.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1.30 would need B ≈ 754 Hz.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 3.40 would need B ≈ 288 Hz.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 4.35 would need B ≈ 225 Hz.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Q = f₀/B with f₀ = √(f_L f_H).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-125", year: 2023, no: 125, subject: "ad", topic: "Carrier power of an AM wave",
q: `An AM broadcast radio transfer radiates 10 k Watts of power if modulation percentage is 60. What is the carrier power?`,
opts: ["6·25 kW", "8·47 kW", "13·60 kW", "10·00 kW"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 8·47 kW</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In AM the total power is P_t = P_c(1 + m²/2); the carrier power is unchanged by modulation, so P_c = P_t/(1 + m²/2).</p><div class="formula">P_c = P_t/(1 + m²/2)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>1 + 0.6²/2 = 1.18.</li><li>P_c = 10/1.18 = <b>8.47 kW</b>; sidebands 1.53 kW.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 6.25 kW = 10/1.6 uses (1 + m) instead of (1 + m²/2).</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 8.47 kW.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 13.6 kW = 10 × 1.36 multiplies instead of divides.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 10 kW is the total radiated power.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Each sideband carries m²P_c/4.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2023f.js ----
// ESE 2023 Prelims Paper II, Electrical Engineering, Series A (MNGY-D-ETE), Q126–150
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; none dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2023-126", year: 2023, no: 126, subject: "ad", topic: "FM output of a PLL detector",
q: `A PLL FM detector uses a VCO with k_f = 100 kHz/V. If it receives an FM signal with a deviation of 75 kHz and sine-wave modulation, what is the RMS output voltage from the detector?`,
opts: ["3·57 V", "2·54 V", "1·55 V", "0·53 V"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·53 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a PLL FM detector the loop forces the VCO to track the incoming frequency, so the VCO control voltage is the demodulated output: v_o = Δf/k_f. For sine-wave modulation that peak voltage corresponds to an rms value of peak/√2.</p><div class="formula">V_peak = Δf/k_f</div><div class="formula">V_rms = V_peak/√2</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>V_peak = 75 kHz/(100 kHz/V) = 0.75 V.</li><li>V_rms = 0.75/√2 = <b>0.53 V</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 3.57 V would need Δf ≈ 505 kHz.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 2.54 V would need Δf ≈ 359 kHz.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1.55 V would need Δf ≈ 219 kHz.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 0.53 V.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Higher VCO gain k_f ⇒ smaller demodulated output.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-127", year: 2023, no: 127, subject: "ad", topic: "Noise factor of an amplifier",
q: `The signal power and noise power measured at the input of an amplifier are 150 μW and 1·5 μW respectively. If the signal power at the output 1·5 W and noise power is 40 mW, what is the amplifier noise factor?`,
opts: ["2·666", "3·750", "4·266", "5·625"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 2·666</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Noise factor F is the ratio of input SNR to output SNR (both as power ratios). An ideal noiseless amplifier has F = 1; noise figure NF = 10 log F.</p><div class="formula">F = (S/N)_in/(S/N)_out</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>(S/N)_in = 150 μW/1.5 μW = 100.</li><li>(S/N)_out = 1.5 W/0.04 W = 37.5.</li><li>F = 100/37.5 = <b>2.666</b> (NF = 4.26 dB).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 2.666.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 3.75 = 150/40, mixing input signal with output noise.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 4.266 is close to the NF in dB, not F.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 5.625 = 150/... mixes input signal with the wrong noise term; it is not SNR_in/SNR_out.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Gain here is 10 000 (40 dB); output noise 40 mW vs 15 mW amplified input noise ⇒ amplifier adds 25 mW.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-128", year: 2023, no: 128, subject: "ad", topic: "Entropy of a discrete source",
q: `A discrete source emits one of five symbols once every millisecond with probabilities 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, 1/16 and 1/16 respectively. What is the source entropy?`,
opts: ["0·875 bits/symbol", "1·875 bits/symbol", "2·875 bits/symbol", "3·875 bits/symbol"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1·875 bits/symbol</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula:</b> H = Σ p log₂(1/p).</p><div class="formula">H = Σ pᵢ log₂(1/pᵢ)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>½ × 1 = 0.5; ¼ × 2 = 0.5; ⅛ × 3 = 0.375; 2 × (1/16 × 4) = 0.5.</li><li>H = 0.5 + 0.5 + 0.375 + 0.5 = 1.875 bits/symbol.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.875 omits the 0.5 of the first term.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2.875 adds one bit too many.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 3.875 adds two bits too many.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> H = Σ p log(1/p).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-129", year: 2023, no: 129, subject: "computer", topic: "AND instruction of the 8086",
q: `According to the description of the AND instruction of 8086 microprocessor, what are the essential conditions to be satisfied while executing it? 1. The source can be a register or a memory location. 2. The destination can be a register or a memory location. 3. The source and destination must both be bytes or words. 4. The source and the destination cannot both be memory locations in an instruction.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4", "1, 2 and 3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For AND destination, source in the 8086: the source may be a register or a memory location (or immediate data), the destination may be a register or a memory location, both operands must be of the same size (both bytes or both words), and the two operands cannot both be memory locations. All four conditions are true.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> 8086: no memory-to-memory operations.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-130", year: 2023, no: 130, subject: "ad", topic: "Modulation index of an AM wave",
q: `What is the modulation index, if the E_max = 150 mV and E_min = 70 mV?`,
opts: ["40%", "30%", "36·4%", "44·6%"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 36·4%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a trapezoidal/envelope display of an AM wave, the modulation index follows from the maximum and minimum envelope amplitudes: E_max = E_c(1 + m), E_min = E_c(1 − m).</p><div class="formula">m = (E_max − E_min)/(E_max + E_min)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>m = (150 − 70)/(150 + 70) = 80/220 = <b>0.364 = 36.4 %</b>.</li><li>Carrier amplitude E_c = (150 + 70)/2 = 110 mV.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 40 % = 80/200 (wrong denominator).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 30 % does not match the formula.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 36.4 %.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 44.6 % does not match the formula.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> m = 1 when E_min = 0.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-131", year: 2023, no: 131, subject: "ad", topic: "Thermal noise power",
q: `A receiver has a noise power bandwidth of 12 kHz. A resistor which matches with the receiver input impedance is conducted across the antenna terminals. What is the noise power contributed by this resistor in the receiver bandwidth? (Take temperature as 30 °C)`,
opts: ["2·581 × 10⁻¹⁷ W", "3·181 × 10⁻¹⁷ W", "4·636 × 10⁻¹⁷ W", "5·017 × 10⁻¹⁷ W"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 5·017 × 10⁻¹⁷ W</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A resistor matched to the receiver input delivers its available thermal noise power kTB, independent of the resistance value (maximum power transfer of the noise source 4kTRB into a matched load).</p><div class="formula">N = kTB</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>T = 303 K, B = 12 kHz.</li><li>N = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 303 × 12 × 10³ = <b>5.017 × 10⁻¹⁷ W</b> (−163 dBW).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Would need T ≈ 156 K.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Would need T ≈ 192 K.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Would need T ≈ 280 K (7 °C).</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> kTB at 30 °C.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> kT at 290 K = −174 dBm/Hz.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-132", year: 2023, no: 132, subject: "ad", topic: "Thermal noise voltage",
q: `An amplifier has a bandwidth of 4 MHz with 10 kΩ as the input resistor. What is the rms noise voltage at the input to this amplifier if the room temperature is 25 °C?`,
opts: ["35·23 μV", "40·55 μV", "25·65 μV", "14·62 μV"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 25·65 μV</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Thermal (Johnson) noise voltage of a resistor: open-circuit rms value √(4kTBR).</p><div class="formula">V_n = √(4kTBR)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>4kTBR = 4 × 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 298 × 4 × 10⁶ × 10⁴ = 6.58 × 10⁻¹⁰ V².</li><li>V_n = √(6.58 × 10⁻¹⁰) = <b>25.65 μV</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 35.23 μV would need about twice the R·B product.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 40.55 μV would need 2.5 times R·B.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 25.65 μV.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 14.62 μV would need about one-third of R·B.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> V_n ∝ √(RB): quadruple R or B to double the noise voltage.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-133", year: 2023, no: 133, subject: "signals", topic: "FFT multiplications",
q: `How many numbers of multiplications are needed in the calculation of FFT with 64 point sequence?`,
opts: ["160", "172", "192", "200"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 192</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A radix-2 FFT has log₂N stages, each with N/2 butterflies, and each butterfly has one complex multiplication (by a twiddle factor). A direct DFT needs N² multiplications.</p><div class="formula">FFT: (N/2) log₂N</div><div class="formula">DFT: N²</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>N = 64: (64/2) × 6 = <b>192</b> complex multiplications (direct DFT: 4096).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 160 = 32 × 5 uses one stage too few.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 172 does not fit (N/2)log₂N.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 192.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 200 does not fit (N/2)log₂N.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Speed-up = 2N/log₂N ≈ 21 for N = 64.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-134", year: 2023, no: 134, subject: "signals", topic: "Butterworth polynomial",
q: `Which one of the following is the correct Butterworth polynomial for second order filter?`,
opts: ["s² + s + 1", "s² + √2 s + 1", "s² + 2s + 1", "s² + s + 2"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) s² + √2 s + 1</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A Butterworth low-pass filter has a maximally flat magnitude |H(jω)|² = 1/(1 + ω²ⁿ). Its poles lie equally spaced on the unit circle in the left half-plane. For n = 2 they are at e^(±j135°) = −0.707 ± j0.707, giving ζ = 0.707.</p><div class="formula">n = 2: (s + 0.707)² + 0.707² = s² + √2 s + 1</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Poles −1/√2 ± j/√2 ⇒ s² + √2 s + 1.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> s² + s + 1 has ζ = 0.5 (peaked; a 2nd-order section of a 3rd-order Butterworth).</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> s² + √2 s + 1.</li><li><b>(c)</b> (s + 1)²: critically damped, not maximally flat.</li><li><b>(d)</b> s² + s + 2 has ω_n = √2, not normalised.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Butterworth: ζ = 0.707, −3 dB at ω = 1 for any order.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-135", year: 2023, no: 135, subject: "signals", topic: "Bilinear transformation",
q: `Which one of the following is the correct relation of mapping from the ‘s-plane’ to the ‘z-plane’ in bilinear transformation?`,
opts: ["s = (2/T)[(1 + z⁻¹)/(1 − z⁻¹)]", "s = (T/2)[(1 − z⁻¹)/(1 + z⁻¹)]", "s = (T/2)[(1 + z⁻¹)/(1 − z⁻¹)]", "s = (2/T)[(1 − z⁻¹)/(1 + z⁻¹)]"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) s = (2/T)[(1 − z⁻¹)/(1 + z⁻¹)]</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The bilinear transformation is derived from trapezoidal integration of 1/s: y(n) − y(n − 1) = (T/2)[x(n) + x(n − 1)] ⇒ 1/s ↔ (T/2)(1 + z⁻¹)/(1 − z⁻¹). Inverting gives s in terms of z.</p><div class="formula">s = (2/T)(1 − z⁻¹)/(1 + z⁻¹)</div><div class="formula">Warping: Ω = (2/T) tan(ω/2)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Left half s-plane → inside the unit circle; jΩ axis → unit circle (one-to-one, no aliasing).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Numerator and denominator inverted.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Factor T/2 belongs to 1/s, not s.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Wrong factor and inverted.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Pre-warp critical frequencies with tan(ω/2) before design.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-136", year: 2023, no: 136, subject: "signals", topic: "Fundamental period of a complex exponential",
q: `The fundamental period of complex signal x(t) = e^(jω₀t) is`,
opts: ["2π/ω₀", "π/ω₀", "2πω₀", "πω₀"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 2π/ω₀</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> x(t) = e^(jω₀t) = cos ω₀t + j sin ω₀t is periodic if x(t + T) = x(t), i.e. e^(jω₀T) = 1, which needs ω₀T = 2πk. The smallest positive T is the fundamental period.</p><div class="formula">T₀ = 2π/|ω₀|</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ω₀T = 2π ⇒ T₀ = <b>2π/ω₀</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 2π/ω₀.</li><li><b>(b)</b> π/ω₀ gives e^(jπ) = −1 ≠ 1.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Dimensionally wrong (rad²/s).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Dimensionally wrong.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> A constant (ω₀ = 0) is periodic with any period – no fundamental period.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-137", year: 2023, no: 137, subject: "signals", topic: "Energy of a signal",
q: `The energy contained of the signal x(t) = e⁻²ᵗ u(t) is`,
opts: ["1/2", "2", "1/4", "4"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1/4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Energy of a signal: E = ∫|x(t)|²dt. For x(t) = e^(−at)u(t), E = ∫₀^∞ e^(−2at)dt = 1/(2a).</p><div class="formula">E = ∫₋∞^∞ |x(t)|² dt</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>a = 2: E = ∫₀^∞ e^(−4t)dt = <b>1/4</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1/2 is ∫x(t)dt (the area), not the energy.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 2 is not 1/(2a).</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 1/4.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 4 = 2a, the reciprocal of the energy.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Energy signals have zero average power.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-138", year: 2023, no: 138, subject: "signals", topic: "Scaling property of the impulse",
q: `Which one of the following is a useful property of the unit impulse signal δ(t)?`,
opts: ["δ(at) = aδ(t)", "δ(at) = δ(t)", "δ(at) = [δ(t)]⁻ᵃ", "δ(at) = (1/|a|) δ(t)"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) δ(at) = (1/|a|) δ(t)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Time-scaling an impulse compresses it in time while keeping its height infinite, so its area changes: ∫δ(at)dt = 1/|a| (substitute τ = at). Hence δ(at) = δ(t)/|a|.</p><div class="formula">δ(at) = δ(t)/|a|</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>∫δ(at)dt, put τ = at ⇒ (1/|a|)∫δ(τ)dτ = 1/|a|.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Factor is 1/|a|, not a.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Ignores the change in area.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Not a property of δ.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> δ(t)/|a|.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Consequence: δ(−t) = δ(t) (impulse is even).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-139", year: 2023, no: 139, subject: "signals", topic: "Classification of y(t) = x(sin t)",
q: `Which one of the following is correct for the system represented by the relation y(t) = x(sin(t)), where x(t) and y(t) are input and output respectively?`,
opts: ["It is causal and linear.", "It is non-causal and linear.", "It is causal and non-linear.", "It is non-causal and non-linear."], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) It is non-causal and linear</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Causality:</b> for t &lt; 0, sin t &gt; t (for example at t = −1, sin t = −0.84), so the output at time t uses the input at a later time sin t: the system is non-causal.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> It is non-causal.</li><li><b>(c)</b> It is non-causal and linear.</li><li><b>(d)</b> It is linear.</li></ul><p><b>Linearity:</b> the response to a₁x₁ + a₂x₂ is a₁x₁(sin t) + a₂x₂(sin t) = a₁y₁ + a₂y₂. The system is linear (but time-varying).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Check causality and superposition separately.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-140", year: 2023, no: 140, subject: "signals", topic: "Basis of Fourier series",
q: `The Fourier series representations are based on using`,
opts: ["constant coefficients", "cosine functions only", "sine functions only", "orthogonal functions"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) orthogonal functions</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A Fourier series expands a periodic signal in a set of orthogonal functions (sines and cosines or complex exponentials), which makes the coefficients obtainable by integration.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Constant coefficients are the result, not the basis.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Sines are also needed.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Cosines are also needed.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Fourier series: orthogonal basis.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-141", year: 2023, no: 141, subject: "signals", topic: "Fourier transform of a rectangular pulse",
q: `What is the Fourier transform of rectangular pulse as shown in the figure below? (a pulse of height K from −T₀ to T₀)<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2023-141.png" alt="Figure for question 141">`,
opts: ["2KT₀ sinc(ωT₀/π)", "2KT₀ sinc(ωT₀/2π)", "KT₀ sinc(ωT₀/π)", "KT₀ sinc(ωT₀/2π)"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 2KT₀ sinc(ωT₀/π)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula:</b> X(ω) = ∫₋T₀^T₀ K e^(−jωt) dt = 2K sin(ωT₀)/ω.</p><div class="formula">X(ω) = ∫ x(t)e^(−jωt) dt</div><div class="formula">sinc(x) = sin(πx)/(πx)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>X(ω) = 2KT₀ sin(ωT₀)/(ωT₀).</li><li>With sinc(x) = sin(πx)/(πx), sin(ωT₀)/(ωT₀) = sinc(ωT₀/π).</li><li>X(ω) = 2KT₀ sinc(ωT₀/π).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The argument ωT₀/2π would be correct for a pulse of total width T₀ (not 2T₀).</li><li><b>(c)</b> The area of the pulse is 2KT₀, not KT₀.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Wrong factor and wrong argument.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Pulse of area A: X(0) = A.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-142", year: 2023, no: 142, subject: "signals", topic: "Fourier transform of t e^(−|t|)",
q: `What is the Fourier transform of the function f(t) = t e^(−|t|)?`,
opts: ["4jω/(1 + ω²)²", "−4jω/(1 + ω²)²", "4jω/(1 + ω)²", "−4jω/(1 + ω)²"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) −4jω/(1 + ω²)²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The transform of e^(−|t|) is 2/(1 + ω²). The differentiation-in-frequency property gives F{t f(t)} = j dF/dω.</p><div class="formula">e^(−|t|) ↔ 2/(1 + ω²)</div><div class="formula">t f(t) ↔ j dF/dω</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>d/dω[2/(1 + ω²)] = −4ω/(1 + ω²)².</li><li>F{t e^(−|t|)} = j × (−4ω)/(1 + ω²)² = −4jω/(1 + ω²)².</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The sign is wrong.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The denominator should be (1 + ω²)², not (1 + ω)², and the sign is wrong.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The denominator should be (1 + ω²)².</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> t f(t) ↔ j dF/dω.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-143", year: 2023, no: 143, subject: "signals", topic: "Signal from Fourier-series facts",
q: `What is the signal x(t), based on the following facts: 1. x(t) is periodic with period T₀ = 4 and has Fourier series coefficients c[k]. 2. x(t) is real-valued. 3. c[k] = 0 for |k| > 1. 4. The signal y(t), whose Fourier series coefficients are d[k] = e^(−jπk/2) c[k], is odd. 5. (1/4)∫₋₂² |x(t)|² dt = 1/2.`,
opts: ["(2)^m cos(ω₀t)", "(−1)^m cos(ω₀t)", "(−1)^m cos(2ω₀t)", "(−2)^m cos(ω₀/2t)"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) (−1)^m cos(ω₀t)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> With T₀ = 4, ω₀ = 2π/4 = π/2 rad/s.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Facts 2 and 3: x(t) = c₀ + 2|c₁| cos(ω₀t + φ), a real signal with harmonics up to k = 1.</li><li>d[k] = e^(−jπk/2)c[k] = e^(−jkω₀)c[k] is a delay of 1 s: y(t) = x(t − 1). An odd y(t) has no d.c. term, so c₀ = 0.</li><li>Fact 5 (Parseval): power = 2|c₁|² = 1/2, so |c₁| = 1/2 and x(t) = cos(ω₀t + φ).</li><li>y(t) = cos(ω₀t − π/2 + φ) is odd only when φ = 0 or π, so x(t) = ±cos(ω₀t) = (−1)^m cos(ω₀t).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The amplitude 2^m contradicts the power condition (amplitude 1).</li><li><b>(c)</b> A 2ω₀ term would be the k = 2 harmonic, which is zero by fact 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The frequency ω₀/2 is not a harmonic of ω₀.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Delay property, then Parseval.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-144", year: 2023, no: 144, subject: "signals", topic: "Stability triangle of a second-order discrete system",
q: `From the plot shown below, what are the necessary and sufficient conditions for a second order causal LTI discrete time system to be stable?<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2023-144.png" alt="Figure for question 144">`,
opts: ["|α₁| < 1 & |α₂| < 1 + α₁", "|α₂| < 1 & |α₁| < 1 + α₂", "|α₁| > 1 & |α₂| > 1 + α₁", "|α₂| > 1 & |α₁| > 1 + α₂"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) |α₂| < 1 & |α₁| < 1 + α₂</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a causal second-order system with denominator 1 + α₁z⁻¹ + α₂z⁻² (poles from z² + α₁z + α₂ = 0), the poles lie inside the unit circle if and only if (α₁, α₂) is inside the 'stability triangle' with vertices (−2, 1), (2, 1), (0, −1): |α₂| &lt; 1 and |α₁| &lt; 1 + α₂ (Jury test).</p><div class="formula">|α₂| &lt; 1</div><div class="formula">|α₁| &lt; 1 + α₂</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Product of the poles = α₂ ⇒ |α₂| &lt; 1.</li><li>D(1) = 1 + α₁ + α₂ &gt; 0 and D(−1) = 1 − α₁ + α₂ &gt; 0 ⇒ |α₁| &lt; 1 + α₂.</li><li>These are the edges of the triangle.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Roles of α₁ and α₂ interchanged.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> These describe the outside (unstable) region.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Outside region with roles interchanged.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Above the parabola α₂ = α₁²/4 the poles are complex.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-145", year: 2023, no: 145, subject: "basic", topic: "Zener diodes",
q: `Statement (I): Diodes designed expressly to operate in the forward biased region are called zener diodes. Statement (II): The principal operating region for a zener diode is negative in terms of both voltage and current.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A Zener diode is heavily doped and designed to operate in the reverse-breakdown region, where the voltage stays nearly constant over a wide current range (voltage regulation). On the standard V–I axes this region has negative voltage and negative current.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Statement I: Zeners are designed for reverse breakdown, not forward bias. False.</li><li>Statement II: operating region is in the third quadrant (negative V and I). True.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> I is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> I is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> I is false and II true – the opposite.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I false, II true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> V_Z &lt; 5 V: Zener (tunnelling) breakdown; &gt; 5 V: avalanche.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-146", year: 2023, no: 146, subject: "basic", topic: "BJT small-signal model and gain",
q: `Statement (I): A small-signal equivalent circuit of a BJT that applies to both n-p-n and p-n-p transistors is valid at lower frequencies by ignoring capacitance effects. Statement (II): Despite the structural similarities, a p-n-p BJT has higher current gain than a comparable n-p-n BJT because holes are less mobile than electrons.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At low frequencies the junction capacitances can be neglected, and the same hybrid-π (or h-parameter) model serves n-p-n and p-n-p devices (only the current directions change). Because hole mobility (≈ 480 cm²/V·s in Si) is lower than electron mobility (≈ 1350), the minority-carrier diffusion through a p-n-p base is slower, giving a lower β and lower f_T than a comparable n-p-n.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Statement I: true.</li><li>Statement II: claims p-n-p has higher gain because holes are less mobile – the conclusion is reversed. False.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I true, II false.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> n-p-n is preferred for higher β and speed.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-147", year: 2023, no: 147, subject: "basic", topic: "MOSFET versus JFET", doubt: true,
q: `Statement (I): MOSFETs are preferred over JFETs for digital integrated circuits, either p-channel metal-oxide semiconductor or n-channel metal-oxide semiconductor logic circuits can be constructed. Statement (II): The usage of a p-channel MOSFET as the active load for an n-channel MOSFET leads to a logic family known as complementary-symmetry MOS or CMOS.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)</h3><p class="note">Doubt: statement II gives a further development (CMOS) rather than the reason for the preference in statement I; our solution was (b). Followed the UPSC key (a).</p><p><b>Concept:</b> MOSFETs need no gate current, occupy little area and can be made in both p- and n-channel forms, so they dominate digital ICs (PMOS, NMOS logic). Using a p-channel MOSFET as the active load of an n-channel MOSFET (complementary pair) gives CMOS, which draws almost no static power.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Statement I: true.</li><li>Statement II: true (CMOS definition).</li><li>The key treats II as explaining the preference for MOSFETs.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Key's choice.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Our original reading (II is a consequence, not the reason).</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> CMOS static power ≈ 0; dynamic power = CV²f.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-148", year: 2023, no: 148, subject: "signals", topic: "LTI systems and the convolution sum", doubt: true,
q: `Statement (I): A system is LTI if and only if there exists a signal, called the system unit impulse response. Statement (II): The output of a discrete-time LTI system is equal to the convolution sum between the input signal and its unit impulse response.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)</h3><p class="note">Doubt: as worded, (I) is false, since every system has an impulse response and LTI is defined by linearity and time-invariance, not by the existence of h; our solution was (d). Followed the UPSC key (a).</p><p><b>Concept:</b> For an LTI system, the response to any input is the convolution sum y(n) = Σx(k)h(n − k), so the unit impulse response h(n) completely characterises the system. The key reads statement I in this sense.</p><div class="formula">y(n) = Σ_k x(k) h(n − k)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Statement II: convolution sum – true.</li><li>Statement I: the key accepts it as 'an LTI system is fully described by its impulse response', explained by II.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Key's choice.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Not the key.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Our reading: I false as worded.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Non-LTI systems also have impulse responses, but these do not predict other outputs.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-149", year: 2023, no: 149, subject: "control", topic: "Root locus and frequency response",
q: `Statement (I): The root locus is the path of the roots of the characteristic equation traced out in the s-plane as a system parameter varies from zero to infinity. Statement (II): The frequency response of a system is defined as the steady-state response of the system to a non-sinusoidal unique input signal, and the resulting output signal for a linear system is sinusoidal in the steady-state; it differs from the input only in amplitude and phase angle.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Root locus: the path of the closed-loop poles (roots of 1 + KG(s)H(s) = 0) as K varies from 0 to ∞. Frequency response: the steady-state response of a stable LTI system to a sinusoidal input; the output is a sinusoid of the same frequency, scaled by |G(jω)| and shifted by ∠G(jω).</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Statement I: correct definition of root locus. True.</li><li>Statement II: says the input is non-sinusoidal – it must be sinusoidal. False.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I true, II false.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Frequency response = G(s) evaluated at s = jω.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-150", year: 2023, no: 150, subject: "machines", topic: "Transformer non-linearity", doubt: true,
q: `Statement (I): Transformers take advantage of the high magnetic energy density of ferromagnetic material to allow economical device design, however, the non-linear nature of ferromagnetic material leads to introduction of harmonics in current or voltage. Statement (II): The non-linear nature of the transformer magnetizing inductance can lead to a transient inrush current significantly greater than the rated value of current upon energizing a transformer.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)</h3><p class="note">Doubt: statement II gives another consequence of the same non-linearity rather than an explanation of statement I; our solution was (b). Followed the UPSC key (a).</p><p><b>Concept:</b> A ferromagnetic core gives a high flux density for little magnetising current, allowing compact transformers. Its B–H curve is non-linear (saturation, hysteresis), so a sinusoidal flux needs a peaky, harmonic-rich magnetising current (mainly 3rd harmonic). The same non-linearity causes inrush current when the transformer is switched on near a voltage zero: the flux can reach about 2φ_m plus remanence, driving the core into deep saturation and drawing 5–10 times rated current.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Statement I: true (harmonics).</li><li>Statement II: true (inrush).</li><li>Key: II explains I.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Key's choice.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Our reading: both are consequences of the same non-linearity.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Inrush current is rich in 2nd harmonic – used for harmonic restraint in differential relays.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2022a.js ----
// ESE 2022 Prelims Paper II, Electrical Engineering, Series A (PKL-D-ETE), Q1–25
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; none dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2022-1", year: 2022, no: 1, subject: "basic", topic: "Base current from β and I_C",
q: `A transistor has β = 105 and I_C = 840 μA. What is the value of I_B?`,
opts: ["0·008 μA", "0·08 μA", "0·8 μA", "8 μA"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 8 μA</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Given:</b> β = 105, I_C = 840 μA.</p><div class="formula">I_B = I_C/β</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I_B = I_C/β = 840/105 μA.</li><li>I_B = 8 μA.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.008 μA is 1000 times too small (a milli/micro slip).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.08 μA is 100 times too small.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.8 μA is 10 times too small; it would need I_C = 84 μA.</li></ul><p><b>Formula:</b> β = I_C/I_B.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> I_B = I_C/β.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-2", year: 2022, no: 2, subject: "basic", topic: "Power gain of an amplifier",
q: `What is the power gain of a transistor amplifier, if its current gain is 40 and voltage gain is 25?`,
opts: ["100", "1200", "1000", "950"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1000</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Power gain is the ratio of output to input power. Since P = VI at each port, the power gain equals the product of the voltage gain and the current gain (magnitudes).</p><div class="formula">A_P = P_o/P_i = (V_oI_o)/(V_iI_i) = A_V × A_I</div><div class="formula">A_P(dB) = 10 log A_P</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>A_P = 25 × 40 = <b>1000</b> = 30 dB.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 100 is 25 × 4, a decimal slip.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1200 would need A_I = 48.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 1000.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 950 is not a product of the two gains.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> CE stage has the highest power gain because both A_V and A_I are large.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-3", year: 2022, no: 3, subject: "basic", topic: "Common-base amplifier",
q: `Which one of the following statements is not correct regarding common-base amplifier?`,
opts: ["The output is in the same phase as the input alternating signal.", "It cannot operate at higher frequency as compared to CE amplifier.", "The current gain is less than unity.", "Impedance matching is needed when cascading because there is very large difference in the input and output impedances, the input impedance is low and the output high."], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Statement (b) is not correct</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The common-base stage has no Miller multiplication of the collector–base capacitance, so its upper cut-off frequency is much higher than that of a CE stage. Hence the claim that it cannot work at a higher frequency than CE is false.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> True: CB is non-inverting.</li><li><b>(c)</b> True: α = I_C/I_E is slightly less than 1.</li><li><b>(d)</b> True: input impedance is a few tens of ohms and output impedance is hundreds of kilo-ohms.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> CB: best high-frequency response of the three configurations.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-4", year: 2022, no: 4, subject: "basic", topic: "Class of amplifier by Q-point",
q: `When the quiescent point of an amplifier is biased just at the cut-off axis, so that only the positive half of the signal input is amplified and the negative half of the signal is cut off, it is referred to as`,
opts: ["class AB amplification", "class A amplification", "class B amplification", "class C amplification"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) class B amplification</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Biasing at cut-off gives a conduction angle of 180°, which is the definition of class B operation: one device amplifies only one half-cycle.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Class AB is biased slightly above cut-off, conduction angle slightly over 180°.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Class A is biased at the middle of the load line, conduction angle 360°.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Class C is biased below cut-off, conduction angle under 180°.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Conduction angle: A 360°, AB &gt; 180°, B 180°, C &lt; 180°.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-5", year: 2022, no: 5, subject: "basic", topic: "Disadvantage of CE amplifier",
q: `Which one of the following is a disadvantage of CE amplifier?`,
opts: ["It provides good current as well as voltage gain", "It provides the maximum power gain of the three configurations", "It has medium both input and output impedances", "Its frequency response bandwidth is lower than the amplifiers of the other two configurations"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Lower bandwidth</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The CE stage gets a large Miller capacitance C_μ(1 + A_V) at its input, which pushes the upper cut-off frequency down. CB and CC stages have much wider bandwidth, so lower bandwidth is the real drawback.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Good current and voltage gain is an advantage.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Maximum power gain is an advantage.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Medium input and output impedances suit cascading, an advantage.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> CE: high gain, low bandwidth.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-6", year: 2022, no: 6, subject: "basic", topic: "R-C phase-shift oscillator",
q: `Which one of the following consists of op-amp in inverting mode and network of R-C components, and the op-amp being in inverting mode it serves two purposes of amplifying and at its output 180° shifted phase is obtained?`,
opts: ["Wien's bridge oscillator", "R-C phase-shift oscillator", "Triangular wave generator", "Charging capacitor"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) R-C phase-shift oscillator</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The inverting op-amp gives 180° and the three-section R-C ladder gives another 180° at the oscillation frequency f = 1/(2πRC√6). The loop phase is then 360°, and the gain must be at least 29. This describes the R-C phase-shift oscillator.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A Wien bridge oscillator uses a non-inverting amplifier with zero phase shift in the lead-lag network.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A triangular generator is an integrator plus a comparator, not a sinusoidal feedback oscillator.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A charging capacitor is a passive element.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> RC phase shift: 3 sections × 60°.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-7", year: 2022, no: 7, subject: "basic", topic: "Oscillator conditions",
q: `Which one of the following statements is not correct related to oscillators?`,
opts: ["The frequency of a sinusoidal oscillator is determined by the condition that the loop-gain phase shift is zero.", "In every practical oscillator, the loop gain is slightly larger than unity and the amplitude of the oscillations is limited by the onset of non-linearity.", "The condition of unity loop gain −Aβ = 1 is called the Barkhausen criterion.", "Oscillations will be sustained if, at the oscillator frequency, the magnitude of the product of the transfer gain of the amplifier and the magnitude of the feedback factor of the feedback network are less than unity."], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Statement (d) is not correct</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Sustained oscillations need |Aβ| ≥ 1 at the frequency where the loop phase is 0° (360°). If |Aβ| &lt; 1 the oscillation dies out.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> True: the phase condition fixes the frequency.</li><li><b>(b)</b> True: start-up needs |Aβ| slightly above 1, non-linearity then limits the amplitude.</li><li><b>(c)</b> True: the Barkhausen criterion.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Barkhausen: |Aβ| = 1, phase 0.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-8", year: 2022, no: 8, subject: "basic", topic: "Transistor doping and structure",
q: `Which one of the following statements is not correct for a transistor?`,
opts: ["The region at the centre is always base region.", "Emitter region is more heavily doped and base region is very lightly doped.", "Collector region is very lightly doped compared to base region.", "Low power input can be converted to a large power output with the help of a small piece of semiconductor without any hassles of preheating and handling of large heat dissipation."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Statement (c) is not correct</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The collector is lightly doped relative to the emitter, but it is doped more heavily than the base. The doping order is emitter &gt; collector &gt; base, so the collector is not very lightly doped compared with the base; the base is the most lightly doped region.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> True: the base is the thin middle layer.</li><li><b>(b)</b> True: heavy emitter doping gives high injection efficiency.</li><li><b>(d)</b> True: a small solid-state device amplifies without heating a filament.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Doping: E &gt; C &gt; B.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-9", year: 2022, no: 9, subject: "basic", topic: "Punch-through",
q: `For certain of the reverse voltage in a transistor, the effective base width may reduce to zero resulting into the voltage breakdown. This phenomenon is called`,
opts: ["early effect", "avalanche multiplication", "punch through", "zones breakdown"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) punch through</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> As the collector junction reverse bias rises, its depletion region widens into the base. When it reaches the emitter depletion region the neutral base width becomes zero and a large current flows: this is punch-through (reach-through).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The Early effect is only the narrowing of the base, which changes α and β but does not break down the device.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Avalanche multiplication is carrier multiplication by impact ionisation.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Zone breakdown is not a standard term.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Base width → 0: punch-through.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-10", year: 2022, no: 10, subject: "materials", topic: "Electron mobility from Hall data",
q: `An n-type semiconductor specimen has a Hall coefficient of 300 cm³/C and its resistivity is 0·1 Ω-cm. Its electron mobility is`,
opts: ["300 cm²/V-sec", "30 cm²/V-sec", "3000 cm²/V-sec", "3 cm²/V-sec"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3000 cm²/V-sec</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Hall coefficient of an n-type sample: |R_H| = 1/(nq). Conductivity σ = nqμ_n. Multiplying: σR_H = μ_n, so mobility = R_H/ρ.</p><div class="formula">R_H = 1/(nq)</div><div class="formula">σ = nqμ ⇒ μ = σR_H = R_H/ρ</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>μ = 300 cm³/C ÷ 0.1 Ω·cm = <b>3000 cm²/V·s</b>.</li><li>Carrier density n = 1/(qR_H) = 1/(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 300) = 2.1 × 10¹⁶ cm⁻³.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 300 would need ρ = 1 Ω·cm.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 30 would need ρ = 10 Ω·cm.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 3000 cm²/V·s.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 3 would need ρ = 100 Ω·cm.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Hall measurement gives carrier type (sign), density and mobility together.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-11", year: 2022, no: 11, subject: "machines", topic: "Motors in power electronic systems", doubt: true,
q: `Which of the following motors are commonly used in power electronic systems? 1. Synchronous motors 2. d.c. motors 3. d.c. motors with brushes. Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2 only</h3><p class="note">Doubt: a brush-type d.c. motor is itself a d.c. motor, so statement 3 overlaps with statement 2; our solution was all three (d). Followed the UPSC key (b). Web check: a Testbook solution of the same item reasons that DC motors with and without brushes are controlled by power electronics (answering 2 and 3), so sources disagree with the key.</p><p><b>Concept:</b> Power-electronic drives are built around synchronous machines (including permanent-magnet and brushless types) and d.c. motors with controlled rectifier or chopper supplies. Brush-type commutator machines are not the type preferred in modern power-electronic systems because the brushes need maintenance and limit speed and power rating.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> Used: synchronous motors fed from inverters.</li><li><b>2</b> Used: d.c. motors on converter or chopper supplies.</li><li><b>3</b> Not counted by the key as a separate common choice.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Key: synchronous and d.c. motors.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-12", year: 2022, no: 12, subject: "basic", topic: "Reverse saturation current and temperature",
q: `In P-N junction diode, the reverse saturation current increases by 7·2% by a degree rise in junction temperature (in Celsius) and gets`,
opts: ["halved for every 10 °C rise in temperature", "doubled for every 10 °C rise in temperature", "halved for every 20 °C rise in temperature", "doubled for every 20 °C rise in temperature"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) doubled for every 10 °C rise</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Given:</b> 7.2 % per °C, so the factor per degree is 1.072.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>For 10 °C: 1.072¹⁰ = e^(10 × 0.0695) = e^0.695 ≈ 2.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Reverse current rises, not falls, with temperature.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Same reason, and the period is wrong.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1.072²⁰ ≈ 4, not 2.</li></ul><p>I_0 therefore doubles for every 10 °C rise (the usual rule for both Si and Ge).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> I_0 doubles per 10 °C.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-13", year: 2022, no: 13, subject: "computer", topic: "Interrupt controller",
q: `Which one of the following controllers is to check the status of each device and inform the central processing unit of the status of each?`,
opts: ["Programmable I/O interrupt controller", "DMA controller", "Disk controller", "Pipeline controller"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Programmable I/O interrupt controller</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A programmable interrupt controller (such as the 8259) collects the interrupt requests from the devices, checks which are pending, ranks them by priority and reports them to the CPU.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> A DMA controller moves data between memory and I/O without the CPU.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A disk controller operates only the disk drive.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A pipeline controller handles hazards and stalls inside the CPU.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> PIC reports device status to the CPU.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-14", year: 2022, no: 14, subject: "computer", topic: "Bus for video throughput",
q: `Which one of the following bus architectures is used to maximize throughput of video graphics memory?`,
opts: ["EISA bus", "VESA bus", "PCI bus", "MCA bus"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) VESA bus</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The VESA local bus (VL-bus) connects the video adapter directly to the processor's local bus at CPU speed. It was designed to maximise graphics throughput.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> EISA is a 32-bit extension of ISA for servers.</li><li><b>(c)</b> PCI is a general-purpose bus; it later replaced VL-bus.</li><li><b>(d)</b> MCA is the proprietary IBM micro channel bus.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> VESA = video local bus.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-15", year: 2022, no: 15, subject: "computer", topic: "Bus with maximum data rate",
q: `Which one of the following bus architectures is having maximum data rate (MB/s)?`,
opts: ["VESA bus", "PCI-64 bus", "MCA bus", "EISA bus"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) PCI-64 bus</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Peak bus bandwidth = (bus width in bytes) × (clock rate) × (transfers per clock). Typical peaks: EISA 33 MB/s, MCA 20–40 MB/s, VESA local bus (32-bit, 33 MHz) 132 MB/s, PCI-64 at 33 MHz 264 MB/s (528 MB/s at 66 MHz).</p><div class="formula">Rate = width (bytes) × f_clk</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>PCI-64: 8 bytes × 33 MHz = 264 MB/s – the highest of those listed.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> VESA: 132 MB/s.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> PCI-64: 264–528 MB/s.</li><li><b>(c)</b> MCA: about 40 MB/s.</li><li><b>(d)</b> EISA: about 33 MB/s.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> PCI was later replaced by serial PCI Express.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-16", year: 2022, no: 16, subject: "computer", topic: "USB host controller",
q: `Which one of the following functions is not performed by the USB host controller?`,
opts: ["Configure the scheduling algorithms", "Packet generation", "Serializer/Deserializer", "Process request from device and host"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Configure the scheduling algorithms</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The USB host controller hardware generates the packets, serialises and deserialises the bit stream and processes requests from the devices and the host. The choice of scheduling policy is made by the host software (USB driver stack), not by the controller.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Packet generation is done by the controller.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The SERDES is part of the controller.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Request processing is a controller function.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Controller executes the schedule, software sets it.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-17", year: 2022, no: 17, subject: "computer", topic: "PCI Express layers",
q: `Which one of the following layers in PCI express protocol architecture is used for compatibility with PCI, initialization and enumeration of the devices connected to the PCI express?`,
opts: ["PCI express physical layer", "Software layer", "Data link layer", "Hardware layer"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Software layer</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The PCIe architecture has a software layer on top of the transaction, data-link and physical layers. The software layer provides PCI compatibility, device initialisation and enumeration.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The physical layer carries the serial lanes.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The data-link layer provides ACK/NAK and sequencing.</li><li><b>(d)</b> There is no layer called the hardware layer in the PCIe stack.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> PCIe: software, transaction, data link, physical.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-18", year: 2022, no: 18, subject: "computer", topic: "Critical sections and race conditions",
q: `Which one of the following tools is used to protect critical sections and prevent race conditions?`,
opts: ["Mutex lock", "Semaphore lock", "Spooling lock", "Livelock"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Mutex lock</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A mutex (mutual-exclusion) lock is acquired before the critical section and released afterwards, so only one process runs in it at a time and races are avoided.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> A semaphore is a general synchronisation tool; the standard answer for protecting a critical section is the mutex lock.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Spooling lock is not a synchronisation tool.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Livelock is a failure condition, not a tool.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Mutex = lock for critical sections.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-19", year: 2022, no: 19, subject: "computer", topic: "Deadlock conditions",
q: `To arise a deadlock situation, which of the following conditions hold simultaneously in a system? 1. Mutual exclusion 2. Hold and wait 3. Preemption`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Coffman's four necessary conditions for deadlock must all hold at once: mutual exclusion (non-sharable resources), hold and wait, no pre-emption (resources cannot be forcibly taken), and circular wait.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>1 Mutual exclusion – a condition.</li><li>2 Hold and wait – a condition.</li><li>3 'Pre-emption' is the opposite of the condition (no pre-emption); allowing pre-emption prevents deadlock.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is the opposite condition.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 1 and 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Breaking any one condition prevents deadlock.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-20", year: 2022, no: 20, subject: "computer", topic: "Purpose of RAID",
q: `Which of the following issues are addressed by redundant arrays of independent disks?`,
opts: ["Performance and reliability", "Performance and stability", "Performance and process", "Performance and storage space"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Performance and reliability</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> RAID stripes data across disks for speed and keeps parity or mirror copies for fault tolerance, so it improves performance and reliability.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Stability is not a RAID objective.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Process is unrelated.</li><li><b>(d)</b> RAID levels with redundancy actually reduce usable space.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> RAID = performance + reliability.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-21", year: 2022, no: 21, subject: "computer", topic: "Stack-oriented machine",
q: `Stack-oriented machine`,
opts: ["contains any accumulator or general purpose registers", "does not contain only a stack pointer which points to the stack top", "requires any operand address for arithmetic, logical and comparison instructions", "does not contain any accumulator or general purpose registers"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) does not contain any accumulator or general purpose registers</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A zero-address (stack) machine takes the operands from the top of the stack and leaves the result there. It therefore needs no accumulator, no general-purpose registers and no operand address for ALU instructions; only a stack pointer is kept.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> It does not contain them.</li><li><b>(b)</b> It does contain a stack pointer.</li><li><b>(c)</b> ALU instructions need no address field.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Stack machine: zero-address.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-22", year: 2022, no: 22, subject: "computer", topic: "Virtual memory translation",
q: `Virtual memory implements the translation of a program's address space to`,
opts: ["virtual addresses", "physical addresses", "mapping addresses", "page addresses"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) physical addresses</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The memory-management unit translates the virtual (logical) addresses of a program into the physical addresses of main memory, using the page table.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Virtual addresses are the source of the mapping.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Mapping address is not a standard term.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A page address is just a part of the translation.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Virtual → physical.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-23", year: 2022, no: 23, subject: "computer", topic: "Client–server model",
q: `Which one of the following provides an interface to which a client can send a request to perform an action, in response, the server executes the action and sends back results to the client?`,
opts: ["File server system", "Open-source system", "Compute server system", "Peer-to-peer system"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Compute server system</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A compute server exposes an interface for actions: a client sends a request to perform an operation, the server runs it and returns the result. A file server instead offers file access.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A file server provides file storage and retrieval.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Open source is a licensing model.</li><li><b>(d)</b> In peer-to-peer systems nodes are both clients and servers.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Compute server runs actions for clients.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-24", year: 2022, no: 24, subject: "computer", topic: "Multiprocessing",
q: `Which of the following statements are correct regarding multiprocessing architecture? 1. It can cause a system to change its memory access model from uniform memory access to non-uniform memory access. 2. There are two types of systems such as asymmetric multiprocessing and symmetric multiprocessing. 3. It adds CPUs to decrease computing power.`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Multiprocessing adds CPUs to increase computing power. As processors are added, a shared-bus (UMA) memory model may become NUMA, where access time depends on which processor's local memory is used. Systems are asymmetric (master-slave) or symmetric (SMP, all processors equal).</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>1 UMA → NUMA as CPUs are added. Correct.</li><li>2 Asymmetric and symmetric types. Correct.</li><li>3 CPUs are added to increase, not decrease, computing power. False.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Statement 3 is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 1 and 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Statement 3 is false.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Statement 3 is false.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Amdahl's law limits the speed-up from extra CPUs.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-25", year: 2022, no: 25, subject: "measure", topic: "Errors in parallel branch currents",
q: `Consider a parallel circuit having three branches—the current in first branch is 50 ± 2 A, in the second branch is 100 ± 3 A and in the third branch is 200 ± 5 A. What is the value of the total current, assuming the errors as standard deviations?`,
opts: ["350 ± 10 A", "350 ± 3·16 A", "350 ± 6·16 A", "350 ± 5 A"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 350 ± 6·16 A</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Given:</b> sum of three independent measurements with standard deviations 2, 3 and 5 A.</p><div class="formula">σ_total = √(σ₁² + σ₂² + σ₃²)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I = 50 + 100 + 200 = 350 A.</li><li>σ = √(4 + 9 + 25) = √38 = 6.16 A.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 10 A is the arithmetic (worst-case) sum 2 + 3 + 5.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 3.16 = √10 is the quadrature of two of the errors only.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 5 A is only the largest single error.</li></ul><p><b>Formula:</b> for a sum, the standard deviations add in quadrature: σ = √(σ₁² + σ₂² + σ₃²).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Independent errors add as root-sum-square.</p>` }
);

