window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2022-51", year: 2022, no: 51, subject: "materials", topic: "Superconductivity statements",
q: `Which one of the following statements is not correct regarding superconductivity of material?`,
opts: ["Superconducting compounds and alloys do not necessarily have compounds which are themselves superconducting.", "The metals which are very good conductors at room temperature do not exhibit superconducting properties.", "The metals and compounds which are superconducting are rather bad conductors at ordinary temperature.", "Monovalent metals, ferromagnetic and anti-ferromagnetic metals are superconducting."], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Statement (d) is not correct</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Good room-temperature conductors (Cu, Ag, Au) and the monovalent alkali metals do not become superconducting, and ferromagnetic or antiferromagnetic materials do not either, because their magnetic order destroys the electron pairing. Superconducting metals are often rather poor conductors at room temperature (strong electron–phonon coupling).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> True: alloys may be superconducting though their constituents are not.</li><li><b>(b)</b> True: Cu, Ag, Au are not superconductors.</li><li><b>(c)</b> True: Pb, Nb, Hg are poor room-temperature conductors.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Strong electron–phonon coupling: good superconductor, poor normal conductor.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-52", year: 2022, no: 52, subject: "materials", topic: "Bragg's law",
q: `According to Bragg's law, if an electromagnetic wave is diffracted when it is passed through a series of small slits spaced a distance d apart and order of diffraction is n and if the angle between the diffracted beam and incident beam is 2θ, then the relationship between the wavelength λ and this angle of diffraction is`,
opts: ["nλ = 2d sin θ", "λ = 2dn sin θ", "λd = n sin 2θ", "nλ = d sin 2θ"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) nλ = 2d sin θ</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Constructive interference needs a path difference of a whole number of wavelengths. For plane spacing d and glancing angle θ (so the deviation is 2θ) the path difference is 2d sin θ, giving nλ = 2d sin θ.</p><div class="formula">nλ = 2d sin θ</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Path difference between adjacent planes = 2d sin θ.</li><li>Constructive interference: = nλ ⇒ <b>nλ = 2d sin θ</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> n is misplaced; it multiplies λ.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The relation is dimensionally wrong.</li><li><b>(d)</b> It uses sin 2θ instead of 2 sin θ.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Bragg: nλ = 2d sin θ.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-53", year: 2022, no: 53, subject: "materials", topic: "Wire diameter from resistance",
q: `What is the approximate diameter of a copper wire of length 100 m, if it is to be used as winding material in a transformer such that the resistance of the whole winding is 2 Ω? (Take resistivity of copper as 1·7 × 10⁻⁸ Ω-m)`,
opts: ["0·25 mm", "1·05 mm", "2·25 mm", "3·05 mm"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1·05 mm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Given:</b> L = 100 m, R = 2 Ω, ρ = 1.7×10⁻⁸ Ω·m.</p><div class="formula">A = ρL/R</div><div class="formula">d = √(4A/π)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>A = ρL/R = 1.7×10⁻⁸ × 100/2 = 8.5×10⁻⁷ m².</li><li>d = √(4A/π) = √(1.082×10⁻⁶) = 1.04×10⁻³ m ≈ 1.05 mm.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.25 mm would give R about 35 Ω.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2.25 mm would give R about 0.43 Ω.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 3.05 mm would give R about 0.23 Ω.</li></ul><p><b>Formula:</b> R = ρL/A, A = πd²/4.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> A = ρL/R; d = √(4A/π).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-54", year: 2022, no: 54, subject: "materials", topic: "Curie temperature",
q: `The temperature above which the ferromagnetic materials lose their magnetic properties is called`,
opts: ["saturation point", "breakdown point", "Curie point", "peak point"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Curie point</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Above the Curie temperature thermal agitation destroys the parallel alignment of the domains and a ferromagnet becomes paramagnetic, following the Curie–Weiss law.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Saturation is the flux density limit.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Breakdown relates to dielectrics.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Peak point is not a magnetic term.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Curie point: ferro → para.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-55", year: 2022, no: 55, subject: "materials", topic: "Superconductivity",
q: `There are some metals and chemical compounds whose resistivity becomes zero when their temperature is brought near 0 K (−273 °C). Such metals or compounds are said to have attained`,
opts: ["piezoelectricity", "superconductivity", "semiconductivity", "electromagnetism"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) superconductivity</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Zero electrical resistivity below a critical temperature is the definition of superconductivity (discovered in mercury at 4.2 K).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Piezoelectricity links stress and polarisation.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A semiconductor has a resistivity that falls with temperature but not to zero.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Electromagnetism is a field effect, not a state of matter.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Zero resistance = superconductivity.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-56", year: 2022, no: 56, subject: "materials", topic: "Photons emitted per second",
q: `A blue lamp emits light of mean wavelength of 4500 Å. The lamp is rated at 150 W and 8% of the energy appears as emitted light. How many photons are emitted by the lamp per second? (Take h = 6·625 × 10⁻³⁴ J-sec and c = 3 × 10⁸ m/sec)`,
opts: ["10·55 × 10¹⁸", "13·62 × 10¹⁸", "27·15 × 10¹⁸", "33·25 × 10¹⁸"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 27·15 × 10¹⁸</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Given:</b> λ = 4500 Å = 4.5×10⁻⁷ m, P = 150 W, efficiency 8 %.</p><div class="formula">E_photon = hc/λ</div><div class="formula">n = P_light/E_photon</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>P_light = 0.08 × 150 = 12 W.</li><li>E_photon = hc/λ = 6.625×10⁻³⁴ × 3×10⁸/4.5×10⁻⁷ = 4.417×10⁻¹⁹ J.</li><li>n = 12/4.417×10⁻¹⁹ = 2.72×10¹⁹ = 27.2×10¹⁸ per second (option 27.15×10¹⁸).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 10.55×10¹⁸ is too small by a factor 2.6.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 13.62×10¹⁸ is half the correct value (a factor of 2 slip).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 33.25×10¹⁸ would need about 14.7 W of light.</li></ul><p><b>Formula:</b> n = P_light/(hc/λ).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> n = ηP λ/(hc).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-57", year: 2022, no: 57, subject: "materials", topic: "Hysteresis loss per hour",
q: `What is the energy lost per hour in a specimen of iron subjected to magnetization at 50 c/s, if the specimen weighs 50 kg and the hysteresis loop is equivalent in area to 250 J/m³ and the density of iron is 7500 kg/m³?`,
opts: ["3 × 10⁵ J", "4 × 10⁵ J", "5 × 10⁵ J", "6 × 10⁵ J"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 3 × 10⁵ J</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Given:</b> loop area 250 J/m³ per cycle, f = 50 Hz, mass 50 kg, density 7500 kg/m³.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Volume = 50/7500 = 6.667×10⁻³ m³.</li><li>Loss per cycle = 250 × 6.667×10⁻³ = 1.667 J.</li><li>Power = 1.667 × 50 = 83.3 W.</li><li>Energy per hour = 83.3 × 3600 = 3.0×10⁵ J.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 4×10⁵ J would need a loop area of 333 J/m³.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 5×10⁵ J would need 417 J/m³.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 6×10⁵ J is twice the correct value.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> W_h = (loop area) × V × f.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-58", year: 2022, no: 58, subject: "materials", topic: "Properties of a good insulator",
q: `A good insulating material should possess which of the following characteristics? 1. High dielectric strength 2. Low permittivity 3. Low thermal strength. Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A good insulator must withstand a high electric field without breakdown (high dielectric strength) and, for most uses, it has a low permittivity and low dielectric loss. It must also keep its properties at high temperature, so it needs high, not low, thermal strength. Statement 3 is wrong.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Insulator: high dielectric and thermal strength.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-59", year: 2022, no: 59, subject: "materials", topic: "Why most substances are not magnets",
q: `Most substances are not magnets, because`,
opts: ["they do not have sufficient energy to produce magnetic behaviour", "their electrons do not move truly", "the electrons usually pair up with their spins opposite to each other, so that their fields cancel each other", "their electrons strongly bind to the nucleus as they have more number of protons than electrons"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) electrons pair up with opposite spins</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In most atoms electrons occupy orbitals in pairs with opposite spins, so the spin and orbital magnetic moments cancel and the net moment is zero. Only atoms with unpaired electrons (Fe, Co, Ni) give a net moment and ferromagnetism.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Energy is not the limiting factor.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Electrons do move; the net moment cancels.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Atoms are neutral; protons and electrons are equal in number.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Paired spins cancel.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-60", year: 2022, no: 60, subject: "materials", topic: "Angular momentum of an atom",
q: `Whenever a particle has angular momentum, it will contribute to permanent dipole moment. Which one of the following does not contribute to the angular momentum of an atom?`,
opts: ["Orbital angular momentum of electron", "Proton spin angular momentum", "Electron spin angular momentum", "Nuclear spin angular momentum"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Proton spin angular momentum</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The angular momentum of an atom is built from the orbital motion of the electrons, their spins and the spin of the nucleus as a whole. The proton spin is part of the nuclear spin and is not counted separately in the atomic angular momentum.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The orbital motion contributes.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The electron spin contributes.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The nuclear spin contributes (hyperfine structure).</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Orbital + electron spin + nuclear spin.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-61", year: 2022, no: 61, subject: "maths", topic: "Poisson probability",
q: `A workshop has several machines. During a typical month, two machines will break down. The probability of more than two machines will break down in a month is`,
opts: ["1 − 3e⁻²", "1 − 4e⁻²", "1 − 5e⁻²", "1 − 6e⁻²"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 − 5e⁻²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Given:</b> Poisson with mean λ = 2.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>P(X ≤ 2) = e⁻²(1 + 2 + 4/2) = 5e⁻².</li><li>P(X &gt; 2) = 1 − 5e⁻².</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1 − 3e⁻² omits the term for k = 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1 − 4e⁻² omits one of the terms.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1 − 6e⁻² adds an extra term.</li></ul><p><b>Formula:</b> P(X = k) = e⁻λ λᵏ/k!.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> P(X &gt; 2) = 1 − P(0) − P(1) − P(2).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-62", year: 2022, no: 62, subject: "maths", topic: "Connectivity probability of a network",
q: `Villages A, B, C and D are connected by overhead telephone lines joining AB, AC, BC, BD and CD. As a result of severe gales, there is a probability p (the same for each link) that any particular link is broken. Then the probability that a call can be made from A to B is`,
opts: ["1 − p² − 2p³ + 3p⁴ − p⁵", "1 + 2p² − 2p³ + 4p⁴ − 2p⁵", "1 − 3p² + 2p³ − 3p⁴ + p⁵", "1 + 4p² + 2p³ − 4p⁴ + 2p⁵"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 − p² − 2p³ + 3p⁴ − p⁵</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Let q = 1 − p be the probability that a link works. Condition on the direct link AB.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>If AB works (prob q), the call connects.</li><li>If AB is broken (prob p), the call needs AC to work and then C must reach B, through BC or through CD and DB: P(C→B) = 1 − p(1 − q²).</li><li>P = q + p·q[1 − p(1 − q²)] = (1 − p)[1 + p − 2p³ + p⁴].</li><li>Expanding: P = 1 − p² − 2p³ + 3p⁴ − p⁵.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The coefficient of p² is wrong; P must be 1 at p = 0 and 0 at p = 1.</li><li><b>(c)</b> It gives 1 − 3 + 2 − 3 + 1 = −2 at p = 1.</li><li><b>(d)</b> It exceeds 1 for small p, so it is not a probability.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Check p = 0 → 1 and p = 1 → 0.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-63", year: 2022, no: 63, subject: "maths", topic: "Regression line of Y on X",
q: `What is regression line of Y on X for the following six pairs of observations? x: 1, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8; y: 2, 8, 9, 10, 14, 19`,
opts: ["Y = 1·18X − 0·49", "Y = 1·18X − 0·16", "Y = 2·18X + 0·49", "Y = 2·18X + 0·16"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Y = 2·18X + 0·16</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Given:</b> n = 6, Σx = 28, Σy = 62, Σxy = 362, Σx² = 164.</p><div class="formula">b = S_xy/S_xx</div><div class="formula">a = ȳ − b x̄</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Sxy = 362 − 28 × 62/6 = 72.67; Sxx = 164 − 784/6 = 33.33.</li><li>b = 72.67/33.33 = 2.18.</li><li>x̄ = 4.667, ȳ = 10.333; a = 10.333 − 2.18 × 4.667 = 0.16.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A slope of 1.18 does not fit the data (the points rise by about 17 in 7 units of x).</li><li><b>(b)</b> Same slope error.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The intercept would be 0.49 only with a different slope.</li></ul><p><b>Formula:</b> b = (Σxy − ΣxΣy/n)/(Σx² − (Σx)²/n); a = ȳ − b x̄.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> b = Sxy/Sxx.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-64", year: 2022, no: 64, subject: "maths", topic: "Probability of one red and one black ball",
q: `A bag contains 50 balls of which 10 are red and the remainder black. If two balls are drawn successively from the bag at random, what is the probability of selecting one red and one black ball?`,
opts: ["9/245", "156/245", "16/49", "156/49"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 16/49</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Given:</b> 10 red, 40 black, drawn without replacement.</p><div class="formula">P = P(R)P(B|R) + P(B)P(R|B)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>P = P(R then B) + P(B then R) = (10/50)(40/49) + (40/50)(10/49).</li><li>P = 400/2450 + 400/2450 = 800/2450 = 16/49.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 9/245 is P(two red).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 156/245 is the probability of two blacks (1560/2450 reduced).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 156/49 exceeds 1.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Add the two orderings.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-65", year: 2022, no: 65, subject: "maths", topic: "Expected value",
q: `A random variable y has a known probability distribution given by y: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 with P(y) = 0·17, 0·23, 0·2, 0·3, 0·1. Then the expected value of y is`,
opts: ["7·67", "6·88", "5·86", "4·89"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 5·86</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Expected value of a discrete random variable: E(y) = Σ y_i P(y_i). The probabilities must add to 1 (0.17 + 0.23 + 0.2 + 0.3 + 0.1 = 1).</p><div class="formula">E(y) = Σ y P(y)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>2 × 0.17 = 0.34; 4 × 0.23 = 0.92; 6 × 0.2 = 1.2; 8 × 0.3 = 2.4; 10 × 0.1 = 1.0.</li><li>E(y) = 0.34 + 0.92 + 1.2 + 2.4 + 1.0 = <b>5.86</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 7.67 is too large for these weights.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 6.88 is too large.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 5.86.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 4.89 is too small.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> The mean must lie between the smallest and largest values (2 and 10).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-66", year: 2022, no: 66, subject: "maths", topic: "Fourier series of sin² x",
q: `The Fourier series for f(x) = sin²x defined over the range −π ≤ x ≤ π is`,
opts: ["1/2 − cos 2x/2", "1 + cos 2x", "1/2 − cos x/2", "cos 2x/2 + 1/2"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1/2 − cos 2x/2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Using the double-angle identity cos 2x = 1 − 2 sin²x, sin²x = (1 − cos 2x)/2 = 1/2 − (cos 2x)/2. This is already a finite Fourier series (the function is even with period π).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> At x = 0 it gives 2, but sin²0 = 0.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The frequency is wrong: cos x gives period 2π.</li><li><b>(d)</b> It has the wrong sign: it gives 1 at x = 0.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> sin²x = (1 − cos 2x)/2.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-67", year: 2022, no: 67, subject: "maths", topic: "First-order PDE (Lagrange method)",
q: `What is the general solution of the partial differential equation ∂ψ/∂x + 2∂ψ/∂y + (2x − y)ψ = 0?`,
opts: ["ψ(x, y) = f(2x − y) e^(−(2x² + 2y² − 3xy)/5)", "ψ(x, y) = f(2x − y) e^(−(−2x² − 2y² + 3xy)/5)", "ψ(x, y) = f(2x − y) e^(−(−2x² + 2y² + 3xy)/5)", "ψ(x, y) = f(2x − y) e^(−(2x² − 2y² + 3xy)/5)"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) f(2x − y) e^(−(2x² − 2y² + 3xy)/5)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Write ψ = f(2x − y) e^g. Along the characteristic direction (1, 2) the function 2x − y is constant, so f drops out of the derivative; we need g to satisfy g_x + 2g_y = −(2x − y) = y − 2x.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Try g = a x² + b y² + c xy: g_x + 2g_y = (2a + 2c)x + (c + 4b)y.</li><li>So 2a + 2c = −2 and c + 4b = 1.</li><li>Option (d) has g = −(2x² − 2y² + 3xy)/5: a = −2/5, b = 2/5, c = −3/5. Then 2a + 2c = −2 and c + 4b = −3/5 + 8/5 = 1. ✓.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Gives 2a + 2c = 2/5, not −2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Not a solution of the equation.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Not a solution of the equation.</li></ul><p>Other options fail one of the two conditions; for example (a) has a = b = −2/5 and c = 3/5, which gives 2a + 2c = 2/5 ≠ −2.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Check g_x + 2g_y = y − 2x.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-68", year: 2022, no: 68, subject: "maths", topic: "Differentiation under the integral sign",
q: `The functions f(x, t) and F(x) are defined by f(x, t) = e^(−xt) and F(x) = ∫₀ˣ f(x, t) dt. Then dF/dx =`,
opts: ["f(x, t) + ∫₀ˣ ∂f(x, t)/∂x dt", "f(x, x) + ∫₀ˣ ∂f(x, t)/∂x dt", "f(0, 0) + ∫₀ˣ ∂f(x, t)/∂x dt", "f(t, t) + ∫₀ˣ ∂f(x, t)/∂x dt"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) f(x, x) + ∫₀ˣ ∂f/∂x dt</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Leibniz rule: d/dx ∫ₐ^(b(x)) f(x, t) dt = f(x, b(x)) b′(x) − f(x, a)a′ + ∫ ∂f/∂x dt. Here a = 0, b = x, so the boundary term is f(x, x).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The upper limit must be substituted for t.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The lower limit is constant and contributes nothing.</li><li><b>(d)</b> f(t, t) has the wrong arguments.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Leibniz: substitute the upper limit.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-69", year: 2022, no: 69, subject: "maths", topic: "Triple integral in spheroidal coordinates",
q: `The distances of the variable point P, which has coordinates x, y, z, from the fixed points (0, 0, 1) and (0, 0, −1) are denoted by u and v respectively. New variables ξ, η, φ are defined by ξ = ½(u + v), η = ½(u − v) and φ is the angle between the plane y = 0 and the plane containing the three points, i.e. φ = tan⁻¹(y/x) over 1 ≤ ξ < ∞, −1 ≤ η < 1, 0 ≤ φ < 2π. The Jacobian of ∂(ξ, η, φ)/∂(x, y, z) has the value (ξ² − η²)⁻¹, then ∭ over all space of ((u − v)²/(uv)) exp(−(u + v)/2) dx dy dz =`,
opts: ["16π/e", "8π/(3e)", "16π/(3e)", "8π/e"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 16π/(3e)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Given:</b> ∂(ξ,η,φ)/∂(x,y,z) = 1/(ξ² − η²), so dx dy dz = (ξ² − η²) dξ dη dφ.</p><div class="formula">dx dy dz = |∂(x,y,z)/∂(ξ,η,φ)| dξ dη dφ</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>u = ξ + η, v = ξ − η: (u − v)² = 4η², uv = ξ² − η², (u + v)/2 = ξ.</li><li>Integrand × volume element: [4η²/(ξ² − η²)] e^(−ξ)(ξ² − η²) = 4η² e^(−ξ).</li><li>Integral = 4 ∫₋₁¹ η² dη ∫₁^∞ e^(−ξ) dξ ∫₀^(2π) dφ = 4 × (2/3) × e⁻¹ × 2π = 16π/(3e).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 16π/e forgets the factor ∫η² dη = 2/3.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 8π/(3e) drops the factor 2 from the range of η or the factor 4.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 8π/e drops the factor 2/3 from ∫η²dη and halves the result.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Change variables, the Jacobian cancels the denominator.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-70", year: 2022, no: 70, subject: "maths", topic: "Stationary points of a function of two variables",
q: `The function f(x, y) = x³ − 12xy + 48x + by², b ≠ 0 has two, one or zero stationary points, according to whether |b| is`,
opts: ["less than, equal to or greater than 3", "less than, equal to or greater than 4", "less than, equal to or greater than 8", "less than, equal to or greater than 2"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) less than, equal to or greater than 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Stationary points: f_x = 3x² − 12y + 48 = 0 and f_y = −12x + 2by = 0.</p><div class="formula">Stationary points: f_x = 0, f_y = 0</div><div class="formula">Quadratic: discriminant &gt; 0, = 0, &lt; 0</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>From f_y: y = 6x/b.</li><li>Substitute: 3x² − 72x/b + 48 = 0, i.e. x² − (24/b)x + 16 = 0.</li><li>Discriminant: 576/b² − 64. Two real roots if b² &lt; 9, one if b² = 9, none if b² &gt; 9.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The threshold 4 does not come from the discriminant.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The threshold 8 does not come from the discriminant.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The threshold 2 does not come from the discriminant.</li></ul><p>So two, one or zero stationary points for |b| less than, equal to or greater than 3.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Discriminant of the quadratic in x decides the count.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-71", year: 2022, no: 71, subject: "maths", topic: "Exact differential equation",
q: `What are the values of α and β that make dF(x, y) = (1/(x² + 2) + α/y) dx + (xy^β + 1) dy an exact differential equation?`,
opts: ["α = −1, β = −2", "α = 1, β = −2", "α = −1, β = 2", "α = −2, β = −1"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) α = −1, β = −2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Exactness requires ∂M/∂y = ∂N/∂x.</p><div class="formula">Exact ⇔ ∂M/∂y = ∂N/∂x</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>M = 1/(x² + 2) + α/y: ∂M/∂y = −α/y².</li><li>N = xy^β + 1: ∂N/∂x = y^β.</li><li>−α/y² = y^β for all y, so β = −2 and −α = 1, α = −1.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> α = 1 gives −1/y² ≠ y⁻².</li><li><b>(c)</b> β = 2 does not match the y⁻² dependence.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Neither value satisfies the equation.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Exact: M_y = N_x.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-72", year: 2022, no: 72, subject: "maths", topic: "Orthogonal matrix condition",
q: `A and B are real non-zero 3×3 matrices and satisfy the equation (AB)ᵀ + B⁻¹A = 0. If B is orthogonal, then A is`,
opts: ["symmetric", "anti-symmetric", "Hermitian", "anti-Hermitian"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) anti-symmetric</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> B orthogonal means B⁻¹ = Bᵀ.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>(AB)ᵀ = BᵀAᵀ, so the equation becomes BᵀAᵀ + BᵀA = 0.</li><li>Bᵀ(Aᵀ + A) = 0; Bᵀ is invertible, so Aᵀ + A = 0.</li><li>Aᵀ = −A: A is anti-symmetric (skew-symmetric).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A symmetric matrix has Aᵀ = A.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A real Hermitian matrix is symmetric.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A real anti-Hermitian matrix is anti-symmetric as well, but the standard description for a real matrix is anti-symmetric.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Orthogonal: B⁻¹ = Bᵀ.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-73", year: 2022, no: 73, subject: "pe", topic: "Buck converter output voltage",
q: `Consider a buck converter with the controlled switch as MOSFET and the uncontrolled switch as diode, the input to the buck converter is 60 V. The MOSFET is turned on for 20 μsec and turned off for 10 μsec periodically. Assuming ideal components, the output voltage of the buck converter is`,
opts: ["20 V", "30 V", "40 V", "50 V"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 40 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a buck converter in continuous conduction the inductor's average voltage is zero, so the output is the duty ratio times the input: V_o = DV_in with D = T_on/(T_on + T_off).</p><div class="formula">V_o = D V_in</div><div class="formula">D = T_on/T</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>T = 20 + 10 = 30 μs ⇒ f = 33.3 kHz.</li><li>D = 20/30 = 2/3.</li><li>V_o = (2/3) × 60 = <b>40 V</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 20 V corresponds to D = 1/3 (T_off/T).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 30 V corresponds to D = 1/2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 40 V.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 50 V corresponds to D = 5/6.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Boost: V_o = V_in/(1 − D) = 180 V for the same D.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-74", year: 2022, no: 74, subject: "pe", topic: "Polarity of d.c.–d.c. converter outputs",
q: `Consider the following statements related to d.c.–d.c. converters: 1. The polarity of output voltage and input voltage of a single-ended primary inductance converter is opposite. 2. The polarity of output voltage and input voltage of a Cuk converter is opposite. 3. The polarity of output voltage and input voltage of a buck-boost converter is same. 4. The polarity of output voltage and input voltage of a boost converter is same. Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2, 3 and 4", "1, 2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 4 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 2 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Cuk and the buck-boost converters invert the polarity (output negative for a positive input). The boost converter keeps the polarity, and the SEPIC (single-ended primary inductance converter) also gives a non-inverted output.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: the SEPIC output has the same polarity as the input.</li><li><b>2</b> True: Cuk inverts.</li><li><b>3</b> False: the buck-boost inverts.</li><li><b>4</b> True: the boost keeps the polarity.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Inverting: buck-boost, Cuk. Non-inverting: buck, boost, SEPIC.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-75", year: 2022, no: 75, subject: "pe", topic: "Switching frequency in d.c. supplies",
q: `In d.c. power supplies, the switching frequency is much greater than`,
opts: ["the d.c. power source frequency, enabling the transformer to be small", "the d.c. power source frequency, enabling the transformer to be large", "the a.c. power source frequency, enabling the transformer to be large", "the a.c. power source frequency, enabling the transformer to be small"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) the a.c. power source frequency, enabling the transformer to be small</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a switched-mode supply the line a.c. (50/60 Hz) is rectified and then chopped at tens to hundreds of kHz. The transformer's core size is inversely related to frequency (V = 4.44 f N Φ_m A), so at a much higher frequency than the a.c. source frequency the transformer and filter components are small.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A d.c. source has no frequency.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A d.c. source has no frequency, and a higher frequency makes the transformer smaller.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A higher frequency makes the transformer smaller, not larger.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Higher switching frequency → smaller magnetics.</p>` }
);

