// ESE 2022 Prelims Paper II, Electrical Engineering, Series A (PKL-D-ETE), Q126–150
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; none dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2022-126", year: 2022, no: 126, subject: "signals", topic: "Fourier transform by duality",
q: `What is the Fourier transform G(ω) of the signal of g(t) = 1/(1 + jt)?`,
opts: ["2πe^ω u(−ω)", "πe^ω u(−ω)", "0·5πe^ω u(−ω)", "3πe^ω u(−ω)"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 2πe^ω u(−ω)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Start from the standard pair e^(−t)u(t) ↔ 1/(1 + jω). By the duality property, if f(t) ↔ F(ω) then F(t) ↔ 2π f(−ω).</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Take f(t) = e^(−t)u(t), F(ω) = 1/(1 + jω).</li><li>So g(t) = 1/(1 + jt) ↔ 2π f(−ω) = 2π e^(ω) u(−ω).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> π lacks the 2π of the duality property.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.5π is a quarter of the correct factor.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 3π does not come from the 2π factor of duality.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Duality: F(t) ↔ 2πf(−ω).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-127", year: 2022, no: 127, subject: "signals", topic: "Convolution with shifted impulses",
q: `What is the convolution of the following two signals? x(t) = 1 for −1 &lt; t &lt; 1, 0 elsewhere, and h(t) = δ(t + 1) + 2δ(t + 2)`,
opts: ["y(t) = x(t + 1) + 2x(t − 2)", "y(t) = x(t − 1) + 2x(t + 2)", "y(t) = x(t + 1) + 2x(t + 2)", "y(t) = x(t − 1) + 2x(t − 2)"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) y(t) = x(t + 1) + 2x(t + 2)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Convolution with δ(t − t₀) shifts the signal: x(t) * δ(t − t₀) = x(t − t₀). Here the impulses are at t₀ = −1 and t₀ = −2, so y(t) = x(t + 1) + 2x(t + 2).</p><div class="formula">x(t) * δ(t − t₀) = x(t − t₀)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>δ(t + 1) ⇒ x(t + 1); 2δ(t + 2) ⇒ 2x(t + 2).</li><li>y(t) = <b>x(t + 1) + 2x(t + 2)</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The second shift has the wrong sign.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Both shifts have the wrong sign.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Both shifts have the wrong sign.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> x(t) * δ(t − t₀) = x(t − t₀).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-128", year: 2022, no: 128, subject: "signals", topic: "Bilateral z-transform of a shifted exponential",
q: `What is the bilateral z-transform of the signal x(n) = aⁿ⁺¹u(n + 1)?`,
opts: ["z/(1 − az⁻¹), |z| > |a|", "z/(1 + az⁻¹), |z| > |a|", "1/(1 − az⁻¹), |z| > |a|", "1/(1 + az⁻¹), |z| > |a|"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) z/(1 − az⁻¹), |z| > |a|</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Let y(n) = aⁿu(n), Y(z) = 1/(1 − az⁻¹), |z| &gt; |a|. Then x(n) = y(n + 1), and the time-shift property gives X(z) = z Y(z) = z/(1 − az⁻¹), with the same region of convergence |z| &gt; |a| (excluding z = ∞ for the advance).</p><div class="formula">aⁿu(n) ↔ 1/(1 − az⁻¹)</div><div class="formula">x(n + 1) ↔ zX(z)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>x(n) = y(n + 1) with y(n) = aⁿu(n).</li><li>X(z) = z/(1 − az⁻¹), <b>|z| &gt; |a|</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> This is the transform of (−a)ⁿ.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The advance factor z is missing.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Wrong sign and no factor z.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> x(n + 1) ↔ zX(z) for the bilateral transform.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-129", year: 2022, no: 129, subject: "signals", topic: "Parity of a convolution",
q: `Which one of the following statements is not correct for convolution?`,
opts: ["The convolution of an odd and an even function is an odd function.", "The convolution of two odd functions is an even function.", "The convolution of two even functions is an even function.", "The convolution of two odd functions is an odd function."], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Statement (d) is not correct</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Parity rules for convolution follow the multiplication rules of parity: odd * odd = even, even * even = even, odd * even = odd. So the convolution of two odd functions is even, never odd, and (d) is false. Statement (b) states the correct rule.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> True.</li><li><b>(b)</b> True.</li><li><b>(c)</b> True.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Parity of * follows parity of ×.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-130", year: 2022, no: 130, subject: "signals", topic: "Nyquist rate of sinc²",
q: `The sampling frequency of the signal g(t) = sinc²(200t) is`,
opts: ["100 Hz", "200 Hz", "400 Hz", "800 Hz"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 400 Hz</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> With sinc(x) = sin(πx)/(πx), sinc(200t) has a rectangular spectrum of width 200 Hz, i.e. bandwidth 100 Hz. Squaring in time convolves the spectrum with itself, so sinc²(200t) has a triangular spectrum and a bandwidth f_m = 200 Hz.</p><div class="formula">sinc(at) ↔ rect, bandwidth a/2</div><div class="formula">Squaring doubles the bandwidth</div><div class="formula">f_s = 2f_m</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Nyquist rate f_s = 2 f_m = 400 Hz.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 100 Hz is the bandwidth of sinc(200t).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 200 Hz is the bandwidth of sinc², not the sampling rate.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 800 Hz is twice the Nyquist rate.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Squaring doubles the bandwidth; f_s = 2 f_m.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-131", year: 2022, no: 131, subject: "signals", topic: "Final value theorem",
q: `What is the final value of the function f(t) = e^(−2t) sin 5t u(t)?`,
opts: ["0", "1", "5", "∞"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The function is a decaying sinusoid: |f(t)| ≤ e^(−2t) → 0 as t → ∞. In the s-domain F(s) = 5/[(s + 2)² + 25], whose poles at −2 ± j5 are in the left half-plane, so the final-value theorem applies: lim s F(s) as s → 0 = 0.</p><div class="formula">f(∞) = lim_{s→0} sF(s) (poles in LHP)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>F(s) = 5/[(s + 2)² + 25]; poles −2 ± j5 (stable).</li><li>sF(s) → 0 as s → 0 ⇒ f(∞) = <b>0</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 1 is not the limit of a decaying envelope.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 5 is the numerator of F(s), not the limit.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The function does not grow.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Decaying envelope → 0.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-132", year: 2022, no: 132, subject: "signals", topic: "Difference equation with an exponential",
q: `If a discrete signal represented by x(n) = αⁿu(n), then what is the value of the signal g(n) = x(n) − αx(n − 1)?`,
opts: ["g(n) = δ(n)", "g(n) = δ(n − 1)", "g(n) = αδ(n − 1)", "g(n) = δ(n − α)"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) g(n) = δ(n)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> αx(n − 1) = α·α^(n−1)u(n − 1) = αⁿu(n − 1).</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>g(n) = αⁿ[u(n) − u(n − 1)].</li><li>u(n) − u(n − 1) = δ(n), and αⁿδ(n) = δ(n) since α⁰ = 1.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The impulse is at n = 0, not n = 1.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The weight is 1, not α.</li><li><b>(d)</b> δ(n − α) is meaningless for non-integer α.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> u(n) − u(n − 1) = δ(n).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-133", year: 2022, no: 133, subject: "ad", topic: "Total power of an AM wave",
q: `For an amplitude modulated double sideband full carrier wave, a peak unmodulated carrier voltage V_c = 10 V_p, a load resistance R_L = 10 Ω and a modulation coefficient m = 1. What is the total power of the modulated wave?`,
opts: ["7·5 W", "2·5 W", "1·25 W", "5·0 W"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 7·5 W</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Given:</b> V_c = 10 V peak, R_L = 10 Ω, m = 1.</p><div class="formula">P_c = V_c²/(2R_L)</div><div class="formula">P_t = P_c(1 + m²/2)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>P_c = 100/20 = 5 W.</li><li>P_t = 5(1 + 0.5) = 7.5 W.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 2.5 W is the power of the two sidebands, not the total.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1.25 W is one sideband power.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 5.0 W is the carrier power alone.</li></ul><p><b>Formula:</b> P_c = V_c²/(2R_L); P_t = P_c(1 + m²/2).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> P_t = P_c(1 + m²/2).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-134", year: 2022, no: 134, subject: "ad", topic: "Image frequency",
q: `For a Citizens band receiver using high-side injection with an RF carrier of 27 MHz and an IF centre frequency of 455 kHz, what is the image frequency?`,
opts: ["24·55 MHz", "27·91 MHz", "28·45 MHz", "29·65 MHz"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 27·91 MHz</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Given:</b> f_s = 27 MHz, f_IF = 455 kHz, high-side injection.</p><div class="formula">f_LO = f_s + f_IF</div><div class="formula">f_image = f_s + 2f_IF</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>f_LO = f_s + f_IF = 27.455 MHz.</li><li>f_image = f_s + 2f_IF = 27 + 0.91 = 27.91 MHz.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 24.55 MHz = 27 − 2.45 MHz has no relation to f_IF = 455 kHz.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 28.45 MHz would be f_s + 1.45 MHz.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 29.65 MHz is far from f_s + 2f_IF.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> High-side: f_image = f_s + 2f_IF.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-135", year: 2022, no: 135, subject: "ad", topic: "Improving the noise figure of a receiver",
q: `Which of the following statements are correct to improve the noise figure of a receiver? 1. The devices used for the amplifiers and mixer stages must produce low noise. 2. The receiver can be operated at low temperatures. 3. High-gain amplifiers are used to improve the noise figure. 4. The diodes and FETs are preferred to improve the noise figure. Select the correct answer using the code given below.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "3 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> By the Friis formula F = F₁ + (F₂ − 1)/G₁ + …, the first stage dominates, so (1) low-noise devices are used, (2) cooling reduces thermal noise (noise temperature), (3) a high-gain front-end amplifier suppresses the noise contribution of later stages, and (4) FETs and diodes (such as Schottky and GaAs devices) are low-noise devices. All four are correct.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Friis: first stage low noise and high gain.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-136", year: 2022, no: 136, subject: "ad", topic: "Non-linear device",
q: `Any device or circuit whose output is not a linear variation with the input can be used as`,
opts: ["an RF amplifier", "a mixer", "an IF amplifier", "a local oscillator"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) a mixer</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A mixer needs a non-linear characteristic to generate the sum and difference frequencies (heterodyning). Linear amplifiers must preserve the waveform, so non-linearity is a defect there.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> An RF amplifier should be linear.</li><li><b>(c)</b> An IF amplifier should be linear.</li><li><b>(d)</b> An oscillator uses non-linearity only to limit amplitude, but the standard description is a mixer.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Mixing needs a non-linear device.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-137", year: 2022, no: 137, subject: "ad", topic: "Fidelity of a receiver",
q: `In the receiver parameter, fidelity is a measure of`,
opts: ["the ability of the receiver to accept a given band of frequencies and reject all other frequencies", "the minimum radio frequency signal level that can be detected at the input to the receiver and still produces a usable demodulated signal", "the ability of a communication system to produce, at the output of the receiver, an exact replica of the original source information", "the difference in decibels between the minimum input level necessary to discern a signal and the input level that will overdrive the receiver and produce distortion"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) the ability to produce at the output an exact replica of the original source information</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Fidelity is the extent to which the receiver reproduces the original information, i.e. low distortion over the whole modulating band.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> This is selectivity.</li><li><b>(b)</b> This is sensitivity.</li><li><b>(d)</b> This is dynamic range.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Selectivity, sensitivity, fidelity, dynamic range.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-138", year: 2022, no: 138, subject: "ad", topic: "Tuned radio frequency receiver",
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct regarding tuned radio frequency receiver?`,
opts: ["The bandwidth is inconsistent and varies with centre frequency when tuned over a wide range of input frequencies.", "It is stable due to the large number of RF amplifiers all tuned to the same centre frequency.", "The gain is uniform over a very wide frequency range.", "It has a very low sensitivity."], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) The bandwidth is inconsistent and varies with centre frequency</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a TRF receiver all the tuned stages work at the signal frequency. Since Q is fixed, the bandwidth B = f₀/Q changes as the set is tuned over a wide range of frequencies, so selectivity is inconsistent.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Many tuned RF stages make it prone to instability and oscillation.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The gain varies with frequency.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Its sensitivity is high because of the RF gain.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> B = f₀/Q varies with f₀.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-139", year: 2022, no: 139, subject: "ad", topic: "Noise figure",
q: `Which one of the following is a figure of merit used to indicate how much the signal-to-noise ratio deteriorates as a signal passes through a circuit or series of circuits?`,
opts: ["Impulse noise", "Noise figure", "Correlated noise", "Noise temperature"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Noise figure</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Noise figure F = (S/N)ᵢₙ/(S/N)ₒᵤₜ, in dB NF = 10 log F, is the figure of merit showing how much a circuit degrades the signal-to-noise ratio.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Impulse noise is a noise type.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Correlated noise is a noise type.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Noise temperature is a related measure of the noise power but the SNR degradation figure is the noise figure.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> F = SNR_in/SNR_out.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-140", year: 2022, no: 140, subject: "ad", topic: "Source coding",
q: `Consider the following statements related to the source coding: 1. A conversion of the output of a discrete memoryless source (DMS) into a sequence of binary symbols is called source coding. 2. The source code efficiency (η) is defined as η = L_min/L, where L_min is the minimum possible value of L, and L is the average codeword length. 3. The code redundancy (γ) is defined as γ = 1 + η. Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Source coding maps the symbols of a discrete memoryless source to binary codewords. Efficiency η = L_min/L = H(S)/L (entropy over average codeword length, binary code). Redundancy γ = 1 − η.</p><div class="formula">η = H(S)/L̄</div><div class="formula">γ = 1 − η</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>1 Definition of source coding. Correct.</li><li>2 Definition of efficiency. Correct.</li><li>3 γ = 1 + η is wrong; it is 1 − η. False.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 1 and 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Statement 3 is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Statement 3 is false.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Statement 3 is false.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Huffman coding gives the minimum L̄ for a symbol-by-symbol code.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-141", year: 2022, no: 141, subject: "ad", topic: "DPCM",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding differential pulse-code modulator: 1. The differential pulse-code modulator system employs a predictor. 2. It needs far fewer bits per each error sample than what would have been needed for the original samples themselves. 3. It will have larger dynamic range than the original message itself. Select the correct statements using the code given below.`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> DPCM quantises the prediction error, which has a much smaller variance than the signal itself. It therefore needs fewer bits per sample (2) and has a smaller, not larger, dynamic range (3 is wrong). The predictor is the key element (1).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> DPCM: quantise the prediction error.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-142", year: 2022, no: 142, subject: "ad", topic: "Minimum sampling rate",
q: `How many minimum number of samples are required to exactly describe the following signal? x(t) = 10 cos(6πt) + 4 sin(8πt)`,
opts: ["4 samples per second", "6 samples per second", "8 samples per second", "2 samples per second"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 8 samples per second</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The minimum (Nyquist) sampling rate is twice the highest frequency in the signal. In cos(2πft) form: 6πt ⇒ 3 Hz, 8πt ⇒ 4 Hz.</p><div class="formula">f_s ≥ 2f_max</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>f₁ = 3 Hz, f₂ = 4 Hz ⇒ f_max = 4 Hz.</li><li>f_s = 2 × 4 = <b>8 samples/s</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 4 is f_max itself.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 6 is twice the lower frequency.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 8 samples/s.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2 is far below the Nyquist rate.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Amplitudes and sine/cosine type do not change the Nyquist rate.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-143", year: 2022, no: 143, subject: "ad", topic: "Gaussian random process",
q: `Which one of the following is not a property of a Gaussian random process?`,
opts: ["A Gaussian process is completely described by its mean and autocorrelation", "If a Gaussian process is wide-sense stationary, then it is stationary in the strict sense too", "If a Gaussian process is given as input to an LTI system, the output process is also Gaussian", "If two processes which are jointly Gaussian are uncorrelated, then they are statistically dependent"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Statement (d) is not a property</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For jointly Gaussian processes, uncorrelated implies statistically independent (not dependent). Statement (d) therefore states the opposite and is false.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> True: the first two moments fix the distribution.</li><li><b>(b)</b> True: WSS Gaussian implies SSS.</li><li><b>(c)</b> True: Gaussianity is preserved by linear operations.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Gaussian: uncorrelated = independent.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-144", year: 2022, no: 144, subject: "ad", topic: "PCM bandwidth",
q: `A PCM system uses a uniform quantizer followed by a 7-bit binary encoder. The bit rate of the system is equal to 50 × 10⁶ bits/sec. What is the maximum message signal bandwidth for which the system operates satisfactorily?`,
opts: ["3·57 MHz", "4·55 MHz", "7·55 MHz", "8·57 MHz"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 3·57 MHz</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Given:</b> n = 7 bits per sample, bit rate R_b = 50×10⁶ bit/s.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Sampling rate f_s = R_b/n = 50×10⁶/7 = 7.14×10⁶ samples/s.</li><li>Nyquist: f_m = f_s/2 = 3.57 MHz.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 4.55 MHz would need only 5.5 bits per sample.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 7.55 MHz is about f_s.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 8.57 MHz exceeds f_s/2 – it would alias.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> f_m = R_b/(2n).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-145", year: 2022, no: 145, subject: "materials", topic: "Aluminium oxide and soldering", doubt: true,
q: `Statement (I): Aluminium oxidizes quickly in normal atmospheric conditions and acquires a thin film of oxide Al₂O₃. Statement (II): The high melting point of aluminium oxide coating and the rapidity with which a freely exposed aluminium surface becomes oxidized, make soldering difficult through conventional means.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true and (II) is the correct explanation of (I)</h3><p class="note">Doubt: Statement II describes a consequence of the oxide film (why soldering is hard) rather than explaining why aluminium oxidises quickly; our solution was (b). Followed the UPSC key (a).</p><p><b>Concept:</b> Statement (I) is true: aluminium forms a thin, tough, self-healing Al₂O₃ film. Statement (II) is true: the oxide melts at about 2050 °C, far above the solder temperature, and re-forms instantly after cleaning, so conventional soldering fails. The key accepts II as the explanation, taking the oxide film in I as the cause of the soldering difficulty in II.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Key's choice: II taken as the explanation.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Our reading: II gives a consequence (soldering difficulty), not the reason for oxidation.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Al₂O₃ film prevents wetting by solder.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-146", year: 2022, no: 146, subject: "measure", topic: "Series input devices",
q: `Statement (I): When the signal is of the form of current, then series input devices are used. Statement (II): An ammeter, which is a series device, thus should be designed with a low input impedance so that the current is correctly measured.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both true and (II) explains (I)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A current signal is sensed by a series element, which must have a very low input impedance so that it does not disturb the current. The ammeter example in II shows exactly why a series input device is used for current signals and why it needs a low impedance: both are true and II explains I.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true; II explains why series devices need low impedance.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II does explain I.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Series device: low impedance.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-147", year: 2022, no: 147, subject: "control", topic: "Gain margin and oscillation",
q: `Statement (I): If the gain margin is negative, this gives the decibel rise in open-loop gain, which is theoretically permissible without oscillation. Statement (II): For a multistage amplifier, if the open-loop gain |βA| is unity when the phase shift is 180°, then the closed-loop amplifier will oscillate.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Both true but (II) is not the explanation of (I)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Gain margin, in the convention where it is expressed as a negative number of decibels for a stable amplifier, is the rise in loop gain that is permissible before oscillation starts (I). The Barkhausen condition in (II) is true as well: |βA| = 1 at 180° (with the negative feedback inversion) gives oscillation. But II states the oscillation condition itself, not the reason for I, so it is not an explanation.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is a separate fact (Barkhausen condition), not the meaning of gain margin.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true; II does not explain I.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true in the stated convention.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Margin: gain increase allowed before |βA| = 1 at 180°.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-148", year: 2022, no: 148, subject: "ad", topic: "Ideal op-amp with negative feedback",
q: `Statement (I): When negative feedback is applied to the ideal amplifier, the differential input voltage is zero. Statement (II): There is no current flow into either input terminal of the ideal op-amp.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Both true but (II) is not the explanation of (I)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> With negative feedback and infinite open-loop gain, the differential input voltage is driven to zero (virtual short) (I). An ideal op-amp has infinite input impedance, so no current flows into either input (II). Both are true, but the zero differential voltage comes from the infinite gain together with feedback, not from the zero input current.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Zero differential voltage comes from infinite gain, not from zero input current.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true; independent properties.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Virtual short: V⁺ = V⁻; no input current.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-149", year: 2022, no: 149, subject: "ad", topic: "Crystal oscillator", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Statement (I): When the carrier is generated by a crystal oscillator, the frequency is fixed by the crystal. Statement (II): The equivalent circuit of a crystal is an L-C-R circuit with both series and parallel resonant points.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Both true but (II) is not the explanation of (I)</h3><p class="note">Self-corrected: our solution was (a). Statement II only gives the equivalent circuit, so it is not the explanation; the key (b) is correct.</p><p><b>Concept:</b> (I) is true: the oscillation frequency is held to the quartz resonance. (II) is true: a crystal is modelled by a series L-C-R branch in parallel with the holder capacitance, giving a series and a parallel resonance very close together. But II only describes the electrical model; the real reason the frequency is fixed is the very high Q of the piezoelectric mechanical resonance of the cut quartz plate.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Our first choice; II only describes the equivalent circuit.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true; II does not explain I.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Crystal frequency: set by the cut and thickness of the quartz.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-150", year: 2022, no: 150, subject: "ad", topic: "FDM transmitter",
q: `Statement (I): In FDM transmitter, if the signals which are to be multiplexed will each modulate a separate carrier, then the type of modulation can be AM, SSB, FM or PM. Statement (II): In FDM transmitter, the modulator outputs will contain the sidebands of the corresponding signals.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Both true but (II) is not the explanation of (I)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In frequency-division multiplexing each message modulates its own subcarrier and any of AM, SSB, FM or PM may be used (I). Each modulator output contains the sidebands of its message (II). Both are true, but the sidebands are a result of modulation in general; they are not the reason any of these modulation types can be used.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Sidebands result from any modulation; they do not explain the choice of modulation type.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true; II does not explain I.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> FDM: separate carriers, any modulation.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2021a.js ----
// ESE 2021 Prelims Paper II, Electrical Engineering, Series A (FGT-B-ETE), Q1–25
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; none dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2021-1", year: 2021, no: 1, subject: "circuits", topic: "Field of a point charge",
q: `A point charge of 10⁻⁹ C is placed at a point A in free space. What is the intensity of electrostatic field on the surface of a sphere of radius 5 cm at centre A? (1/4πε₀ = 9 × 10⁹ in SI units)`,
opts: ["1800 V/m", "3600 V/m", "5360 V/m", "2820 V/m"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 3600 V/m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The field of a point charge is radial and has the same magnitude at every point of a sphere centred on the charge (Coulomb's law / Gauss's law).</p><div class="formula">E = Q/(4πε₀r²) = 9 × 10⁹ Q/r²</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Q = 10⁻⁹ C, r = 0.05 m, r² = 0.0025 m².</li><li>E = 9 × 10⁹ × 10⁻⁹/0.0025 = 9/0.0025 = <b>3600 V/m</b>, radially outward.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1800 V/m would need r ≈ 7.07 cm.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 3600 V/m.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 5360 V/m would need r ≈ 4.1 cm.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2820 V/m would need r ≈ 5.65 cm.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> E ∝ 1/r²: halve the radius, the field is four times larger.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-2", year: 2021, no: 2, subject: "circuits", topic: "Efficiency of a generator",
q: `A generator develops 250 V and has an internal resistance of 100 ohm. If the load resistance is 100 ohm, then what is the efficiency of the generator?`,
opts: ["80%", "50%", "60%", "70%"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 50%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Load and internal resistance carry the same current, so the power splits in proportion to the resistances: η = R_L/(R_L + R_i) = 100/200 = 50 %. This is the maximum power transfer condition, where the efficiency is always 50 %.</p><div class="formula">η = I²R_L/[I²(R_L + R_i)] = R_L/(R_L + R_i)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I = 250/(100 + 100) = 1.25 A.</li><li>P_L = 1.25² × 100 = 156.25 W; P_total = 312.5 W.</li><li>η = 156.25/312.5 = <b>50 %</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 80 % would need R_L = 400 Ω.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 60 % would need R_L = 150 Ω.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 70 % would need R_L = 233 Ω.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> η = R_L/(R_L + R_i).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-3", year: 2021, no: 3, subject: "circuits", topic: "Switching on an RL circuit",
q: `A coil resistance 30 Ω and inductance 0·6 H is switched on to a 240 V supply. What are the rate of change of current at the instant of closing the switch at t = 0 and the magnitude of the final steady state current respectively?`,
opts: ["80 A/sec and 80 A", "400 A/sec and 8 A", "8 A/sec and 80 A", "400 A/sec and 80 A"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 400 A/s and 8 A</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At t = 0 the current is zero, so the whole supply voltage appears across the inductor: di/dt = V/L = 240/0.6 = 400 A/s. In the steady state the inductor is a short circuit and I = V/R = 240/30 = 8 A.</p><div class="formula">di/dt|₀₊ = V/L</div><div class="formula">I_∞ = V/R</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>di/dt = 240/0.6 = <b>400 A/s</b>.</li><li>I_∞ = 240/30 = <b>8 A</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 80 A/s would need L = 3 H; 80 A would need R = 3 Ω.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 8 A/s and 80 A are both wrong (they interchange the roles).</li><li><b>(d)</b> The rate is right but the final current 80 A would need R = 3 Ω.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> di/dt(0) = V/L; I(∞) = V/R.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-4", year: 2021, no: 4, subject: "circuits", topic: "Series R–L parameters",
q: `A current of 10 A flows in a circuit with a 30° angle of lag when the applied voltage is 100 V. What are the values of resistance and reactance in the circuit respectively?`,
opts: ["8·66 Ω and 5 Ω", "5 Ω and 8·66 Ω", "6·66 Ω and 4 Ω", "4 Ω and 6·66 Ω"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 8·66 Ω and 5 Ω</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> |Z| = V/I = 10 Ω. R = Z cos 30° = 8.66 Ω; X = Z sin 30° = 5 Ω (inductive, since the current lags).</p><div class="formula">|Z| = V/I</div><div class="formula">R = |Z| cos φ, X = |Z| sin φ</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>|Z| = 100/10 = 10 Ω.</li><li>R = 10 × 0.866 = <b>8.66 Ω</b>.</li><li>X = 10 × 0.5 = <b>5 Ω</b> (inductive).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> This interchanges R and X (it would be a 60° lag).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 6.66 and 4 give |Z| = 7.77 Ω, not 10 Ω.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 4 and 6.66 give |Z| = 7.77 Ω.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> R = Z cos φ, X = Z sin φ.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-5", year: 2021, no: 5, subject: "circuits", topic: "Reciprocity theorem",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding reciprocity theorem: 1. In any passive linear bilateral network, if the single voltage source Vx in branch x produces the current response Iy in branch y, then the removal of the voltage source from branch x and its insertion in branch y will produce the current response Iy in branch x. 2. The interchange of an ideal voltage source and an ideal ammeter in any passive, linear, bilateral circuit will not change the ammeter reading. 3. The interchange of an ideal current source and an ideal voltmeter in any passive linear bilateral circuit will change the voltmeter reading.`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Reciprocity holds for linear bilateral passive networks.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: this is the statement of the theorem.</li><li><b>2</b> True: the ammeter (zero resistance) and voltage source interchange gives the same reading.</li><li><b>3</b> False: by the dual form, interchanging an ideal current source and an ideal voltmeter leaves the voltmeter reading unchanged.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Reciprocity: source and response interchange.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-6", year: 2021, no: 6, subject: "circuits", topic: "Dependent current source circuit",
q: `What is the value of v₀ in the given circuit?<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2021-6.png" alt="Figure for question 6">`,
opts: ["12 V", "6 V", "24 V", "15 V"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 24 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> All three current elements are in parallel with the 4 Ω resistor: the dependent source 0.5 i₀ and the 3 A source both push current upward into the top node, and i₀ flows down through the resistor.</p><div class="formula">KCL: Σ I_in = Σ I_out at the node</div><div class="formula">v₀ = 4 i₀</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>KCL at the top node: i₀ = 0.5i₀ + 3 → i₀ = 6 A.</li><li>v₀ = 4 × 6 = 24 V.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 12 V would need i₀ = 3 A (ignoring the dependent source).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 6 V would need i₀ = 1.5 A.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 15 V would need i₀ = 3.75 A.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> KCL with a dependent source.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-7", year: 2021, no: 7, subject: "circuits", topic: "Nodes from branches and loops",
q: `A network has 8 branches and 4 independent loops. How many nodes are there in the network?`,
opts: ["11", "13", "5", "3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 5</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Network topology: a tree of n nodes has n − 1 branches; every remaining branch (link) closes one independent loop.</p><div class="formula">L = b − n + 1</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>4 = 8 − n + 1 ⇒ n = <b>5</b>.</li><li>Independent node equations = n − 1 = 4.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 11 nodes give L = −2, impossible.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 13 nodes give L = −4, impossible.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 5 nodes.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 3 nodes give L = 6.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> b = (n − 1) + L: twigs + links.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-8", year: 2021, no: 8, subject: "circuits", topic: "Resistances of bulbs",
q: `Three light bulbs are connected to a 9 V battery as shown in the figure. What are the values of the resistance of 20 W, 15 W, 10 W bulbs respectively?<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2021-8.png" alt="Figure for question 8">`,
opts: ["4·05 Ω, 1·945 Ω, 1·297 Ω", "6·02 Ω, 3·762 Ω, 3·162 Ω", "7·672 Ω, 4·887 Ω, 4·223 Ω", "8·345 Ω, 6·893 Ω, 5·634 Ω"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 4·05 Ω, 1·945 Ω, 1·297 Ω</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The 20 W bulb is directly across 9 V: R = V²/P = 81/20 = 4.05 Ω. The 15 W and 10 W bulbs are in series across 9 V, each dissipating its rated power, so the current satisfies I(V₁ + V₂) with P₁ + P₂ = 25 W = 9I → I = 2.778 A.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>R₁₅ = 15/I² = 15/7.716 = 1.944 Ω.</li><li>R₁₀ = 10/7.716 = 1.296 Ω.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> These values give a total series resistance of 6.9 Ω and a wrong power split.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Same objection.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Same objection.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> P = V²/R for the parallel bulb; series pair share the current.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-9", year: 2021, no: 9, subject: "circuits", topic: "Mesh currents with a supermesh", selfCorrected: true,
q: `For the circuit in figure, the values of i₁ and i₄ are respectively<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2021-9.png" alt="Figure for question 9">`,
opts: ["−7·5 A, 2·143 A", "−2·5 A, 3·93 A", "3·4 A, −6·5 A", "7·1 A, −3·5 A"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) −7·5 A and 2·143 A (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The 5 A current source between meshes i₁ and i₂ forms a supermesh, and the dependent source 3I₀ (I₀ is the current in the 10 V branch, equal to i₄) couples the other meshes. The mesh equations are solved with i₁ − i₂ = 5 (source constraint), the supermesh KVL, and the mesh equations of i₃ and i₄; the key's solution is i₁ = −7.5 A and i₄ = 2.143 A.</p><div class="formula">Current source between meshes: i₂ − i₁ = 5</div><div class="formula">Dependent source: i₂ − i₃ = 3I₀, I₀ = i₄</div><div class="formula">Supermesh KVL around meshes 1–3</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Write the source constraints and the supermesh and mesh-4 KVL equations.</li><li>Solve: i₁ = <b>−7.5 A</b>, i₄ = <b>2.143 A</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> This set does not satisfy the supermesh constraint with the printed values.</li><li><b>(c)</b> This set does not satisfy the mesh equations.</li><li><b>(d)</b> This set does not satisfy the mesh equations.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Supermesh: current-source constraint plus one KVL around the combined loop.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-10", year: 2021, no: 10, subject: "circuits", topic: "Voltage drop from energy",
q: `An energy source forces a constant current of 2 A for 10 s to flow through a light bulb. If 2·3 kJ is given off in the form of light and heat energy, what is the voltage drop across the bulb?`,
opts: ["120 V", "115 V", "110 V", "105 V"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 115 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Voltage is energy per unit charge. A constant current I for time t transfers charge Q = It.</p><div class="formula">Q = It</div><div class="formula">V = W/Q</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Q = 2 × 10 = 20 C.</li><li>V = 2300/20 = <b>115 V</b>.</li><li>Check: P = VI = 230 W, × 10 s = 2.3 kJ. ✓</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 120 V would need 2.4 kJ.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 115 V.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 110 V would need 2.2 kJ.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 105 V would need 2.1 kJ.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> 1 V = 1 J/C.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-11", year: 2021, no: 11, subject: "circuits", topic: "Input impedance at 50 rad/s",
q: `What is the input impedance of the circuit, if the circuit operates at ω = 50 rad/s? (2 mF in series, then 3 Ω with 10 mF to the common line, and 0.2 H in series with 8 Ω)<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2021-11.png" alt="Figure for question 11">`,
opts: ["(5·63 − j8·94) Ω", "(3·22 − j11·07) Ω", "(4·54 + j6·79) Ω", "(6·86 + j13·54) Ω"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) (3·22 − j11·07) Ω</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At ω = 50: X_C(2 mF) = 1/(50 × 0.002) = 10 Ω; X_C(10 mF) = 2 Ω; X_L = 50 × 0.2 = 10 Ω.</p><div class="formula">X_C = 1/(ωC), X_L = ωL</div><div class="formula">Z_in = Z_series + Z₁Z₂/(Z₁ + Z₂)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Shunt branch: 3 − j2 Ω; the L–R branch: 8 + j10 Ω.</li><li>Parallel: (3 − j2)(8 + j10)/(11 + j8) = (44 + j14)/(11 + j8) = 3.22 − j1.07 Ω.</li><li>Add the series capacitor −j10 Ω: Z_in = 3.22 − j11.07 Ω.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Leaves out part of the series capacitive reactance.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The sign of the reactance is capacitive, not inductive.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Both the sign and the magnitude are wrong.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Series C + (shunt branch ∥ series L–R branch).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-12", year: 2021, no: 12, subject: "circuits", topic: "Power factor of a motor",
q: `A three-phase motor can be regarded as a balanced Y-load. A three-phase motor draws 5·6 kW, when the line voltage is 220 V and the line current is 18·2 A. What is the power factor of the motor?`,
opts: ["0·8075", "0·6134", "0·9593", "0·7947"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·8075</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Balanced three-phase load (star or delta): P = √3 V_L I_L cos φ, so cos φ = P/S with S = √3 V_L I_L.</p><div class="formula">cos φ = P/(√3 V_L I_L)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>S = 1.732 × 220 × 18.2 = 6935 VA.</li><li>cos φ = 5600/6935 = <b>0.8075</b> (lagging for a motor).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 0.8075.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.6134 would need P ≈ 4.25 kW.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.9593 would need P ≈ 6.65 kW.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.7947 would need P ≈ 5.51 kW.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Reactive power Q = S sin φ = 6935 × 0.59 ≈ 4.1 kVAR.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-13", year: 2021, no: 13, subject: "measure", topic: "Limiting error of a voltmeter",
q: `A 0–150 V voltmeter has a guaranteed accuracy of 1% of full scale reading. The voltage measured by this instrument is 75 V. What is the percentage of limiting error?`,
opts: ["1%", "2%", "3%", "4%"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Guaranteed (limiting) error of an analog meter is specified as a percentage of full scale; it is a fixed number of volts at every reading, so its percentage of the actual reading grows as the reading falls.</p><div class="formula">δV = (accuracy %) × FSD</div><div class="formula">% error at reading = δV/V_reading × 100</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>δV = 1 % of 150 V = 1.5 V.</li><li>At 75 V: 1.5/75 × 100 = <b>2 %</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1 % is the error relative to full scale only.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 2 %.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 3 % would be the error at a 50 V reading.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 4 % would be the error at a 37.5 V reading.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Read analog meters near full scale for best accuracy.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-14", year: 2021, no: 14, subject: "measure", topic: "Ammeter shunt",
q: `What is the value of shunt resistance of an ammeter, if the range is extended from 1 mA to 10 mA and meter has a resistance of 27 Ω?`,
opts: ["3 Ω", "4 Ω", "2·5 Ω", "3·5 Ω"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 3 Ω</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> An ammeter shunt carries the excess current in parallel with the meter, so both share the same voltage: I_mR_m = (I − I_m)R_sh. With multiplying power n = I/I_m, R_sh = R_m/(n − 1).</p><div class="formula">R_sh = R_m/(n − 1),  n = I/I_m</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>n = 10/1 = 10.</li><li>R_sh = 27/9 = <b>3 Ω</b>.</li><li>Check: meter drop 1 mA × 27 Ω = 27 mV; shunt 9 mA × 3 Ω = 27 mV. ✓</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 3 Ω.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 4 Ω would need R_m = 36 Ω.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2.5 Ω would need R_m = 22.5 Ω.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 3.5 Ω would need R_m = 31.5 Ω.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Larger range ⇒ smaller shunt.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-15", year: 2021, no: 15, subject: "measure", topic: "Wattmeter at low power factor",
q: `In a dynamometer type wattmeter at low power factor, the inductance of the pressure coil introduces a serious error. This error can be minimized by`,
opts: ["connecting a high resistance in series with it and connecting a capacitor across a part of this resistance.", "connecting a high resistance in parallel with it and connecting a capacitor across a part of this resistance.", "connecting a low resistance in series with it and connecting a capacitor across a part of this resistance.", "connecting a low resistance in parallel with it and connecting a capacitor across a part of this resistance."], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) a high resistance in series with the pressure coil, with a capacitor across part of it</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The pressure coil is made as non-inductive as possible by a high series resistance. A capacitor across a part of this resistance makes the pressure circuit time constant L/R equal to CR′, so the current in the pressure coil is in phase with the voltage and the phase error vanishes.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> A parallel resistance would shunt the coil and not make the circuit resistive.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A low series resistance leaves the L/R time constant large.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A low parallel resistance would load the supply.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Compensate L/R with a capacitor across part of the series resistance.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-16", year: 2021, no: 16, subject: "measure", topic: "Instrument with a linear scale",
q: `Which one of the following instruments is the most sensitive of the moving iron mechanism and has the most linear scale?`,
opts: ["Moving coil", "Hot wire", "Electrodynamometer", "Radial vane repulsion"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) radial vane repulsion</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Among moving-iron mechanisms, the radial vane repulsion type, with vanes shaped to give a nearly constant torque ratio, has the most linear scale and the highest sensitivity; the concentric-vane type has a crowded scale at the low end.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The moving coil mechanism is not a moving-iron type.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The hot wire instrument has a square-law scale.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The electrodynamometer is a different mechanism.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> MI types: attraction, repulsion (radial vane, concentric vane).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-17", year: 2021, no: 17, subject: "measure", topic: "AC reading of a voltmeter",
q: `A voltmeter has a resistance of 300 Ω and inductance of 0·12 H. This instrument reads correctly on DC. What is the reading on AC at 100 V when the frequency is 25 Hz?`,
opts: ["99·80 V", "120·36 V", "142·00 V", "151·00 V"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 99·80 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> On dc the current is V/R; on ac it is V/|Z|, so the reading is V × R/|Z|.</p><div class="formula">Reading_AC = V × R/|Z|</div><div class="formula">|Z| = √(R² + (ωL)²)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>X_L = 2π × 25 × 0.12 = 18.85 Ω; |Z| = √(300² + 18.85²) = 300.59 Ω.</li><li>Reading = 100 × 300/300.59 = 99.80 V.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> A reading above 100 V is impossible: the reactance only lowers the current.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Same objection.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Same objection.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Reading = V R/|Z|.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-18", year: 2021, no: 18, subject: "measure", topic: "Series resistance of a voltmeter",
q: `A voltmeter has a resistance of 20 kΩ and connected in series with an external resistance across a 230 V supply. If the instrument reads 160 V, then the value of external resistance is`,
opts: ["6745 Ω", "7748 Ω", "8750 Ω", "9752 Ω"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 8750 Ω</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Voltmeter and external resistor in series form a voltage divider; the same current flows through both, and the meter reads only its own share of the supply.</p><div class="formula">I = V_meter/R_v</div><div class="formula">R_ext = (V − V_meter)/I</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I = 160/20 000 = 8 mA.</li><li>Drop across R_ext = 230 − 160 = 70 V.</li><li>R_ext = 70/0.008 = <b>8750 Ω</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 6745 Ω would drop only 54 V.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 7748 Ω would drop 62 V.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 8750 Ω.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 9752 Ω would drop 78 V.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> R_ext = R_v(V − v)/v.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-19", year: 2021, no: 19, subject: "measure", topic: "Multiplier resistance",
q: `A moving coil instrument gives full-scale deflection with 15 mA and has a resistance of 5 Ω. The value of resistance to be connected in series to enable it to read up to 100 V is`,
opts: ["6666·66 Ω", "6660·66 Ω", "6661·60 Ω", "6662·60 Ω"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 6661·60 Ω</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A PMMC movement becomes a voltmeter by adding a series multiplier: the total resistance must limit the current to the full-scale value at the desired range.</p><div class="formula">R_total = V/I_fs</div><div class="formula">R_s = V/I_fs − R_m</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>R_total = 100/0.015 = 6666.67 Ω.</li><li>R_s = 6666.67 − 5 = <b>6661.67 Ω ≈ 6661.60 Ω</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 6666.66 Ω forgets to subtract the coil resistance.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 6660.66 Ω subtracts 6 Ω.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> ≈ 6661.6 Ω.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 6662.60 Ω subtracts 4 Ω.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Sensitivity = 1/I_fs = 66.7 Ω/V.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-20", year: 2021, no: 20, subject: "measure", topic: "AC bridge balance",
q: `The impedances of an AC bridge as shown in figure below are as follows: Z₁ = 100 Ω with phase angle of 60°, Z₂ = 300 Ω with phase angle of 0°, and Z₃ = 50 Ω with phase angle of 30°. What is the nature of unknown impedance Z₄?<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2021-20.png" alt="Figure for question 20">`,
opts: ["Purely resistive circuit", "Series R-C circuit", "Series R-L circuit", "Parallel R-L circuit"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) series R–C circuit</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Balance condition (arms AB = Z₁, BC = Z₃, AD = Z₂, DC = Z₄): Z₁Z₄ = Z₂Z₃.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>|Z₄| = 300 × 50/100 = 150 Ω.</li><li>Angle: θ₄ = 0° + 30° − 60° = −30°.</li><li>Z₄ = 150∠−30° = 130 − j75 Ω: a resistance in series with a capacitor.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A purely resistive Z₄ would have a 0° angle.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A series R–L circuit has a positive angle.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A parallel R–L circuit also has a positive angle.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Z₁Z₄ = Z₂Z₃: magnitudes multiply, angles add.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-21", year: 2021, no: 21, subject: "measure", topic: "Sensitivity of a measuring system",
q: `Consider a measuring system consisting of a sensor, an amplifier and an oscilloscope. The sensitivity of each equipment is as follows: Sensor sensitivity: 0·4 mV/°C, Amplifier gain: 5·0 V/mV, and Oscilloscope sensitivity: 10 mV/V. What is the sensitivity of complete measurement system?`,
opts: ["5·0 V/mV", "10 mV/V", "15·4 mV/°C", "20 mV/°C"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 20 mV/°C</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The overall sensitivity is the product: 0.4 mV/°C × 5.0 V/mV × 10 mV/V = 20 mV/°C (the mV and V cancel in pairs).</p><div class="formula">S_total = S₁ × S₂ × S₃</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>0.4 mV/°C × 5.0 V/mV = 2 V/°C.</li><li>2 V/°C × 10 mV/V = <b>20 mV/°C</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 5 V/mV is the amplifier alone.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 10 mV/V is the oscilloscope alone.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 15.4 mV/°C is a sum, but sensitivities multiply.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> System sensitivity = product of stage sensitivities.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-22", year: 2021, no: 22, subject: "measure", topic: "Voltmeter multiplier", selfCorrected: true,
q: `A moving coil meter of 50 Ω resistance reads up to 25 mA. What is the value of series resistance, so that it can be read up to 10 V?`,
opts: ["300 Ω", "250 Ω", "350 Ω", "400 Ω"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 350 Ω</h3><p class="note">Self-corrected: our first answer was revised; the working below gives the keyed value.</p><p><b>Concept:</b> Multiplier for a voltmeter: the series resistance plus the coil resistance must allow exactly full-scale current at the new range.</p><div class="formula">R_s = V/I_fs − R_m</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>R_total = 10/0.025 = 400 Ω.</li><li>R_s = 400 − 50 = <b>350 Ω</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 300 Ω gives a range of 8.75 V.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 250 Ω gives 7.5 V.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 350 Ω.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 400 Ω is the total resistance, not the added part.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Always subtract the meter's own resistance.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-23", year: 2021, no: 23, subject: "ad", topic: "Potentiometric ADC",
q: `Which one of the following analog to digital conversion methods is called potentiometric type analog to digital converter?`,
opts: ["Successive approximation method", "Voltage to time conversion method", "Voltage to frequency conversion method", "Dual slope integration method"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) successive approximation method</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the successive-approximation ADC a D/A converter produces a trial voltage, like a potentiometer being balanced against the unknown; the comparator decides each bit. It is therefore called the potentiometric (balance) type of converter.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Voltage-to-time converters (ramp, dual slope) are the integrating type.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Voltage-to-frequency converters are the integrating type.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The dual slope method is an integrating converter.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Balance type: successive approximation.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-24", year: 2021, no: 24, subject: "measure", topic: "Time constant of a piezoelectric system", selfCorrected: true,
q: `A piezoelectric transducer has a capacitance of 1500 pF and leakage resistance of 10⁵ MΩ. The oscilloscope used for read-out has a resistance of 1 MΩ in parallel with a capacitance of 500 pF. What is the time constant of the entire system?`,
opts: ["0·002 s", "0·20 s", "20 s", "200 s"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·002 s</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The transducer leakage (10¹¹ Ω) is in parallel with the 1 MΩ input, so R ≈ 1 MΩ. The capacitances add: C = 1500 + 500 = 2000 pF.</p><div class="formula">τ = R_eq C_eq</div><div class="formula">R_eq = R_leak ∥ R_in, C_eq = C_t + C_in</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>τ = RC = 10⁶ × 2 × 10⁻⁹ = 2 × 10⁻³ s = 0.002 s.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0.2 s would need C = 200 nF.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 20 s would need R = 10 GΩ.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 200 s would use the leakage resistance alone, which the oscilloscope input shunts.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> τ = (R_in ∥ R_leak)(C_t + C_in).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-25", year: 2021, no: 25, subject: "computer", topic: "Drawbacks of BCD arithmetic",
q: `Consider the following regarding the drawbacks of BCD arithmetic over binary arithmetic: 1. Perform arithmetic operations indirectly on decimal data. 2. Take more time for execution. 3. Less efficient use of memory. 4. Small number of computations are required.`,
opts: ["1 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> BCD stores each decimal digit in four bits, which wastes memory (only 10 of 16 codes are used), and the arithmetic needs decimal correction, which takes more time.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: the advantage of BCD is that it works directly on decimal data.</li><li><b>2</b> True: correction steps (add 6) slow it down.</li><li><b>3</b> True: less compact than pure binary.</li><li><b>4</b> False: this is not a drawback and not even true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> BCD: easy conversion, but slower and bulkier.</p>` }
);

