window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2021-126", year: 2021, no: 126, subject: "ad", topic: "Reconstruction filter",
q: `Which one of the following filters is used to recover original signal from its sample?`,
opts: ["Low pass filter", "High pass filter", "Band pass filter", "Band reject filter"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) low-pass filter</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The spectrum of a sampled signal repeats around multiples of the sampling frequency; a low-pass (reconstruction) filter keeps the baseband copy and recovers the original signal.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> A high-pass filter keeps the images.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A band-pass filter would remove the baseband.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A band-reject filter removes a band only.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Reconstruct = low-pass filter.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-127", year: 2021, no: 127, subject: "ad", topic: "First-order low-pass filter",
q: `In the first order low pass filter, which one of the following statements is not correct?`,
opts: ["It has the maximum gain at frequency of 0 Hz.", "At higher cutoff frequency, the gain falls to 0·707 times the maximum gain.", "For frequency greater than higher cutoff frequency, the gain decreases at a constant rate of −20 dB/decade.", "It has the maximum gain in stop band."], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) (d) is not correct</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A low-pass filter has its maximum gain in the pass band (at 0 Hz); the stop band is where the gain is attenuated.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Correct: −3 dB at the cut-off.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Incorrect: the stop band has the minimum gain.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Pass band: max gain; stop band: attenuated.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-128", year: 2021, no: 128, subject: "ad", topic: "Quantisation error",
q: `The quantization error is equivalent to`,
opts: ["internal noise generated within system.", "impulse noise.", "inter modulation noise as it produces harmonics.", "additive white noise as it alters the signal amplitude."], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) additive white noise</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The quantisation error is modelled as additive white noise, uniformly distributed over one step, with variance Δ²/12.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> It arises from the quantiser, not from system components.</li><li><b>(b)</b> It is not an impulse.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Intermodulation noise comes from non-linearity.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Quantisation noise: uniform, white.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-129", year: 2021, no: 129, subject: "ad", topic: "FDM bandwidth",
q: `A frequency division multiplexing system is used to multiplex 24 independent voice signals. Single sideband modulation is used for the transmission. Each voice signal is allotted a bandwidth of 4 kHz. What is the overall transmission bandwidth of the channel?`,
opts: ["4 kHz", "6 kHz", "24 kHz", "96 kHz"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 96 kHz</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In FDM each channel is shifted to its own frequency slot. With SSB each voice channel occupies only its own message bandwidth (no second sideband), so the total bandwidth is the number of channels × the slot width (guard bands included in the 4 kHz allotment).</p><div class="formula">B = N × B_channel</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>B = 24 × 4 kHz = <b>96 kHz</b>.</li><li>(DSB would need 192 kHz.)</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 4 kHz is one channel.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 6 kHz is not a multiple of the 4 kHz slot for 24 channels.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 24 kHz would be 1 kHz per channel.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 96 kHz.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> This is the classic 12/24-channel telephone group.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-130", year: 2021, no: 130, subject: "ad", topic: "Sync spacing in TDM",
q: `A Time Division Multiplexing system is used to multiplex four independent voice signals using pulse amplitude modulation. Each voice signal is sampled at the rate of 8 kHz. The system incorporates a synchronizing pulse train for its proper operation. What is the timing between the synchronizing pulse trains and the impulse trains used to sample the four voice signals?`,
opts: ["5 μs", "10 μs", "15 μs", "25 μs"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 25 μs</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a PAM-TDM frame every channel is sampled once per sampling period, and a sync pulse occupies one extra slot. Equal slots ⇒ slot width = frame period/(channels + 1).</p><div class="formula">T_frame = 1/f_s</div><div class="formula">T_slot = T_frame/(N + 1)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>T_frame = 1/8000 = 125 μs.</li><li>Slots = 4 + 1 = 5.</li><li>Spacing = 125/5 = <b>25 μs</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 5 μs is 1/25 of the frame.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 10 μs is 1/12.5 of the frame.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 15 μs does not divide 125 μs into equal slots.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 25 μs.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Without the sync slot the spacing would be 31.25 μs.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-131", year: 2021, no: 131, subject: "ad", topic: "Full AM spectrum",
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct for full amplitude modulation?`,
opts: ["The spectrum consists of two sidebands (one termed the upper sideband and the other termed the lower sideband).", "The spectrum consists of one sideband (termed the upper sideband).", "The spectrum consists of one sideband (termed the lower sideband).", "The spectrum consists of three sidebands (one termed the upper sideband, the second termed the lower sideband, and the third termed the lowest sideband)."], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) The spectrum consists of two sidebands (one termed the upper sideband and the other termed the lower sideband).</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Full (DSB-FC) AM: v = A_c[1 + m cos ω_mt] cos ω_ct = A_c cos ω_ct + (mA_c/2)cos(ω_c + ω_m)t + (mA_c/2)cos(ω_c − ω_m)t. The spectrum has the carrier plus two sidebands, upper (f_c + f_m) and lower (f_c − f_m). Bandwidth = 2f_m.</p><div class="formula">USB: f_c + f_m;  LSB: f_c − f_m</div><div class="formula">B = 2f_m</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Expanding the product gives carrier + USB + LSB.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Two sidebands (with the carrier).</li><li><b>(b)</b> One sideband is SSB.</li><li><b>(c)</b> One sideband is SSB.</li><li><b>(d)</b> There is no third ('lowest') sideband.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Carrier carries ≥ 2/3 of the power but no information.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-132", year: 2021, no: 132, subject: "ad", topic: "Sampling rate for speech",
q: `The highest frequency component of a speech signal needed for telephonic communications is about 3·1 kHz. What is the suitable value for the sampling rate?`,
opts: ["1 kHz", "2 kHz", "4 kHz", "8 kHz"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 8 kHz</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Nyquist needs at least 2 × 3.1 = 6.2 kHz; the telephone standard is 8 kHz, which also leaves a guard band for the anti-aliasing filter.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1 kHz is far below the Nyquist rate.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 2 kHz is below the Nyquist rate.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 4 kHz is below the 6.2 kHz minimum.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> f_s ≥ 2f_max; telephony: 8 kHz.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-133", year: 2021, no: 133, subject: "signals", topic: "FIR filter advantage",
q: `Which one of the following is the advantage of FIR filter over IIR filter?`,
opts: ["FIR filter can have an exact linear phase.", "FIR filter is always unstable.", "For FIR filter, the design methods are non-linear.", "FIR filter cannot be realized efficiently in hardware."], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) an FIR filter can have an exact linear phase</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> FIR filters with symmetric coefficients have exactly linear phase (constant group delay) and are always stable, since they have no feedback.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> FIR filters are always stable.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Their design methods (windowing, frequency sampling) are linear.</li><li><b>(d)</b> They are easily realised in hardware.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> FIR: linear phase, always stable.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-134", year: 2021, no: 134, subject: "signals", topic: "Spectra of periodic signals",
q: `Consider the following statements for a periodic signal: 1. Both the magnitude and phase spectra are line spectra. 2. For real-valued signals, the magnitude spectrum has even symmetry. 3. For real-valued signals, the phase has odd symmetry.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A periodic signal has a Fourier series: power only at harmonics kf₀, so its magnitude and phase spectra are line (discrete) spectra. For a real signal c₋ₖ = cₖ*, hence |c₋ₖ| = |cₖ| (even magnitude) and ∠c₋ₖ = −∠cₖ (odd phase).</p><div class="formula">x(t) = Σ cₖ e^(jkω₀t)</div><div class="formula">Real x: c₋ₖ = cₖ*</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>1 Line spectra. Correct.</li><li>2 Even magnitude. Correct.</li><li>3 Odd phase. Correct.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> All three.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 2.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Aperiodic signals have continuous spectra.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-135", year: 2021, no: 135, subject: "signals", topic: "Energy and power signals",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding power of a continuous-time and discrete-time signals: 1. Power is the time average of energy. 2. A signal with finite energy has zero power. 3. A signal with finite power has infinite energy. 4. All finite periodic signals are power signals.`,
opts: ["1, 2, 3 and 4", "1, 2 and 3 only", "2, 3 and 4 only", "1 and 4 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Power is energy averaged over time. A finite-energy signal averaged over infinite time gives zero power; a finite non-zero power signal has infinite energy; and periodic signals have finite average power over one period.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Energy signal: P = 0; power signal: E = ∞.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-136", year: 2021, no: 136, subject: "signals", topic: "Comb filter",
q: `Which one of the following filters is used for reduction of power line harmonics, suppression of clutter from fixed objects in moving target indication radar?`,
opts: ["NOTCH filter", "IIR filter", "FIR filter", "COMB filter"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) comb filter</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A comb filter has notches at the harmonics of a fundamental frequency, so it removes power-line harmonics and the fixed-clutter returns at multiples of the pulse repetition frequency in MTI radar.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A notch filter removes a single frequency.</li><li><b>(b)</b> IIR is a type of implementation.</li><li><b>(c)</b> FIR is a type of implementation.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Comb: periodic notches.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-137", year: 2021, no: 137, subject: "maths", topic: "Ratio of complex numbers",
q: `For z₁ = 2e^{jπ/4} and z₂ = 8e^{jπ/3}, the value of z₁/z₂² is`,
opts: ["e^{−j5π/12}", "(1/8)e^{−j6π/17}", "(1/32)e^{−j5π/12}", "(1/14)e^{−j5π/17}"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) (1/32)e^{−j5π/12}</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Complex numbers in polar form: multiply/divide moduli and add/subtract angles; raising to a power n raises the modulus to n and multiplies the angle by n.</p><div class="formula">(re^(jθ))ⁿ = rⁿe^(jnθ)</div><div class="formula">z₁/z₂ = (r₁/r₂)e^(j(θ₁ − θ₂))</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>z₂² = 64e^(j2π/3).</li><li>Modulus 2/64 = 1/32; angle π/4 − 2π/3 = (3π − 8π)/12 = −5π/12.</li><li>z₁/z₂² = <b>(1/32)e^(−j5π/12)</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Modulus is 1/32, not 1.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Neither modulus nor angle is right.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> (1/32)e^(−j5π/12).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Neither modulus nor angle is right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> −5π/12 = −75°.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-138", year: 2021, no: 138, subject: "maths", topic: "Integral of x² cos ax",
q: `The simplified value of ∫x² cos ax dx is`,
opts: ["(1/a³)(2ax cos ax − 2 sin ax + a²x² sin ax)", "(1/a²)(2ax cos ax − 2 sin ax + a²x sin ax)", "(1/a)(2ax cos ax − 2 sin ax + ax sin ax)", "(1/a)(2ax cos ax − 2 sin ax + a²x² sin ax)"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) (1/a³)(2ax cos ax − 2 sin ax + a²x² sin ax)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Integrate by parts twice: ∫x² cos ax dx = x² sin ax/a + 2x cos ax/a² − 2 sin ax/a³ = (1/a³)(a²x² sin ax + 2ax cos ax − 2 sin ax).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The factor must be 1/a³ throughout.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The terms are inconsistent in powers of a.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The prefactor must be 1/a³.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Parts twice.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-139", year: 2021, no: 139, subject: "signals", topic: "Average power of a square wave",
q: `What is the total average power in the unit-amplitude square wave of period, T, and 50% duty cycle?`,
opts: ["1·5", "0·25", "0·5", "1"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·5</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Average power of a periodic signal: P = (1/T)∫|x(t)|²dt over one period. A unit-amplitude square wave that is 1 for half the period and 0 for the other half has x² = x.</p><div class="formula">P = (1/T)∫₀ᵀ x²(t)dt</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>P = (1/T)[1² × T/2 + 0] = <b>0.5</b>.</li><li>(A ±1 square wave would have P = 1.)</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1.5 exceeds the peak power 1.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.25 is the square of the mean (DC power only).</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> 0.5.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1 is for a ±1 wave or a constant 1.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> P = duty ratio × A² for a 0/A pulse train.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-140", year: 2021, no: 140, subject: "signals", topic: "Zero-state response of a discrete system",
q: `For a system with unit impulse response h[k] = (0·5)ᵏu[k], what is the zero state response y[k] for the input f[k] = (0·8)ᵏu[k]?`,
opts: ["y[k] = [−(2/3)(0·5)ᵏ + (7/3)(0·6)ᵏ]u[k]", "y[k] = [−(5/3)(0·5)ᵏ + (8/3)(0·8)ᵏ]u[k]", "y[k] = [−(1/3)(0·5)ᵏ + (2/3)(0·8)ᵏ]u[k]", "y[k] = [−(0·5)ᵏ + (0·6)ᵏ]u[k]"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) y[k] = [−(5/3)(0·5)ᵏ + (8/3)(0·8)ᵏ]u[k]</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> y[k] = Σ_{m=0}^{k}(0.5)^m(0.8)^{k−m} = 0.8ᵏ Σ(0.625)^m = 0.8ᵏ(1 − 0.625^{k+1})/0.375.</p><div class="formula">y[k] = Σ_{m=0}^{k} h[m]f[k − m]</div><div class="formula">Σ_{m=0}^{k} rᵐ = (1 − r^(k+1))/(1 − r)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>= (0.8^{k+1} − 0.5^{k+1})/0.3 = (8/3)(0.8)ᵏ − (5/3)(0.5)ᵏ.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The 0.6 exponential is not a pole of the system or the input.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The coefficients do not give y[0] = 1.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The 0.6 exponential is not a pole.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Convolve: partial fractions of 1/((1 − 0.5z⁻¹)(1 − 0.8z⁻¹)).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-141", year: 2021, no: 141, subject: "maths", topic: "Integral of x² eᵃˣ",
q: `The value of ∫x²eᵃˣ dx is`,
opts: ["(eᵃˣ/a)(a²x² + 3ax − 2)", "(eᵃˣ/a⁴)(a³x² − a²x + 2ax + 1)", "(eᵃˣ/a³)(a²x² − 2ax + 2)", "(eᵃˣ/a²)(a³x² − 2a²x + 4)"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) (eᵃˣ/a³)(a²x² − 2ax + 2)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> By parts twice: ∫x²eᵃˣdx = eᵃˣ(x²/a − 2x/a² + 2/a³) = (eᵃˣ/a³)(a²x² − 2ax + 2). Differentiating confirms it.</p><div class="formula">∫u dv = uv − ∫v du</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>∫x²e^(ax)dx = x²e^(ax)/a − (2/a)∫xe^(ax)dx.</li><li>∫xe^(ax)dx = e^(ax)(x/a − 1/a²).</li><li>Total = e^(ax)(x²/a − 2x/a² + 2/a³) = <b>(e^(ax)/a³)(a²x² − 2ax + 2)</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The sign of the 3ax term and the prefactor are wrong.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The powers of a are inconsistent.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The powers of a are inconsistent.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> ∫xⁿeᵃˣ by repeated parts.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-142", year: 2021, no: 142, subject: "signals", topic: "Convolution of two exponentials",
q: `What is the convolution y[n] = x[n]*h[n]: where x[n] = αⁿu[n], h[n] = βⁿu[n], α ≠ β, α = 0·8, β = 0·9?`,
opts: ["y[n] = [9(0·9)ⁿ − 8(0·8)ⁿ]u[n]", "y[n] = [0·9(9)ⁿ − 0·8(8)ⁿ]u[n]", "y[n] = [0·3(9)ⁿ − 0·4(8)ⁿ]u[n]", "y[n] = [9(0·3)ⁿ − 8(0·4)ⁿ]u[n]"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) y[n] = [9(0·9)ⁿ − 8(0·8)ⁿ]u[n]</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> y[n] = (β^{n+1} − α^{n+1})/(β − α) = 10[0.9·0.9ⁿ − 0.8·0.8ⁿ] = 9(0.9)ⁿ − 8(0.8)ⁿ.</p><div class="formula">αⁿu[n] * βⁿu[n] = (β^(n+1) − α^(n+1))/(β − α) u[n]</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>β − α = 0.1.</li><li>y = [0.9 × 0.9ⁿ − 0.8 × 0.8ⁿ]/0.1 = <b>9(0.9)ⁿ − 8(0.8)ⁿ</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The bases 9 and 8 would grow without bound.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Same objection.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The bases 0.3 and 0.4 are not the pole values.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> (β^{n+1} − α^{n+1})/(β − α).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-143", year: 2021, no: 143, subject: "signals", topic: "Parseval's equality",
q: `Which one of the following is correct for parseval equality?`,
opts: ["∫|x(t)|²dt = (1/2π)∫|X(jω)|²dω over (−∞, ∞)", "∫|x(t)|²dt = (1/2π)∫₀^∞|X(jω)|^{1/2}dω", "∫|x(t)|²dt = (1/π)∫₀^∞|X(jω)|^{1/2}dω", "∫|x(t)|²dt = (4/3π)∫|X(jω)|²dω"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) ∫|x(t)|²dt = (1/2π)∫|X(jω)|²dω over (−∞, ∞)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Parseval's relation states that the energy of a signal is the same in the two domains: E = ∫|x|²dt = (1/2π)∫|X(jω)|²dω.</p><div class="formula">E = ∫|x(t)|²dt = (1/2π)∫|X(jω)|²dω</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Energy is conserved between time and frequency domains; the 1/2π factor comes from using ω.</li><li>Option (a).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The exponent 1/2 is wrong.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The exponent 1/2 and the factor are wrong.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The factor 4/3π does not occur.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Energy in time = energy in frequency.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-144", year: 2021, no: 144, subject: "signals", topic: "Sampling interval",
q: `For x(t) = sin(10πt)/πt, what is the condition on the sampling interval T so that x(t) is uniquely represented by the discrete-time sequence x[n] = x(nT)?`,
opts: ["T &lt; 1/10", "T &lt; 1/5", "T &gt; 1/5", "T &gt; 1/10"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) T &lt; 1/10</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> sin(10πt)/πt has a spectrum limited to |ω| ≤ 10π, i.e. f_max = 5 Hz. The sampling rate must exceed 10 Hz, so T &lt; 1/10 s.</p><div class="formula">f_s &gt; 2f_max ⇒ T &lt; 1/(2f_max)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>sin(10πt)/πt is band-limited to ω = 10π ⇒ f_max = 5 Hz.</li><li>f_s &gt; 10 Hz ⇒ <b>T &lt; 1/10 s</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> T &lt; 1/5 would allow a 5 Hz sampling rate, below Nyquist.</li><li><b>(c)</b> T &gt; 1/5 is far too coarse.</li><li><b>(d)</b> T &gt; 1/10 violates the Nyquist condition.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> T < 1/(2f_max).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-145", year: 2021, no: 145, subject: "materials", topic: "Ferromagnetic materials and ferrites", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Statement (I): Ferromagnetic properties are confined almost entirely to iron, nickel and cobalt and their alloys. Statement (II): By substituting the basic ferrite of magnetite with other divalent oxides, a wide range of ferrimagnetic compounds with useful properties can be produced, which have high electrical resistivity to minimize eddy currents at high frequencies.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Ferromagnetism (spontaneous parallel alignment of atomic moments by the exchange interaction) occurs at room temperature essentially only in Fe, Ni, Co (and some rare earths such as Gd) and their alloys. Ferrites are oxides based on magnetite Fe₃O₄ = FeO·Fe₂O₃; replacing Fe²⁺ by other divalent ions (Mn, Zn, Ni, Mg) gives ferrimagnetic materials with tailored permeability and very high resistivity, so eddy-current loss is negligible at high frequencies.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Statement I: true.</li><li>Statement II: true (soft ferrites MnZn, NiZn).</li><li>The key takes II as explaining I (the ferrites extend magnetic materials beyond Fe, Ni, Co).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Key's choice.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Alternative reading: II describes ferrimagnets, a different class.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Ferrites: ρ ≈ 1–10⁶ Ω·m, B_sat ≈ 0.3–0.5 T.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-146", year: 2021, no: 146, subject: "materials", topic: "Suspension insulators",
q: `Statement (I): The inorganic materials are used to manufacture suspension insulators for high-voltage overhead lines and bushings on high-voltage transformers and switchgear. Statement (II): The ceramic and glass materials are formed into a series of flanged discs to decrease the creepage distance along the surface of the complete insulator.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Suspension insulators and bushings are made of inorganic materials – porcelain, toughened glass. Each disc has corrugated skirts (sheds) so that the surface leakage (creepage) path from cap to pin is long; this raises the flashover voltage under wet or polluted conditions.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Statement I: true.</li><li>Statement II: says the discs decrease the creepage distance – they increase it. False.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I true, II false.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Polluted areas use 'anti-fog' discs with deeper skirts for even longer creepage.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-147", year: 2021, no: 147, subject: "circuits", topic: "Resonant circuits",
q: `Statement (I): In resonant circuits, the capacitor voltage or inductor current could be much lower than the source voltage or source current, and a large input signal can produce a small output signal when resonance appears in a circuit. Statement (II): In resonant circuits, the quality factor for a coil is defined as the ratio of the inductive reactance and the winding resistance, which is a dimensionless parameter.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At series resonance the voltages across L and C are Q times the source voltage; at parallel resonance the currents in L and C are Q times the source current. Resonance therefore magnifies, it does not reduce. The quality factor of a coil, Q = ωL/R, is a dimensionless ratio of reactance to resistance.</p><div class="formula">Series: V_L = V_C = QV</div><div class="formula">Q = ωL/R</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Statement I: claims the capacitor voltage or inductor current is much lower – it is much higher. False.</li><li>Statement II: Q = ωL/R, dimensionless. True.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> I is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> I is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> I is false.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I false, II true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> High-Q series circuits can develop dangerous capacitor voltages.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-148", year: 2021, no: 148, subject: "circuits", topic: "Electric field lines",
q: `Statement (I): The pattern of the electric field lines strongly depends on the geometric arrangement of charge carriers and the field lines always enter or exit the charge carrier vertically. Statement (II): From the field pattern, a small distance between adjacent field lines (high field line density) indicates low field strength.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Electric field lines start on positive and end on negative charges; on a conductor surface they are always normal (perpendicular) to it, and their pattern depends on the charge geometry. The density of lines (lines per unit area) is proportional to the field strength: closely spaced lines mean a strong field.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Statement I: true (lines meet conducting charge carriers at right angles).</li><li>Statement II: high line density means low field – reversed. False.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is false.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I true, II false.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Field is strongest at sharp points, where lines crowd together (corona).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-149", year: 2021, no: 149, subject: "measure", topic: "Strain gauge and Bourdon tube",
q: `Statement (I): A strain gauge pressure sensor converts the physical quantity 'pressure' into an electrical signal and electrical resistance of the strain gauge changes with the tensile strain. Statement (II): Bourdon tube pressure sensors work on the principle of change of any form of deformation, the cross-sectional tubing tends to recover its circular form under the action of pressure, which is used as mechanical pressure measuring instrument.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A strain-gauge pressure sensor bonds a gauge to a diaphragm: pressure strains it and its resistance changes (ΔR/R = G·ε, gauge factor G ≈ 2 for metal foil), read with a Wheatstone bridge. A Bourdon tube is a flattened curved tube that tends to straighten (recover its circular section) under internal pressure; the tip movement drives a pointer mechanically.</p><div class="formula">ΔR/R = G ε</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Statement I: true.</li><li>Statement II: true.</li><li>They describe two different sensors, so II does not explain I.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II does not explain I.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true; independent.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Bourdon tube can be made electrical by linking it to an LVDT or potentiometer.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-150", year: 2021, no: 150, subject: "pe", topic: "Power diode structure",
q: `Statement (I): Lightly doped n⁻ epitaxial layer is present in pn-junction diode. Statement (II): Power diodes have lightly doped n⁻ epitaxial layer which can absorb the depletion layer during reverse biased.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) I false, II true</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A signal-level pn-junction diode has no such layer; it is a feature of power diodes.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>I</b> False as a general statement.</li><li><b>II</b> True: the lightly doped n⁻ drift layer holds the depletion region and supports the reverse voltage.</li></ul><p>Why the layer is there: the breakdown voltage of a junction rises as the doping of its lighter side falls, roughly V_BR ∝ 1/N_D, and the depletion width needed to hold V_BR grows as √(V_BR/N_D). A power diode therefore adds a thick, lightly doped n⁻ drift region between the p⁺ anode and the n⁺ substrate so that the depletion layer spreads into it under reverse bias (punch-through design). The price is a higher on-state drop and stored charge, partly offset by conductivity modulation when the diode conducts. A small-signal diode handles a few volts, so it is a simple p-n junction without this layer; hence Statement I is false and Statement II, which describes the power diode correctly, is true.</p><p><b>Verdict on each option:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> I is false, so (a) is not right.</li><li><b>(b)</b> I is false, so (b) is not right.</li><li><b>(c)</b> I is false and II is true, so (c) is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is false and II is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Power diode: p⁺ n⁻ n⁺.</p>` }
);

