// ESE 2020 Prelims Paper II, Electrical Engineering, Series A (KJL-S-ETE), Q26–50
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; none dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2020-26", year: 2020, no: 26, subject: "circuits", topic: "Fundamental loop matrix",
q: `For a network graph having its fundamental loop matrix B_f and its sub-matrices B_t and B_l corresponding to twigs and links, respectively, which of the following statements are correct? 1. B_l is always an identity matrix. 2. B_t is an identity matrix. 3. B_f has a rank of b − (n − 1), where b is the number of branches and n is the number of nodes of the graph.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The fundamental loop matrix is B_f = [B_t | B_l] with one row per link (b − n + 1 rows) and one column per branch.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: each fundamental loop contains exactly one link, so the link part is the unit matrix.</li><li><b>2</b> False: B_t is a general matrix of 0, +1, −1.</li><li><b>3</b> True: the rank equals the number of independent loops, b − (n − 1).</li></ul><p>The cut-set matrix Q_f = [U | Q_l] is the dual of the tie-set matrix: its number of rows is n − 1, the number of twigs, and its rank is n − 1.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> B_f = [B_t | U], rank b − n + 1.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-27", year: 2020, no: 27, subject: "circuits", topic: "Resistance in terms of field and current density",
q: `The resistance R of a conductor is (E = electric field intensity, A = cross-sectional area, J = current density, l = length of conductor)`,
opts: ["EA/Jl", "EJ/Al", "El/JA", "JA/El"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) El/(JA)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> R = V/I with V = El and I = JA, so R = El/(JA). (With J = σE this gives l/σA, the familiar formula.)</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> EA/Jl is the inverse of a resistance times area².</li><li><b>(b)</b> EJ/Al has the wrong dimensions.</li><li><b>(d)</b> JA/El is the conductance.</li></ul><p>Writing J = σE gives R = l/(σA) = ρl/A, and the units check: (V/m)(m)/((A/m²)(m²)) = V/A = Ω. This is the basis of the resistivity relation ρ = E/J: the field needed per unit current density is a property of the material, and R then follows from the geometry.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> R = V/I = El/JA.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-28", year: 2020, no: 28, subject: "circuits", topic: "Ideal constant voltage source",
q: `Which of the following statements are correct for an ideal constant voltage source? 1. Its output voltage remains absolutely constant whatever the change in load current. 2. It possesses zero internal resistance so that internal voltage drop in the source is zero. 3. Output voltage provided by the source would remain constant irrespective of the amount of current drawn from it. 4. Output voltage provided by the source varies with the amount of current drawn from it.`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 3 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> An ideal voltage source has zero internal resistance.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True.</li><li><b>2</b> True.</li><li><b>3</b> True: this restates statement 1.</li><li><b>4</b> False: a source whose voltage varies with current is a practical source with internal resistance.</li></ul><p>Real sources have a small internal resistance, so their terminal voltage falls slightly with load: V = E − IR_i; an ideal source has R_i = 0 and an ideal current source R_i = ∞. Because the source maintains its terminal voltage, the load current is set entirely by the load, I = V/R_L, and an ideal voltage source can supply unlimited power in principle.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Ideal V source: R_int = 0.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-29", year: 2020, no: 29, subject: "circuits", topic: "Filters",
q: `Which of the following statements are correct? 1. A lowpass filter passes low frequencies and stops high frequencies. 2. A highpass filter passes high frequencies and rejects low frequencies. 3. A bandpass filter passes frequencies within a frequency band and attenuates frequencies outside the band. 4. A bandstop filter passes frequencies within the band and blocks/attenuates frequencies outside a frequency band.`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 3 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Statement 4 describes a band-pass filter, not a band-stop filter; a band-stop filter blocks frequencies <b>within</b> the band and passes those outside it.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True.</li><li><b>2</b> True.</li><li><b>3</b> True.</li><li><b>4</b> False: reversed.</li></ul><p>A band-stop (notch) filter has the opposite response to a band-pass filter and is used to remove a single interfering frequency, such as 50 Hz hum in measurement circuits. Active and passive realisations exist for each type; the pass-band edge frequencies (corner or cut-off frequencies) are the points at which the gain has fallen by 3 dB.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Band stop = notch: rejects a band.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-30", year: 2020, no: 30, subject: "circuits", topic: "Potential difference due to a point charge",
q: `A point charge of 10⁻⁹ C is placed at a point A in the free space. The potential difference between the two points 20 cm and 10 cm away from the charge at A will be`,
opts: ["40 V", "45 V", "50 V", "55 V"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 45 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> V = Q/(4πε₀r) = 9 × 10⁹ × 10⁻⁹/r = 9/r volts.</p><div class="formula">V = Q/(4πε₀r) = 9 × 10⁹ Q/r</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>At 0.1 m: 90 V; at 0.2 m: 45 V.</li><li>Difference = 90 − 45 = 45 V.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 40 V would need a charge of 0.89 nC.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 50 V would need a charge of 1.11 nC.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 55 V would need a charge of 1.22 nC.</li></ul><p>The potential at a point due to a point charge is V = Q/(4πε₀r), so the difference between two radii is Q(1/r₁ − 1/r₂)/(4πε₀), which here is 9(10 − 5) = 45 V. The potential difference between two points depends only on the radial distances, because the electrostatic field of a point charge is conservative and the work done is path independent.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> V = 9Q/r (Q in nC, r in m).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-31", year: 2020, no: 31, subject: "circuits", topic: "Gauss's theorem",
q: `According to Gauss's theorem, the surface integral of the normal component of electric flux density D over a closed surface, containing free charge is`,
opts: ["Q", "Q/ε₀", "ε₀Q", "Q²/ε₀"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Q</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> ∮D·dS = Q (free charge enclosed). The ε₀ appears only when the integral is written for E: ∮E·dS = Q/ε₀, because D = ε₀E in free space.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Q/ε₀ is the flux of E, not of D.</li><li><b>(c)</b> ε₀Q has the wrong dimensions.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Q²/ε₀ has the wrong dimensions.</li></ul><p>Gauss's law is the first of Maxwell's equations; in point form ∇·D = ρ, and it is used to find the fields of symmetric charge distributions by choosing a suitable Gaussian surface. For a charge Q placed in a dielectric of permittivity ε the field is E = Q/(4πεr²), but the flux of D through any enclosing surface is still the free charge Q.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Flux of D = enclosed free charge.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-32", year: 2020, no: 32, subject: "circuits", topic: "Unit magnetic pole", selfCorrected: true,
q: `A unit magnetic pole may be defined as that pole which when placed in vacuum at a distance of one metre from a similar and equal pole repels it with a force of`,
opts: ["1/4π Newtons", "μ₀/4π Newtons", "π/4μ₀ Newtons", "1/(4πμ₀) Newtons"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1/(4πμ₀) newtons (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Coulomb's law for magnetic poles: F = m₁m₂/(4πμ₀r²) (pole strength in A·m, the "older" SI convention in which the force between poles is 1/(4πμ₀)). For unit poles at 1 m: F = 1/(4πμ₀) N.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> This omits μ₀.</li><li><b>(b)</b> This is the form in the other convention, which we first chose.</li><li><b>(c)</b> π/(4μ₀) is not a form of the law.</li></ul><p>In the convention where F = μ₀m₁m₂/(4πr²) (poles in Wb/μ₀), the force would be μ₀/4π.</p><p>In the SI system the unit pole is not used any more; the older Coulomb law of magnetism gave the force between poles of strengths m₁, m₂ as m₁m₂/(4πμ₀r²), the magnetic analogue of Coulomb's law of electrostatics.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Magnetic Coulomb law: F = m₁m₂/(4πμ₀r²).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-33", year: 2020, no: 33, subject: "circuits", topic: "Analogy between magnetic and electric circuits",
q: `An analogous of magnetic circuit 'permeability' in electrical circuit is`,
opts: ["Reluctivity", "Conductance", "Conductivity", "Resistivity"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) conductivity</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The analogy: mmf ↔ emf, flux ↔ current, reluctance ↔ resistance, permeability ↔ conductivity, reluctivity ↔ resistivity.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Reluctivity (1/μ) corresponds to resistivity.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Conductance corresponds to permeance.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Resistivity corresponds to reluctivity.</li></ul><p>Reluctance S = l/(μA) corresponds to R = l/(σA), so the analogue of permeability μ is conductivity σ, and flux Φ = mmf/S corresponds to I = E/R. The other analogues: magnetic flux density B corresponds to current density J, magnetic field intensity H to electric field E, and the mmf drop in a part is Φ times its reluctance.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> μ ↔ σ.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-34", year: 2020, no: 34, subject: "circuits", topic: "Magnetising force at the centre of a coil",
q: `The magnetizing force at the centre of a circular coil varies 1. Directly as the number of its turns. 2. Directly as the current. 3. Directly as its radius. 4. Inversely as its radius.`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1 and 4", "1, 2 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1, 2 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At the centre of a circular coil H = NI/(2r).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: H ∝ N.</li><li><b>2</b> True: H ∝ I.</li><li><b>3</b> False.</li><li><b>4</b> True: H ∝ 1/r.</li></ul><p>For a solenoid the field is H = nI inside and the formula for a circular loop is H = NI/(2r); the same coil with radius r halved would give twice the field at the centre. For a long straight conductor the corresponding result is H = I/(2πr), while for a solenoid of n turns per metre it is H = nI inside, independent of the radius. The formula comes from the Biot–Savart law for a circular turn of radius r carrying I: B = μ₀I/(2r) at the centre, and N turns multiply the effect.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> H = NI/2r.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-35", year: 2020, no: 35, subject: "circuits", topic: "Capacitor charged by a current",
q: `An uncharged capacitor of 0·01 F is charged first by a current of 2 mA for 30 s and then by a current of 4 mA for 30 s. The final voltage in it will be`,
opts: ["12 V", "18 V", "24 V", "30 V"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 18 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> V = Q/C = (∫i dt)/C.</p><div class="formula">Q = Σ I Δt</div><div class="formula">V = Q/C</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Charge = 2 mA × 30 s + 4 mA × 30 s = 0.06 + 0.12 = 0.18 C.</li><li>V = 0.18/0.01 = 18 V.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 12 V would need 0.12 C (the second interval only).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 24 V would need 0.24 C.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 30 V would need 0.30 C.</li></ul><p>A constant-current charging of a capacitor gives a linear voltage rise, v = It/C, so two successive currents give a piecewise linear ramp reaching 6 V and then 18 V here. The energy stored in the capacitor finally is ½CV² = 0.5 × 0.01 × 18² = 1.62 J, supplied by the current source against the rising voltage.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> V = ∫i dt/C.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-36", year: 2020, no: 36, subject: "circuits", topic: "Field in a capacitor with reduced spacing",
q: `A capacitor of 10 pF is connected to a voltage source of 100 V. If the distance between the capacitor plates is reduced to 50%, while it remains connected to the 100 V supply, the value of potential gradient in the second case will be`,
opts: ["Half of earlier value", "Same as earlier value", "Twice of earlier value", "One-fourth of earlier value"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) twice the earlier value</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The potential gradient is E = V/d. The voltage stays 100 V, but d is halved, so E doubles. (The capacitance also doubles to 20 pF and the charge doubles.)</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Halving the spacing cannot halve the field at constant voltage.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The field is inversely proportional to d.</li><li><b>(d)</b> One-fourth would need d to be four times larger.</li></ul><p>The charge on the capacitor also doubles, since Q = CV with C doubled at the same V; the stored energy ½CV² doubles as well. With the voltage fixed by the supply, the capacitance rises to 20 pF and the charge to 2 nC, taken from the source as the plates approach.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> E = V/d at fixed V.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-37", year: 2020, no: 37, subject: "measure", topic: "Accuracy and precision",
q: `Which of the following statements are correct? 1. Accuracy is the closeness with which an instrument approaches the true value of the quantity being measured. 2. Precision is a measure of the reproducibility of the measurement. 3. Precision of an instrument can be improved upon by calibration. 4. Accuracy may be specified in terms of limits of errors.`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1, 2 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Measurement terms:</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: the definition of accuracy.</li><li><b>2</b> True: precision is repeatability.</li><li><b>3</b> False: calibration corrects systematic errors and so improves accuracy; precision (scatter) is a property of the instrument.</li><li><b>4</b> True: accuracy is quoted as ± a percentage of reading or of full scale.</li></ul><p>Good practice is to take the average of several readings (to reduce random scatter) and to calibrate against a standard (to remove systematic error), which are separate operations.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Calibration improves accuracy, not precision.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-38", year: 2020, no: 38, subject: "measure", topic: "Electrodynamometer instrument uses",
q: `An electrodynamometer instrument can be used as 1. Wattmeter and VAR meter. 2. Power factor meter and Frequency meter. 3. Transfer instrument.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The dynamometer movement responds to the product of two currents.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> Used as a wattmeter, and with a phase shift as a VAR meter.</li><li><b>2</b> Used as a power-factor meter and, with LC circuits, as a frequency meter.</li><li><b>3</b> Used as an ac/dc transfer instrument, because its calibration is the same on dc and ac.</li></ul><p>All three are correct.</p><p>A transfer instrument is one that can be calibrated on dc and used on ac, so that a laboratory standard on dc is used to calibrate ac meters; the electrodynamometer is the most common.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Dynamometer: transfer standard between dc and ac.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-39", year: 2020, no: 39, subject: "measure", topic: "Hysteresis error in moving iron instruments",
q: `The moving iron instruments when measuring voltages or currents`,
opts: ["Indicate the same values of the measurement for both ascending and descending values of current", "Indicate higher values of the measurement for ascending values of current", "Indicate higher values of the measurement for descending values of current", "Indicate lower values of the measurement for both ascending and descending values of current"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) indicate higher values for descending values of current</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The iron vane retains some magnetisation (hysteresis), so for the same current the flux, and hence the deflection, is greater on the way down than on the way up; the descending reading is higher.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Hysteresis makes the two readings differ.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The ascending reading is lower, not higher.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Both cannot be lower; the descending reading is higher.</li></ul><p>The error due to hysteresis is a few tenths of a percent and is reduced by using nickel-iron alloys of small coercivity for the vane, or by taking the mean of ascending and descending readings.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> MI hysteresis error: descending > ascending.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-40", year: 2020, no: 40, subject: "measure", topic: "True RMS voltmeter",
q: `True RMS-reading voltmeter 1. Measures the RMS value of voltage accurately. 2. Eliminates the error due to waveform. 3. Uses the thermocouple for heating.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A true-rms voltmeter responds to the heating effect of the waveform.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True.</li><li><b>2</b> True: unlike average-responding meters, it is independent of waveform shape.</li><li><b>3</b> True: a thermocouple heated by the signal gives an output proportional to V²_rms.</li></ul><p>Besides the thermocouple method, true-rms converters use thermistor bridges or computational circuits that square, average and take the square root of the signal. The thermocouple converter responds to the heating effect, so it measures true rms even for very non-sinusoidal waveforms up to high crest factors, but it can be damaged by overload.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> True rms: thermal converter, waveform independent.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-41", year: 2020, no: 41, subject: "measure", topic: "Instrument transformers",
q: `Instrument transformers are`,
opts: ["Used to extend the range of the AC measuring instruments only", "Used to isolate the measuring instruments from the high voltage only", "Used to extend the range and isolate the measuring instruments", "Not used at generating stations and transformer stations"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) used to extend the range and to isolate the measuring instruments</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> CTs and PTs reduce high currents and voltages to standard low values (5 A or 1 A, 110 V) so that standard instruments can be used, and also isolate the instruments and personnel from the high voltage.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Isolation is a second purpose.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Range extension is a second purpose.</li><li><b>(d)</b> They are used in all stations for measurement and protection.</li></ul><p>Instrument transformers have a standard secondary of 5 A (or 1 A) for current transformers and 110 V for potential transformers, so that the instruments are standard whatever the primary rating.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> CT, PT: range + isolation.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-42", year: 2020, no: 42, subject: "measure", topic: "Two-wattmeter method readings",
q: `The power in a 3-phase circuit is measured with the help of 2-wattmeters; the readings of one wattmeter is positive and that of the other is negative. The magnitude of readings is different. It can be concluded that the power factor of the circuit will be`,
opts: ["Unity", "Zero", "0·5", "Less than 0·5"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) less than 0·5</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> W₁ = VI cos(30° − φ), W₂ = VI cos(30° + φ). One reading is negative when 30° + φ &gt; 90°, i.e. φ &gt; 60°, so cos φ &lt; 0.5. If the magnitudes are unequal then φ is neither 90° (zero pf, equal and opposite readings) nor 60° (one reading zero).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> At unity pf both readings are positive and equal.</li><li><b>(b)</b> At zero pf the readings are equal and opposite.</li><li><b>(c)</b> At pf = 0.5 one wattmeter reads zero.</li></ul><p>If the power factor were exactly 0.5 one wattmeter would read zero; for a power factor below this the lower wattmeter must be reversed, and its (negative) reading is subtracted from the other.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> W₂ negative ⇔ φ > 60°.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-43", year: 2020, no: 43, subject: "measure", topic: "Distributed capacitance by a Q-meter",
q: `In a Q-meter, distributed capacitance of a coil is measured by changing the capacitance of the tuning capacitor. The values of tuning capacitor are C₁ and C₂ for resonant frequencies f₁ and 2f₁ respectively. The value of distributed capacitance will be`,
opts: ["(C₁ − C₂)/2", "(C₁ − 2C₂)/3", "(C₁ − 4C₂)/3", "(C₁ − 3C₂)/2"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) (C₁ − 4C₂)/3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Resonance requires f² ∝ 1/(C + C_d). At 2f₁: (C₁ + C_d) = 4(C₂ + C_d).</p><div class="formula">f ∝ 1/√(C + C_d)</div><div class="formula">f₂ = 2f₁ ⇒ C₁ + C_d = 4(C₂ + C_d)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>C₁ + C_d = 4C₂ + 4C_d → 3C_d = C₁ − 4C₂.</li><li>C_d = (C₁ − 4C₂)/3.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> This would result if f₂ = √2 f₁ … (C₁ + C_d) = 2(C₂ + C_d) gives C₁ − 2C₂, and the denominator would be 1.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Ratio factor 3 in the denominator needs a factor 4 in the numerator.</li><li><b>(d)</b> (C₁ − 3C₂)/2 does not come from C₁ + C_d = 4(C₂ + C_d).</li></ul><p>The Q-meter works by resonating the coil with a variable calibrated capacitor, and the Q is read as the ratio of the voltage across the capacitor to the injected voltage at resonance.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> (C₁ + C_d) = (f₂/f₁)²(C₂ + C_d).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-44", year: 2020, no: 44, subject: "measure", topic: "Auto-zeroing in a digital voltmeter",
q: `In a digital voltmeter, during start of conversion, zero indication is displayed and is called auto zeroing. This is achieved by`,
opts: ["Using a positive reference voltage", "Using a negative reference voltage", "Properly charging the differentiator circuit capacitance to ground", "Properly discharging the integrator circuit capacitance to ground"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) properly discharging the integrator capacitor to ground</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In an integrating DVM the capacitor of the integrator must start each conversion at zero charge, so it is discharged (shorted) to ground at the start. The offset of the amplifier is also stored during this phase, which gives the zero reading.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The reference voltage is used for the measurement, not for zeroing.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Same: the reference does not zero the display.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A differentiator is not part of the dual-slope DVM.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Auto-zero: reset the integrator.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-45", year: 2020, no: 45, subject: "measure", topic: "CRT deflection sensitivity",
q: `A CRT has an anode voltage of 2000 V and parallel deflecting plates 2 cm long and 5 mm apart. The screen is 30 cm from the centre of the plates. If the input voltage is applied to the deflecting plates through amplifiers having an overall gain of 100, the input voltage required to deflect the beam through 3 cm will be`,
opts: ["1 V", "3 V", "5 V", "7 V"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Electrostatic deflection on the screen: D = lLV_d/(2dV_a).</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>D/V_d = (2 cm × 30 cm)/(2 × 0.5 cm × 2000 V) = 60/2000 = 0.03 cm/V.</li><li>For 3 cm: V_d = 3/0.03 = 100 V across the plates.</li><li>With an amplifier gain of 100: input = 100/100 = 1 V.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 3 V would need a plate voltage of 300 V.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 5 V would need 500 V.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 7 V would need 700 V.</li></ul><p>The deflection sensitivity of the CRT, 0.03 cm/V here, is inversely proportional to the anode voltage, so a higher accelerating voltage gives a brighter but less sensitive trace.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> D = lLV_d/(2dV_a).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-46", year: 2020, no: 46, subject: "measure", topic: "Aquadag",
q: `An aquadag is used in a CRO to collect`,
opts: ["Primary electrons only", "Secondary emission electrons only", "Both primary electrons and secondary emission electrons", "Heat emission electrons"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) secondary emission electrons</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Aquadag is a graphite coating on the inside of the CRT bulb, connected to the final anode. It collects the secondary electrons emitted by the screen when the beam strikes it, and so keeps the screen from charging negatively.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The primary beam hits the screen.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Primary electrons are not collected by it.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Thermionic emission occurs at the cathode.</li></ul><p>The aquadag coating is held at the second anode potential, which lets the secondary electrons from the screen flow back to the anode circuit and prevents a negative charge from building on the screen.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Aquadag: collects secondary electrons.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-47", year: 2020, no: 47, subject: "measure", topic: "Strain gauge resistance change",
q: `A resistance wire strain gauge with a gauge factor of 2 is bonded to a steel structural member subjected to a stress of 100 MN/m². The modulus of elasticity of steel is 200 GN/m². The percentage change in the value of the gauge resistance due to the applied stress will be`,
opts: ["0·1%", "0·3%", "0·5%", "0·7%"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·1%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Strain = σ/E = 100 × 10⁶/200 × 10⁹ = 5 × 10⁻⁴. ΔR/R = G ε = 2 × 5 × 10⁻⁴ = 0.1 %.</p><div class="formula">ε = σ/E</div><div class="formula">ΔR/R = Gε</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ε = 100 × 10⁶/200 × 10⁹ = 5 × 10⁻⁴.</li><li>ΔR/R = 2 × 5 × 10⁻⁴ = 10⁻³ = <b>0.1 %</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0.3 % would need a gauge factor of 6.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.5 % would need a gauge factor of 10.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.7 % would need a gauge factor of 14.</li></ul><p>A typical foil gauge has a resistance of 120 Ω, so this strain would change it by only 0.12 Ω, which is why a bridge with an amplifier is used to read it. Because strain gauges respond to temperature as well as strain, a dummy gauge on an unstrained piece of the same material is placed in the adjacent bridge arm to cancel it.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> ΔR/R = G σ/E.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-48", year: 2020, no: 48, subject: "measure", topic: "Capacitive level transducer",
q: `Capacitive transducers can be used for the measurement of liquid level. The principle of operation used in this case is the change of capacitance with change of`,
opts: ["Distance between plates", "Area of plates", "Dielectric", "Resonance"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) dielectric</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a level sensor the probe and tank wall form a capacitor. As the liquid (permittivity higher than air) rises, the proportion of the dielectric between the electrodes changes, so the capacitance changes linearly with the level.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The plate spacing is fixed.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The plate area is fixed.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Resonance is not the principle.</li></ul><p>Capacitive level gauges are used for non-conductive and conductive liquids; for conductive liquids the probe is insulated, and the liquid then acts as one plate.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Level: C changes as dielectric fills the gap.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-49", year: 2020, no: 49, subject: "computer", topic: "Binary to hexadecimal",
q: `The hexadecimal of the binary number (11010011)₂ is`,
opts: ["D3₁₆", "D4₁₆", "C3₁₆", "C4₁₆"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) D3₁₆</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Group in fours: 1101 0011 = D (13) and 3, so D3₁₆.</p><div class="formula">Group bits in fours from the point; each group = one hex digit</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>1101 = 13 = D; 0011 = 3.</li><li>(11010011)₂ = <b>D3₁₆</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0100 would be the last nibble of D4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1100 is C, but the first nibble is 1101.</li><li><b>(d)</b> C4 has both nibbles wrong.</li></ul><p>Hexadecimal is used in computing because each digit corresponds to exactly four bits: D = 1101 and 3 = 0011, so a byte is always two hex digits. Converting back, D3₁₆ = 13 × 16 + 3 = 211 and 11010011₂ = 128 + 64 + 16 + 2 + 1 = 211, which confirms the conversion. In octal the same number is 323, obtained by grouping the bits in threes: 11 010 011 = 3, 2, 3.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> 4 bits = 1 hex digit.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-50", year: 2020, no: 50, subject: "computer", topic: "Boolean AND relations",
q: `Which one of the following relations from the Boolean algebra pertaining to 'AND' operation cannot be verified when A and B can take on only the value 0 or 1?`,
opts: ["AB = BA", "AA = A", "A1 = 1", "A0 = 0"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) A·1 = 1</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In Boolean algebra A·1 = A, not 1: when A = 0, A·1 = 0. So the relation "A1 = 1" is false for A = 0 and cannot be verified.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Commutativity holds.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Idempotence holds.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Anything ANDed with 0 is 0.</li></ul><p>The other AND identities are A·A̅ = 0 and A·(B + C) = AB + AC; testing each relation for A = 0 and A = 1 is the way to verify a law by exhaustive substitution. The result A·1 = A is the identity (neutral) element law of AND, while A + 1 = 1 is the null element law of OR; students often confuse the two.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> A·1 = A; A·0 = 0.</p>` }
);

