// ESE 2020 Prelims Paper II, Electrical Engineering, Series A (KJL-S-ETE), Q76–100
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; none dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2020-76", year: 2020, no: 76, subject: "ad", topic: "Active filters",
q: `Which one of the following statements is not correct for an active filter used in the field of communications and signal processing?`,
opts: ["It is more economical.", "It does not cause loading of the source or load.", "It is easier to tune or adjust.", "It exhibits insertion loss."], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) it exhibits insertion loss</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Active filters use op-amps, so they can have gain instead of loss; they are cheaper than inductor-based passive filters at low frequencies, isolate the source and the load, and are easy to tune. Insertion loss is a characteristic of passive filters.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> True: they avoid costly inductors.</li><li><b>(b)</b> True: the op-amp buffers the stages.</li><li><b>(c)</b> True: resistors can be trimmed.</li></ul><p>Active filters can provide gain and have a high input and a low output impedance, and since they use no inductors they are suitable for low frequencies and for integration on a chip.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Active filters: gain, no inductors, buffered.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-77", year: 2020, no: 77, subject: "ad", topic: "Diffusion in semiconductor fabrication",
q: `A two-step procedure in a typical diffusion apparatus to obtain the complementary-error-function Gaussian distribution involves the first step and second step respectively as`,
opts: ["Predeposition and Drive-in", "Predeposition and Drive-out", "Drive-in and Postdeposition", "Drive-out and Postdeposition"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) predeposition and drive-in</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Doping by diffusion is done in two steps. In <b>predeposition</b>, dopant atoms are introduced into a thin surface layer from a constant source, giving a complementary-error-function profile. In the <b>drive-in</b>, the source is removed and the wafer heated so the dopant diffuses deeper; the profile becomes Gaussian.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> "Drive-out" is not the standard second step.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Drive-in is the second step, not the first.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Neither term is the standard sequence.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Predeposition (erfc) then drive-in (Gaussian).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-78", year: 2020, no: 78, subject: "ad", topic: "Efficiency of AM",
q: `In AM modulation, the equation of the modulating signal is given by f(t) = A_m cos ω_m t. If the amplitude of the carrier wave is A and there is no over-modulation, the modulation efficiency will be`,
opts: ["33·3%", "38·6%", "43·3%", "48·6%"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 33·3%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Efficiency η = m²/(2 + m²). With no over-modulation the largest modulation index is m = A_m/A = 1.</p><div class="formula">η = m²/(2 + m²)</div><div class="formula">No over-modulation ⇒ m ≤ 1</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>η = 1/3 = 33.3 %.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 38.6 % would need m = 1.12 (over-modulation).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 43.3 % would need m = 1.24 (over-modulation).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 48.6 % would need m = 1.37 (over-modulation).</li></ul><p>If the modulation index were above 1 the envelope would be distorted (over-modulation), so the largest undistorted efficiency of 33 % is a property of the tone-modulated AM wave. A higher modulation index also means a larger side-band power, so the transmission efficiency rises with m, from 11 % at m = 0.5 to 33 % at m = 1 and no further.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Maximum η = 33.3 % at m = 1.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-79", year: 2020, no: 79, subject: "ad", topic: "BPSK sidebands",
q: `For a binary phase-shift keying modulator with a carrier frequency of 70 MHz and input bit rate of 10 Mbps, the maximum Upper Side Frequency (USF) and minimum Lower Side Frequency (LSF) are respectively`,
opts: ["85 MHz and 65 MHz", "75 MHz and 65 MHz", "55 MHz and 45 MHz", "75 MHz and 45 MHz"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 75 MHz and 65 MHz</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For BPSK the highest fundamental frequency of the bit stream is f_b/2 = 5 MHz. The output spectrum then spans f_c ± f_b/2.</p><div class="formula">BPSK spectrum: f_c ± f_b/2</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>USF = 70 + 5 = 75 MHz; LSF = 70 − 5 = 65 MHz.</li><li>The minimum bandwidth is f_b = 10 MHz.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 85 MHz would need a 15 MHz offset.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 55 and 45 MHz are not centred on 70 MHz.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 45 MHz would need a 25 MHz offset.</li></ul><p>The BPSK bandwidth is twice the highest fundamental frequency of the bit stream, equal to the bit rate, so the 10 Mbps stream needs 10 MHz centred at 70 MHz.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> BPSK: f_c ± f_b/2.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-80", year: 2020, no: 80, subject: "ad", topic: "Eb/No",
q: `In modulation system, the energy per bit-to-noise power density ratio E_b/N₀ is (N = noise power, B = bandwidth, C = carrier power, f_b = bit rate)`,
opts: ["(C/N) × (f_b/B)", "(N/C) × (B/f_b)", "(C/N) × (B/f_b)", "(N/C) × (f_b/B)"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) (C/N) × (B/f_b)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> E_b = C/f_b (carrier power per bit) and N₀ = N/B. So E_b/N₀ = (C/f_b)(B/N) = (C/N)(B/f_b).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The bandwidth and bit rate are interchanged.</li><li><b>(b)</b> This is the inverse of the signal-to-noise form.</li><li><b>(d)</b> This is also inverted.</li></ul><p>The ratio E_b/N₀ is the key figure in comparing digital modulation schemes, because the bit error rate depends on it, independent of the data rate and bandwidth chosen. The product (C/N)(B/f_b) shows that for a given carrier-to-noise ratio the bit-energy to noise density ratio rises as the bandwidth is widened relative to the bit rate.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> E_b/N₀ = (C/N)(B/f_b).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-81", year: 2020, no: 81, subject: "ad", topic: "Private line parameters",
q: `Which one of the following is not a transmission parameter of a private line data circuit that utilizes public telephone network?`,
opts: ["Geographical parameter", "Bandwidth parameter", "Interface parameter", "Facility parameter"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) geographical parameter (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The standard parameter classes for data circuits on telephone lines are bandwidth (frequency response), interface (connection standards) and facility (line type, conditioning). "Geographical parameter" is not one of them.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Bandwidth parameters are one of the classes.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Interface parameters are one of the classes.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Facility parameters are one of the classes.</li></ul><p>A private line circuit leased from the telephone company is specified by its bandwidth (frequency response, distortion), by the interface to the terminal equipment and by the facility (two-wire or four-wire).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Parameters: bandwidth, interface, facility.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-82", year: 2020, no: 82, subject: "ad", topic: "Shannon's capacity",
q: `The Shannon limit for information capacity I is (N = noise power, B = bandwidth, S = signal power)`,
opts: ["B log₂(1 − S/N)", "B log₂(1 + S/N)", "B log₁₀(1 − S/N)", "B log₁₀(1 + S/N)"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) B log₂(1 + S/N)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Shannon–Hartley: C = B log₂(1 + S/N) bits per second, the maximum error-free rate of a channel of bandwidth B with signal-to-noise ratio S/N.</p><div class="formula">C = B log₂(1 + S/N)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Shannon–Hartley theorem: base-2 log, (1 + S/N).</li><li>Option (b).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The minus sign would make the logarithm negative.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The base is 2 to get bits.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The base is 2, giving bits per second.</li></ul><p>The capacity increases with bandwidth and with SNR; for a 3 kHz telephone channel at 30 dB SNR the Shannon limit is about 30 kbps. Doubling the bandwidth doubles the capacity, but doubling the signal power adds only B bits per second, since the capacity grows logarithmically with the SNR.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> C = B log₂(1 + SNR).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-83", year: 2020, no: 83, subject: "ad", topic: "Line speed in TDM",
q: `In a time division multiplexing, there are 8000 samples for a digital signal-0 channel that uses 8 kHz sample rate and 8 bit PCM code. The line speed will be`,
opts: ["56 kbps", "64 kbps", "76 kbps", "84 kbps"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 64 kbps</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Line speed = samples per second × bits per sample = 8000 × 8 = 64 000 bits/s = 64 kbps (the standard DS-0 channel).</p><div class="formula">Line rate = f_s × n</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>8000 samples/s × 8 bits = 64 000 bit/s = <b>64 kbps</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 56 kbps would use 7 bits.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 76 kbps would need 9.5 bits per sample.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 84 kbps would need 10.5 bits per sample.</li></ul><p>The 24 DS-0 channels are combined in the T1 line, adding 8 kbps of framing, to give 1.544 Mbps. A DS-1 (T1) frame holds 24 such channels of 8 bits plus a framing bit, 193 bits every 125 μs, which gives 1.544 Mbps. The bit rate also fixes the bandwidth required: a baseband PCM channel needs at least half the bit rate, 32 kHz.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> DS-0 = 8000 × 8 = 64 kbps.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-84", year: 2020, no: 84, subject: "ad", topic: "Baud rate of 8-PSK",
q: `For an 8-PSK system, operating with an information bit rate of 24 kbps, the baud rate will be`,
opts: ["16,000", "12,000", "8,000", "6,000"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 8,000</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> 8-PSK has 8 = 2³ phase states, so each symbol carries 3 bits. Baud = bit rate/bits per symbol = 24 000/3 = 8000 baud.</p><div class="formula">Bits/symbol = log₂M</div><div class="formula">Baud = R_b/log₂M</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>log₂8 = 3.</li><li>Baud = 24 000/3 = <b>8000</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 16 000 would use 1.5 bits per symbol.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 12 000 would use 2 bits per symbol (QPSK).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 6000 would use 4 bits per symbol (16-PSK).</li></ul><p>For M-ary PSK the baud rate is f_b/log₂M and the minimum bandwidth equals the baud rate, so 8-PSK needs one-third of the bandwidth of BPSK for the same bit rate. The symbol alphabet of 8 phases (45° apart) is also used with amplitude changes in 8-QAM, which gives a different spectral efficiency.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> baud = f_b/log₂M.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-85", year: 2020, no: 85, subject: "signals", topic: "Time shift",
q: `During transformation of independent variable, if two signals identical in shape are displaced relative to each other, then the difference in propagation time from point of origin of transmitted signal results in`,
opts: ["Time shift", "Time reversal", "Time scaling", "Time reduction"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) time shift</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A displacement of a signal along the time axis, x(t − t₀), is a time shift. It arises, for example, when the same signal travels different distances.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Time reversal is x(−t).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Time scaling is x(at).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Not a standard transformation.</li></ul><p>For a transmitted pulse that reaches two receivers at different distances the received signals are time-shifted copies of each other, with the shift equal to the difference in propagation delay. An example in radar is the echo from a target at distance d, which arrives 2d/c seconds after the transmitted pulse: a pure time shift of the same pulse shape.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Time shift: x(t − t₀).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-86", year: 2020, no: 86, subject: "signals", topic: "System definitions",
q: `Which of the following statements is/are correct? 1. A continuous-time system is a system in which continuous-time input signals are applied, resulting in continuous-time output signals. 2. A system is said to be linear if it follows the superposition theorem. 3. A system is said to be non-linear if it follows the superposition theorem.`,
opts: ["1 only", "1 and 2 only", "2 only", "1 and 3 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> 1 and 2 are the standard definitions. Statement 3 is false: a system that obeys superposition (additivity and homogeneity) is linear; non-linear systems violate it.</p><p>The system is continuous-time because the input and output are defined for all t; a discrete-time system has sequences as input and output. Systems may be further classified as linear or non-linear, time-invariant or time-varying, causal or non-causal, and stable or unstable, each by a defining test.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Linear ⇔ superposition holds.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-87", year: 2020, no: 87, subject: "signals", topic: "Unit sample sequence",
q: `A discrete time signal is said to be a unit sample sequence if`,
opts: ["δ(n) = 1 for n = 0 and 0 for n ≠ 0", "δ(n) = 2 for n = 0 and 0 for n ≠ 0", "δ(n) = −1 for n = 0 and 0 for n ≠ 0", "δ(n) = −2 for n = 0 and 0 for n ≠ 0"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) δ(n) = 1 for n = 0, 0 otherwise</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The unit sample (unit impulse) sequence has amplitude one at n = 0 and is zero elsewhere; any sequence can be written as Σ x(k)δ(n − k).</p><div class="formula">δ(n) = 1 (n = 0), 0 (n ≠ 0)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Unit sample has amplitude 1 at n = 0 only ⇒ option (a).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> A value of 2 at n = 0 gives 2δ(n), a scaled impulse.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A value of −1 gives −δ(n), an inverted impulse.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A value of −2 gives −2δ(n).</li></ul><p>Any discrete-time signal can be represented as a weighted sum of shifted unit samples, which leads to the convolution sum for LTI systems. The shifted impulse δ(n − k) places the unit sample at n = k, and an arbitrary sequence is the weighted sum of such shifted samples, which is the basis for the convolution sum.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> δ[n]: single unit sample at n = 0.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-88", year: 2020, no: 88, subject: "signals", topic: "Deterministic and random signals",
q: `A signal is said to be 1. Deterministic if there is no uncertainty over the signal at any instant of time. 2. Deterministic if it is expressible through a mathematical equation. 3. Random or non-deterministic if there is uncertainty over the signal at the instant of time. 4. Random or non-deterministic if it is not expressible through a mathematical equation.`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "3 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> All four statements are standard definitions: a deterministic signal has completely predictable values and can be described by a formula; a random signal has uncertainty at each instant and cannot be described exactly by an equation (noise, speech).</p><p>Examples: a sine wave and a square pulse are deterministic; thermal noise and a speech signal are random and are described by statistical properties such as mean and variance.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Deterministic: predictable; random: statistical description only.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-89", year: 2020, no: 89, subject: "signals", topic: "Frequency-selective filters",
q: `Linear time-invariant systems that are designed to pass some frequencies essentially undistorted and significantly attenuate or eliminate others are`,
opts: ["Frequency-shaping filters", "Frequency-selective filters", "Time-shaping filters", "Time-selective filters"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) frequency-selective filters</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Filters that pass certain bands and reject others (low-pass, high-pass, band-pass, band-stop) are frequency-selective filters. Frequency-shaping filters modify the spectrum smoothly (e.g. equalisers or differentiators) instead of selecting bands.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Shaping filters alter the spectrum gradually.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Not a standard term.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Not a standard term.</li></ul><p>Ideal filters have a sharp cut-off and no phase distortion, which cannot be realised; practical filters (Butterworth, Chebyshev) approximate them.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Frequency-selective: pass band, stop band.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-90", year: 2020, no: 90, subject: "signals", topic: "Convolution of an exponential and a delayed step",
q: `If the input signal x(t) and impulse response h(t) of a continuous-time system are described as x(t) = e⁻³ᵗu(t) and h(t) = u(t − 1), the output y(t) will be`,
opts: ["(1/3)[1 − e^{−3(t−1)}]", "(1/3)[1 − e⁻³ᵗ]", "(1/3)[1 + e^{−3(t−1)}]", "(1/3)[1 + e⁻³ᵗ]"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) (1/3)[1 − e^{−3(t−1)}] (for t &gt; 1)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> y(t) = ∫x(τ)h(t − τ)dτ = ∫e^{−3τ}u(τ)u(t − τ − 1)dτ. The integrand is non-zero for 0 &lt; τ &lt; t − 1 (t &gt; 1).</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>y = ∫₀^{t−1}e^{−3τ}dτ = (1/3)[1 − e^{−3(t−1)}] for t &gt; 1, and 0 for t &lt; 1.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The delay of 1 s in h must appear in the exponent.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The exponential decays, so the plus sign is wrong.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Neither the delay nor the sign is right.</li></ul><p>The delay in h(t) simply delays the output by one second, so y(t) = y₀(t − 1) where y₀ is the response with h(t) = u(t); that is why the exponent is 3(t − 1).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Convolution limits: 0 < τ < t − 1.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-91", year: 2020, no: 91, subject: "signals", topic: "Impulse response from input and output",
q: `Consider an LTI system whose response to the input x(t) = [e⁻ᵗ + e⁻³ᵗ]u(t) is y(t) = [2e⁻ᵗ − 2e⁻⁴ᵗ]u(t). The system's impulse response will be`,
opts: ["(3/2)[e⁻²ᵗ + e⁻⁴ᵗ]u(t)", "(3/2)[e⁻²ᵗ − e⁻⁴ᵗ]u(t)", "(1/2)[e⁻²ᵗ + e⁻⁴ᵗ]u(t)", "(1/2)[e⁻²ᵗ − e⁻⁴ᵗ]u(t)"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) (3/2)[e⁻²ᵗ + e⁻⁴ᵗ]u(t)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> X(s) = 1/(s + 1) + 1/(s + 3) = (2s + 4)/((s + 1)(s + 3)). Y(s) = 2/(s + 1) − 2/(s + 4) = 6/((s + 1)(s + 4)).</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>H = Y/X = 6(s + 3)/[(2s + 4)(s + 4)] = 3(s + 3)/[(s + 2)(s + 4)].</li><li>Partial fractions: 3(s + 3)/((s + 2)(s + 4)) = (3/2)/(s + 2) + (3/2)/(s + 4).</li><li>h(t) = (3/2)[e⁻²ᵗ + e⁻⁴ᵗ]u(t).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The two residues are both +3/2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The factor is 3/2, not 1/2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The factor is 3/2 and the sign between the terms is +.</li></ul><p>The poles of H(s) are at −2 and −4, so the system is stable; the response to an impulse decays as e⁻²ᵗ and e⁻⁴ᵗ.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> H(s) = Y(s)/X(s), then partial fractions.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-92", year: 2020, no: 92, subject: "signals", topic: "Difference equation from a system function",
q: `Consider an LTI system with a system function H(z) = 1/(1 − (1/4)z⁻¹). Its difference equation will be`,
opts: ["y(n) − (1/2)y(n − 1) = x(n)", "y(n) − (1/4)y(n − 1) = x(n)", "y(n) + (1/2)y(n − 1) = x(n)", "y(n) − (1/4)y(n + 1) = x(n)"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) y(n) − (1/4) y(n − 1) = x(n)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> H(z) = Y/X = 1/(1 − ¼z⁻¹) → Y(1 − ¼z⁻¹) = X → y(n) − ¼y(n − 1) = x(n).</p><div class="formula">H(z) = Y(z)/X(z)</div><div class="formula">z⁻¹Y(z) ↔ y(n − 1)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Y(z)(1 − ¼z⁻¹) = X(z).</li><li>Inverse z: <b>y(n) − ¼y(n − 1) = x(n)</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The coefficient must be 1/4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The sign and coefficient are wrong.</li><li><b>(d)</b> y(n + 1) would be non-causal.</li></ul><p>The pole at z = 1/4 lies inside the unit circle, so the causal system is stable, with the impulse response h(n) = (1/4)ⁿu(n). The system is a recursive first-order IIR filter with one feedback tap of 1/4; its impulse response h(n) = (1/4)ⁿu(n) decays geometrically and so never becomes exactly zero.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> z⁻¹ ↔ one-sample delay.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-93", year: 2020, no: 93, subject: "signals", topic: "Quantisation error model",
q: `It is assumed that quantization error, e(n) is a sequence of random variables where 1. The statistics do not change with time. 2. It is a sequence of uncorrelated random variables. 3. It is uncorrelated with the quantizer input x(n). 4. The probability density function is uniformly distributed over the range of values of quantization error.`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "3 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The standard statistical model of quantisation noise assumes e(n) is stationary white noise (1, 2), uncorrelated with the input (3), and uniformly distributed over one quantisation step (4), giving a variance Δ²/12.</p><p>The variance of the uniform quantisation error is Δ²/12 with Δ the step size, so the signal-to-noise ratio improves by 6 dB for each added bit. Because the noise variance depends only on the step size, doubling the number of levels halves Δ and cuts the noise power by a factor of four, i.e. 6 dB, the well-known rule.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Quantisation noise: stationary, white, uniform, uncorrelated with input.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-94", year: 2020, no: 94, subject: "signals", topic: "Step response of a second-order system",
q: `For a given differential equation d²y/dt² + 4dy/dt + 5y = 5x with y(0⁻) = 1 and dy/dt(0⁻) = 2 and input x(t) = u(t). The output y(t) will be`,
opts: ["2u(t) − 2e⁻²ᵗ sin t", "u(t) + 2e⁻²ᵗ sin t", "u(t) − e⁻ᵗ sin t", "2u(t) + e⁻ᵗ sin t"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) u(t) + 2e⁻²ᵗ sin t</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The characteristic roots are −2 ± j1. The forced response to a unit step is y_f = 1 (since 5y = 5). So y = 1 + e⁻²ᵗ(A cos t + B sin t) for t ≥ 0.</p><div class="formula">Characteristic: s² + 4s + 5 = 0 ⇒ s = −2 ± j</div><div class="formula">y = y_forced + e^(−2t)(A cos t + B sin t)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>y(0) = 1 → A = 0.</li><li>y′(0) = −2A + B = 2 → B = 2.</li><li>y(t) = u(t) + 2e⁻²ᵗ sin t.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The forced part is 1, not 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The decay rate is e^{−2t}, set by the real part of the roots.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The forced part is 1 and the decay is e^{−2t}.</li></ul><p>The forced response is the particular solution 1 for the unit step input and the natural response is the decaying oscillation at 1 rad/s with the damping e^{−2t}.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Forced + natural response; initial conditions fix A, B.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-95", year: 2020, no: 95, subject: "signals", topic: "Nyquist rate of a product signal",
q: `The Nyquist rate for the signal x(t) = (1/2π)cos(4000πt)cos(1000πt) will be`,
opts: ["5 kHz", "10 kHz", "15 kHz", "20 kHz"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 5 kHz</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Product of cosines: cos A cos B = ½[cos(A + B) + cos(A − B)]. The frequencies are (4000π ± 1000π)/2π = 2500 Hz and 1500 Hz.</p><div class="formula">cos A cos B = ½[cos(A + B) + cos(A − B)]</div><div class="formula">f_N = 2f_max</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Highest frequency = 2.5 kHz.</li><li>Nyquist rate = 2 × 2.5 = 5 kHz.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 10 kHz would correspond to a 5 kHz highest frequency.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 15 kHz would correspond to 7.5 kHz.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 20 kHz would correspond to 10 kHz.</li></ul><p>The frequencies 2.5 kHz and 1.5 kHz are the sum and difference of the two cosine frequencies; sampling at 5 kHz is just sufficient for the highest.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Use the product-to-sum identity first.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-96", year: 2020, no: 96, subject: "signals", topic: "FIR and recursive systems",
q: `Which of the following statements is/are correct? 1. A system is said to be Finite Impulse Response (FIR), if the output samples of the system depend only on the present input and a finite number of past or previous input samples. 2. If the output of a system y(n) depends only on the present input and past inputs, but not on past outputs, then it is called a non-recursive system. 3. If the output of a system y(n) depends only on the present input and past inputs, but not on past outputs, then it is called a recursive system.`,
opts: ["1 only", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "3 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> FIR systems depend on a finite set of inputs; a system without feedback of past outputs is non-recursive, while a recursive system uses past outputs (feedback). Statement 3 mixes the two up.</p><p>A moving-average filter is a simple example of an FIR (non-recursive) system, and a first-order low-pass filter with feedback of y(n − 1) is a recursive (IIR) system. Linear-phase FIR filters are possible only with symmetric coefficients; recursive IIR filters give sharper cut-offs for the same number of coefficients but cannot have exactly linear phase.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Non-recursive: no output feedback; recursive: feedback.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-97", year: 2020, no: 97, subject: "control", topic: "Ramp error of a first-order system",
q: `The value of the steady state error for first order system 1/(sT + 1) with Unit Ramp Function will be`,
opts: ["1/T", "T", "T(1 − e^{−t/T})", "(1/T)e^{−t/T}"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) T</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For the ramp input r(t) = t the output is c(t) = t − T + Te^{−t/T}. The error e = r − c = T(1 − e^{−t/T}) → T as t → ∞.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1/T has the wrong dimension for an error in the output units.</li><li><b>(c)</b> T(1 − e^{−t/T}) is the error as a function of time, before it reaches steady state.</li><li><b>(d)</b> This decays to zero and is not an error expression.</li></ul><p>For a unit step the error of the same system falls to zero; the ramp error T equals the time constant, which is the lag of the output behind the input.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> First-order ramp lag: steady error = T.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-98", year: 2020, no: 98, subject: "control", topic: "Zeros at infinity",
q: `If the number of zeros are less than the number of poles, i.e. Z &lt; P, then the value of the transfer function becomes zero for s → ∞. Hence we say that there are zeros at infinity and the order of such zeros is`,
opts: ["P + Z", "P − Z", "Z − P", "Z"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) P − Z</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The transfer function behaves as s^{−(P − Z)} at large s, so there are P − Z zeros at infinity.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> P + Z is not the pole–zero excess.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Z − P is negative here.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Z is the number of finite zeros.</li></ul><p>The same count gives the high-frequency slope of the Bode plot, −20(P − Z) dB/decade, as well as the phase −90°(P − Z). For a function such as 1/(s + 1)³ there are three zeros at infinity, each reached as s increases without bound; including them, the number of zeros always equals the number of poles.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Zeros at ∞ = P − Z.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-99", year: 2020, no: 99, subject: "control", topic: "Nyquist method", doubt: true,
q: `The method for determination of the stability of the feedback systems as a function of an adjustable gain parameter which does not provide detailed information concerning location of closed-loop poles as a function of gain K is called`,
opts: ["Root locus method", "Nyquist criterion method", "Bode plot method", "Routh-Hurwitz criterion method"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Nyquist criterion method (as keyed)</h3><p class="note">In doubt (key followed). Routh–Hurwitz also gives stability against K without pole locations, so (d) was our answer. The key's (b) Nyquist is shown.</p><p><b>Concept:</b> The Nyquist criterion decides stability for the full range of gain K by counting encirclements of −1 by the frequency response, but gives no information on the actual positions of the closed-loop poles. The root locus, in contrast, shows the poles as K varies.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The root locus does show the pole locations.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The Bode plot gives margins, not stability versus K directly.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Routh–Hurwitz also does not show the poles and was our choice; it tests a polynomial for given numerical coefficients.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Nyquist: stability from the frequency response; root locus: pole positions.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-100", year: 2020, no: 100, subject: "control", topic: "Asymptotic Bode magnitude of a first-order factor",
q: `Consider the sinusoidal transfer function in time-constant form G(jω) = 2(1 + jω)/(1 + jω/10)². Asymptotic log magnitude characteristic of factor (1 + jω) is straight line of 1. 0 dB for ω ≤ 1. 2. +20 dB/decade for ω ≥ 1. 3. −20 dB/decade for ω ≥ 1.`,
opts: ["1 only", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For the zero factor (1 + jω) with corner frequency 1 rad/s the Bode asymptotes are 0 dB below the corner and a rising line of +20 dB/decade above it (a pole would give −20 dB/decade).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True.</li><li><b>2</b> True.</li><li><b>3</b> False: −20 dB/decade is for a pole.</li></ul><p>Complete the factor chain: the double pole at ω = 10 reduces the slope by 40 dB/decade above 10 rad/s, giving a net −20 dB/decade after the zero at 1 rad/s. The corner frequency of the factor (1 + jω) is 1 rad/s where the exact magnitude is 3 dB above the asymptote, the largest departure between the exact curve and its straight-line approximation.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Zero: +20 dB/dec above corner.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2020e.js ----
// ESE 2020 Prelims Paper II, Electrical Engineering, Series A (KJL-S-ETE), Q101–125
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; none dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2020-101", year: 2020, no: 101, subject: "control", topic: "Root locus method",
q: `A graphical technique for plotting the closed-loop poles of a rational system functions as a function of the value of gain for both continuous-time and a discrete-time system is`,
opts: ["Root locus method", "Nyquist criterion method", "Bode plot method", "Routh-Hurwitz criterion method"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) root locus method</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The root locus plots the closed-loop pole positions in the s-plane (or z-plane) as the gain varies from 0 to ∞.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Nyquist uses the open-loop frequency response.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Bode plots gain and phase against frequency.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Routh–Hurwitz is an algebraic test.</li></ul><p>Root locus rules include the number of branches (equal to the number of poles), the real axis segments, the asymptotes and the break-away points. For discrete-time systems the same locus is drawn in the z-plane, and stability requires the closed-loop poles to remain inside the unit circle instead of the left half plane.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Root locus: poles vs gain.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-102", year: 2020, no: 102, subject: "control", topic: "Effect of poles and zeros on the root locus",
q: `Which one of the following statements regarding 'Root locus' is not correct?`,
opts: ["By addition of poles to left half, the root locus shifts towards right hand side and stability of system decreases.", "By addition of zero towards left, the root locus shifts towards left half, since root locus shifts towards left half, the relative stability of control system increases.", "By addition of zero towards left side, the root locus shifts towards left half, the relative stability remains same.", "By addition of poles to the left half, the system stability decreases, while by addition of zeros towards left half, the stability of system increases."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) (c) is not correct</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Adding a pole in the left half plane bends the locus to the right, which reduces stability. Adding a zero in the left half plane pulls the locus to the left and <b>increases</b> the relative stability.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Incorrect: the relative stability increases, not stays the same. This is the answer.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Correct.</li></ul><p>The statements follow from the rule that the root locus is attracted by zeros and repelled by poles, so the relative stability changes accordingly.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Pole: locus right (less stable); zero: locus left (more stable).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-103", year: 2020, no: 103, subject: "control", topic: "Relative stability in the frequency domain",
q: `In time domain, the relative stability is measured by maximum overshoot and damping ratio. In frequency domain, the relative stability is measured by`,
opts: ["Steady state error", "Damping ratio", "Resonant peak", "Bandwidth"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) resonant peak</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the frequency domain the relative stability is shown by the resonant peak M_r (a large peak means a lightly damped system), and by the gain and phase margins.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Steady-state error measures accuracy.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The damping ratio is a time-domain measure.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Bandwidth measures speed of response.</li></ul><p>The resonant peak M_r is related to the damping ratio by M_r = 1/(2ζ√(1 − ζ²)) for a second-order system, so a smaller peak means more damping. Other frequency-domain measures of relative stability are the gain margin and the phase margin, and the peak frequency ω_r = ω_n√(1 − 2ζ²) of the closed-loop response.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> M_r large ⇒ poorly damped.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-104", year: 2020, no: 104, subject: "control", topic: "Asymptotes of a root locus",
q: `Consider a feedback system with the characteristic equation 1 + K/(s(s + 1)(s + 2)) = 0 for root locus. The angles of asymptotes φ_A and the centroid of the asymptotes −σ_A are respectively`,
opts: ["60°, 120°, 180° and −1", "45°, 90°, 300° and 0", "60°, 180°, 300° and −1", "45°, 90°, 180° and 0"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 60°, 180°, 300° and −1</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> There are P = 3 poles (0, −1, −2) and no zeros, so 3 asymptotes at angles (2k + 1)180°/3 = 60°, 180°, 300°.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Centroid = (Σpoles − Σzeros)/(P − Z) = (0 − 1 − 2)/3 = −1.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 120° is not an asymptote angle for P − Z = 3.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The angles are not multiples of 45°.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Same objection, and the centroid is −1, not 0.</li></ul><p>The asymptotes meet at the centroid σ = −1 and the break-away point lies at s = −0.42; the locus crosses the imaginary axis at ±j√2 for K = 6.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> φ = (2k + 1)180°/(P − Z); σ = Σp/(P − Z).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-105", year: 2020, no: 105, subject: "control", topic: "Effects of a phase-lead network",
q: `Which one of the following statements regarding an effect of phase lead network is not correct?`,
opts: ["The velocity constant is usually increased", "The slope of the magnitude curve is reduced at the gain crossover frequency, as a result relative stability improves", "Phase margin increased", "The bandwidth decreased"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) the bandwidth decreased</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A phase-lead network raises the gain at high frequencies. It therefore <b>increases</b> the bandwidth, speeds up the response, increases the phase margin, reduces the slope at the crossover frequency and (with the added gain) can raise the velocity constant.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> True: the gain K_v is usually raised.</li><li><b>(b)</b> True: it improves the relative stability.</li><li><b>(c)</b> True: this is its main purpose.</li></ul><p>The lead network improves the phase margin by adding phase near the gain crossover, at the cost of higher noise gain at high frequency.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Lead: more bandwidth, more phase margin.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-106", year: 2020, no: 106, subject: "control", topic: "Lead compensator",
q: `A lead compensator 1. Speeds up the transient response. 2. Increases the margin of stability of a system. 3. Helps to increase the system error constant though to a limited extent.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The lead network adds positive phase and gain at high frequencies.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: wider bandwidth, quicker response.</li><li><b>2</b> True: extra phase margin.</li><li><b>3</b> True to a limited extent: the lead compensator may raise the low-frequency gain slightly, and so the error constant.</li></ul><p>The key's (d) is shown.</p><p>A lead compensator also reduces the rise time and settling time, but because it amplifies noise the high-frequency gain must be limited. The positive phase of the lead network is greatest at the geometric mean of its pole and zero frequencies, and the compensator is placed so that this frequency coincides with the gain crossover.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Lead: speed + margin (+ slightly K).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-107", year: 2020, no: 107, subject: "control", topic: "Duality of controllability and observability",
q: `Which of the following statements are correct? 1. The pair (A, B) is controllable implies that the pair (Aᵀ, Bᵀ) is observable. 2. The pair (A, B) is controllable implies that the pair (Aᵀ, Bᵀ) is unobservable. 3. The pair (A, C) is observable implies that the pair (Aᵀ, Cᵀ) is controllable. 4. The pair (A, C) is observable implies that the pair (Aᵀ, Cᵀ) is uncontrollable.`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only", "2 and 4 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The principle of duality: (A, B) is controllable if and only if (Aᵀ, Bᵀ) is observable, and (A, C) is observable if and only if (Aᵀ, Cᵀ) is controllable.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True.</li><li><b>2</b> False.</li><li><b>3</b> True.</li><li><b>4</b> False.</li></ul><p>The test matrices are the controllability matrix [B AB A²B …] and the observability matrix [C; CA; CA²; …]; duality swaps them through transposition. In practice the duality means that a computer routine written to test controllability also tests observability when it is given the transposed matrices.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Duality: controllable ⇔ dual observable.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-108", year: 2020, no: 108, subject: "control", topic: "BIBO and asymptotic stability",
q: `Bounded-input bounded-output stability implies asymptotic stability for 1. Completely controllable system 2. Completely observable system 3. Uncontrollable system 4. Unobservable system`,
opts: ["1 and 4 only", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "3 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> BIBO stability concerns the transfer function; it ignores any uncontrollable or unobservable modes, which may be unstable and hidden. For a system that is both completely controllable and completely observable, the transfer function contains all the modes, so BIBO stability implies asymptotic stability.</p><p>A system with an unstable but uncontrollable mode may still be BIBO stable, since that mode does not appear in the transfer function, but it is not asymptotically stable.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Minimal realisation: BIBO ⇔ asymptotic stability.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-109", year: 2020, no: 109, subject: "machines", topic: "Transformer hum",
q: `The degree of humming level of the noise caused in the transformers may be reduced by`,
opts: ["Magnetostriction", "High flux density in core", "Tightening of core by clamps", "Quality of transformer oil"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) tightening the core with clamps</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Transformer hum results from magnetostriction: the laminations change length with the alternating flux and vibrate at twice the supply frequency. Tightly clamping the core stops the laminations from vibrating, and a lower flux density also helps.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Magnetostriction is the cause of the noise.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A higher flux density increases the magnetostrictive vibration.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The oil quality does not affect the noise level.</li></ul><p>Transformer hum has a frequency of twice the supply frequency, 100 Hz at 50 Hz supply, plus its harmonics, and is reduced by clamping, by lower flux densities and by sound-proof tanks.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Hum: magnetostriction at 2f; clamp the core.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-110", year: 2020, no: 110, subject: "machines", topic: "Load impedance of a transformer",
q: `A transformer with a 10 : 1 ratio and rated at 50 kVA, 2400/240 V, 50 Hz is used to step down the voltage of a distribution system. The low tension voltage is to be kept constant at 240 V. If the transformer is fully loaded at 0·8 power factor (lag), the load impedance connected to low-tension side will be nearly`,
opts: ["3·15 Ω", "2·60 Ω", "1·15 Ω", "0·60 Ω"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1·15 Ω</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Full load current on the LT side I = 50 000/240 = 208.3 A.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>|Z| = V/I = 240/208.3 = 1.152 Ω (with phase angle 36.9° lagging).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 3.15 Ω would carry only 76 A.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 2.6 Ω would carry 92 A.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.6 Ω would carry 400 A.</li></ul><p>The 10:1 ratio gives a primary full-load current of 20.8 A, and the load impedance referred to the HV side would be 100 times larger, 115 Ω. On the HT side the full load current is 50 000/2400 = 20.8 A, and the impedance transferred to it by the square of the ratio, 1.152 × 100 = 115.2 Ω, is consistent with 2400/20.8.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> |Z| = V²/S.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-111", year: 2020, no: 111, subject: "machines", topic: "Induced emf of a shunt generator",
q: `A DC shunt generator supplies a load of 7·5 kW at 200 V. If the armature resistance is 0·6 Ω and field resistance is 80 Ω, the induced emf will be`,
opts: ["224 V", "218 V", "212 V", "204 V"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 224 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Load current I_L = 7500/200 = 37.5 A; shunt current I_sh = 200/80 = 2.5 A; armature current I_a = 40 A.</p><div class="formula">I_a = I_L + I_sh</div><div class="formula">E = V + I_aR_a</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>E = V + I_aR_a = 200 + 40 × 0.6 = 224 V.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 218 V omits the field current.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 212 V corresponds to an armature current of 20 A, half the actual 40 A.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 204 V corresponds to an armature current of about 6.7 A.</li></ul><p>The armature current is the sum of the load current and the field current, I_a = I_L + I_sh, which is why the shunt generator needs the field current added. The terminal power of 7.5 kW is carried by the load current of 37.5 A, while the shunt field takes 500 W, which is lost in the field circuit and not supplied to the load.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> E = V + I_a R_a, I_a = I_L + I_sh.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-112", year: 2020, no: 112, subject: "machines", topic: "Torque of a dc motor",
q: `A 4-pole DC motor is lap-wound with 400 conductors. The pole shoe is 20 cm long and the average flux density over one-pole-pitch is 0·4 T. The armature diameter is 30 cm. When the motor is drawing 25 A and running at 1500 rpm, the torque developed will be nearly`,
opts: ["30 Nm", "40 Nm", "50 Nm", "60 Nm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 30 N·m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Pole pitch = πD/P = π × 0.3/4 = 0.2356 m. Flux per pole φ = B × (pole pitch × length) = 0.4 × 0.2356 × 0.2 = 0.01885 Wb.</p><div class="formula">φ = B × (πD/P) × L</div><div class="formula">T = ZPφI_a/(2πA)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>T = ZPφI_a/(2πA) with A = P = 4 (lap): T = 400 × 4 × 0.01885 × 25/(2π × 4) = 754/25.13 = 30.0 N·m.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 40 N·m would need 33 A.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 50 N·m would need 42 A.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 60 N·m would need 50 A.</li></ul><p>The torque can also be written T = 9.55 E_a I_a/N: here E_a = ZφNP/(60A) = 400 × 0.01885 × 1500 × 4/(60 × 4) = 188.5 V, so T = 9.55 × 188.5 × 25/1500 = 30 N·m, the same as the direct formula.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> T = (ZP/2πA) φ I_a.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-113", year: 2020, no: 113, subject: "machines", topic: "Voltage build-up in a shunt generator",
q: `In an unloaded shunt generator, when switch is closed, a small field current is produced which leads to generation of still larger voltages due to addition of`,
opts: ["Armature voltage", "Residual flux voltage", "Generated voltage", "Voltage drop"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) residual flux voltage (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A shunt generator starts from the residual magnetism, which gives a small emf. This drives a small field current that adds to the residual flux, and so on, until the saturation of the iron limits the voltage. The key words the addition as that of the residual-flux voltage.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Armature voltage is the output being built.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The generated voltage is the result, not the added term.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Voltage drop reduces the output.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Build-up: residual flux → field current → more flux.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-114", year: 2020, no: 114, subject: "machines", topic: "Maximum efficiency of a dc generator",
q: `The generator efficiency of a shunt generator will be maximum when its variable loss is equal to`,
opts: ["Constant loss", "Stray loss", "Iron loss", "Friction and windage loss"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) constant loss</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The efficiency is a maximum when the variable (copper) loss equals the constant loss (iron, friction, windage and shunt field loss).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The stray loss is only part of the constant loss.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Iron loss is only part of the constant loss.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Friction and windage is only part of the constant loss.</li></ul><p>At maximum efficiency the shunt-generator armature current is I_a = √(P_c/R_a), where P_c is the constant loss; load current beyond this lowers the efficiency.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> η max: variable loss = constant loss.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-115", year: 2020, no: 115, subject: "machines", topic: "Induction motor as a generalised transformer",
q: `Induction motor can be regarded as a generalized transformer due to certain similarities except rated`,
opts: ["Frequency", "Flux", "Speed", "Induced emf"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) speed</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a transformer the secondary is stationary and the primary and secondary have the same frequency. In an induction motor the rotor rotates, so the rotor frequency and emf depend on the slip; the rated speed has no counterpart in a transformer.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The rotor frequency is sf, which is a similarity with a transformer at s = 1.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Flux is common to both.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Induced emf appears in both.</li></ul><p>A wound-rotor induction motor at standstill is exactly a transformer with a short-circuited secondary, and at speed the rotor emf and frequency are reduced by the slip.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Transformer: no speed; induction motor: speed, slip.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-116", year: 2020, no: 116, subject: "machines", topic: "Rotor resistance for maximum starting torque",
q: `A 3-phase, 400/200 V, Y–Y connected wound-rotor induction motor has 0·06 Ω rotor resistance and 0·3 Ω standstill reactance per phase. To make the starting torque equal to the maximum torque, the additional resistance required in the rotor circuit will be`,
opts: ["0·24 Ω/phase", "0·34 Ω/phase", "0·42 Ω/phase", "0·52 Ω/phase"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·24 Ω/phase</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The slip at maximum torque is s_m = R₂/X₂. For the starting torque to equal the maximum torque, s_m = 1, so the total rotor resistance must equal the standstill reactance.</p><div class="formula">s_m = R₂/X₂</div><div class="formula">T_st = T_max ⇔ s_m = 1</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>R₂ + R_ext = X₂ = 0.3 → R_ext = 0.3 − 0.06 = 0.24 Ω per phase.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0.34 Ω would give s_m = 1.33.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.42 Ω would give s_m = 1.6.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.52 Ω would give s_m = 1.9.</li></ul><p>The additional resistance also reduces the starting current, since the rotor impedance rises, so the starting torque is increased without a large current.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Max starting torque: R_total = X₂.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-117", year: 2020, no: 117, subject: "machines", topic: "Potier method",
q: `Potier triangle method is helpful in obtaining the voltage regulation of synchronous machines by determining the armature`,
opts: ["Leakage reactance and its reaction mmf", "Leakage reactance and air-gap flux", "Resistance and its reaction mmf", "Resistance and air-gap flux"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) leakage reactance and its reaction mmf</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Potier triangle, obtained from the open-circuit and zero-power-factor lagging curves, gives the armature leakage reactance X_L (the vertical side) and the armature reaction mmf (the horizontal side), which are then used in the mmf method to find the regulation.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The air-gap flux is read from the OCC.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Resistance is measured directly.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Resistance and flux are not determined by the triangle.</li></ul><p>The Potier triangle is drawn from the zero power factor characteristic at rated current, and the horizontal side gives the field current for the armature reaction.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Potier: X_L and armature reaction.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-118", year: 2020, no: 118, subject: "machines", topic: "Back emf of a synchronous motor",
q: `In a synchronous motor, the magnitude of stator back emf, E_b depends on`,
opts: ["Speed of the motor", "Load on the motor", "Both the speed and rotor flux", "Rotor excitation only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) rotor excitation only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A synchronous motor runs at constant synchronous speed, so E_b = 4.44fφN_s depends only on the rotor flux, i.e. the field excitation. The load changes the torque angle δ, not the magnitude of E_b.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The speed is fixed at synchronous speed.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Load changes the angle, not the magnitude of E_b.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The speed is constant, so only the flux matters.</li></ul><p>The torque angle δ between the stator and rotor fields increases with load, but the back emf magnitude stays fixed at its excitation value E_b = 4.44 f φ T.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> E_b ∝ excitation.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-119", year: 2020, no: 119, subject: "machines", topic: "Number of steps of a stepper motor",
q: `A stepper motor has a step angle of 2·5°. If the shaft is to make 25 revolutions, the number of steps required will be`,
opts: ["1800", "2200", "2800", "3600"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 3600</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Steps per revolution = 360/2.5 = 144. For 25 revolutions: 144 × 25 = 3600 steps.</p><div class="formula">Steps/rev = 360°/β</div><div class="formula">Steps = rev × steps/rev</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>360/2.5 = 144 steps/rev.</li><li>144 × 25 = <b>3600</b> steps.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1800 would be 12.5 revolutions.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 2200 would be 15.3 revolutions.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2800 would be 19.4 revolutions.</li></ul><p>The step angle also fixes the resolution: 2.5° gives 144 steps per revolution, and the shaft turns exactly 25 revolutions after 3600 pulses if no step is lost. If one step were lost the final position would be in error by 2.5°, which is why open-loop steppers must be sized so that the load torque never exceeds the pull-out torque.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Steps = (360/θ) × revolutions.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-120", year: 2020, no: 120, subject: "machines", topic: "Servomotors compared with industrial motors",
q: `In which of the following respects, the servomotors differ in application capabilities from large industrial motors? 1. They produce high torque at all speeds including zero speed. 2. They are capable of holding a static position. 3. They do not overheat at standstill or lower speeds. 4. Due to low-inertia they are not able to reverse direction quickly.`,
opts: ["1, 2, 3 and 4", "1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "3 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Servomotors are designed for position and speed control: high torque at all speeds including zero, ability to hold a position, and no overheating at standstill. Their low inertia lets them reverse <b>quickly</b>, so statement 4 is false.</p><p>Servomotors respond to a command signal with a speed that is proportional to the control voltage, and are used in robotics, machine tools and aircraft control surfaces. Industrial motors are designed for continuous running at their rated speed with high efficiency, while servomotors are designed for rapid acceleration and precise positioning, even at the cost of efficiency.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Servo: high torque, low inertia, quick reversal.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-121", year: 2020, no: 121, subject: "power", topic: "Steam boilers", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which of the following statements regarding steam boilers are correct? 1. The boiler must be capable of quick starting and loading. 2. The boiler should have no joints exposed to flames. 3. The boiler must be capable of burning low ash content coal efficiently.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Requirements of a good boiler:</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: it must follow load changes and start quickly.</li><li><b>2</b> True: joints in the flame zone would be overheated and leak.</li><li><b>3</b> False: Indian coals have a <b>high</b> ash content; the boiler must burn high-ash coal efficiently, not low-ash coal.</li></ul><p>Other boiler requirements are that it should be able to burn a variety of fuels, give high thermal efficiency, be easy to inspect and clean, and have a good circulation of water.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Good boiler: quick steaming, no exposed joints, burns high-ash coal.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-122", year: 2020, no: 122, subject: "power", topic: "Parts of a power system",
q: `Which of the following are the main parts of a power system? 1. Generating stations 2. Transmission systems 3. Distribution networks`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A power system has three main parts: generating stations that produce electric power, transmission systems that carry it at high voltage over long distances to load centres, and distribution networks that deliver it to consumers.</p><p>A power system is also provided with protection and control equipment; the generating stations feed the grid at 11–25 kV, which is stepped up for transmission and stepped down for distribution. With 0.0124 pu leakage reactance, a short circuit on the secondary at rated voltage would give a current of 1/0.0124 = 80 times full load current, if the supply were infinitely strong.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Generation → transmission → distribution.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-123", year: 2020, no: 123, subject: "power", topic: "Factors affecting corona",
q: `Which of the following factors affect Corona? 1. Atmospheric conditions, temperature, humidity, moisture, ice and fog. 2. Current of conductor. 3. Waveform. 4. Condition of surface of conductors, smoothness and dust.`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Corona depends on the electric field at the conductor surface:</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: the air density and humidity change the breakdown strength.</li><li><b>2</b> False: it depends on the voltage, not on the current.</li><li><b>3</b> True: the waveform (peak voltage, frequency) affects it.</li><li><b>4</b> True: rough, dirty or rain-wet surfaces raise the local field.</li></ul><p>Corona also produces a visible violet glow, a hissing sound, ozone and power loss, so EHV lines use large-diameter or bundled conductors to keep the gradient below about 30 kV/cm.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Corona: voltage, air, surface, spacing, waveform.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-124", year: 2020, no: 124, subject: "power", topic: "Corona voltage of bundled conductors",
q: `Bundled conductors that are used to increase line voltage in EHV lines for raising critical corona voltage depend on`,
opts: ["Number of conductors in the group", "Voltage gradient", "Optimum spacing", "Communication interference"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) number of conductors in the group</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A bundle of n sub-conductors has a larger equivalent radius, which lowers the surface gradient and so raises the critical corona voltage. The benefit depends on the number of conductors in the bundle (and on their spacing).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The voltage gradient is the result of the arrangement, not the factor chosen.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Optimum spacing is also relevant, but the key names the number of conductors.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Communication interference is a consequence, not a design variable.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Bundle: larger effective radius, lower gradient.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-125", year: 2020, no: 125, subject: "power", topic: "Power transfer over a line",
q: `In a 275 kV transmission line with line constants A = 0·85∠5°, B = 200∠75°, if the voltage profile at each end is to be maintained at 275 kV, the power at Unity Power Factor (UPF) will be nearly`,
opts: ["98 MW", "118 MW", "144 MW", "184 MW"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 118 MW</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Receiving-end equations with V_s = V_r = V: P_r = (V²/B)cos(β − δ) − (AV²/B)cos(β − α); Q_r = (V²/B)sin(β − δ) − (AV²/B)sin(β − α).</p><div class="formula">P_r = (V²/B)cos(β − δ) − (AV²/B)cos(β − α)</div><div class="formula">Q_r = (V²/B)sin(β − δ) − (AV²/B)sin(β − α)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>UPF: Q_r = 0 → sin(75° − δ) = 0.85 sin 70° = 0.7988 → 75° − δ = 53.0°.</li><li>P_r = (275²/200)[cos 53.0° − 0.85 cos 70°] = 378.1 × (0.6018 − 0.2907) = 117.6 MW.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 98 MW is too low.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 144 MW is too high.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 184 MW is too high.</li></ul><p>The same expressions with δ and the Q_r = 0 condition give the power the line can carry at unity power factor without reactive support, here about 118 MW at the stated voltages.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Set Q_r = 0 to find δ, then compute P_r.</p>` }
);

