window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2020-126", year: 2020, no: 126, subject: "power", topic: "Short-circuit current components",
q: `When the sinusoidal steady state current is called the symmetrical short circuit current, then the unidirectional transient component is called`,
opts: ["AC short circuit current", "DC short circuit current", "AC offset current", "DC offset current"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) dc offset current</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A short-circuit current has a symmetrical sinusoidal part and a decaying unidirectional component that arises from the inductance of the circuit. The latter is the dc offset current; it makes the first-cycle current asymmetrical.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> This is the sinusoidal (symmetrical) component itself.</li><li><b>(b)</b> "DC short circuit current" is not the standard name.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The offset is unidirectional, not ac.</li></ul><p>For a fault at a given point, the maximum dc offset depends on the instant at which the fault occurs; it is zero if the fault occurs at the voltage peak and maximum if it occurs at the voltage zero in an inductive circuit.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Asymmetrical = symmetrical + dc offset.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-127", year: 2020, no: 127, subject: "power", topic: "Symmetrical components of balanced voltages",
q: `Consider the following balanced line-to-neutral voltages with abc sequence: V_an = 277∠0°, V_bn = 277∠−120°, V_cn = 277∠+120° volts. The values of V₀, V₁ and V₂ are respectively`,
opts: ["0, 177∠0° and 177∠0°", "277∠0°, 0 and 0", "0, 277∠0° and 0", "277∠0°, 0 and 177∠0°"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0, 277∠0° and 0</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a balanced positive-sequence set, only the positive-sequence component exists: V₀ = (V_a + V_b + V_c)/3 = 0, V₁ = (V_a + aV_b + a²V_c)/3 = 277∠0°, and V₂ = (V_a + a²V_b + aV_c)/3 = 0.</p><div class="formula">V₀ = (V_a + V_b + V_c)/3</div><div class="formula">V₁ = (V_a + aV_b + a²V_c)/3</div><div class="formula">V₂ = (V_a + a²V_b + aV_c)/3</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Balanced: V_a + V_b + V_c = 0 ⇒ V₀ = 0.</li><li>aV_b = 277∠0°, a²V_c = 277∠0° ⇒ V₁ = 277∠0°.</li><li>a²V_b + aV_c terms cancel ⇒ V₂ = 0.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A balanced abc set has no negative- or zero-sequence parts.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The zero-sequence is zero because the phasors sum to zero.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The zero- and negative-sequence components are zero.</li></ul><p>The synthesis equations are V_a = V₀ + V₁ + V₂, V_b = V₀ + a²V₁ + aV₂, V_c = V₀ + aV₁ + a²V₂, and an unbalanced set would produce non-zero V₂ and V₀.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Balanced abc set: only V₁.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-128", year: 2020, no: 128, subject: "pe", topic: "Inverter operation of an HVDC converter",
q: `In an HVDC transmission, the DC output voltage can be controlled to get inverter operation when the firing angle α is`,
opts: ["α = 0°", "0° &lt; α &lt; 90°", "90° &lt; α &lt; 180°", "0° &lt; α &lt; 180°"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 90° &lt; α &lt; 180°</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The average dc voltage of a converter is V_d = V_do cos α. For α &gt; 90° it is negative, so power flows from the dc side to the ac side: inversion (in practice limited to about 150–165°).</p><div class="formula">V_d = V_do cos α</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>α &lt; 90°: V_d &gt; 0 (rectifier).</li><li>α &gt; 90°: V_d &lt; 0 ⇒ <b>inverter</b> (90° &lt; α &lt; 180°).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> At α = 0 the output is the maximum rectifier voltage.</li><li><b>(b)</b> α between 0° and 90° gives rectifier operation.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The full range includes the rectifier region.</li></ul><p>At α = 90° the average voltage is zero, and for inversion the practical limit is α less than about 165°, to leave a commutation margin and avoid commutation failure.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> α < 90°: rectifier; α > 90°: inverter.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-129", year: 2020, no: 129, subject: "pe", topic: "HVDC links",
q: `In an HVDC transmission mode, the link which has two circuits that are almost independent of each other is called`,
opts: ["Monopolar link", "Bipolar link", "Homopolar link", "Dualpolar link"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) bipolar link</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A bipolar link has two conductors, one at positive and one at negative polarity to earth, each with its own converters; the two poles work almost independently, so one can carry load if the other fails.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A monopolar link has a single conductor with earth return.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A homopolar link has two conductors of the same polarity.</li><li><b>(d)</b> "Dualpolar" is not a standard type.</li></ul><p>In a monopolar link the earth or sea is the return path; in a homopolar link two conductors of the same polarity (usually negative) are used, to reduce corona and radio interference.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Bipolar: two independent poles.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-130", year: 2020, no: 130, subject: "pe", topic: "Dark current",
q: `In a photovoltaic system, there is a thermally generated small reverse saturation current which flows even in the absence of light, called`,
opts: ["Photon current", "Diode current", "Leakage current", "Dark current"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) dark current</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A solar cell in darkness behaves as a diode with a small reverse saturation current generated by thermal carriers: the dark current.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The photon current is the light-generated current.</li><li><b>(b)</b> "Diode current" is the forward current.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Leakage current is the surface and shunt leakage, a different effect.</li></ul><p>The dark current limits the sensitivity of photodetectors, since it adds noise; it increases with temperature, roughly doubling every 6–10 °C in silicon.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> I = I_L − I_dark(e^{V/V_T} − 1).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-131", year: 2020, no: 131, subject: "power", topic: "Reliability",
q: `In a power system, due to interconnection or grid formation and transmission line redundancy, the ability to serve all power demands without failure over long periods of time is due to`,
opts: ["Power system quality", "Power system reliability", "Computers and microprocessors", "Reserve generating capacity"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) power system reliability</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Reliability is the ability of the system to supply the demand without interruption over long periods; interconnection and redundant lines improve it. Quality concerns the voltage and frequency.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Quality means voltage and frequency within limits.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Computers are tools, not the property described.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Reserve capacity is one means of achieving reliability.</li></ul><p>Reliability is measured by indices such as loss of load probability and expected unserved energy, and it improves with reserve capacity, redundant lines and quick restoration.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Reliability = continuity of supply.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-132", year: 2020, no: 132, subject: "power", topic: "Wind turbine parameter", selfCorrected: true,
q: `In wind power, the speed which is considered as the single most important parameter is`,
opts: ["Wind speed", "Peripheral speed", "Tip speed", "Blade speed"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) tip speed</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The tip-speed ratio λ = ωR/V (blade-tip speed over wind speed) determines the power coefficient of a turbine; for each rotor type there is an optimum λ. The key regards the tip speed as the single most important speed parameter.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The wind speed is the input; the cube of it fixes the available power, but the design parameter is the tip speed ratio.</li><li><b>(b)</b> "Peripheral speed" is the same quantity named differently.</li><li><b>(d)</b> "Blade speed" is not a standard term.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> λ = tip speed/wind speed; C_p peaks at optimum λ.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-133", year: 2020, no: 133, subject: "pe", topic: "Forced commutation",
q: `Commutation circuitry is an extra circuit used to turn off`,
opts: ["Line-commutated thyristors", "Phase-commutated thyristors", "Forced-commutated thyristors", "Reverse-commutated thyristors"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) forced-commutated thyristors</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In dc circuits there is no natural current zero, so an auxiliary commutation circuit (capacitor and inductor) must force the thyristor current to zero: forced commutation. Line commutation uses the ac supply zero crossing and needs no extra circuit.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Line commutation is natural.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Phase commutation is the same as line commutation.</li><li><b>(d)</b> "Reverse-commutated" is not a standard type.</li></ul><p>Typical forced-commutation circuits include the class A (load commutated resonance), class B (resonant pulse), class C (complementary), class D (auxiliary) and class E (external pulse).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Forced commutation: extra circuit; line commutation: natural.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-134", year: 2020, no: 134, subject: "pe", topic: "TRIAC applications",
q: `TRIAC as a bidirectional triode thyristor is used to control the output voltage by varying conduction time or firing delay angle in`,
opts: ["AC-DC converters (Controlled rectifiers)", "AC-AC converters (AC voltage controllers)", "DC-DC converters (DC choppers)", "DC-AC converters (Inverters)"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) AC–AC converters (ac voltage controllers)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A TRIAC conducts in both directions, so it is used in ac voltage controllers (light dimmers, fan regulators, heater control) by changing the firing angle in each half cycle.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Controlled rectifiers use SCRs.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Choppers use dc switches (transistors, GTOs).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Inverters use controllable switches with forced commutation.</li></ul><p>A TRIAC can be triggered by a gate pulse of either polarity in any of its four modes, but it has a lower dv/dt and commutation capability than two back-to-back SCRs, so it is used at low power.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> TRIAC: phase-controlled ac switch.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-135", year: 2020, no: 135, subject: "pe", topic: "Multiphase rectifiers", selfCorrected: true,
q: `For large power output, multiphase rectifiers are used along with filters to reduce level of harmonics by increasing the fundamental frequency in`,
opts: ["Diode rectifier", "Bridge rectifier", "Star rectifier", "Delta rectifier"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) star rectifier (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Multiphase star (and similar) rectifiers with 6, 12 or more pulses raise the ripple frequency to a multiple of the supply frequency, which makes the ripple easier to filter and lowers the harmonic level. The key names the star rectifier.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> "Diode rectifier" is the device type, not the arrangement.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The bridge is a configuration that has been used for six-pulse rectification.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A delta arrangement is not the standard multiphase rectifier.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> p-pulse rectifier: ripple at p × f.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-136", year: 2020, no: 136, subject: "pe", topic: "Second breakdown",
q: `In a Bipolar Junction Transistor (BJT) due to current flow to small portion of the base, hot spots are produced causing localized excessive heating and damaging the transistor. This switching limit is called`,
opts: ["Forward-Biased Safe Operating Area (FBSOA)", "Reverse-Biased Safe Operating Area (RBSOA)", "Power Derating", "Second Breakdown (SB)"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) second breakdown</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Second breakdown is the localised thermal runaway caused by current crowding in a small region (hot spot) at high voltage and current; it destroys the transistor within microseconds.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> FBSOA is the safe region in forward bias; second breakdown is one of its limits.</li><li><b>(b)</b> RBSOA concerns turn-off.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Power derating is the reduction of the rating with temperature.</li></ul><p>The safe operating area diagrams include the second breakdown limit as a curve between the dc limit and the maximum voltage; staying within it is essential for power BJTs.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Second breakdown: hot spot, current crowding.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-137", year: 2020, no: 137, subject: "pe", topic: "Three-phase inverter modes",
q: `In a three-phase inverter with 180° conduction, there are six modes of operation in a cycle where duration of each mode is`,
opts: ["90°", "75°", "60°", "45°"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 60°</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The 360° cycle is divided into six equal modes of 60° each, and in each mode three switches (one in each leg) conduct.</p><div class="formula">Mode duration = 360°/6</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Six switching events per cycle ⇒ six equal modes.</li><li>360/6 = <b>60°</b> each.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 90° would give four modes.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 75° does not divide 360°.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 45° would give eight modes.</li></ul><p>In 180° conduction mode each switch conducts for 180°, and at any instant three switches are on, one from each leg; in the 120° mode only two are on at a time. In a 120° conduction mode there are also six modes but with only two switches on at any time, and the output voltage then has six steps per cycle with a zero level.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> 360°/6 = 60°.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-138", year: 2020, no: 138, subject: "pe", topic: "Field oriented control",
q: `In a closed-loop control of squirrel cage induction motor, the field oriented control strategy implemented is`,
opts: ["Scalar control", "Vector control", "Adaptive control", "Frequency control"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) vector control</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Field-oriented control (vector control) transforms the stator currents into flux-producing and torque-producing components, so the induction motor can be controlled like a separately excited dc motor, with fast torque response.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Scalar (V/f) control regulates magnitudes only.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Adaptive control adjusts parameters of a controller.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Frequency control is a scalar method.</li></ul><p>Vector control needs the rotor flux position, which is obtained from sensors or flux observers, and then the d-axis current produces flux and the q-axis current produces torque.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> FOC = vector control.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-139", year: 2020, no: 139, subject: "pe", topic: "Braking of a dc drive", selfCorrected: true,
q: `In a DC motor drive, if the armature current is reversed by keeping field current positive producing a braking torque, then the drive is said to be operating in`,
opts: ["Motoring mode", "Regenerative braking mode", "Dynamic braking mode", "Plugging mode"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) plugging mode (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Plugging (reverse-current braking) reverses the armature connections, or the armature current, while the field is kept the same, so the motor torque opposes the rotation and brakes it quickly. The key names this plugging.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> In motoring the torque aids the rotation.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Regeneration returns energy to the supply while the back emf exceeds the supply.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Dynamic braking dissipates the energy in a resistor connected across the armature.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Plugging: reverse the armature current.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-140", year: 2020, no: 140, subject: "pe", topic: "Static Scherbius drive",
q: `If the induction motor drive is capable of bidirectional power flow where limited range of speed control is required for large power applications, then this arrangement is called`,
opts: ["Static conductance drive", "Static Scherbius drive", "Static compressive drive", "Static reluctance drive"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) static Scherbius drive</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the static Scherbius drive the slip power of a wound-rotor induction motor is rectified and fed back to the supply through an inverter. Power can flow in either direction, which gives sub- and super-synchronous operation over a limited speed range, suitable for large pumps and fans.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Not a standard term.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Not a standard term.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A reluctance drive uses a reluctance motor.</li></ul><p>The Scherbius drive returns the slip power to the supply through a rectifier and line-commutated inverter; the Kramer drive uses the slip power to drive an auxiliary dc motor on the shaft.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Scherbius: slip-power recovery.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-141", year: 2020, no: 141, subject: "pe", topic: "Inverting dc–dc regulator",
q: `In a DC-DC switched-mode converter, if the output voltage polarity is opposite to input voltage, then this inverting regulator is called`,
opts: ["Buck regulator", "Boost regulator", "Buck-Boost regulator", "Ćuk regulator"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) buck–boost regulator</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The buck–boost converter gives V_o = −V_inD/(1 − D): inverted polarity, with the magnitude either higher or lower than the input. (The Ćuk converter also inverts, but the standard answer for the "inverting regulator" is the buck–boost.)</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The buck gives a lower voltage of the same polarity.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The boost gives a higher voltage of the same polarity.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The Ćuk converter also inverts, but it is a different circuit with capacitive energy transfer.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Buck–boost: V_o = −V_in D/(1 − D).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-142", year: 2020, no: 142, subject: "pe", topic: "Zero-current switching resonant converter", selfCorrected: true,
q: `In a zero current switching resonant converter, the switching loss and noise are increased due to presence of capacitive coupling called`,
opts: ["Miller capacitor", "Series resonant capacitor", "Parallel resonant capacitor", "Switch capacitor"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Miller capacitor</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a ZCS converter the switch current is made zero before it turns off, but the switch still sees a voltage change, so its output (drain–gate) capacitance, the Miller capacitance, is charged at turn-on. This capacitive coupling adds switching loss and noise.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The series resonant capacitor is part of the resonant tank.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The parallel resonant capacitor is part of the resonant tank.</li><li><b>(d)</b> "Switch capacitor" is a general term and not the coupling named in the key.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> ZCS: capacitive turn-on loss via the Miller/output capacitance.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-143", year: 2020, no: 143, subject: "pe", topic: "Switched-mode supplies", doubt: true,
q: `Which one of the following devices is not a switched-mode DC power supply?`,
opts: ["Flyback forward converter", "Full bridge converter", "Push-pull converter", "Resonant converter"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) resonant converter (as keyed)</h3><p class="note">In doubt (key followed). Resonant converters are also switched-mode power supplies (in the broader sense), so the question is poorly framed; (a) was our answer because "flyback forward converter" is not a single standard name. The key's (d) is shown.</p><p><b>Concept:</b> The key names the resonant converter as the one that is not classed with the hard-switched SMPS topologies (flyback, forward, push-pull, full-bridge), since it uses a resonant tank in place of a hard-switched pulse-width-modulated stage. Resonant converters are, however, widely used as switched-mode supplies.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Flyback and forward converters are SMPS topologies.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The full-bridge converter is an SMPS topology.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The push-pull converter is an SMPS topology.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Hard-switched PWM topologies: flyback, forward, push-pull, bridge.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-144", year: 2020, no: 144, subject: "pe", topic: "Cores for magnetics",
q: `The ideal core should exhibit very high permeability in case of transformers and inductor core due to magnetic saturation caused by DC imbalance condition that can be minimized by`,
opts: ["Low permeability core only", "High permeability core only", "Low and high permeability combination core", "No permeability core"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) a combination of low and high permeability (gapped) core</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A high-permeability core saturates easily when a dc component flows. Introducing an air gap or a low-permeability section in series with the high-permeability one stores the dc energy in the gap and prevents saturation, while the high-permeability part keeps the inductance high.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A low-permeability core alone needs many more turns.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A high-permeability core alone saturates under dc imbalance.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A core must have permeability to work.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Gapped core: dc-bias tolerance.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-145", year: 2020, no: 145, subject: "materials", topic: "Substitutional defects and Schottky defects",
q: `Statement (I): In a substitutional semiconductor, atom is replaced by an occasional foreign atom. The imperfections may be deliberately controlled or created in transistor material. Statement (II): The lattice vacancies created when certain atoms in a semiconductor are missing are known as Schottky defects.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) both true, II not the explanation</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Point defects in a crystal:</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>I</b> True: substitutional impurities (doping) are introduced on purpose.</li><li><b>II</b> True: a missing atom leaves a vacancy, called a Schottky defect.</li></ul><p>The vacancy statement does not explain substitutional doping, so (b).</p><p>In a semiconductor, doping with a pentavalent impurity (phosphorus) gives n-type and with a trivalent one (boron) gives p-type; the impurity atoms substitute for silicon atoms on lattice sites.</p><p><b>Verdict on each option:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is true but does not explain I, so (a) is not right.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true; II does not explain I.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so (c) is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so (d) is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Substitutional: foreign atom; Schottky: vacancy.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-146", year: 2020, no: 146, subject: "computer", topic: "Cache",
q: `Statement (I): A cache is a memory unit placed between the CPU and main memory M and is used to store instructions, data or both. Statement (II): The cache's effect is to increase the average time required to access an instruction or data word, typically to just a single-clock cycle.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) I true, II false</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A cache is a small fast memory between the CPU and main memory.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>I</b> True.</li><li><b>II</b> False: the cache effect is to <b>decrease</b> (not increase) the average access time, ideally to a single clock cycle.</li></ul><p>So (c).</p><p>The average access time is t_avg = h t_c + (1 − h) t_m for hit ratio h, so with a high hit ratio it approaches the cache access time. A typical cache of 32 KB with a hit ratio of 95 % and an access time of one cycle reduces the average access time from perhaps 100 cycles to about 6 cycles.</p><p><b>Verdict on each option:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is false, so (a) is not right.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is false, so (b) is not right.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is true and II is false.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so (d) is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Cache: reduces average access time.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-147", year: 2020, no: 147, subject: "computer", topic: "Buffer and spooling",
q: `Statement (I): A buffer is not an area in RAM or on the hard drive designated to hold input and output on their way in or out of the system. Statement (II): The process of placing items in a buffer so they can be retrieved by the appropriate device when needed is called spooling.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) I false, II true</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A buffer is a region of memory (RAM or disk) holding data in transit.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>I</b> False: this is precisely what a buffer is.</li><li><b>II</b> True: placing items in a buffer for the appropriate device to retrieve (print jobs, for example) is spooling.</li></ul><p>So (d).</p><p>Printers use a spool queue: the jobs are written to a disk buffer first, so the programs are not slowed by the speed of the printer. In a print spooler the buffer is the temporary storage; the term spooling comes from simultaneous peripheral operations on-line, which describes the overlapping of the slow I/O with processing.</p><p><b>Verdict on each option:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> I is false, so (a) is not right.</li><li><b>(b)</b> I is false, so (b) is not right.</li><li><b>(c)</b> I is false and II is true, so (c) is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is false and II is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Spooling: queue jobs in a buffer for a slow device.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-148", year: 2020, no: 148, subject: "pe", topic: "Power diode structure", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Statement (I): The power diodes are three-layer devices. Statement (II): The impurity concentrations of power diodes vary layer to layer.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) both true, II not the explanation (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A power diode has a p⁺–n⁻–n⁺ structure: a heavily doped p region, a lightly doped drift region and a heavily doped n substrate.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>I</b> True: three layers (p⁺, n⁻, n⁺), so that the thick, lightly doped n⁻ layer can block high voltage.</li><li><b>II</b> True: the doping differs from layer to layer.</li></ul><p>The doping variation is a feature, not the reason for the three layers, so (b).</p><p>The high-resistivity n⁻ layer carries the blocking voltage, and the heavily doped end layers give low-resistance contacts; the structure is why the diode can hold off high voltage and still conduct a large current.</p><p><b>Verdict on each option:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is true but does not explain I, so (a) is not right.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true; II does not explain I.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so (c) is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so (d) is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Power diode: p⁺ n⁻ n⁺.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-149", year: 2020, no: 149, subject: "computer", topic: "Microprocessor registers",
q: `Statement (I): Registers are used for storage of small data in the microprocessor. Statement (II): All registers are accessible to the user through instructions.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) I true, II false</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Registers are the fast storage inside the CPU.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>I</b> True.</li><li><b>II</b> False: some registers (instruction register, memory address and data buffer registers, temporary registers) are internal and cannot be accessed by the programmer.</li></ul><p>So (c).</p><p>The accumulator, the general-purpose registers B, C, D, E, H, L and the flag register are accessible to the programmer in the 8085, whereas the instruction register is not. The internal registers are used by the control unit to carry out instructions and to hold intermediate values, and they are not named in any instruction, which is why the programmer cannot use them.</p><p><b>Verdict on each option:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is false, so (a) is not right.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is false, so (b) is not right.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is true and II is false.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so (d) is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> User-visible vs internal registers.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-150", year: 2020, no: 150, subject: "machines", topic: "Maximum torque of an induction motor",
q: `Statement (I): In a three-phase induction motor, the maximum torque is directly proportional to standstill reactance. Statement (II): In a three-phase induction motor, the speed or the slip at which maximum torque occurs is determined by the rotor resistance.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) I false, II true</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> T_max = (3/2ω_s)V²/(X₂) (with the stator impedance neglected), so it is <b>inversely</b> proportional to the standstill reactance, and independent of the rotor resistance. The slip at maximum torque is s_m = R₂/X₂.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>I</b> False: T_max ∝ 1/X₂.</li><li><b>II</b> True: the slip (and speed) at maximum torque is fixed by R₂.</li></ul><p>So (d).</p><p>The maximum torque T_max = 3V₁²/[2ω_s(X₁ + X₂)] occurs at s_m = R₂/√(R₁² + X²), so adding rotor resistance moves the peak to higher slip without changing its size.</p><p><b>Verdict on each option:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> I is false, so (a) is not right.</li><li><b>(b)</b> I is false, so (b) is not right.</li><li><b>(c)</b> I is false and II is true, so (c) is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is false and II is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> T_max ∝ 1/X₂; s_m = R₂/X₂.</p>` }
);

