// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; none dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2019-26", year: 2019, no: 26, subject: "circuits", topic: "Thevenin's equivalent",
q: `Thevenin's equivalent circuit consists of`,
opts: ["Current source and series impedance", "Voltage source and series impedance", "Voltage source and shunt impedance", "Current source and shunt impedance"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) a voltage source and a series impedance</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Thevenin's theorem replaces a linear two-terminal network by an open-circuit voltage V_th in series with the equivalent impedance Z_th seen from the terminals with the independent sources deactivated.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A current source with series impedance is not an equivalent of any simple form.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A voltage source with a shunt impedance would be short-circuited by the source.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A current source with shunt impedance is the Norton equivalent.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Thevenin: V in series with Z; Norton: I in parallel with Z.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-27", year: 2019, no: 27, subject: "circuits", topic: "Superposition with dependent sources", selfCorrected: true,
q: `When the voltage sources are replaced with short circuits and current sources are replaced with open circuits, leaving dependent sources in the circuit, the theorem applied is`,
opts: ["Superposition", "Thevenin", "Norton", "Millman"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) superposition</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the superposition theorem each independent source acts alone: the other independent voltage sources are shorted and the other independent current sources opened, while dependent sources stay in the circuit because they are controlled by circuit variables.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Thevenin deactivates sources only to find the equivalent impedance and also needs the open-circuit voltage.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Norton is the dual of Thevenin and needs the short-circuit current.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Millman combines parallel branches containing sources.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Superposition: one independent source at a time; dependent sources remain.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-28", year: 2019, no: 28, subject: "circuits", topic: "Maximum power from a source", selfCorrected: true,
q: `The maximum power is delivered from a source to a load when the source resistance is`,
opts: ["Greater than the load resistance", "Equal to zero", "Less than the load resistance", "Equal to the load resistance"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) equal to zero</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a fixed load R_L and a source of EMF E with resistance R_s, P_L = E²R_L/(R_s + R_L)². Varying R_s, the power is largest when R_s = 0. Matching R_s = R_L maximises the power only when the <b>load</b> is the variable (and the source resistance is fixed).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A larger source resistance lowers the load power.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Less than R_L is better than greater, but the maximum is at zero.</li><li><b>(d)</b> This is the answer for a fixed source and variable load, which is the usual maximum power transfer theorem; it is not the case asked here.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Fixed load: R_s = 0; fixed source: R_L = R_s.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-29", year: 2019, no: 29, subject: "circuits", topic: "Maximum power transfer",
q: `A network delivers maximum power to the load resistance when it is`,
opts: ["Greater than Norton's equivalent resistance of the network", "Equal to Thevenin's equivalent resistance of the network", "Less than source resistance", "Less than Norton's equivalent resistance of the network"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) equal to Thevenin's equivalent resistance</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> With the load as the variable, P = V_th²R_L/(R_th + R_L)², maximum at R_L = R_th (= R_N, since the Norton and Thevenin resistances are equal).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A larger load gives less power (and the Norton resistance equals R_th).</li><li><b>(c)</b> The source resistance is not the relevant quantity; the Thevenin equivalent is.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A smaller load also lowers the power.</li></ul><p>The maximum power transferred is V_th²/(4R_th), with an efficiency of only 50 %. Therefore in power systems where efficiency matters, the load resistance is kept much larger than the source resistance.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> R_L = R_th gives P_max = V_th²/4R_th.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-30", year: 2019, no: 30, subject: "circuits", topic: "Parallel equivalent of a series impedance",
q: `The impedance of a parallel circuit is (10 − j30) Ω at 1 MHz. The values of circuit elements will be`,
opts: ["10 Ω and 6·4 mH", "100 Ω and 4·7 nF", "10 Ω and 4·7 mH", "100 Ω and 6·4 nF"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 100 Ω and 4·7 nF</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Y = 1/(10 − j30) = (10 + j30)/1000 = 0.01 + j0.03 S.</p><div class="formula">Y = 1/Z = G + jB</div><div class="formula">R = 1/G, C = B/ω</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Conductance G = 0.01 S → R = 100 Ω.</li><li>Susceptance +j0.03 is capacitive: ωC = 0.03 → C = 0.03/(2π × 10⁶) = 4.77 nF.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> R = 10 Ω is the series resistance, not the parallel one.</li><li><b>(c)</b> An inductor would give a negative susceptance.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 6.4 nF does not match ωC = 0.03.</li></ul><p>For a parallel R–C circuit the admittance is Y = 1/R + jωC, so reading off G = 0.01 S and B = 0.03 S gives R and C at once; the equivalent series form is the given 10 − j30 Ω.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Convert to admittance: G = Re Y, B = Im Y.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-31", year: 2019, no: 31, subject: "circuits", topic: "Impedance parameters",
q: `The defining equations for V₁ and V₂ analyzing a two-port network in terms of its impedance parameters are respectively`,
opts: ["Z₁₂I₁ + Z₁₂I₂ and Z₂₁I₁ + Z₂₁I₂", "Z₁₁I₁ + Z₁₂I₂ and Z₂₁I₁ + Z₂₂I₂", "Z₂₁I₁ + Z₂₁I₂ and Z₁₂I₁ + Z₁₂I₂", "Z₁₂I₁ + Z₁₂I₂ and Z₂₂I₁ + Z₂₂I₂"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) V₁ = Z₁₁I₁ + Z₁₂I₂ and V₂ = Z₂₁I₁ + Z₂₂I₂</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The z-parameters are defined by the matrix equation [V₁; V₂] = [Z₁₁ Z₁₂; Z₂₁ Z₂₂][I₁; I₂], each row being the voltage at a port in terms of the two port currents.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Repeats the same coefficient in both terms.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Uses the wrong coefficients in the wrong rows.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Repeats coefficients.</li></ul><p>The other parameter sets: y-parameters I = YV, h-parameters (V₁, I₂) = h(I₁, V₂), and ABCD (V₁, I₁) = T(V₂, −I₂). Z-parameters are found by open-circuiting the ports.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> V = ZI for the two ports.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-32", year: 2019, no: 32, subject: "circuits", topic: "Band-stop filter",
q: `A filter that allows high and low frequencies to pass but attenuates any signal with a frequency between two corner frequencies is a`,
opts: ["Notch filter", "Band pass filter", "Band stop filter", "Multiband filter"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) band stop filter</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A band-stop (band-reject) filter passes frequencies below the lower corner and above the upper corner and rejects the band between them.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A notch filter rejects a very narrow band; the general wide-band version is the band stop filter.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A band-pass filter does the opposite.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A multiband filter passes several bands.</li></ul><p>Related filters: a low-pass filter passes frequencies below a corner, a high-pass above it, a band-pass between two corners, and a notch filter rejects a very narrow band, as used to remove 50 Hz mains hum.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Band stop = low-pass + high-pass in parallel.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-33", year: 2019, no: 33, subject: "circuits", topic: "Cascade of two-ports",
q: `When a number of two-port networks are cascaded then`,
opts: ["z-parameters are added up", "y-parameters are added up", "h-parameters are multiplied", "ABCD-parameters are multiplied"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) ABCD parameters are multiplied</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a cascade the output of one network is the input of the next, so the transmission matrices multiply: [T] = [T₁][T₂]…</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> z-parameters add for series-series connections.</li><li><b>(b)</b> y-parameters add for parallel connections.</li><li><b>(c)</b> h-parameters add for series-parallel connections; for cascades ABCD multiply.</li></ul><p>The ABCD matrix multiplication order follows the signal flow, so [T] = [T₁][T₂]; a transmission line built of identical sections has T = T₁ⁿ, which is the basis of ladder-network analysis.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Cascade: ABCD multiply; parallel: y add; series: z add.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-34", year: 2019, no: 34, subject: "power", topic: "Currents in a star alternator and delta motor",
q: `A 3-phase star-connected 1000 volt alternator supplies power to a 500 kW delta-connected induction motor. If the motor power factor is 0·8 lagging and its efficiency 0·9, then the current in each alternator and motor phase respectively are nearly`,
opts: ["321 A and 231·5 A", "401 A and 231·5 A", "321 A and 185·4 A", "401 A and 185·4 A"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 401 A and 231·5 A</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Input power = 500/0.9 = 555.6 kW (500 kW taken as the motor output).</p><div class="formula">P_in = P_out/η</div><div class="formula">I_L = P_in/(√3V_L cos φ)</div><div class="formula">Delta: I_ph = I_L/√3</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Line current I_L = 555 600/(√3 × 1000 × 0.8) = 401 A.</li><li>Alternator phase current (star) = line current = 401 A.</li><li>Motor phase current (delta) = 401/√3 = 231.5 A.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 321 A would use 500 kW as input (500/(√3 × 0.8) = 361 A is also different).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 321 A and 185 A divide by 1.25 incorrectly.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 185.4 A is not 401/√3.</li></ul><p>In a delta connection the phase voltage equals the line voltage (1000 V), so the motor phase current is 231.5 A; the alternator is star connected, so its phase current is equal to the line current, 401 A.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Star: I_ph = I_L; delta: I_ph = I_L/√3.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-35", year: 2019, no: 35, subject: "circuits", topic: "Mutual inductance",
q: `Consider: 1. Mutual inductance describes the voltage induced at the ends of a coil due to the magnetic field generated by a second coil. 2. The dot convention allows a sign to be assigned to the voltage induced due to mutual inductance term. 3. The coupling coefficient is given by k = M/√(L₁ + L₂).`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Magnetically coupled coils:</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: the definition of mutual inductance.</li><li><b>2</b> True: the dots fix the polarity of the mutually induced voltage.</li><li><b>3</b> False: k = M/√(L₁L₂), the product, not the sum, under the root.</li></ul><p>The coupling coefficient k lies between 0 and 1; for k = 1 the coils are perfectly coupled with M = √(L₁L₂), and the series connection has an equivalent inductance L₁ + L₂ ± 2M depending on the dots. Using the dot convention, the mutually induced voltage has the same sign as the self-induced voltage when the currents enter both dotted terminals, so the effective inductance increases by 2M in the series-aiding case.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> k = M/√(L₁L₂) ≤ 1.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-36", year: 2019, no: 36, subject: "circuits", topic: "Series and parallel rules for L and C",
q: `Consider: 1. The rules for series and parallel combinations of capacitors are opposite to those for resistors. 2. The rules for series and parallel combinations of inductors are same as those for resistors. 3. An inductor is a short circuit to dc currents.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Combination rules:</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: capacitors in parallel add (like resistors in series), and in series combine as reciprocals.</li><li><b>2</b> True: inductors in series add and in parallel combine by reciprocals, as resistors do (without mutual coupling).</li><li><b>3</b> True: with v = L di/dt, a steady dc current gives v = 0, so an ideal inductor acts as a short circuit.</li></ul><p>For capacitors, C_eq (parallel) = ΣC and 1/C_eq (series) = Σ1/C, which is opposite to resistors; for inductors the series and parallel rules are the same as for resistors, if there is no mutual coupling.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> C opposite to R; L same as R; L is short at dc.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-37", year: 2019, no: 37, subject: "materials", topic: "Standard resistor material",
q: `The standard resistor is a coil of wire of some alloy having the properties of`,
opts: ["Low electrical resistivity and high temperature coefficient of resistance", "High electrical resistivity and high temperature coefficient of resistance", "Low electrical resistivity and low temperature coefficient of resistance", "High electrical resistivity and low temperature coefficient of resistance"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) high resistivity and low temperature coefficient</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A standard resistor (manganin, constantan) must give a large resistance in a small volume, so it needs a high resistivity, and its value must stay constant with temperature, so the temperature coefficient must be very small.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Low resistivity needs long wire; high coefficient drifts.</li><li><b>(b)</b> High coefficient makes the value drift.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Low resistivity makes the coil bulky.</li></ul><p>Standard resistors are wound with manganin wire, annealed and aged, and sealed in oil to keep their value to within 0.001 % over many years; constantan is used for ordinary resistors.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Manganin: ρ ≈ 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ Ω·m, α ≈ 0.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-38", year: 2019, no: 38, subject: "measure", topic: "Swamping resistance material",
q: `Which one of the following materials is used for the swamping resistance of moving coil instruments?`,
opts: ["Carbon", "Manganin", "Silver", "Brass"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) manganin</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A swamping resistance is put in series with the copper coil to reduce the effect of temperature on the instrument. It must have a negligible temperature coefficient, and manganin (≈ 0) is the standard choice.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Carbon has a negative coefficient.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Silver has a high positive coefficient.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Brass has a positive coefficient.</li></ul><p>The coil resistance of a PMMC instrument changes by about 0.4 % per °C. A series swamping resistance of manganin about 20–30 times the coil resistance reduces this error to roughly 0.02 % per °C, at the cost of sensitivity.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Swamping: manganin in series with the copper coil.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-39", year: 2019, no: 39, subject: "measure", topic: "Purpose of a swamping resistor",
q: `In a PMMC instrument, the swamping resistor is used to`,
opts: ["Increase the damping of the instrument", "Reduce the current within safe limits", "Compensate for temperature variations", "Increase the full-scale sensitivity"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) compensate for temperature variations</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The copper coil has a positive temperature coefficient (0.4 %/°C). A manganin swamping resistor in series makes the total resistance change only slightly with temperature, thereby reducing the temperature error of a voltmeter.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Damping is set by the coil former and magnets.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Current limiting is the job of the shunt or multiplier.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Extra series resistance reduces sensitivity.</li></ul><p>The swamping resistor also forms part of the multiplier in a voltmeter, so that the instrument keeps its calibration despite changes in the ambient temperature.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Swamping resistor: temperature compensation.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-40", year: 2019, no: 40, subject: "measure", topic: "Current through the moving coil with a shunt",
q: `A moving coil ammeter has a fixed shunt of 0·02 Ω. With a coil resistance of R = 1000 Ω and a potential difference of 500 mV across it, full scale deflection is obtained. The current through the moving coil to give full scale deflection will be`,
opts: ["25 A", "0·5 × 10⁻² A", "0·25 × 10⁻³ A", "0·5 × 10⁻³ A"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·5 × 10⁻³ A</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The coil and shunt are in parallel across the 500 mV drop, so the coil current is I_m = V/R_m = 0.5/1000 = 0.5 × 10⁻³ A (0.5 mA). The shunt carries 0.5/0.02 = 25 A, the total full-scale current.</p><div class="formula">I_m = V/R_m</div><div class="formula">I_sh = V/R_sh</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Coil current I_m = 0.5/1000 = <b>0.5 × 10⁻³ A</b>.</li><li>Shunt current = 0.5/0.02 = 25 A (the full-scale current).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 25 A is the shunt (and total) current.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.5 × 10⁻² A would need a 100 Ω coil.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.25 × 10⁻³ A would need a 2000 Ω coil.</li></ul><p>The total full scale current is the sum of the coil current and the shunt current: 0.5 mA + 25 A, so the shunt multiplying power is nearly 50 000.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> I_m = V/R_m; shunt carries the rest.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-41", year: 2019, no: 41, subject: "measure", topic: "Self-heating error of a voltmeter",
q: `A moving iron instrument has full scale current of 100 mA. It is converted into a 250 V voltmeter by using a series resistance of negligible temperature coefficient. The meter has a resistance of 320 Ω at 20°C. After carrying a steady current of 100 mA for a long time, the resistance of the coil increases to 369 Ω due to self heating. When a voltage of 250 V is applied continuously, the error due to self-heating will be nearly`,
opts: ["−1·1%", "−1·9%", "−2·5%", "−3·3%"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) −1·9%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Total resistance at 20 °C: 250/0.1 = 2500 Ω, so the series resistance is 2180 Ω. After heating the total is 2180 + 369 = 2549 Ω.</p><div class="formula">R_s = V/I_fs − R_m</div><div class="formula">Error = (I_new − I_fs)/I_fs</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Current falls to 250/2549 = 98.08 mA.</li><li>Error = (98.08 − 100)/100 = −1.9 %.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> −1.1 % would need a total of 2528 Ω.</li><li><b>(c)</b> −2.5 % would need 2564 Ω.</li><li><b>(d)</b> −3.3 % would need 2585 Ω.</li></ul><p>A moving-iron voltmeter has a rather low sensitivity (the full scale current 100 mA here), so the coil resistance forms a large fraction of the total and its heating produces an appreciable error.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Reading ∝ current; error = (R₂₀ − R_hot)/R_hot.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-42", year: 2019, no: 42, subject: "measure", topic: "Electrodynamometer power factor meter",
q: `There will be serious errors if power factor of non-sinusoidal waveform is measured by electrodynamometer power factor meter. This is true for`,
opts: ["Single-phase meters alone", "3-phase meters only", "Both Single-phase meters and 3-phase meters", "3-phase meters with balanced loads"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) both single-phase and three-phase meters</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A dynamometer power-factor meter deflects according to the phase angle between voltage and current of the <b>fundamental</b>. With harmonics the pointer takes up a position based on a mixture of components, so it shows a serious error for both single-phase and three-phase types.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Three-phase meters are equally affected.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Single-phase meters are equally affected.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Balanced loading does not remove the harmonic error.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Power factor of non-sinusoidal waves is not uniquely defined by the pointer.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-43", year: 2019, no: 43, subject: "measure", topic: "Ramp type digital voltmeter",
q: `The ramp type digital voltmeter can measure accurately with`,
opts: ["A positive going ramp voltage only", "A negative or positive going linear ramp voltage", "A negative going ramp voltage only", "An asymptotic ramp voltage only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) a negative or positive going linear ramp voltage</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a ramp DVM a linear ramp of known slope is compared with the unknown input; the time taken by the ramp to cross the input (and then ground) is counted. The ramp may run in either direction provided it is linear, so the measurement time is proportional to the input.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A negative-going ramp works equally well.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A positive-going ramp works equally well.</li><li><b>(d)</b> An asymptotic (exponential) ramp is not linear and would give a non-linear scale.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Ramp DVM: time ∝ voltage, requires a linear ramp.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-44", year: 2019, no: 44, subject: "measure", topic: "Distributed capacitance of a coil",
q: `The self-capacitance of a coil is measured by the resonating capacitor. The measurement gives the value of tuning capacitor as C₁ = 460 pF at a frequency f₁ = 2 MHz. The second measurement at f₂ = 4 MHz yields a new value of tuning capacitor C₂ = 100 pF. The distributed capacitance C_d will be`,
opts: ["10 pF", "20 pF", "30 pF", "40 pF"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 20 pF</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Resonance: f = 1/(2π√(L(C + C_d))). With f₂ = 2f₁: (C₁ + C_d) = 4(C₂ + C_d).</p><div class="formula">f ∝ 1/√(C + C_d)</div><div class="formula">f₂ = 2f₁ ⇒ C₁ + C_d = 4(C₂ + C_d)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>460 + C_d = 400 + 4C_d → 60 = 3C_d.</li><li>C_d = 20 pF.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 10 pF gives 470 against 440, not equal.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 30 pF gives 490 against 520.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 40 pF gives 500 against 560.</li></ul><p>The self-capacitance (distributed capacitance) of a coil makes the apparent inductance and Q depend on frequency, so it is measured with a Q-meter at two frequencies; this is the standard two-frequency method used here. Algebraically the two measurements give two equations in the two unknowns L and C_d; eliminating L (through the frequency ratio f₂ = 2f₁) leaves the simple relation (C₁ + C_d) = 4(C₂ + C_d), solved above.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> (C₁ + C_d) = (f₂/f₁)²(C₂ + C_d).</p>` },

{ id: "2019-45", year: 2019, no: 45, subject: "measure", topic: "Vertical delay line in a CRO",
q: `Vertical delay line in CRO`,
opts: ["Gives proper time for thermionic emission of electrons", "Delays the signal voltage by 200 ns", "Allows the horizontal sweep to start prior to vertical deflection", "Delays the generation of sweep voltage"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) allows the horizontal sweep to start before the vertical deflection</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The trigger circuit needs a short time to start the sweep. A delay line in the vertical channel (typically 50–200 ns) delays the signal so that the leading edge of the waveform is displayed from the start of the sweep.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Cathode heating is unrelated to the delay line.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The delay is about the order of 200 ns, but the stated purpose in the key is to let the sweep start first; option (b) names a number and not the purpose.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The delay is in the signal path, not in the sweep generator.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Delay line: see the leading edge of the trigger event.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-46", year: 2019, no: 46, subject: "measure", topic: "Limiting error of a voltmeter",
q: `A 0–150 V voltmeter has a guaranteed accuracy of 1% full scale reading. The voltage measured by this instrument is 83 V. The limiting error will be nearly`,
opts: ["1·2%", "1·8%", "2·4%", "3·2%"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1·8%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The maximum error is 1 % of 150 V = 1.5 V. At a reading of 83 V the limiting error is 1.5/83 = 1.81 %.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1.2 % would need a 125 V reading.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2.4 % would need a 62 V reading.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 3.2 % would need a 47 V reading.</li></ul><p>A voltmeter's accuracy is quoted as a percentage of full-scale deflection, so the percentage error grows as the reading falls; readings should therefore be taken in the upper part of the scale. Example: the same voltmeter reading 150 V would have a limiting error of 1 %, reading 30 V it would have 5 %; that is why the middle and upper scale are used.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Error % at reading = (FSD × % accuracy)/reading.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-47", year: 2019, no: 47, subject: "measure", topic: "Cross sensitivity",
q: `The variations in the measured quantity due to sensitivity of transducer to any plane other than the required plane is`,
opts: ["Cross sensitivity", "Sensitivity", "Interference", "Distributed sensitivity"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) cross sensitivity</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Cross (transverse) sensitivity is the response of a transducer to inputs acting along an axis perpendicular to the intended measurement axis. A strain gauge, accelerometer or Hall sensor is specified with its cross-sensitivity to show how much it picks up off-axis signals.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Sensitivity is the output per unit input in the intended direction.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Interference is an unwanted signal from outside sources.</li><li><b>(d)</b> "Distributed sensitivity" is not a standard term.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Cross sensitivity: response to off-axis input.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-48", year: 2019, no: 48, subject: "measure", topic: "Resistance change of a strain gauge",
q: `A resistance strain gauge with a gauge factor of 2 is fastened to a steel member subjected to a stress of 1050 kg/cm². The modulus of elasticity of steel is 2·1 × 10⁶ kg/cm². The change in resistance ΔR of the strain gauge element due to the applied stress will be`,
opts: ["0·1%", "0·2%", "0·3%", "0·4%"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·1%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Strain ε = σ/E = 1050/2.1 × 10⁶ = 5 × 10⁻⁴. ΔR/R = G·ε = 2 × 5 × 10⁻⁴ = 10⁻³ = 0.1 %.</p><div class="formula">ε = σ/E</div><div class="formula">ΔR/R = Gε</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ε = 1050/(2.1 × 10⁶) = 5 × 10⁻⁴.</li><li>ΔR/R = 2 × 5 × 10⁻⁴ = 10⁻³ = <b>0.1 %</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0.2 % would need a gauge factor of 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.3 % would need a gauge factor of 6.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.4 % would need a gauge factor of 8.</li></ul><p>The gauge factor of a metallic foil gauge is about 2, and of a semiconductor gauge 100 or more; a bridge circuit converts the small ΔR/R of 10⁻³ into a measurable voltage. Strain gauges are placed in a Wheatstone bridge, and four active gauges give four times the output of a single gauge; temperature compensation uses a dummy gauge in an adjacent arm.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> ΔR/R = G σ/E.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-49", year: 2019, no: 49, subject: "computer", topic: "Architecture and organisation",
q: `In which one of the following classes of computers, is the relationship between architecture and organization very close?`,
opts: ["Microcomputers", "Mini computers", "Mainframe computers", "Super computers"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) microcomputers</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In small machines (microcomputers) the organisation (the hardware implementation) is fixed to match the architecture (the instruction set), so the two are closely related. In mainframes a family of machines shares one architecture with many different organisations.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Minicomputers also have a close link, but the textbook statement is for microcomputers.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Mainframes keep the architecture constant while the organisation varies from model to model.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Supercomputers vary the organisation widely.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Architecture = programmer-visible; organisation = implementation.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-50", year: 2019, no: 50, subject: "computer", topic: "Binary to decimal",
q: `The decimal equivalent of binary number 1001·101 is`,
opts: ["9·750", "9·625", "10·750", "10·625"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 9·625</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Integer part 1001₂ = 8 + 1 = 9. Fraction .101₂ = 1/2 + 0 + 1/8 = 0.625.</p><div class="formula">Value = Σ bᵢ 2ⁱ (i negative after the point)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>1001₂ = 8 + 0 + 0 + 1 = 9.</li><li>.101₂ = 0.5 + 0 + 0.125 = 0.625.</li><li>Result <b>9.625</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.75 would be .11.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 10.75 has the wrong integer part.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 10.625 has the wrong integer part.</li></ul><p>The binary fraction 0.101 equals 1/2 + 1/8 = 0.625; each digit to the right of the point carries a weight of half the previous one. Another check is to convert 1001.101 as 8 + 1 + 0.5 + 0.125 = 9.625; the three fractional bits cover the resolution 1/8 = 0.125. Octal and hexadecimal groupings give the same value: in hexadecimal the integer part is 9 and the fraction .101₂ = .1010₂ = A/16, so the value is 9.A₁₆ = 9.625.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Weights: 2³…2⁰ . 2⁻¹ 2⁻² 2⁻³.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2019c.js ----
// ESE 2019 Prelims Paper II, Electrical Engineering, Series A (VBY-S-ETE), Q51–75
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; none dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2019-51", year: 2019, no: 51, subject: "computer", topic: "Decimal to binary",
q: `Convert decimal 41·6875 into equivalent binary`,
opts: ["100101·1011", "100101·1101", "101001·1011", "101001·1101"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 101001·1011</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> 41 = 32 + 8 + 1 = 101001₂. 0.6875 = 0.5 + 0.125 + 0.0625 = 0.1011₂ (×2: 1.375 → 1; 0.375 × 2 = 0.75 → 0; 0.75 × 2 = 1.5 → 1; 0.5 × 2 = 1.0 → 1).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 100101 is 37.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 100101 is 37 and .1101 is 0.8125.</li><li><b>(d)</b> .1101 is 0.8125.</li></ul><p>So 41.6875 = 101001.1011₂.</p><p>The conversion can be checked by converting back: 101001.1011₂ = 32 + 8 + 1 + 0.5 + 0.125 + 0.0625 = 41.6875. The integer part is found by successive division: 41 ÷ 2 gives remainders 1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1 reading upward, which is 101001; the fraction 0.6875 by successive multiplication gives 1, 0, 1, 1.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Repeated division by 2; repeated multiplication of the fraction.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-52", year: 2019, no: 52, subject: "computer", topic: "CPU components",
q: `The Central Processing Unit (CPU) consists of`,
opts: ["ALU and Control unit only", "ALU, Control unit and Registers only", "ALU, Control unit and System bus only", "ALU, Control unit, Registers and Internal bus"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) ALU, control unit, registers and internal bus</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The CPU contains the arithmetic logic unit, the control unit, the register set and the internal CPU bus which connects them. The system bus lies outside, connecting the CPU to memory and I/O.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Leaves out the registers and the internal bus.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Leaves out the internal bus.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The system bus is external to the CPU.</li></ul><p>The control unit fetches, decodes and executes instructions; the ALU performs arithmetic and logic operations; and the registers (program counter, accumulator, instruction register) hold the data and addresses being processed.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> CPU = ALU + CU + registers + internal bus.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-53", year: 2019, no: 53, subject: "computer", topic: "Fragmentation",
q: `When enough total memory space exists to satisfy a request, but it is not contiguous, this problem is known as`,
opts: ["Internal Fragmentation", "External Fragmentation", "Overlays", "Partitioning"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) external fragmentation</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> External fragmentation occurs when free memory is split into small non-contiguous holes, none big enough for a request even though the total is enough. Internal fragmentation is waste inside an allocated block.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Internal fragmentation is unused space inside an allocated partition.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Overlays reuse memory for different program parts.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Partitioning is the division of memory, not the problem.</li></ul><p>Compaction (moving allocated blocks together) and paging are the two ways to remove external fragmentation; paging eliminates it by allocating fixed-size frames.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Cure: compaction or paging.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-54", year: 2019, no: 54, subject: "computer", topic: "Disk access time",
q: `The total average read or write time T_total is (T_s = average seek time, b = bytes to be transferred, N = bytes on a track, r = rotation speed in revolutions per second)`,
opts: ["T_s + 1/2r + b/N", "T_s + 1/2r + b/rN", "T_s/rN + b/N", "T_s + 2r + b/rN"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) T_s + 1/2r + b/rN</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Total = seek time + rotational latency + transfer time. Average rotational latency is half a revolution = 1/(2r). Transfer time = b/(rN) (the whole track of N bytes passes in 1/r seconds).</p><div class="formula">T = T_s + 1/(2r) + b/(rN)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Seek time T_s.</li><li>Average rotational latency = half a revolution = 1/(2r).</li><li>Transfer: N bytes pass in 1/r s ⇒ b bytes take b/(rN).</li><li>Total = <b>T_s + 1/2r + b/rN</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The transfer term is b/(rN), not b/N.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The seek time is added, not divided.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Rotational latency is 1/(2r), not 2r.</li></ul><p>For a disk with r = 120 rev/s the average rotational latency is 1/(2r) = 4.2 ms; the seek time is usually of the order of 5–10 ms and dominates for small transfers.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> T = Ts + 1/2r + b/rN.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-55", year: 2019, no: 55, subject: "computer", topic: "Cache line fetch",
q: `If a cache has 64-byte cache lines, how long does it take to fetch a cache line if the main memory takes 20 cycles to respond to each memory request and returns 2 bytes of data in response to each request?`,
opts: ["980 cycles", "640 cycles", "320 cycles", "160 cycles"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 640 cycles</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A 64-byte line needs 64/2 = 32 requests; each takes 20 cycles. Total = 32 × 20 = 640 cycles.</p><div class="formula">Requests = line size/bytes per request</div><div class="formula">Time = requests × cycles per request</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>64/2 = 32 requests.</li><li>32 × 20 = <b>640 cycles</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 980 is not a multiple of 20 × 32.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 320 would be 16 requests (4-byte returns).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 160 would be 8 requests.</li></ul><p>A wider memory bus, returning 8 bytes per request, would cut the line-fill time to 8 × 20 = 160 cycles, which is why cache line fills use burst transfers. The count assumes no overlap of the memory cycles: if the memory could return data every cycle after the first access (pipelined or burst mode), the fill would take only 20 + 31 cycles.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Requests = line size / bytes per request.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-56", year: 2019, no: 56, subject: "computer", topic: "SRAM versus DRAM",
q: `Which of the following statements are correct about SRAM? 1. Faster access than DRAM. 2. Cheaper than DRAM. 3. More expensive than DRAM. 4. Higher bit density than DRAM.`,
opts: ["1 and 4 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "2 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> SRAM stores a bit in a six-transistor flip-flop:</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: no refresh and faster access.</li><li><b>2</b> False: it is more costly per bit.</li><li><b>3</b> True.</li><li><b>4</b> False: more transistors per bit means lower density (DRAM uses one transistor and one capacitor).</li></ul><p>SRAM is used for cache memory, DRAM for main memory; DRAM needs periodic refresh because the charge on its capacitor leaks away, which also makes it slower. SRAM bit cells have six transistors against DRAM's one transistor plus one capacitor, which explains both the higher cost per bit and the lower bit density of SRAM.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> SRAM: fast, costly, less dense; DRAM: slow, cheap, dense.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-57", year: 2019, no: 57, subject: "computer", topic: "Solid state drives",
q: `Features of solid state drives (SSDs) are 1. High performance in input/output operations per second 2. More power consumption than comparable size HDDs 3. Lower access times and latency rates 4. More susceptible to physical shock and vibration`,
opts: ["2 and 3 only", "2 and 4 only", "1 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> SSDs have no moving parts:</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: high IOPS.</li><li><b>2</b> False: they consume less power than HDDs.</li><li><b>3</b> True: no seek or rotational delay.</li><li><b>4</b> False: with no moving parts they are <b>less</b> susceptible to shock and vibration.</li></ul><p>Flash-based SSDs have limited write endurance, since each cell can be written only a limited number of times, but their reading speed and lack of mechanical parts give them a large advantage over hard disks. IOPS stands for input/output operations per second: an SSD reaches tens of thousands, a hard disk only about 100–200, because the disk head has to move between requests.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> SSD: fast, low power, rugged.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-58", year: 2019, no: 58, subject: "computer", topic: "One's complement",
q: `The decimal value of signed binary number 11101000 expressed in 1's complement is`,
opts: ["−223", "−184", "−104", "−23"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) −23</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The sign bit is 1, so the number is negative. Invert all bits: 00010111 = 16 + 4 + 2 + 1 = 23. The value is −23.</p><div class="formula">1's complement negative: value = −(bitwise inverse)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>MSB = 1 ⇒ negative.</li><li>Invert 11101000 → 00010111 = 23.</li><li>Value = <b>−23</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 223 is the unsigned complement 255 − 32 misread.</li><li><b>(b)</b> −184 does not come from inverting the bits (00010111 = 23).</li><li><b>(c)</b> −104 would be the two's-complement-style misreading of the magnitude bits.</li></ul><p>In one's complement the number range for 8 bits is −127 to +127, and there are two zeros (00000000 and 11111111); two's complement removes the double zero. To read the magnitude, invert 11101000 to 00010111 = 23 and apply the sign: the answer is −23; in two's complement the same word would be −24.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> 1's complement: invert the bits and read the magnitude.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-59", year: 2019, no: 59, subject: "computer", topic: "Virtual memory functions",
q: `The memory management function of virtual memory includes 1. Space allocation 2. Program relocation 3. Program execution 4. Code sharing`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 2 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Virtual memory management covers allocating space, relocating programs (any page can be placed anywhere) and sharing code pages between processes. Program execution is a function of the CPU and the scheduler, not a memory management function.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> Yes.</li><li><b>2</b> Yes.</li><li><b>3</b> No: execution belongs to the processor.</li><li><b>4</b> Yes: shared pages (e.g. libraries).</li></ul><p>Virtual memory lets a program use more address space than the physical memory by keeping pages on disk; the memory management unit translates the virtual addresses to physical ones, and page faults bring missing pages in.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Memory management: allocation, relocation, protection, sharing.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-60", year: 2019, no: 60, subject: "computer", topic: "Implied addressing in 8085",
q: `Which of the following instructions of 8085 are the examples of implied addressing? 1. CMA 2. IN byte 3. RET`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In implied addressing the operand is implicit in the opcode.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> CMA complements the accumulator: implied.</li><li><b>2</b> IN byte reads from a port address given in the instruction: direct (I/O) addressing.</li><li><b>3</b> RET pops the return address from the stack: implied.</li></ul><p>Other 8085 addressing modes are register (MOV A, B), immediate (MVI A, 05H), direct (LDA 2050H) and register indirect (MOV A, M); implied instructions need no operand in the instruction. CMA (complement accumulator) and RET (return) are implied-address instructions because the accumulator and the stack pointer are named by the opcode itself, so no operand bytes follow.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Implied: operands understood from the opcode.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-61", year: 2019, no: 61, subject: "basic", topic: "Collector current",
q: `The important fact about the collector current is`,
opts: ["It is greater than emitter current", "It equals the base current divided by the current gain", "It is small", "It approximately equals the emitter current"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) it approximately equals the emitter current</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> I_E = I_C + I_B and the base current is small, so I_C = αI_E ≈ I_E (α ≈ 0.98–0.99).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> I_C is slightly smaller than I_E.</li><li><b>(b)</b> I_B = I_C/β, not the other way round.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The collector current is the large current.</li></ul><p>The base current is only 1–5 % of the emitter current, so I_C ≈ I_E and the current gain β = I_C/I_B is typically 50–300. The collector current is controlled by the base-emitter voltage and is almost independent of the collector-emitter voltage in the active region, which is why the transistor acts as a current source.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> I_C = α I_E ≈ I_E.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-62", year: 2019, no: 62, subject: "basic", topic: "Shockley's diode equation",
q: `What is Shockley's equation of a semiconductor diode in the forward bias regions?`,
opts: ["I_D = I_S(e^{V_D²/nV_T} − 1)", "I_D = I_S(e^{V_D/nV_T} − 1)", "I_D = I_S(e^{nV_D/V_T} − 1)", "I_D = I_S(e^{V_T/nV_D} − 1)"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) I_D = I_S(e^{V_D/nV_T} − 1)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The diode current is I_D = I_S(exp(V_D/(nV_T)) − 1), with n the ideality factor (1–2) in the denominator along with V_T.</p><div class="formula">I_D = I_S(e^(V_D/(nV_T)) − 1)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Forward bias: exponent V_D/(nV_T), with ideality factor n (1–2) and V_T = kT/q.</li><li>Option (b).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> V_D appears to the first power.</li><li><b>(c)</b> n multiplies V_T, not V_D.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The exponent is V_D/(nV_T), not its inverse.</li></ul><p>At room temperature nV_T ≈ 26 mV for n = 1, so the current rises e-fold for every 26 mV increase of the forward voltage; at 0.7 V a silicon diode conducts strongly. At zero bias the exponent is zero and the current is zero; for reverse bias of a few V_T the current saturates at −I_S, the reverse saturation current of the diode.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> I = I_S(e^{V/nV_T} − 1).</p>` },

{ id: "2019-63", year: 2019, no: 63, subject: "basic", topic: "Thermal voltage",
q: `The thermal voltage V_T of a semiconductor diode at 27°C temperature is nearly`,
opts: ["17 mV", "20 mV", "23 mV", "26 mV"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 26 mV</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> V_T = kT/q = 8.617 × 10⁻⁵ × 300 = 25.9 mV ≈ 26 mV.</p><div class="formula">V_T = kT/q</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>T = 27 + 273 = 300 K.</li><li>V_T = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 300/1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 25.9 mV ≈ <b>26 mV</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 17 mV would need T = 197 K.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 20 mV would need 232 K.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 23 mV would need 267 K.</li></ul><p>V_T = kT/q increases with temperature by 0.086 mV/K; at 27 °C (300 K) it equals 25.85 mV, which is commonly rounded to 26 mV in problems. Thermal voltage appears in the Shockley equation, in the Early-effect analysis and in the relation between mobility and diffusion constant (D/μ = V_T, the Einstein relation). At 77 °C (350 K) it would be 30.2 mV and at −40 °C (233 K) only 20 mV, which is why diode drops vary with temperature by about −2 mV/°C at constant current.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> V_T = T/11600 = 25.85 mV at 300 K.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-64", year: 2019, no: 64, subject: "basic", topic: "MOSFET compared with BJT",
q: `The disadvantage of a typical MOSFET as compared to BJT is`,
opts: ["Increased power-handling levels", "Reduced power-handling levels", "Increased voltage-handling levels", "Reduced voltage-handling levels"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) reduced power-handling levels</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A typical small-signal MOSFET has high input impedance and low noise but handles less power than a BJT of the same size, whose collector can carry much larger current.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> This is an advantage, not a disadvantage.</li><li><b>(c)</b> This is an advantage, not a disadvantage.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The key chooses the power-handling disadvantage.</li></ul><p>Power MOSFETs and IGBTs do exist for high power, but a typical small-signal MOSFET has less power-handling capacity than a BJT; MOSFETs have the advantage of high input resistance and of not suffering thermal runaway.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> MOSFET: high Zin, low power capability (typical).</p>` },

{ id: "2019-65", year: 2019, no: 65, subject: "basic", topic: "Impedance matched system",
q: `Which one of the following conditions will be satisfied for an impedance matched system?`,
opts: ["The decibel power gain is equal to twice the decibel voltage gain", "The decibel power gain is equal to the decibel voltage gain", "The decibel power gain is half the decibel voltage gain", "The decibel power gain is equal to thrice the decibel voltage gain"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) the dB power gain equals the dB voltage gain</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> dB power gain = 10 log(P₂/P₁) = 10 log(V₂²R₁/(V₁²R₂)). When the impedances are matched (R₁ = R₂) this becomes 20 log(V₂/V₁), the dB voltage gain.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The factor 2 is already included in 20 log versus 10 log.</li><li><b>(c)</b> No such relation exists.</li><li><b>(d)</b> No such relation exists.</li></ul><p>In the decibel scale the power gain is 10 log(P₂/P₁), the voltage gain 20 log(V₂/V₁), and the two agree if the input and output impedances are equal.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Matched impedances: dB power gain = dB voltage gain.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-66", year: 2019, no: 66, subject: "basic", topic: "Input impedances of FET configurations",
q: `For most FET configurations and for common-gate configurations, the input impedances are respectively`,
opts: ["High and high", "High and low", "Low and low", "Low and high"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) high and low</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The gate of an FET draws almost no current, so in common-source and common-drain configurations the input impedance is very high. In the common-gate configuration the input is at the source, with r_i ≈ 1/g_m, which is low.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The common-gate input is low.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Most FET configurations have a high input impedance.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The order is reversed.</li></ul><p>The common-source FET amplifier has a high input impedance (megaohms) and the common-drain (source follower) has a high input and a low output impedance; the common gate has a low input impedance and is used at high frequency.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> CS, CD: high Zin; CG: ~1/g_m.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-67", year: 2019, no: 67, subject: "basic", topic: "dB gain of cascaded stages",
q: `The dB gain of cascaded systems is simply`,
opts: ["The square of the dB gains of each stage", "The sum of the dB gains of each stage", "The multiplication of the dB gains of each stage", "The division of the dB gains of each stage"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) the sum of the dB gains of each stage</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The gains multiply, and since dB gain = 20 log A, the logarithm turns the product into a sum: A_dB = A₁,dB + A₂,dB + …</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Squares are not involved.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Multiplication applies to the gains as ratios.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Division would give a ratio, not a cascade.</li></ul><p>For cascaded stages with gains of 20 dB and 30 dB the total gain is 50 dB, corresponding to voltage gains of 10 and 31.6 multiplying to 316. A voltage amplifier with 40 dB gain followed by a filter with −6 dB loss and a second amplifier with 26 dB gain has a total of 40 − 6 + 26 = 60 dB.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Cascade: multiply gains = add dB.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-68", year: 2019, no: 68, subject: "basic", topic: "Miller input capacitance",
q: `The Miller effect input capacitance C_Mi is (C_f = feedback capacitance, A_V = V_o/V_i)`,
opts: ["(1 − A_V²)C_f", "(1 − A_V)C_f", "(1 − C_f)A_V", "(1 − C_f²)A_V"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) (1 − A_V) C_f</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A feedback capacitance C_f between output and input carries a current (V_i − V_o)jωC_f = V_i(1 − A_V)jωC_f. It looks like a capacitance (1 − A_V)C_f at the input; for an inverting amplifier with gain A_V = −|A|, C_Mi = (1 + |A|)C_f.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The factor is (1 − A_V), not (1 − A_V²).</li><li><b>(c)</b> The capacitance multiplies (1 − A_V).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Same error.</li></ul><p>The Miller effect makes a small feedback capacitance appear much larger at the input of an inverting amplifier, and is the main cause of the high-frequency roll-off in common-emitter and common-source stages.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Miller: C_in = (1 − A_V) C_f.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-69", year: 2019, no: 69, subject: "ad", topic: "Maximum gain from the slew rate",
q: `For an op-amp having a slew rate of 2 V/μs, if the input signal varies by 0·5 V in 10 μs, the maximum closed-loop voltage gain will be`,
opts: ["50", "40", "22", "20"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 40</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The output can change by at most SR × t = 2 × 10 = 20 V in 10 μs. The input changes by 0.5 V, so the maximum gain is 20/0.5 = 40.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 50 would need an output swing of 25 V.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 22 would need 11 V.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 20 would need 10 V.</li></ul><p>The slew rate limits the large-signal bandwidth: a gain-40 amplifier with this op-amp could not follow an input that changes by 0.5 V in less than 10 μs without the output becoming a distorted ramp. The input would also be limited by the full-power bandwidth f_max = SR/(2πV_p); for SR = 2 V/μs and a 10 V peak output this is about 32 kHz.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> A_max = SR·Δt/ΔV_in.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-70", year: 2019, no: 70, subject: "basic", topic: "Feedback topology",
q: `A negative feedback amplifier where an input current controls an output voltage is called`,
opts: ["Current amplifier", "Transconductance amplifier", "Transresistance amplifier", "Voltage amplifier"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) transresistance amplifier</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Input current to output voltage is the transfer ratio V_o/I_i, a resistance: a transresistance (current-to-voltage) amplifier, which uses shunt-shunt feedback.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A current amplifier has current input and current output.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A transconductance amplifier has voltage input and current output.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A voltage amplifier has voltage input and voltage output.</li></ul><p>The four feedback topologies are: series-shunt (voltage amplifier), shunt-series (current amplifier), series-series (transconductance) and shunt-shunt (transresistance).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Output/input: V/I = transresistance.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-71", year: 2019, no: 71, subject: "basic", topic: "Battery charge maintenance",
q: `In emergency lighting system, the component used for maintaining the charge on the battery is`,
opts: ["LED", "Shockley diode", "Thermistor", "SCR"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) SCR</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A thyristor (SCR) based rectifier with phase control delivers a regulated trickle or float charge to the battery; the SCR can also switch the lamps in on mains failure.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> An LED is an indicator.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A Shockley diode is a four-layer trigger device, used for triggering.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A thermistor senses temperature.</li></ul><p>The same SCR bridge can serve as a battery charger and as an inverter in uninterruptible power supplies; the lamp load of an emergency lighting system is switched by a relay or by the thyristor on failure of the mains.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> SCR charger: controlled rectifier.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-72", year: 2019, no: 72, subject: "basic", topic: "RC phase shift oscillator gain",
q: `For RC phase shift oscillator using FET, the gain of the amplifier stage must be practically somewhat greater than`,
opts: ["27", "28", "29", "30"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 29</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The three-section RC network attenuates the signal by 29 at the frequency where it gives 180° phase shift (β = 1/29), so the amplifier gain must exceed 29.</p><div class="formula">β_RC = 1/29 at f = 1/(2π√6 RC)</div><div class="formula">|A|β ≥ 1</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Network attenuation 1/29 at 180° phase shift.</li><li>|A| ≥ 29 ⇒ practically somewhat greater than <b>29</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 27 is below 29, so oscillation would die out.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 28 is below 29.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 30 is greater than 29, but the minimum is 29.</li></ul><p>The RC phase-shift oscillator oscillates at f = 1/(2π√6 RC) with each section shifting the phase by 60°; the amplifier gain of 29 compensates the loss in the network. With an FET amplifier the gain is g_m R_d (or g_m R_L), and the oscillation condition is g_m R_d &gt; 29 for equal resistors R in the phase-shift network, as used here.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> |A| > 29 for three-section RC.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-73", year: 2019, no: 73, subject: "ad", topic: "Look-ahead carry adder",
q: `The time delay in a look-ahead carry adder is independent of`,
opts: ["Number of operands only", "Propagation delay only", "Number of bits in the operand only", "Bits in the operand, number of operands and propagation delay"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) number of bits in the operand only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A look-ahead carry generator computes all carries in parallel from the generate and propagate signals, so the delay is a fixed number of gate delays, independent of the number of bits.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The delay still depends on the gate propagation delay.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The delay is proportional to the gate propagation delay.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The delay does depend on the propagation delay.</li></ul><p>The ripple-carry adder has a delay proportional to the number of bits, since each carry must wait for the previous stage; the look-ahead adder replaces this by parallel carry generation at the cost of more gates.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Look-ahead carry: constant delay.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-74", year: 2019, no: 74, subject: "ad", topic: "SISO shift register delay",
q: `The time delay Δt introduced by a SISO shift register in digital signals is given by (N = number of stages, f_c = clock frequency)`,
opts: ["N² × 1/f_c", "N² × f_c", "f_c/N", "N × 1/f_c"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) N × 1/f_c</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Each clock pulse moves the data one stage, so it takes N clock periods to reach the output: Δt = N T_c = N/f_c.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The delay is linear in N.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Wrong dimension.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Wrong dimension.</li></ul><p>A SISO register of N stages clocked at f_c delays a serial data stream by N/f_c; with 8 stages and a 1 MHz clock the delay is 8 μs. For example, an 8-stage register with a 2 MHz clock gives a delay of 8 × 0.5 μs = 4 μs; the data are shifted in and out serially, so the first bit appears after N clock pulses.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Delay = N clock periods.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-75", year: 2019, no: 75, subject: "ad", topic: "D/A converter output",
q: `An analog output voltage for the input 1001 to a 4 bit D/A converter, assuming the proportionality factor K = 1, will be`,
opts: ["9", "6", "3", "1"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 9</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> V_o = K × (decimal value of the input) = 1 × 1001₂ = 9.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 6 is the value of 0110.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 3 is the value of 0011.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1 is the value of 0001.</li></ul><p>A 4-bit D/A converter has 16 output levels, from 0 to 15 times K; its resolution is K per step, and the input 1001 selects level 9. The resolution of the converter is K × 1 per step and the full-scale output is 15K; the analog output is the weighted sum 8b₃ + 4b₂ + 2b₁ + b₀ times K, which for 1001 is 8 + 1 = 9. The unit of the output depends on K (volts per count), so a converter with K = 0.5 V would give 4.5 V for the same input; here K = 1 so the answer equals the decimal count.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> V_o = K·D.</p>` }
);

