// ESE 2019 Prelims Paper II, Electrical Engineering, Series A (VBY-S-ETE), Q126–150
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; none dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2019-126", year: 2019, no: 126, subject: "power", topic: "Most frequent fault",
q: `Which one of the following faults occurs more frequently in a power system?`,
opts: ["Grounded star-delta", "Double line faults", "LLG faults", "Single line-to-ground (LG) faults"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) single line-to-ground faults</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> About 70–80 % of the faults on a transmission system are single line-to-ground faults (a flashover of one conductor to earth); line-to-line about 10 %, double line-to-ground 10–15 % and three-phase faults 2–5 %.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A transformer connection is not a fault.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Double line faults are about 10 %.</li><li><b>(c)</b> LLG faults are about 10–15 %.</li></ul><p>Line-to-ground faults are mainly caused by lightning flashover to the tower and by conductors touching trees; they are cleared by a single-pole auto-reclose scheme in many systems.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> LG: ~75 % of all faults.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-127", year: 2019, no: 127, subject: "power", topic: "Permissible de-energisation time", doubt: true,
q: `The maximum permissible time of de-energization of the faulty circuit is dependent on`,
opts: ["Voltage of the system", "The number of conductors involved", "Load carried by the faulty circuit", "Fault current and its duration"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) load carried by the faulty circuit (as keyed)</h3><p class="note">In doubt (key followed). The fault clearing time is set by the energy let through (fault current and its duration), which was our answer (d); the key's (c) is shown.</p><p><b>Concept:</b> The key's answer is the load carried by the faulty circuit. The thermal limit of the equipment depends on I²t, but the question as keyed relates the permissible outage time to how much load is lost, i.e. the criticality of the circuit.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The voltage fixes the insulation level, not the outage time.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The number of conductors involved fixes the fault type.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The fault current and duration fix the thermal damage (I²t), which would normally limit the clearing time; this was our answer.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Clearing time is normally limited by I²t.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-128", year: 2019, no: 128, subject: "power", topic: "Interrupters for HVDC breakers", doubt: true,
q: `Which one of the following is used for communication with the aim of achieving high figure of merit in HVDC circuit breakers?`,
opts: ["Oil interrupter", "Air interrupter", "Vacuum interrupter", "SF₆ interrupter"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) vacuum interrupter (as keyed)</h3><p class="note">In doubt (key followed). The wording of the question is unclear. SF₆ interrupters are widely used for HVDC circuit breakers, but the key's (c) vacuum interrupter is shown.</p><p><b>Concept:</b> HVDC breakers need to create an artificial current zero and to absorb the stored energy. The key names the vacuum interrupter as the one with the best figure of merit in this role (very fast dielectric recovery and small contact gap).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Oil interrupters are slow and obsolete.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Air interrupters give lower dielectric recovery.</li><li><b>(d)</b> SF₆ interrupters are used in ac breakers and some HVDC designs; this was our answer.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> HVDC breaker: needs fast dielectric recovery.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-129", year: 2019, no: 129, subject: "power", topic: "Buses in the Ybus formation",
q: `Which of the following buses are used to form bus admittance matrix for load flow analysis? 1. Load bus 2. Generator bus 3. Slack bus`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The bus admittance matrix Y_bus is built from the network (lines, transformers, shunts) and includes <b>every</b> bus: load buses, generator (PV) buses and the slack bus. Its size is n × n for n buses; the distinction between bus types matters only in the solution algorithm.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> Included.</li><li><b>2</b> Included.</li><li><b>3</b> Included.</li></ul><p>In the load-flow solution the Ybus is formed once from the network data; the slack bus holds the reference voltage and angle and takes up the system losses.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Y_bus has one row for every bus.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-130", year: 2019, no: 130, subject: "power", topic: "Synchronous speeds of a generator",
q: `In a 3-phase, 60 Hz, 500 MVA, 15 kV, 32-pole hydroelectric generating unit, the values of ω_syn and ω_msyn will be nearly`,
opts: ["754 rad/s and 47·6 rad/s", "377 rad/s and 46·7 rad/s", "377 rad/s and 23·6 rad/s", "754 rad/s and 23·6 rad/s"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 377 rad/s and 23·6 rad/s</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The electrical synchronous speed is ω_syn = 2π × 60 = 377 rad/s. The mechanical speed is ω_msyn = ω_syn/(P/2) = 377/16 = 23.6 rad/s (225 rpm).</p><div class="formula">ω_syn = 2πf</div><div class="formula">ω_msyn = ω_syn/(P/2)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ω_syn = 2π × 60 = <b>377 rad/s</b>.</li><li>ω_msyn = 377/16 = <b>23.6 rad/s</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 754 is 4π × 60.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 46.7 is 377/8.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 754 rad/s is wrong for 60 Hz.</li></ul><p>For a hydro-generator with many poles the mechanical speed is low: N = 120f/P = 120 × 60/32 = 225 rpm, which corresponds to 23.6 rad/s. With P = 32 poles, p = 16 pole pairs; ω_m = ω_e/p = 376.99/16 = 23.56 rad/s, and the machine's synchronous speed N_s = 225 rpm.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> ω_m = ω_e/(P/2).</p>` },

{ id: "2019-131", year: 2019, no: 131, subject: "power", topic: "Improving steady-state stability",
q: `The methods adopted for improving the steady state stability of power system are 1. Quick response excitation system 2. Higher excitation voltages 3. Maximum power transfer by use of series capacitor or reactor`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The steady-state limit is P_max = EV/X. It is raised by:</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> A quick-response excitation system, which keeps E high when the load increases.</li><li><b>2</b> Higher excitation voltage (larger E).</li><li><b>3</b> Reducing the transfer reactance X, for example with series capacitors (or by changing the line reactance).</li></ul><p>Besides these, reducing the line reactance by bundling conductors or using parallel lines, and using higher transmission voltage, improves the steady state stability limit.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> P_max = EV/X: raise E and V, reduce X.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-132", year: 2019, no: 132, subject: "pe", topic: "HVDC converter stations",
q: `The HVDC system uses`,
opts: ["Rectifier station at sending end and inverter station at receiving end", "Inverter station at sending as well as at the receiving end", "Rectifier station at sending end as well as at the receiving end", "Inverter station at sending end and rectifier station at receiving end"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) rectifier at the sending end and inverter at the receiving end</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At the sending end the ac is rectified to dc for transmission; at the receiving end the dc is inverted back to ac. (In bidirectional links each station can work in either mode.)</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Two inverters would not transmit power.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Two rectifiers would not deliver ac.</li><li><b>(d)</b> This reverses the direction of power flow.</li></ul><p>At the receiving end of a bipolar HVDC link the inverter works with a firing advance angle of about 15–20°, and the two ac systems need not be synchronised.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Sending: rectifier; receiving: inverter.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-133", year: 2019, no: 133, subject: "pe", topic: "Requirements of a power diode",
q: `Which one of the following is not required for power diode?`,
opts: ["High speed operation", "Fast communication", "Small recovery time", "Low on-state voltage drop"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) fast communication</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A power diode should switch fast, have a short reverse recovery time and a low forward voltage drop (to reduce conduction loss). "Fast communication" is not a power-diode requirement (it relates to data transmission).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Fast switching is a desirable property.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Small recovery time is desirable.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Low on-state drop is desirable.</li></ul><p>Power diodes are specified by peak inverse voltage, average forward current, forward drop and reverse recovery time; fast-recovery diodes have t_rr of 0.1–1 μs and Schottky diodes even less.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Power diode: fast, short t_rr, low V_F.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-134", year: 2019, no: 134, subject: "pe", topic: "Reverse recovery charge",
q: `The reverse recovery time of a diode is t_rr = 3 μs and the rate of fall of the diode current is di/dt = 30 A/μs. The storage charge Q_RR and the peak inverse current I_RR will be respectively`,
opts: ["135 μC and 90 A", "270 μC and 90 A", "270 μC and 60 A", "135 μC and 60 A"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 135 μC and 90 A</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> I_RR = (di/dt) t_rr = 30 × 3 = 90 A (taking the whole t_rr as the fall time). The stored charge is the area of the triangle: Q_RR = ½ I_RR t_rr = ½ × 90 × 3 = 135 μC.</p><div class="formula">I_RR = (di/dt) t_rr</div><div class="formula">Q_RR = ½I_RR t_rr</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I_RR = 30 × 3 = <b>90 A</b>.</li><li>Q_RR = 0.5 × 90 × 3 μs = <b>135 μC</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 270 μC omits the factor ½.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Both values are wrong.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I_RR = 60 A would need di/dt = 20 A/μs.</li></ul><p>The reverse recovery causes a large reverse current spike and a voltage spike across the circuit inductance, which is why snubbers are connected across the diode. A softer recovery (a gradual current decay) lowers the voltage spike; the softness factor is the ratio of the fall time to the rise time of the recovery current.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> I_RR = (di/dt)t_rr; Q = ½ I_RR t_rr.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-135", year: 2019, no: 135, subject: "pe", topic: "Gate voltage of a thyristor",
q: `The i_g–v_g characteristics of a thyristor is a straight line passing through origin with a gradient of 2·5 × 10³. If P_g = 0·015 watt, the value of gate voltage will be nearly`,
opts: ["5·0 V", "6·1 V", "7·5 V", "8·5 V"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 6·1 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The line v_g = 2.5 × 10³ i_g (gate resistance 2.5 kΩ). P_g = v_g i_g = v_g²/2500 = 0.015.</p><div class="formula">v_g = R_g i_g</div><div class="formula">P_g = v_g²/R_g</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>v_g² = 37.5 → v_g = 6.12 V.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 5 V would give P = 10 mW.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 7.5 V would give 22.5 mW.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 8.5 V would give 28.9 mW.</li></ul><p>The gate characteristic of a thyristor lies between the maximum and minimum curves of the manufacturer's data; the gate drive load line must cross it inside the preferred gate-firing area. Gate power: P_g = v_g × i_g with i_g = v_g/2500; so v_g² = 0.015 × 2500 = 37.5 and v_g = 6.12 V, i_g = 2.45 mA, within typical gate-trigger limits.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> v = Rg i, P = v²/R.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-136", year: 2019, no: 136, subject: "pe", topic: "Half-wave controlled rectifier with a resistive load",
q: `A single-phase 220 V, 1 kW heater is connected to half wave controlled rectifier and is fed from a 220 V, 50 Hz ac supply. When the firing angle α = 90°, the power absorbed by the heater will be nearly`,
opts: ["1000 W", "750 W", "500 W", "250 W"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 250 W</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a half-wave controlled rectifier the rms load voltage is V_rms = V_s√[(1/2π)(π − α + sin 2α/2)].</p><div class="formula">V_rms = V_s√[(1/2π)(π − α + sin2α/2)]</div><div class="formula">P = V_rms²/R</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>At α = 90°: (1/2π)(π/2 + 0) = 1/4, so V_rms = V_s/2.</li><li>P = V_rms²/R = (1/4)(V_s²/R) = 1000/4 = 250 W.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1000 W is the full ac power.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 750 W exceeds the half-wave maximum of 500 W (α = 0).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 500 W is the power of an uncontrolled half-wave rectifier (α = 0).</li></ul><p>For comparison, a full-wave controlled rectifier with a resistive load would give 500 W at α = 90°, twice as much, because both half cycles are used.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> P(α) = P₀[1/2 − α/2π + sin 2α/4π].</p>` },

{ id: "2019-137", year: 2019, no: 137, subject: "pe", topic: "Half-bridge and full-bridge converters",
q: `When we compare the half bridge converter and full bridge converter: 1. The maximum collector current of a full bridge is only double that of the half bridge. 2. Full bridge uses 4-power switches instead of 2, as in the double bridge. 3. Output power of a full bridge is twice that of a half bridge with the same input voltage and current.`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Comparison of the two bridges:</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: with the same supply, the full-bridge switch current is the same as in the half bridge, and it is the output voltage that doubles.</li><li><b>2</b> True: four switches against two.</li><li><b>3</b> True: the load voltage swings ±V_dc instead of ±V_dc/2 for the same current, so the power is twice.</li></ul><p>In a full bridge inverter the output is ±V_dc, the maximum available, while in the half bridge it is ±V_dc/2; for this reason the full bridge is used for larger powers.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Full bridge: twice the output voltage, twice the power.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-138", year: 2019, no: 138, subject: "pe", topic: "Single-phase fully controlled bridge with RLE load",
q: `A single-phase fully controlled bridge converter is connected with RLE load where R = 5 Ω, L = 4 mH and E = 50 V. This converter circuit is supplied from 220 V, 50 Hz ac supply. When the firing angle is 60°, the average value of the load current will be nearly`,
opts: ["12·2 A", "9·8 A", "6·4 A", "4·2 A"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 9·8 A</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> V_o = (2V_m/π)cos α = (2 × 311.1/π) × 0.5 = 99.0 V.</p><div class="formula">V_o = (2V_m/π) cos α</div><div class="formula">I_o = (V_o − E)/R</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I_o = (V_o − E)/R = (99.0 − 50)/5 = 9.8 A (continuous conduction assumed).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 12.2 A would need V_o = 111 V.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 6.4 A would need V_o = 82 V.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 4.2 A would need V_o = 71 V.</li></ul><p>For discontinuous conduction the average voltage would be higher than 99 V because the load voltage equals E when the current is zero; with L = 4 mH the current is continuous here. The average output voltage of the single-phase fully controlled bridge is (2V_m/π)cos α = 0.9V_s cos α = 0.9 × 220 × 0.5 = 99 V; subtracting the back-emf of 50 V gives the voltage across R.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> V_o = (2V_m/π) cos α; I_o = (V_o − E)/R.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-139", year: 2019, no: 139, subject: "pe", topic: "AC drives",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding ac drives: 1. For the same kW rating, ac motors are 20% to 40% light weight as compared to dc motors. 2. The ac motors are more expensive as compared to same kW rating dc motors. 3. The ac motors have low maintenance as compared to dc motors.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> AC (induction) motors have no commutator:</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: they are lighter for the same rating.</li><li><b>2</b> False: they are cheaper than dc motors.</li><li><b>3</b> True: no commutator or brushes means less maintenance.</li></ul><p>Induction motors are also more rugged and can be run in explosive atmospheres, but their speed control needs an inverter, whereas dc motors are controlled simply by the armature voltage. A 20–40 % weight advantage for the same power rating also means a lower moment of inertia, so ac motors accelerate faster, an advantage in servo drives.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> AC motor: lighter, cheaper, low maintenance.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-140", year: 2019, no: 140, subject: "pe", topic: "Induction motor driving a blower", doubt: true,
q: `A 3-phase induction motor drives a blower where load torque is directly proportional to speed squared. If the motor operates at 1450 rpm, the maximum current in terms of rated current will be nearly`,
opts: ["2·2", "3·4", "4·6", "6·8"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 2·2 (as keyed)</h3><p class="note">In doubt (key followed). The scheme of speed control is not stated, so the maximum current could not be worked out; the key's 2.2 is shown.</p><p><b>Concept:</b> The load torque is T_L = kN². With the motor operating at 1450 rpm (a slip of about 3.3 % on a 1500 rpm machine) the torque is close to the rated value. The question is incomplete as printed (the control scheme, e.g. rotor-resistance or voltage control, is not given), so the keyed value 2.2 times the rated current could not be derived here.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 3.4 could not be derived.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 4.6 could not be derived.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 6.8 is close to direct-on-line starting current but is not the maximum current during speed control.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Fan/blower load: T ∝ N², P ∝ N³.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-141", year: 2019, no: 141, subject: "pe", topic: "SMPS", doubt: true,
q: `Consider the following statements: 1. SMPS generates both the electromagnetic and radio frequency interference due to high switching frequency. 2. SMPS has high ripple in output voltage and its regulation is poor. 3. The output voltage of SMPS is less sensitive with respect to input voltage variation.`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3 (as keyed)</h3><p class="note">In doubt (key followed). A properly designed SMPS has tight regulation and low ripple, so statement 2 looks false, and (a) 1 and 3 only was our answer; the key's (d) is shown.</p><p><b>Concept:</b> Switched-mode supplies:</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: fast switching causes EMI and RFI and requires filtering and shielding.</li><li><b>2</b> The key counts it as true (a high switching ripple is present at the output before filtering); in good designs, however, regulation is excellent.</li><li><b>3</b> True: feedback keeps the output nearly independent of the input.</li></ul><p>The key's (d) is shown.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> SMPS: efficient, regulated, noisy.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-142", year: 2019, no: 142, subject: "pe", topic: "Flyback converters", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following features with respect to the flyback converters: 1. It is used mostly in application below 100 W. 2. It is widely used for high-output voltage. 3. It has low cost and is simple.`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The flyback converter is a buck–boost derivative with an isolated transformer-inductor.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: it is used mostly at low power (up to about 100 W).</li><li><b>2</b> True: it is widely used for high-voltage, low-current outputs (e.g. CRT supplies).</li><li><b>3</b> True: it uses few components, so it is simple and cheap.</li></ul><p>The flyback converter stores energy in the transformer during the switch-on time and delivers it to the load during the off time; it has only one magnetic component and one switch.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Flyback: low power, high voltage, simple.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-143", year: 2019, no: 143, subject: "pe", topic: "Dc-dc converter in a dc motor drive",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding the function of dc-dc converter in a dc motor: 1. It acts as a regenerative brake. 2. It controls the speed of motor. 3. It controls the armature voltage of a dc motor.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A chopper drive varies the armature voltage by changing the duty ratio, which controls the speed, and with a suitable (two-quadrant) arrangement it returns the braking energy to the supply (regenerative braking).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True.</li><li><b>2</b> True.</li><li><b>3</b> True.</li></ul><p>For the two-quadrant chopper (type C) the motor can operate as a motor in the forward direction and in regenerative braking, giving a smooth speed control down to zero. The converter varies the average armature voltage by changing the duty cycle D, as V_a = D V_s; in the regenerative braking mode the armature current reverses and the energy is returned to the source.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Chopper: voltage control, speed control, regeneration.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-144", year: 2019, no: 144, subject: "pe", topic: "Requirements of industrial power supplies",
q: `The power supplies which are used extensively in industrial applications are required to meet 1. Isolation between the source and the load 2. High conversion efficiency 3. Low power density for reduction of size and weight 4. Controlled direction of power flow`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1, 2 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Industrial supplies need isolation, high efficiency and controlled power flow.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> Required.</li><li><b>2</b> Required.</li><li><b>3</b> False: the requirement is <b>high</b> power density to reduce size and weight; low power density would mean large and heavy.</li><li><b>4</b> Required (e.g. bidirectional flow).</li></ul><p>Isolation is needed for safety and to avoid ground loops; a high power density (watts per unit volume) is obtained by high switching frequency, which allows smaller magnetic components.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Power supply needs: isolation, efficiency, high power density.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-145", year: 2019, no: 145, subject: "materials", topic: "Soft iron", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Statement (I): Soft iron does not retain magnetism permanently. Statement (II): Soft iron has no retentivity.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) both true and II explains I (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Soft iron magnetises and demagnetises easily.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>I</b> True: it loses its magnetism when the field is removed.</li><li><b>II</b> The key treats it as true and explanatory; strictly, soft iron has a small but non-zero retentivity.</li></ul><p>The key's (a) is shown.</p><p>Soft iron has a very narrow hysteresis loop with low retentivity and coercivity; this makes it suitable for the cores of electromagnets, relays and machines. The relation between the two statements is only partial: retentivity is small for soft iron but not exactly zero, so some residual magnetism remains after the field is removed.</p><p><b>Verdict on each option:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is the explanation of I here, so (b) is not right.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so (c) is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so (d) is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Soft magnetic: low coercivity, low retentivity.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-146", year: 2019, no: 146, subject: "power", topic: "Reaction turbines", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Statement (I): Reaction turbines are generally used for sites with high head and low flow. Statement (II): Kaplan and Francis turbines are reaction turbines.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) I false, II true</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Reaction turbines suit low-to-medium head and large flow.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>I</b> False: high head and low flow suit impulse (Pelton) turbines.</li><li><b>II</b> True: Kaplan and Francis turbines are reaction turbines.</li></ul><p>So (d).</p><p>Reaction turbines such as Francis and Kaplan run completely submerged, with the pressure falling across the runner; Pelton wheels (impulse) work at atmospheric pressure under heads of 300 m and above. The specific speed also separates the turbine types: Pelton 10–60, Francis 60–300, Kaplan above 300, which is the same ranking from high head to low head.</p><p><b>Verdict on each option:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> I is false, so (a) is not right.</li><li><b>(b)</b> I is false, so (b) is not right.</li><li><b>(c)</b> I is false and II is true, so (c) is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is false and II is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> High head: Pelton; medium: Francis; low: Kaplan.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-147", year: 2019, no: 147, subject: "computer", topic: "High-level languages",
q: `Statement (I): One can formulate problems more efficiently in a high-level language and need not have a precise knowledge of the architecture of the computer. Statement (II): High level languages permit programmers to describe tasks in a form which is problem oriented than computer oriented.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) both true and II explains I</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> High-level languages hide the machine.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>I</b> True.</li><li><b>II</b> True: tasks are described in problem terms, which is why the architecture need not be known.</li></ul><p>So (a).</p><p>Languages such as FORTRAN and C let the programmer write formulae almost as in mathematics; a compiler translates them into machine instructions for the particular processor. A program written in a high-level language can be moved from one computer to another with only recompilation, which is much easier than rewriting assembly code.</p><p><b>Verdict on each option:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is the explanation of I here, so (b) is not right.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so (c) is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so (d) is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> High level: problem-oriented, portable.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-148", year: 2019, no: 148, subject: "computer", topic: "Sign magnitude representation",
q: `Statement (I): Sign magnitude representation is generally used in implementing the integer portion of the ALU. Statement (II): In sign magnitude representation there are two representations of 0.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) I false, II true</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Sign-magnitude has +0 and −0.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>I</b> False: ALUs use two's complement, because addition and subtraction are then done by the same adder with no sign handling.</li><li><b>II</b> True: +0 (0000) and −0 (1000) are different bit patterns.</li></ul><p>So (d).</p><p>In two's complement the same adder serves for signed and unsigned numbers, and there is only one zero, which is why it is used in all practical ALUs. In sign-magnitude the range for n bits is −(2ⁿ⁻¹ − 1) to +(2ⁿ⁻¹ − 1), the same as in one's complement, but addition needs a separate comparison of the signs and the magnitudes.</p><p><b>Verdict on each option:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> I is false, so (a) is not right.</li><li><b>(b)</b> I is false, so (b) is not right.</li><li><b>(c)</b> I is false and II is true, so (c) is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is false and II is true.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> ALUs use two's complement.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-149", year: 2019, no: 149, subject: "circuits", topic: "Non-linear resistor in series", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Statement (I): When a non-linear resistor, in series with a linear resistor, both being non-inductive, is connected to a voltage source, the current in the circuit cannot be determined by using Ohm's law. Statement (II): If the current-voltage characteristic of the non-linear resistor is known, the current-voltage characteristic of the series circuit can be obtained by graphical solution.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) both true and II explains I (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Ohm's law needs a constant resistance.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>I</b> True: for the non-linear element R is not constant.</li><li><b>II</b> True: the V–I curve of the series circuit follows by adding voltages at the same current, or by the load-line intersection.</li></ul><p>The key's (a) is shown.</p><p>The graphical method plots the load line of the linear resistor on the V–I characteristic of the non-linear one; the intersection is the operating point and gives the circuit current.</p><p><b>Verdict on each option:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true and II explains I.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is the explanation of I here, so (b) is not right.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so (c) is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so (d) is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Non-linear: use load line (graphical) or iteration.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-150", year: 2019, no: 150, subject: "materials", topic: "Soft and hard magnetic materials", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Statement (I): Soft magnetic materials, both metallic and ceramic are used for making transformers core, whereas, hard magnetic materials both metallic and ceramic are used for making permanent magnets. Statement (II): Magnetic materials, both metallic and ceramic are classified as soft or hard according to the magnetic hysteresis loop being narrow or broad.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) both true, II not the explanation (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The classification and the uses are both correct.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>I</b> True: soft materials (silicon steel, soft ferrites) go into cores; hard materials (Alnico, hard ferrites) into permanent magnets.</li><li><b>II</b> True: a narrow loop means soft, a broad loop hard.</li></ul><p>The key's (b) is shown: the hysteresis shape is a classification criterion, and the key does not take it as the explanation of the uses.</p><p>Soft ferrites such as Mn–Zn and Ni–Zn are used for high-frequency transformer cores, and hard ferrites such as barium ferrite for loudspeaker magnets and fridge door seals.</p><p><b>Verdict on each option:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is true but does not explain I, so (a) is not right.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Both true; II does not explain I.</li><li><b>(c)</b> II is true, so (c) is not right.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so (d) is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Narrow loop: soft; broad loop: hard.</p>` }
);

