window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2018-51", year: 2018, no: 51, subject: "computer", topic: "IEEE 754 single precision",
q: `The decimal value 0·5 in IEEE single precision floating point representation has fraction bits of`,
opts: ["000....000 and exponent value of 0", "000....000 and exponent value of −1", "100....000 and exponent value of 0", "100....000 and exponent value of −1"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) fraction all zeros and exponent value −1</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The IEEE single-precision format is (−1)ˢ × 1.f × 2<sup>e</sup>, with a hidden leading 1 in the significand.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>0.5 = 1.0 × 2⁻¹.</li><li>The significand is exactly 1.0, so the 23 fraction bits are all zero.</li><li>The exponent is −1, stored as 127 − 1 = 126 (01111110).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> An exponent of 0 would give the value 1.0, not 0.5.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A fraction of 100…0 means 1.5 and the exponent 0 would give 1.5.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A fraction of 100…0 means 1.5, and with exponent −1 gives 0.75.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> 0.5 = 1.0 × 2⁻¹: fraction 0, exponent −1.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-52", year: 2018, no: 52, subject: "computer", topic: "Pointer assignment in C",
q: `What does the following program print? void f(int *p, int *q) { p = q; *p = 2; } int i = 0, j = 1; int main() { f(&i, &j); printf("%d %d\n", i, j); getchar(); return 0; }`,
opts: ["2 2", "2 1", "0 1", "0 2"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0 2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> C passes the pointers by value. Inside f, p is a local copy of &amp;i; the statement p = q makes the <b>local</b> p point to j. Then *p = 2 stores 2 into j. The caller's i is untouched.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>i stays 0; j becomes 2.</li><li>The program prints "0 2".</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> i would become 2 only if *p = 2 were executed before p = q.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Neither assignment gives i = 2 and j = 1.</li><li><b>(c)</b> j is changed by *p = 2 after p points to j.</li></ul><p>If the function had been declared f(int **p, int *q) and called with the address of a pointer, the assignment *p = q would persist outside the function. Without the extra level of indirection, only the pointed-to value can be changed.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Pointers are passed by value: reassigning p changes only the local copy.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-53", year: 2018, no: 53, subject: "computer", topic: "Shortest remaining time first scheduling",
q: `Consider the set of processes P₁ (arrival 0, burst 12 ms), P₂ (arrival 2, burst 4), P₃ (arrival 3, burst 6), P₄ (arrival 8, burst 5). An operating system uses shortest remaining time first scheduling algorithm for pre-emptive scheduling of processes. The average waiting time of the processes is`,
opts: ["7·5 ms", "6·5 ms", "5·5 ms", "4·5 ms"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 5·5 ms</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Gantt chart: P₁ runs 0–2 (10 ms left); P₂ arrives with 4 ms and pre-empts, running 2–6; at 6, P₃ (6 ms) is shorter than P₁ (10 ms), so P₃ runs 6–12 (at 8, P₄ with 5 ms does not pre-empt P₃, whose remaining time is 4 ms); P₄ runs 12–17; P₁ finishes 17–27.</p><div class="formula">Waiting = completion − arrival − burst</div><div class="formula">SRTF: run the job with the least remaining time</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Waiting = finish − arrival − burst: P₁ = 27 − 0 − 12 = 15; P₂ = 6 − 2 − 4 = 0; P₃ = 12 − 3 − 6 = 3; P₄ = 17 − 8 − 5 = 4.</li><li>Average = (15 + 0 + 3 + 4)/4 = 5.5 ms.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 7.5 ms would be the average for a non-pre-emptive order.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 6.5 ms would need a total wait of 26 ms.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 4.5 ms would need a total wait of 18 ms.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Waiting time = completion − arrival − burst.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-54", year: 2018, no: 54, subject: "computer", topic: "Cable length for Ethernet collision detection",
q: `The length of cable required for transmitting a data at the rate of 500 Mbps in an Ethernet LAN with frames of size 10,000 bits and for signal speed 2,00,000 km/s is`,
opts: ["2·5 km", "2·0 km", "1·5 km", "1·0 km"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2·0 km</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For CSMA/CD the transmission time must be at least twice the propagation delay: T<sub>t</sub> ≥ 2T<sub>p</sub>.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>T<sub>t</sub> = 10 000/(500 × 10⁶) = 20 μs.</li><li>T<sub>p</sub> = 10 μs, so d = 10 × 10⁻⁶ s × 2 × 10⁸ m/s = 2000 m = 2 km.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2.5 km would need T<sub>p</sub> = 12.5 μs.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1.5 km would need T<sub>p</sub> = 7.5 μs.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1.0 km would need T<sub>p</sub> = 5 μs.</li></ul><p>The relation also shows that the minimum frame size grows with the data rate and the length of the cable; this is why Gigabit Ethernet needed carrier extension for half-duplex operation.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> CSMA/CD: T_t = 2T_p, so d = v T_t/2.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-55", year: 2018, no: 55, subject: "computer", topic: "System calls", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements: 1. System calls provide the interface between a process and the operating system 2. PERL implementations include direct system call access 3. System calls occur in different ways depending on the computer in use. Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> System calls are the entry points through which user programs request services from the kernel.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: this is the definition of the system call interface.</li><li><b>2</b> True: Perl (like most scripting languages) provides direct access to system calls such as open, read and fork.</li><li><b>3</b> True: the mechanism (trap, software interrupt, register conventions) differs with the computer architecture.</li></ul><p>All three are correct.</p><p>On many systems, system calls are reached through library wrappers (such as those in the C library) and the trap instruction, so the application programmer does not call the kernel directly. Different processors use different trap instructions.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> System call = user-to-kernel interface.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-56", year: 2018, no: 56, subject: "computer", topic: "Effective access time with page faults",
q: `What is the effective access time, if the average page-fault service time is 25 ms, memory access time is 100 ns and page-fault rate is P?`,
opts: ["100 + 24,999,900 × P ns", "100 + 25,000,000 × P ns", "100 + 25,000 × P ns", "25,000,000 + 100 P ns"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 100 + 24,999,900 × P ns</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> EAT = (1 − P) × (memory access time) + P × (page-fault service time).</p><div class="formula">EAT = (1 − P)t_m + P·t_fault</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>25 ms = 25 000 000 ns.</li><li>EAT = (1 − P)100 + P × 25 000 000 = 100 + P(25 000 000 − 100) = 100 + 24 999 900 P ns.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> This forgets to subtract the memory access time that is not incurred on a fault.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 25 000 treats the fault time as 25 μs.</li><li><b>(d)</b> This reverses the roles of the two times.</li></ul><p>With a page-fault service time of 25 ms, even a very small fault rate dominates: for an effective access time of at most 110 ns, the fault rate must be below 4 × 10⁻⁷, about one fault in 2.5 million accesses.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> EAT = (1 − P) t_mem + P t_fault.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-57", year: 2018, no: 57, subject: "computer", topic: "Counting bits with a shift loop",
q: `Consider the function fun1(int num) { int count = 0; while (num) { count++; num >>= 1; } return (count); } The value returned by fun1(435) is`,
opts: ["10", "9", "8", "7"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 9</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The loop shifts num right by one bit each pass until it becomes zero, so it counts the number of bits in the binary representation of 435 (the position of the highest set bit).</p><div class="formula">Count = number of bits = ⌊log₂n⌋ + 1</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>435 = 256 + 128 + 32 + 16 + 2 + 1 = 110110011₂, which has 9 bits.</li><li>The function returns 9.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 10 bits would need a value from 512 to 1023.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 8 bits would cover only 255.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 7 bits would cover only 127.</li></ul><p>This function is the classic bit-counting loop. The same loop with the test num &amp; 1 added would count the set bits (the population count, 6 for 435) rather than the total number of bits.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Loop counts ⌊log₂ n⌋ + 1.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-58", year: 2018, no: 58, subject: "basic", topic: "Semiconductor diode currents", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements in the relevant context: 1. The two types of currents that flow in semiconductor diodes and transistors are drift and diffusion currents 2. The junction region is called depletion region or space-charge region 3. When currents flow through the diode in forward bias, the depletion region current is mostly of 'diffusion' type`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Carrier motion in a semiconductor is by drift and by diffusion.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True.</li><li><b>2</b> True: the junction region, depleted of mobile carriers and containing the fixed ionised donors and acceptors, is the depletion (space-charge) region.</li><li><b>3</b> Not accepted by the key: the diffusion current flows in the neutral regions on either side of the junction; within the depletion region the carriers cross by the field (drift) and by recombination, so it is not mostly diffusion.</li></ul><p>So 1 and 2 only.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Diffusion current is in the neutral regions; the depletion layer is field-dominated.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-59", year: 2018, no: 59, subject: "control", topic: "Increasing bandwidth of a control system",
q: `The bandwidth of a control system can be increased by using`,
opts: ["Phase-lead network", "Phase-lag network", "Both Phase-lead network and Phase-lag network", "Cascaded amplifier in the system"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) phase-lead network</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A phase-lead network adds a zero before a pole, which raises the gain at higher frequencies. The gain crossover frequency moves to a higher value, so the bandwidth increases and the response becomes faster.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> A lag network reduces the high-frequency gain, so it reduces the bandwidth.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Only the lead network increases it; the lag network does the opposite.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A cascaded amplifier raises the gain, but with more stages the bandwidth of the overall system actually shrinks.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Lead: wider bandwidth, faster. Lag: narrower bandwidth, better accuracy.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-60", year: 2018, no: 60, subject: "basic", topic: "Gain of an amplifier with feedback",
q: `Applications of negative feedback to a certain amplifier reduced its gain from 200 to 100. If the gain with the same feedback is to be raised to 150, in the case of another such appliance, the gain of the amplifier without feedback must have been`,
opts: ["400", "450", "500", "600"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 600</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A<sub>f</sub> = A/(1 + Aβ). First amplifier: 100 = 200/(1 + 200β) → 1 + 200β = 2 → β = 0.005.</p><div class="formula">A_f = A/(1 + Aβ)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Second amplifier with the same β: 150 = A/(1 + 0.005A) → 150 + 0.75A = A → 0.25A = 150.</li><li>A = 600.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A = 400 gives A<sub>f</sub> = 400/3 = 133.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A = 450 gives A<sub>f</sub> = 450/3.25 = 138.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A = 500 gives A<sub>f</sub> = 500/3.5 = 143.</li></ul><p>Negative feedback reduces the gain by the factor 1 + Aβ, called the desensitivity (here 2), and in return it improves the stability of the gain, widens the bandwidth and reduces the distortion by the same factor.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> First find β from A_f = A/(1 + Aβ), then reuse it.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-61", year: 2018, no: 61, subject: "circuits", topic: "Fundamental tie-set matrix",
q: `Consider the following statements for a network graph, if B<sub>f</sub> is its fundamental tie set matrix, and B<sub>t</sub> and B<sub>l</sub> are its sub-matrices corresponding to twigs and links, respectively: 1. B<sub>t</sub> is a unit matrix 2. B<sub>l</sub> is a rectangular matrix 3. Rank of B<sub>f</sub> is (b − n − 1), where b is the number of branches and n is the number of nodes.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "None of the above"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) none of the above</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The fundamental tie-set (loop) matrix is B<sub>f</sub> = [B<sub>t</sub> | U], with one row for each link, so it has (b − n + 1) rows.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: it is the sub-matrix for the links, B<sub>l</sub>, that is the unit matrix.</li><li><b>2</b> False: B<sub>l</sub> is the unit matrix, and the rectangular one is B<sub>t</sub>.</li><li><b>3</b> False: the rank is b − n + 1, the number of independent loops.</li></ul><p>None of the statements is correct.</p><p>The fundamental cut-set matrix is Q<sub>f</sub> = [U | Q<sub>l</sub>] and the tie-set matrix is B<sub>f</sub> = [B<sub>t</sub> | U]. The two are related by B<sub>t</sub> = −Q<sub>l</sub><sup>T</sup>, so the tie-set rows are orthogonal to the cut-set rows.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> B_f = [B_t | U]; rank = b − n + 1.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-62", year: 2018, no: 62, subject: "ad", topic: "Non-inverting amplifier",
q: `An ideal operational amplifier is connected as shown in figure (feedback resistor 2R from the output to the inverting input, a resistor R from the inverting input to ground, and the input V₁ applied to the non-inverting input). What is the output voltage V₂?<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2018-62.png" alt="Figure for question 62">`,
opts: ["3V₁", "2V₁", "1V₁", "V₁/3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 3V₁</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> This is a non-inverting amplifier: V₂ = V₁(1 + R<sub>f</sub>/R<sub>1</sub>) with R<sub>f</sub> = 2R and R<sub>1</sub> = R.</p><div class="formula">Non-inverting: V_o = V_i(1 + R_f/R₁)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>V₂ = V₁(1 + 2R/R) = 3V₁.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 2V₁ would result from R<sub>f</sub> = R.</li><li><b>(c)</b> V₁ would be a voltage follower (no feedback resistor).</li><li><b>(d)</b> V₁/3 is an attenuation; an op-amp amplifier cannot attenuate in this configuration.</li></ul><p>The input resistance seen by V₁ is infinite for the ideal non-inverting stage, so there is no loading on the source; this is why the configuration is preferred where a high input impedance is needed.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Non-inverting gain = 1 + R_f/R₁.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-63", year: 2018, no: 63, subject: "ad", topic: "Total power of an AM signal",
q: `The modulating index of an AM-signal is reduced from 0·8 to 0·5. The ratio of the total power in the new modulated signal to that of the original signal will nearly be`,
opts: ["0·39", "0·63", "0·85", "1·25"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·85</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> P<sub>t</sub> = P<sub>c</sub>(1 + m²/2).</p><div class="formula">P_t = P_c(1 + m²/2)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>m = 0.8: 1 + 0.32 = 1.32. m = 0.5: 1 + 0.125 = 1.125.</li><li>Ratio = 1.125/1.32 = 0.852.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.39 is the ratio of the sideband power only, 0.125/0.32.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.63 is the ratio m² = 0.25/0.64 rounded wrongly (0.39) or another slip.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1.25 is the inverse type of error; the total power must fall when m falls.</li></ul><p>The carrier power is unchanged by modulation; only the sideband power changes with m², so the power efficiency of AM (m²/(2 + m²)) is only 14 % at m = 0.5 and 24 % at m = 0.8, and 33 % at the maximum m = 1.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> P_t/P_c = 1 + m²/2.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-64", year: 2018, no: 64, subject: "ad", topic: "Truth table from minterms",
q: `The Truth table for the function f(ABCD) = Σm(0, 1, 3, 4, 8, 9) is (the table is given for ABC with entries W, X, Y, 0, Z, 0, 0, 0) where W, X, Y, Z are given by (d is the complement of D)`,
opts: ["D, d, 1, 1", "1, d, D, 1", "1, 1, D, d", "1, D, d, 1"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, D, d, 1</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Each row of the table fixes ABC and gives f as a function of D. The minterm number is 8A + 4B + 2C + D.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ABC = 000: minterms 0 and 1 are both present, so f = 1 → W = 1.</li><li>ABC = 001: minterms 2 and 3; only 3 is present (D = 1), so X = D.</li><li>ABC = 010: minterms 4 and 5; only 4 is present (D = 0), so Y = D̄ = d.</li><li>ABC = 100: minterms 8 and 9 are both present → Z = 1.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> W = D would not cover both minterms 0 and 1.</li><li><b>(b)</b> X and Y are swapped.</li><li><b>(c)</b> X = 1 would need minterm 2 as well.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Group each pair of minterms with the same ABC.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-65", year: 2018, no: 65, subject: "ad", topic: "Resolution of a DAC",
q: `An 8-bit DAC uses a ladder network. The full-scale output voltage of the converter is +10 V. The resolution expressed in percentage and in volts is, respectively`,
opts: ["0·25% and 30 mV", "0·39% and 30 mV", "0·25% and 39 mV", "0·39% and 39 mV"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·39% and 39 mV</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> An 8-bit DAC has 2⁸ − 1 = 255 steps up to full scale.</p><div class="formula">% resolution = 100/(2ⁿ − 1)</div><div class="formula">Step = V_FS/(2ⁿ − 1)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Resolution (percentage) = 1/255 × 100 = 0.39 %.</li><li>Resolution (volts) = 10 V/255 = 39.2 mV.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.25 % is 1/400, and 30 mV is not 10/255.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.39 % is right, but 30 mV is not.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 39 mV is right but 0.25 % is not.</li></ul><p>The resolution of an n-bit DAC is also written as 1 LSB, equal to V<sub>FS</sub>/(2ⁿ − 1), and this is the smallest change that the converter can produce. A 12-bit DAC with a 10 V range has an LSB of 2.44 mV.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Resolution = V_FS/(2ⁿ − 1).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-66", year: 2018, no: 66, subject: "ad", topic: "Analog to digital converters",
q: `Consider the following statements: 1. Flash type ADCs are considered the fastest 2. In successive approximation type ADCs, conversion time depends upon the magnitude of the analog voltage 3. Counter-type ADCs work with fixed conversion time 4. Dual slope ADCs are considered the slowest. Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["2 and 3 only", "2 and 4 only", "1 and 4 only", "1 and 3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> ADC types differ in speed.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: a flash ADC compares the input with all the levels at once, in a single clock.</li><li><b>2</b> False: a successive approximation ADC takes a fixed number of clock cycles (one per bit) whatever the input.</li><li><b>3</b> False: a counter-type ADC counts up until the comparator trips, so its conversion time is proportional to the input.</li><li><b>4</b> True: the dual-slope ADC integrates over a long period and is the slowest, but is accurate and rejects noise.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Flash fastest; SAR fixed n clocks; counter variable; dual slope slowest.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-67", year: 2018, no: 67, subject: "control", topic: "Gain for a given steady-state error",
q: `For the feedback control system shown (unity feedback, forward path K/(1 + 4s) followed by 1/(1 + s)), if the steady state error is 20% for the unit step input signal, then the value of K must be<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2018-67.png" alt="Figure for question 67">`,
opts: ["80", "40", "20", "4"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The system is type 0 with position error constant K<sub>p</sub> = lim G(s) = K × 1.</p><div class="formula">K_p = lim_{s→0} G(s)</div><div class="formula">e_ss = 1/(1 + K_p)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>e<sub>ss</sub> = 1/(1 + K<sub>p</sub>) = 1/(1 + K) = 0.2.</li><li>1 + K = 5, so K = 4.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> K = 80 gives e<sub>ss</sub> = 1.2 %.</li><li><b>(b)</b> K = 40 gives e<sub>ss</sub> = 2.4 %.</li><li><b>(c)</b> K = 20 gives e<sub>ss</sub> = 4.8 %.</li></ul><p>The steady-state error for a unit step falls as the loop gain K rises but never reaches zero for a type 0 system; to eliminate the step error one needs an integrator in the loop (type 1). The error of 20 % for K = 4 can be reduced by increasing K, but a high gain in this two-pole system will make the response oscillatory, since the closed-loop damping falls as the gain rises.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Type 0: e_ss = 1/(1 + K_p).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-68", year: 2018, no: 68, subject: "control", topic: "Signal flow graph gain", selfCorrected: true,
q: `The closed-loop transfer function C(s)/R(s) of the system represented by the signal flow graph as shown in figure (forward path R → G₁ → G₂ → G₃ → G₄ → C; feedback arcs of gain −1 around G₁ and G₂ in the first half, and around G₃ and G₄ in the second half) is<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2018-68.png" alt="Figure for question 68">`,
opts: ["G₁G₂G₃G₄/(1 + G₁ + G₂)", "G₁G₂G₃G₄/(1 + G₃ + G₄)", "G₁G₂G₃G₄/((1 + G₁ + G₂)(1 + G₃ + G₄))", "G₁G₂G₃G₄/((1 + G₁G₂)(1 + G₃G₄))"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) G₁G₂G₃G₄/((1 + G₁ + G₂)(1 + G₃ + G₄))</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Mason's gain formula: T = ΣP<sub>k</sub>Δ<sub>k</sub>/Δ. There is one forward path, P = G₁G₂G₃G₄, which touches every loop, so Δ<sub>k</sub> = 1.</p><div class="formula">Mason: T = ΣP_kΔ_k/Δ</div><div class="formula">Δ = 1 − ΣL_i + ΣL_iL_j (non-touching pairs) − …</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>The loops are L₁ = −G₁, L₂ = −G₂ (first half) and L₃ = −G₃, L₄ = −G₄ (second half).</li><li>Each loop in the first half does not touch the loops of the second half, so the non-touching products L₁L₃, L₁L₄, L₂L₃, L₂L₄ appear in Δ.</li><li>Δ = 1 − (L₁ + L₂ + L₃ + L₄) + (L₁L₃ + L₁L₄ + L₂L₃ + L₂L₄) = (1 + G₁ + G₂)(1 + G₃ + G₄).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Leaves out the factor from the second half.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Leaves out the factor from the first half.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Treats the loops as −G₁G₂ and −G₃G₄ (one loop per half), which does not match the arcs drawn around each block.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Non-touching loops multiply: Δ factorises.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-69", year: 2018, no: 69, subject: "control", topic: "Block diagram reduction",
q: `For the block diagram as shown in figure (R minus feedback H₁ into a summing junction, then G₁, a second summing junction with a positive feedback from the output through H₂, then G₂ to C), the overall transfer function C/R is<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2018-69.png" alt="Figure for question 69">`,
opts: ["G₁G₂H₁/(1 − G₁H₁ − G₂H₂)", "G₁G₂/(1 − G₁H₁ + G₂H₂)", "G₁G₂H₂/(1 + G₁H₁ + G₂H₂)", "G₁G₂/(1 + G₁H₁ − G₂H₂)"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) G₁G₂/(1 + G₁H₁ − G₂H₂)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Forward path gain: G₁G₂. Loops: the H₁ loop is a negative feedback loop, with gain −G₁H₁; the H₂ loop is a positive feedback loop, with gain +G₂H₂.</p><div class="formula">Mason: T = P/Δ, Δ = 1 − Σ loop gains (touching loops)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Δ = 1 − (−G₁H₁ + G₂H₂) = 1 + G₁H₁ − G₂H₂ (the loops touch each other).</li><li>C/R = G₁G₂/(1 + G₁H₁ − G₂H₂).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The numerator contains H₁, which is not in the forward path.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The signs are reversed: the negative loop should add 1 + G₁H₁.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The numerator contains H₂, which is not in the forward path.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Mason: Δ = 1 − Σ loop gains.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-70", year: 2018, no: 70, subject: "control", topic: "Steady-state error of a type 0 system",
q: `The block diagram shows a unity feedback closed-loop system (forward path 5/(s + 10) followed by 3/(s + 2)). The steady-state error in the response to a unit step input is<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2018-70.png" alt="Figure for question 70">`,
opts: ["14%", "28%", "42%", "57%"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 57%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> K<sub>p</sub> = G(0) = (5/10)(3/2) = 0.75.</p><div class="formula">K_p = G(0)</div><div class="formula">e_ss = 1/(1 + K_p)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>e<sub>ss</sub> = 1/(1 + K<sub>p</sub>) = 1/1.75 = 0.571 = 57 %.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 14 % would need K<sub>p</sub> = 6.1.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 28 % would need K<sub>p</sub> = 2.6.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 42 % would need K<sub>p</sub> = 1.4.</li></ul><p>The system has Kp = 0.75, so the output settles at 0.75/1.75 = 43 % of the input and the error is 57 %. A type 0 system with such a small gain is a poor servo and would normally be given extra gain or integral action. A larger forward gain of 10 would give K<sub>p</sub> = 1.5 and an error of 40 %, still large, so a type 0 system always leaves a finite steady-state error for a step input.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> e_ss = 1/(1 + K_p) for a type 0 system.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-71", year: 2018, no: 71, subject: "control", topic: "Range of gain for stability",
q: `The open-loop transfer function of a negative feedback is G(s)H(s) = K/(s(s + 5)(s + 12)). For ensuring system stability the gain K should be in the range`,
opts: ["0 &lt; K &lt; 60", "0 &lt; K &lt; 600", "0 &lt; K &lt; 1020", "K &gt; 1020"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0 &lt; K &lt; 1020</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The characteristic equation: s(s + 5)(s + 12) + K = s³ + 17s² + 60s + K = 0.</p><div class="formula">Routh: first column &gt; 0</div><div class="formula">s¹ row: (17 × 60 − K)/17</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Routh array: s³: 1, 60; s²: 17, K; s¹: (17 × 60 − K)/17; s⁰: K.</li><li>Stability needs 1020 − K &gt; 0 and K &gt; 0: 0 &lt; K &lt; 1020.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 60 is only one coefficient of the polynomial.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 600 is not the limiting value.</li><li><b>(d)</b> K &gt; 1020 makes the s¹ element negative: unstable.</li></ul><p>The frequency at which the root locus crosses the imaginary axis follows from the auxiliary equation 17s² + 1020 = 0, giving s = ±j7.75, so the system oscillates at 7.75 rad/s when K reaches 1020.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> For s³ + a s² + b s + K: K < ab.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-72", year: 2018, no: 72, subject: "control", topic: "Routh array with a zero in the first column",
q: `The characteristic polynomial of a feedback control system is given by R(s) = s⁵ + 2s⁴ + 2s³ + 4s² + 11s + 10. For this system, the numbers of roots that lie in the left hand and right hand s-plane respectively, are`,
opts: ["5 and 0", "4 and 1", "3 and 2", "2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 and 2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Routh array: s⁵: 1, 2, 11; s⁴: 2, 4, 10; s³: (2·2 − 4)/2 = 0, (2·11 − 10)/2 = 6.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>The first element of the s³ row is zero: replace it by ε &gt; 0, so the row is ε, 6.</li><li>s²: (4ε − 12)/ε → −∞ (negative as ε → 0); s¹: positive; s⁰: 10.</li><li>First column signs: +, +, +, −, +, +: two sign changes.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A sign change shows that the system is not stable.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Only one sign change would give one root in the right half.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Three sign changes would be needed for three roots in the right half.</li></ul><p>Two roots lie in the right half plane and the other three in the left half plane.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Zero in the first column: use ε; count sign changes.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-73", year: 2018, no: 73, subject: "control", topic: "Transfer function from a Bode plot",
q: `The open loop transfer function G(s)H(s) of a Bode's plot for feedback system as shown in figure (slope −40 dB/dec at low frequency, −60 dB/dec from ω = 5, and −40 dB/dec again beyond ω = 10) is<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2018-73.png" alt="Figure for question 73">`,
opts: ["K(s + 5)/(s²(s + 10))", "K(s + 5)/(s(s + 10))", "K(s + 10)/(s²(s + 5))", "K(s + 10)/(s(s + 5))"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) K(s + 10)/(s²(s + 5))</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The initial slope of −40 dB/dec means a double pole at the origin (1/s²). At ω = 5 the slope steepens by 20 dB/dec to −60: a pole at 5. At ω = 10 it flattens by 20 dB/dec to −40: a zero at 10.</p><div class="formula">Initial slope −20n ⇒ 1/sⁿ</div><div class="formula">Slope change −20 ⇒ pole, +20 ⇒ zero</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>G(s)H(s) = K(s + 10)/(s²(s + 5)).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> This would have a zero at 5 and a pole at 10: the slopes would be −40, −20, −40.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Only one pole at the origin: the initial slope would be −20.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Only one pole at the origin: the initial slope would be −20.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Slope falls 20 dB/dec at a pole, rises 20 at a zero.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-74", year: 2018, no: 74, subject: "control", topic: "Roots on the imaginary axis",
q: `A system with characteristic equation s⁴ + 2s³ + 11s² + 18s + 18 = 0, will have closed loop poles such that`,
opts: ["All poles lie in the left half of the s-plane and no pole lies on imaginary axis", "All poles lie in the right half of the s-plane", "Two poles lie symmetrically on the imaginary axis of the s-plane", "All four poles lie on the imaginary axis of the s-plane"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) two poles lie symmetrically on the imaginary axis</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Routh array: s⁴: 1, 11, 18; s³: 2, 18; s²: (22 − 18)/2 = 2, 18; s¹: (2·18 − 2·18)/2 = 0 (a whole row of zeros).</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>The auxiliary equation from the s² row: 2s² + 18 = 0 → s = ±j3.</li><li>The polynomial factorises as (s² + 9)(s² + 2s + 2): roots ±j3 and −1 ± j1.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Two roots (±j3) lie on the axis, so the system is marginally stable, not strictly stable.</li><li><b>(b)</b> No root lies in the right half plane.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Only two of the four roots are on the axis.</li></ul><p>Two poles are on the imaginary axis, the other two in the left half plane.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Zero row → auxiliary polynomial gives the imaginary-axis roots.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-75", year: 2018, no: 75, subject: "control", topic: "Open-loop transfer function from a root locus",
q: `The open-loop transfer function G(s)H(s) of a root locus plot of a system as shown in figure (four asymptotes at 45° meeting at s = −1, with K = 4 on the plot) is<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2018-75.png" alt="Figure for question 75">`,
opts: ["4/(s + 1)¹", "4/(s + 1)²", "4/(s + 1)³", "4/(s + 1)⁴"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 4/(s + 1)⁴</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Four root locus branches leave the same point s = −1 along asymptotes at ±45° and ±135°. The number of asymptotes equals the number of poles minus zeros, so P − Z = 4, and since there are no finite zeros and the centroid is −1, all four poles are at s = −1: G(s)H(s) = K/(s + 1)⁴.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> One pole gives a single branch along the real axis.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Two poles give two asymptotes at ±90°.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Three poles give asymptotes at 60° and 180°.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> n asymptotes at angles (2k + 1)180°/n; 4 give ±45° and ±135°.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2018d.js ----
// ESE 2018 Prelims Paper II, Electrical Engineering, Series A (SKP-D-ETE), Q76–100
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; none dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2018-76", year: 2018, no: 76, subject: "control", topic: "Phase lead of a compensator",
q: `The transfer function of a compensator is (1 + 3sT)/(1 + sT). The maximum possible phase shift is`,
opts: ["30°", "45°", "60°", "90°"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 30°</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a lead compensator (1 + αTs)/(1 + Ts) the maximum phase lead is φ<sub>m</sub> = sin⁻¹[(α − 1)/(α + 1)].</p><div class="formula">φ_m = sin⁻¹[(α − 1)/(α + 1)]</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Here α = 3: φ<sub>m</sub> = sin⁻¹(2/4) = sin⁻¹(0.5) = 30°.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 45° would need α = 5.8.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 60° would need α = 13.9.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 90° would need α → ∞.</li></ul><p>The lead compensator of this problem also raises the high frequency gain by α = 3, so the bandwidth and the noise sensitivity increase by that factor; its maximum phase occurs at the geometric mean of the corner frequencies, ω<sub>m</sub> = 1/(T√3).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> φ_max = sin⁻¹((α − 1)/(α + 1)).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-77", year: 2018, no: 77, subject: "signals", topic: "Steady-state sinusoidal response",
q: `The steady state response c(t) for an input r(t) = sin 2t to a system transfer function 1/(s + 4) is`,
opts: ["0·25 sin 2t", "sin(2t − 45°)", "0·316 sin(2t − 26·5°)", "0·632 cos 2t"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·316 sin(2t − 26·5°)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a sinusoidal input the steady-state output is |G(jω)| sin(ωt + ∠G(jω)).</p><div class="formula">c_ss(t) = |G(jω)| sin(ωt + ∠G(jω))</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>G(j2) = 1/(4 + j2): ∠ = −tan⁻¹(2/4) = −26.6°.</li><li>|G(j2)| = 1/√(16 + 4) = 0.2236.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.25 sin 2t has no phase lag, but a first-order lag always introduces one.</li><li><b>(b)</b> −45° would need ω = 4 rad/s.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A pure cosine would need a phase of +90° relative to sin.</li></ul><p>The phase of the keyed option, −26.5°, matches. Its magnitude 0.316 equals 1/√10, which corresponds to a pole at 3 rather than 4; the printed amplitude differs from 0.224, but no other option has the right phase.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Steady state: multiply by |G(jω)|, add ∠G(jω).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-78", year: 2018, no: 78, subject: "control", topic: "Critical damping with a PD compensator",
q: `Compensation derived from the P-D network whose differential equation is governed by e<sub>o</sub> = 20(e<sub>i</sub> + T de<sub>i</sub>/dt) as shown in the figure (forward plant 40/(10s² + 8s + 800), unity feedback) is to be investigated. For what value of T will the closed-loop response be critically damped?<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2018-78.png" alt="Figure for question 78">`,
opts: ["1·612", "0·806", "0·306", "0·161"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·306</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The PD compensator is G<sub>c</sub>(s) = 20(1 + Ts). The open-loop gain is 800(1 + Ts)/(10s² + 8s + 800).</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Characteristic equation: 10s² + 8s + 800 + 800(1 + Ts) = 10s² + (8 + 800T)s + 1600 = 0.</li><li>ω<sub>n</sub> = √(1600/10) = 12.65 rad/s; 2ζω<sub>n</sub> = (8 + 800T)/10.</li><li>Critical damping ζ = 1: 2 × 12.65 = (8 + 800T)/10 → 8 + 800T = 253 → T = 0.306.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> T = 1.612 gives ζ = 5.1, heavily over-damped.</li><li><b>(b)</b> T = 0.806 gives ζ = 2.6.</li><li><b>(d)</b> T = 0.161 gives ζ = 0.54, under-damped.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Characteristic equation a s² + b s + c: ζ = b/(2√(ac)).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-79", year: 2018, no: 79, subject: "control", topic: "Damping of a second-order system",
q: `The unity feedback system as shown in the figure is characterized by G(s) = 1/(s + 1)². The output time response will have a damping factor ζ, and natural frequency ω<sub>n</sub>, respectively, as<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2018-79.png" alt="Figure for question 79">`,
opts: ["0·707 and 1", "0·866 and √2", "0·707 and √2", "0·866 and 1"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·707 and √2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Closed loop: 1 + G = 0 → (s + 1)² + 1 = s² + 2s + 2 = 0.</p><div class="formula">1 + G = 0 ⇒ s² + 2ζω_n s + ω_n² = 0</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ω<sub>n</sub>² = 2 → ω<sub>n</sub> = √2 rad/s.</li><li>2ζω<sub>n</sub> = 2 → ζ = 1/√2 = 0.707.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> ω<sub>n</sub> = 1 would need s² + 2ζs + 1.</li><li><b>(b)</b> ζ = 0.866 would need 2ζω<sub>n</sub> = 2.45.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Both values are wrong.</li></ul><p>The unity feedback system has the closed loop transfer function 1/(s² + 2s + 2), a second-order system with ζ = 0.707, whose step response overshoots by about 4.3 % and settles in about 4/(ζω<sub>n</sub>) = 4 s. The step response of this system is c(t) = 1 − e<sup>−t</sup>(cos t + sin t), which rises to a peak of about 1.04 at t = π seconds and settles to the final value of 0.5, since the d.c. gain is only 1/2.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Closed-loop characteristic polynomial: s² + 2ζω_n s + ω_n².</p>` },

{ id: "2018-80", year: 2018, no: 80, subject: "control", topic: "State transition matrix",
q: `For a state model ẋ = Ax, where A = [1 0; 1 1], the state transition matrix is`,
opts: ["[te⁻ᵗ 0; eᵗ eᵗ]", "[0 t; eᵗ teᵗ]", "[eᵗ 0; teᵗ eᵗ]", "[t 0; t² eᵗ]"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) [eᵗ 0; teᵗ eᵗ]</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> φ(t) = L⁻¹[(sI − A)⁻¹].</p><div class="formula">φ(t) = L⁻¹[(sI − A)⁻¹]</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>sI − A = [s − 1, 0; −1, s − 1]; (sI − A)⁻¹ = [1/(s − 1), 0; 1/(s − 1)², 1/(s − 1)].</li><li>Inverse transforms: eᵗ, 0, teᵗ, eᵗ, so φ(t) = [eᵗ 0; teᵗ eᵗ].</li><li>Check: φ(0) = I.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> φ(0) would not be the identity.</li><li><b>(b)</b> φ(0) = 0 in the first row, not the identity.</li><li><b>(d)</b> t and t² do not occur for a matrix with both eigenvalues at 1.</li></ul><p>The state transition matrix is used to write the solution of the state equation x(t) = φ(t)x(0). The repeated eigenvalue at 1 produces the te<sup>t</sup> term, which grows without bound, so the system is unstable.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> φ(0) = I, and φ(t) = L⁻¹[(sI − A)⁻¹].</p>` },

{ id: "2018-81", year: 2018, no: 81, subject: "control", topic: "Peak time of a second-order system",
q: `A unit step input to a unity feedback system is shown in the figure (forward path 25/(s(s + 6))), the time for peak overshoot is, nearly<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2018-81.png" alt="Figure for question 81">`,
opts: ["0·35 s", "0·58 s", "0·79 s", "0·96 s"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·79 s</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Closed loop: s² + 6s + 25 = 0, so ω<sub>n</sub> = 5 rad/s, ζ = 6/(2 × 5) = 0.6.</p><div class="formula">ω_d = ω_n√(1 − ζ²)</div><div class="formula">t_p = π/ω_d</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ω<sub>d</sub> = ω<sub>n</sub>√(1 − ζ²) = 5 × 0.8 = 4 rad/s.</li><li>t<sub>p</sub> = π/ω<sub>d</sub> = π/4 = 0.785 s.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.35 s would need ω<sub>d</sub> = 9.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.58 s would need ω<sub>d</sub> = 5.4.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.96 s would need ω<sub>d</sub> = 3.3.</li></ul><p>The maximum overshoot of this system is e<sup>−πζ/√(1−ζ²)</sup> = e<sup>−2.356</sup> = 9.5 %, and the settling time at the 2 % criterion is 4/(ζω<sub>n</sub>) = 4/3 = 1.33 s. The rise time of this system is about 0.55 s, which can be found from the standard relation t<sub>r</sub> = (π − cos⁻¹ζ)/ω<sub>d</sub> = (3.1416 − 0.927)/4 = 0.55 s.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> t_p = π/(ω_n√(1 − ζ²)).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-82", year: 2018, no: 82, subject: "control", topic: "Natural frequency from the transient response",
q: `The transient response of second order under damped system starting from rest is given by c(t) = Ae⁻⁶ᵗ sin(8t + θ), t ≥ 0. The natural frequency of the system is`,
opts: ["8", "9", "10", "100"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 10</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> An under-damped response has the form Ae<sup>−σt</sup> sin(ω<sub>d</sub>t + θ) with σ = ζω<sub>n</sub> = 6 and ω<sub>d</sub> = ω<sub>n</sub>√(1 − ζ²) = 8.</p><div class="formula">σ = ζω_n, ω_d = ω_n√(1 − ζ²)</div><div class="formula">ω_n² = σ² + ω_d²</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ω<sub>n</sub>² = σ² + ω<sub>d</sub>² = 36 + 64 = 100.</li><li>ω<sub>n</sub> = 10 rad/s (and ζ = 0.6).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 8 is the damped frequency, not the natural frequency.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 9 would need ω_n² = 81, but σ² + ω_d² = 100.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 100 is ω<sub>n</sub>², not ω<sub>n</sub>.</li></ul><p>The time constant of the envelope is 1/σ = 1/6 s, so the oscillation decays to 2 % in about 0.67 s. The ratio ω<sub>d</sub>/ω<sub>n</sub> = 0.8 gives the damping ratio √(1 − 0.64) = 0.6. The phase angle θ and the amplitude A of the response are fixed by the initial conditions; the starting from rest condition gives θ = tan⁻¹(ω<sub>d</sub>/σ) = tan⁻¹(8/6) = 53.1°.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> ω_n² = σ² + ω_d².</p>` },

{ id: "2018-83", year: 2018, no: 83, subject: "control", topic: "Pole placement", doubt: true,
q: `For a feedback control system all the roots of the characteristic equation can be placed at the desired location in the s-plane if and only if the system is 1. Observable 2. Controllable. Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) both 1 and 2</h3><p class="note">In doubt (key followed). Strictly, controllability alone is the condition for placing the poles by state feedback (option b, our answer); observability is needed only when the states must be reconstructed. The key's (c) is shown.</p><p><b>Concept:</b> Arbitrary pole placement by state feedback u = −Kx requires the system to be completely state controllable. If the states are not directly measured, an observer is needed, and that requires observability.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> Observability is needed to estimate the states for feedback (the key counts it).</li><li><b>2</b> Controllability is the necessary and sufficient condition for arbitrary pole placement by state feedback.</li></ul><p>The key's (c), both, is shown.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> State-feedback pole placement: controllable; with an observer: also observable.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-84", year: 2018, no: 84, subject: "control", topic: "Natural frequency and damping from the poles",
q: `A second order system with a zero at −2 has its poles located at −3 + j4 and −3 − j4 in the s-plane. The undamped natural frequency and the damping factor of the system respectively are`,
opts: ["3 rad/s and 0·80", "5 rad/s and 0·80", "3 rad/s and 0·60", "5 rad/s and 0·60"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 5 rad/s and 0·60</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The poles −σ ± jω<sub>d</sub> with σ = 3 and ω<sub>d</sub> = 4 give the characteristic polynomial s² + 6s + 25. The zero does not affect ω<sub>n</sub> or ζ (it only shapes the response).</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ω<sub>n</sub> = √(σ² + ω<sub>d</sub>²) = 5 rad/s.</li><li>ζ = σ/ω<sub>n</sub> = 3/5 = 0.6.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 3 rad/s is σ, and 0.8 is cos of the wrong angle.</li><li><b>(b)</b> ζ = 0.8 would need σ = 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> ω<sub>n</sub> = 3 is the real part only.</li></ul><p>The zero at −2 does not change the natural frequency or damping ratio of the pole pair, but it adds a derivative term to the response and increases the overshoot above that of the plain second-order system.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> ω_n = distance of the pole from the origin; ζ = cos θ.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-85", year: 2018, no: 85, subject: "control", topic: "Error-rate damping",
q: `What is the error-rate factor K<sub>e</sub> to yield a damping factor of 0·5 for the system shown in the block diagram (forward path (1 + sK<sub>e</sub>)/(s + 2) followed by 10/s, unity feedback)?<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2018-85.png" alt="Figure for question 85">`,
opts: ["0·116", "0·232", "0·284", "0·332"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·116</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Open loop G(s) = 10(1 + sK<sub>e</sub>)/(s(s + 2)). Characteristic equation: s² + 2s + 10 + 10K<sub>e</sub>s = s² + (2 + 10K<sub>e</sub>)s + 10 = 0.</p><div class="formula">s² + (2 + 10K_e)s + 10 = 0</div><div class="formula">2ζω_n = 2 + 10K_e</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>ω<sub>n</sub> = √10 = 3.162 rad/s.</li><li>2ζω<sub>n</sub> = 3.162 = 2 + 10K<sub>e</sub> → K<sub>e</sub> = 0.1162.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0.232 would give ζ = 0.7.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.284 would give ζ = 0.78.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.332 would give ζ = 0.84.</li></ul><p>The factor (1 + sK<sub>e</sub>) is a proportional-plus-derivative controller on the error signal: the 'error rate' K<sub>e</sub> adds damping without changing the natural frequency, and increases the velocity of response.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Match s² + (2 + 10K_e)s + 10 with s² + 2ζω_n s + ω_n².</p>` },

{ id: "2018-86", year: 2018, no: 86, subject: "machines", topic: "Power supplied by an ideal transformer",
q: `An ideal transformer is having 150 turns primary and 750 turns secondary. The primary coil is connected to a 240 V, 50 Hz source. The secondary winding supplies a load of 4 A at lagging power factor of 0·8. What is the power supplied by the transformer to the load?`,
opts: ["4200 W", "3840 W", "2100 W", "1920 W"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 3840 W</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Secondary voltage V₂ = 240 × 750/150 = 1200 V.</p><div class="formula">V₂ = V₁N₂/N₁</div><div class="formula">P = V₂I₂ cos φ</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>P = V₂I₂ cos φ = 1200 × 4 × 0.8 = 3840 W.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 4200 W would need a power factor of 0.875.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2100 W would need a power factor of about 0.44.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1920 W would use the primary voltage 240 V (240 × 4 × 0.8 = 768) wrongly doubled.</li></ul><p>An ideal transformer has no losses, so the primary draws the same power: 3840 W, and the primary current is 3840/(240 × 0.8) = 20 A, five times the secondary current of 4 A, consistent with the turns ratio.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> V₂ = V₁ N₂/N₁; P = V₂ I₂ cos φ.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-87", year: 2018, no: 87, subject: "machines", topic: "Induction motor current and torque",
q: `In an induction motor for a fixed speed at constant frequency`,
opts: ["Both line current and torque are proportional to voltage", "Both line current and torque are proportional to the square of voltage", "Line current is proportional to voltage and torque is proportional to the square of voltage", "Line current is constant and torque is proportional to voltage"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) line current ∝ voltage and torque ∝ voltage²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At a fixed slip the equivalent circuit impedance is fixed, so the stator current I = V/Z is proportional to V. The air-gap power, and therefore the torque, is I²R/s, so T ∝ V².</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Torque goes with the square of the voltage, not with the first power.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The current is proportional to V, not V².</li><li><b>(d)</b> The line current is not constant; it varies directly with the voltage.</li></ul><p>At a given slip the motor torque ∝ V² means a 10 % drop in the supply voltage reduces the torque by 19 %, which can stall a heavily loaded motor. The starting torque and the maximum torque also fall as V².</p><p><b>Remember:</b> I ∝ V; T ∝ V² at a given slip.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-88", year: 2018, no: 88, subject: "machines", topic: "Staggered joints in a transformer core",
q: `In a core-type single-phase transformer the steel-core is assembled by staggering butt-joints in adjacent layers of laminations vide figures. The purpose served is said to be 1. Avoiding continuous air-gap 2. Preventing loss of mechanical strength 3. Reducing eddy-current loss<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2018-88.png" alt="Figure for question 88">`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> If all the butt joints were in line, there would be a continuous gap across the core.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: staggering means that the joint in one layer is covered by solid iron in the next, so there is no continuous air gap and the magnetising current is smaller.</li><li><b>2</b> True: the overlapping of the layers also makes the core mechanically rigid.</li><li><b>3</b> False: eddy-current loss is reduced by using thin, insulated laminations, not by staggering the joints.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Staggered joints: low reluctance + strength; laminations: low eddy loss.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-89", year: 2018, no: 89, subject: "machines", topic: "Ideal transformer assumptions",
q: `Which of the following would refer to an ideal transformer? 1. Winding-resistances are negligible 2. Leakage-fluxes are included 3. Core-losses are negligible 4. Magnetization characteristic is linear`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> An ideal transformer has zero winding resistance, no leakage flux, no core loss and an infinitely permeable (linear) core.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True.</li><li><b>2</b> False: an ideal transformer has no leakage flux; all the flux links both windings.</li><li><b>3</b> True.</li><li><b>4</b> True: the magnetisation characteristic is taken as linear (no saturation).</li></ul><p>A real transformer departs from the ideal through winding resistance (copper loss), leakage reactance (voltage regulation), finite core permeability (magnetising current) and core losses (hysteresis and eddy-current loss).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Ideal: R = 0, no leakage, no core loss, linear core.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-90", year: 2018, no: 90, subject: "machines", topic: "EMF of a dc machine",
q: `A 24-slot, 2-pole, lap-wound dc machine has 18 turns per coil. The average flux density per pole is 1 Tesla. The effective length of the machine is 20 cm and the radius of the armature is 10 cm. The magnetic poles cover 80% of the armature periphery. For armature angular velocity (ω<sub>m</sub>) of 183·2 rad/sec, the induced emf in the armature winding is nearly`,
opts: ["585 V", "1050 V", "1260 V", "1465 V"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1260 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a lap winding A = P = 2. Number of coils = number of slots = 24 (one coil per slot), so the conductors Z = 24 × 18 × 2 = 864.</p><div class="formula">E = PφZN/(60A)</div><div class="formula">φ = B × pole area</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Flux per pole = B × (pole area) = 1 × 0.8 × (2π × 0.1 × 0.2)/2 = 0.0503 Wb.</li><li>E = (P/A)(Z/2π)φω<sub>m</sub> ... in the standard form E = ZPφN/(60A) with N = 183.2 × 60/2π = 1750 rpm.</li><li>E = 864 × 2 × 0.0503 × 1750/(60 × 2) = 1267 V ≈ 1260 V.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 585 V is less than half the value.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1050 V would need φ = 0.0417 Wb.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1465 V would need φ = 0.058 Wb (a pole covering 92 %).</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> E = ZPφN/(60A).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-91", year: 2018, no: 91, subject: "machines", topic: "EMF of a long-shunt compound generator",
q: `A 220 V dc compound generator connected in long-shunt mode has the following parameters: R<sub>a</sub> = 0·1 Ω, R<sub>sh</sub> = 80 Ω, R<sub>series</sub> = 0·05 Ω. For a load of 150 A at rated terminal voltage, the induced emf of the generator should nearly be`,
opts: ["233 V", "243 V", "251 V", "262 V"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 243 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the long-shunt connection the shunt field is across the terminals, and the series field is in series with the armature.</p><div class="formula">Long shunt: I_a = I_L + I_sh</div><div class="formula">E = V + I_a(R_a + R_se)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I<sub>sh</sub> = 220/80 = 2.75 A.</li><li>I<sub>a</sub> = I<sub>L</sub> + I<sub>sh</sub> = 152.75 A (also the current in the series field).</li><li>E = V + I<sub>a</sub>(R<sub>a</sub> + R<sub>se</sub>) = 220 + 152.75 × 0.15 = 242.9 V ≈ 243 V.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 233 V ignores the series winding and the shunt current.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 251 V would need an armature-circuit drop of 31 V (about 0.2 Ω).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 262 V would need a drop of 42 V (about 0.27 Ω).</li></ul><p>In a short-shunt connection the shunt field is connected across the armature only, and the series field is in the line; the induced emf would then be E = V + I<sub>L</sub>R<sub>se</sub> + I<sub>a</sub>R<sub>a</sub> and the result differs slightly.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Long shunt: I_a = I_L + I_sh, the series field carries I_a.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-92", year: 2018, no: 92, subject: "signals", topic: "Laplace transform of t^n e^(−αt)",
q: `The Laplace transform of f(t) = tⁿe⁻ᵅᵗu(t) is`,
opts: ["(n + 1)!/(s + α)ⁿ⁺¹", "n!/(s + α)ⁿ", "(n − 1)!/(s + α)ⁿ⁺¹", "n!/(s + α)ⁿ⁺¹"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) n!/(s + α)ⁿ⁺¹</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> L{tⁿ} = n!/sⁿ⁺¹. By the frequency-shift property, multiplying by e<sup>−αt</sup> replaces s by s + α: L{tⁿe<sup>−αt</sup>} = n!/(s + α)ⁿ⁺¹.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The numerator would be (n + 1)! only for t<sup>n+1</sup>.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The exponent of the denominator must be n + 1.</li><li><b>(c)</b> (n − 1)! belongs to L{t<sup>n−1</sup>}.</li></ul><p>The result is a special case of the general n-th derivative property of the Laplace transform and is used in partial fraction work with repeated poles, such as 1/(s + α)ⁿ⁺¹ ↔ tⁿe<sup>−αt</sup>/n!. The same pattern gives L{t e<sup>−at</sup>} = 1/(s + a)² and L{t² e<sup>−at</sup>} = 2/(s + a)³, which are the building blocks for the inverse transforms of repeated poles in circuit and control problems.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> L{tⁿ} = n!/s^(n+1); shift s → s + α.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-93", year: 2018, no: 93, subject: "machines", topic: "Speed of a dc shunt motor at a given torque", selfCorrected: true,
q: `A dc shunt motor has the following characteristics, R<sub>a</sub> = 0·5 Ω, R<sub>f</sub> = 200 Ω, base speed = 1000 rpm, rated voltage = 250 V. On no load it draws a current = 5 A. At what speed will this run while delivering a torque of 150 N·m?`,
opts: ["881 rpm", "920 rpm", "950 rpm", "990 rpm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 881 rpm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> No load: I<sub>f</sub> = 250/200 = 1.25 A, I<sub>a0</sub> = 5 − 1.25 = 3.75 A, E₀ = 250 − 3.75 × 0.5 = 248.1 V at 1000 rpm (ω₀ = 104.7 rad/s).</p><div class="formula">Kφ = E₀/ω₀</div><div class="formula">I_a = T/(Kφ)</div><div class="formula">N ∝ E</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Machine constant Kφ = E₀/ω₀ = 2.369 V·s/rad.</li><li>T = KφI<sub>a</sub> → I<sub>a</sub> = 150/2.369 = 63.3 A (rotational losses neglected).</li><li>E = 250 − 63.3 × 0.5 = 218.3 V.</li><li>N = 1000 × 218.3/248.1 = 880 rpm ≈ 881 rpm.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 920 rpm needs E = 228 V, i.e. I<sub>a</sub> = 44 A.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 950 rpm needs E = 236 V, i.e. I<sub>a</sub> = 28 A.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 990 rpm needs E = 246 V, i.e. I<sub>a</sub> = 8 A, much below the value for 150 N·m.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> T = Kφ I_a; N ∝ E at constant flux.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-94", year: 2018, no: 94, subject: "machines", topic: "Speed-current curves of a dc motor", selfCorrected: true,
q: `The figure shows plots of speed (N) vs. armature current (I<sub>a</sub>) of a dc motor for two different operating conditions (curve (1) falls steeply with I<sub>a</sub>; curve (2) is nearly flat or slightly falling). Which one of the following features is relevant?<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2018-94.png" alt="Figure for question 94">`,
opts: ["(1) represents stronger shunt field, and (2) represents stronger series field of a compound motor", "(1) represents stronger series field, and (2) represents stronger shunt field of a compound motor", "(1) represents only shunt excitation, and (2) represents only series excitation", "(1) represents only series excitation, and (2) represents only shunt excitation"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) (1) stronger series field, (2) stronger shunt field of a compound motor</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a series motor the flux rises with the load current, so the speed falls steeply as the load increases. In a shunt motor the flux is nearly constant and the speed is almost constant (slightly drooping). A compound motor lies between them: a stronger series field makes the speed fall more steeply, and a stronger shunt field makes the curve flatter.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> This reverses the roles: the steep curve (1) corresponds to the stronger series field.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A pure shunt motor gives a nearly flat line, and a pure series motor does not stay finite at no load, so these curves are compound characteristics.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The pure series motor runs away at no load; the curve (1) is not that of a pure series machine.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Series field: speed falls steeply with load.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-95", year: 2018, no: 95, subject: "machines", topic: "Single-phase induction motor: backward branch resistance", selfCorrected: true,
q: `A 230 V, 50 Hz, 4-pole, single-phase induction motor is rotating clockwise (forward) direction at a speed of 1425 rpm. If the rotor resistance at standstill is 7·8 Ω, then the effective rotor resistance in the backward branch of the equivalent circuit will be`,
opts: ["2·0 Ω", "4·0 Ω", "78 Ω", "156 Ω"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 4·0 Ω</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Synchronous speed = 120 × 50/4 = 1500 rpm, forward slip s = (1500 − 1425)/1500 = 0.05. The backward slip is 2 − s = 1.95.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>The backward branch rotor resistance is R₂/(2 − s) (R₂ the standstill rotor resistance as given for the branch).</li><li>= 7.8/1.95 = 4.0 Ω.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2.0 Ω would be 0.5R₂/(2 − s), if 7.8 Ω were the total (both branches) value.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 78 Ω does not correspond to either branch.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 156 Ω is the effective resistance of the forward branch, R₂/s.</li></ul><p>The forward branch resistance is R₂/s = 156 Ω.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Backward branch: R₂/(2 − s); forward: R₂/s.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-96", year: 2018, no: 96, subject: "machines", topic: "Air-gap power of an induction motor",
q: `A 400 V, 50 Hz, 30 hp, three phase induction motor is drawing 50 A current at 0·8 power factor lagging. The stator and rotor copper losses are 1·5 kW and 900 W respectively. The friction and windage losses are 1050 W and the core losses are 1200 W. The air gap power of the motor will be nearly`,
opts: ["15 kW", "20 kW", "25 kW", "30 kW"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 25 kW</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Input power P<sub>in</sub> = √3 × 400 × 50 × 0.8 = 27.7 kW.</p><div class="formula">P_in = √3V_LI_L cos φ</div><div class="formula">P_ag = P_in − P_scu − P_core</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Air-gap power = P<sub>in</sub> − stator copper loss − core loss = 27.7 − 1.5 − 1.2 = 25.0 kW.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 15 kW would imply very large losses.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 20 kW would imply 7.7 kW of stator losses.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 30 kW exceeds the input power of 27.7 kW.</li></ul><p>Check: the output would be 25.0 − 0.9 − 1.05 = 23.05 kW = 30.9 hp, consistent with a 30 hp motor.</p><p>The rotor input (air-gap) power can be checked from the output: 30 hp = 22.4 kW plus friction and windage 1.05 kW plus rotor copper 0.9 kW gives 24.3 kW, close to the 25 kW calculated from the stator side.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> P_g = P_in − P_cu1 − P_core.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-97", year: 2018, no: 97, subject: "machines", topic: "Effective rotor resistance near zero slip",
q: `When the value of slip of an induction motor approaches zero, the effective resistance`,
opts: ["is very low and the motor is under no-load", "of the rotor circuit is very high and the motor is under no-load", "is zero", "of the rotor circuit is infinity and the motor is equivalent to short-circuited two-winding transformer"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) of the rotor circuit is very high and the motor is under no-load</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the equivalent circuit the rotor branch resistance is R₂/s. As s → 0 the effective resistance R₂/s becomes very large, so the rotor current is very small: the motor is at no load.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The resistance R₂/s is very high, not low.</li><li><b>(c)</b> R₂/s is not zero (it would be zero only if R₂ = 0).</li><li><b>(d)</b> The resistance does not become infinite at any practical no-load slip; the short-circuited transformer equivalence applies at s = 1 (standstill).</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Rotor equivalent resistance: R₂/s.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-98", year: 2018, no: 98, subject: "machines", topic: "Rotor reactance at maximum torque", selfCorrected: true,
q: `A 4-pole, 50 Hz, 3-phase induction motor with a rotor resistance of 0·25 Ω develops a maximum torque of 25 N·m at 1400 rpm. The rotor reactance x₂ and slip at maximum torque s<sub>max,T</sub> respectively would be`,
opts: ["2·0 and 1/15", "3·75 and 1/12", "2·0 and 1/12", "3·75 and 1/15"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 3·75 and 1/15</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> N<sub>s</sub> = 1500 rpm; the slip at maximum torque is s<sub>m</sub> = (1500 − 1400)/1500 = 1/15.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>s<sub>m</sub> = R₂/X₂ → X₂ = R₂/s<sub>m</sub> = 0.25 × 15 = 3.75 Ω.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> X₂ = 2.0 Ω would give s<sub>m</sub> = 0.125.</li><li><b>(b)</b> s<sub>m</sub> = 1/12 would put the speed at 1375 rpm.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Both values are wrong.</li></ul><p>The maximum torque is T<sub>max</sub> = 3V²/(2ω<sub>s</sub>X₂) and does not depend on R₂; only the slip at which it occurs, s<sub>m</sub> = R₂/X₂, changes with R₂, which is why a wound-rotor motor can be given full torque at starting by adding resistance.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> s_m = R₂/X₂ (stator impedance neglected).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-99", year: 2018, no: 99, subject: "machines", topic: "Slip from the loss distribution", selfCorrected: true,
q: `A 3-phase, 37 kW induction motor has an efficiency of 90% when delivering full load. At this load the stator copper losses and rotor copper losses are equal and are equal to stator iron losses. The mechanical losses are one-third of no-load losses. Then the motor runs at a slip of`,
opts: ["0·01", "0·02", "0·03", "0·04"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·03</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Input = 37/0.9 = 41.11 kW, so the total loss is 4.11 kW. Let each of the stator copper, rotor copper and stator iron losses be x. No-load loss = iron + mechanical = x + m, and m = (x + m)/3 → m = x/2.</p><div class="formula">Input = Output/η</div><div class="formula">s = P_rcu/P_ag</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Total loss = 3x + 0.5x = 3.5x = 4.111 → x = 1.175 kW, m = 0.587 kW.</li><li>Air-gap power = output + mechanical loss + rotor copper = 37 + 0.587 + 1.175 = 38.76 kW.</li><li>s = rotor copper loss/air-gap power = 1.175/38.76 = 0.030.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.01 would need a rotor copper loss of 0.39 kW.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.02 would need a rotor copper loss of 0.78 kW.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.04 would need a rotor copper loss of 1.55 kW.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> s = P_cu2/P_gap.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-100", year: 2018, no: 100, subject: "machines", topic: "EMF of an alternator", doubt: true,
q: `The rotor of a 4-pole ac generator is wound with a 200 turns coil. If the flux per pole is 5 mWb and the rotor runs at a speed of 1500 rpm, the rms value of the induced voltage for this ac generator is nearly`,
opts: ["140 V", "157 V", "164 V", "200 V"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 200 V (as keyed)</h3><p class="note">In doubt (key followed). The rms value from E = 4.44fφN is 222 V, which is not offered; the key's 200 V equals 4fφN.</p><p><b>Concept:</b> f = PN/120 = 4 × 1500/120 = 50 Hz. The standard emf equation is E<sub>rms</sub> = 4.44fφN = 4.44 × 50 × 0.005 × 200 = 222 V, which is not among the options.</p><div class="formula">f = PN/120</div><div class="formula">E_rms = 4.44 f φ N</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>f = 4 × 1500/120 = 50 Hz.</li><li>4.44 × 50 × 0.005 × 200 = 222 V (not offered).</li><li>The key's 200 V = 4 f φ N (form factor taken as 1).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 140 V = 2.8 fφN – no form factor gives it.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 157 V = 222/√2 divides by √2 wrongly (4.44 already gives the rms value).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 164 V = 3.28 fφN – no form factor gives it.</li></ul><p>The key's 200 V equals 4fφN = 4 × 50 × 0.005 × 200 = 200 V, the average value of the emf for a full pitched coil with a form factor of 1.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> E_rms = 4.44 f φ N (form factor 1.11).</p>` }
);

// ---- q2018e.js ----
// ESE 2018 Prelims Paper II, Electrical Engineering, Series A (SKP-D-ETE), Q101–125
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; none dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2018-101", year: 2018, no: 101, subject: "machines", topic: "Synchronizing torque", selfCorrected: true,
q: `A 3-MVA, 6-pole, 50 Hz, 3-phase synchronous generator is connected to an infinite bus of 3300 V; and it is run at 1000 rpm. The synchronous reactance of the machine is 0·915 Ω per phase. The synchronizing torque for 1° mechanical displacement of the rotor is`,
opts: ["7500 N·m", "7000 N·m", "6000 N·m", "4500 N·m"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 6000 N·m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Take the machine floating on the bus with E = V (no load, δ = 0). Per phase V = 3300/√3 = 1905 V.</p><div class="formula">P_s = 3EV/X_s per electrical radian</div><div class="formula">T_s = P_s/ω_s</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Synchronising power per electrical radian = 3EV/X<sub>s</sub> = 3 × 1905²/0.915 = 11.9 MW/rad.</li><li>For 6 poles, 1° mechanical = 3° electrical = 0.05236 rad, so P<sub>s</sub> = 11.9 × 0.05236 = 623 kW.</li><li>ω<sub>s</sub> = 2π × 1000/60 = 104.7 rad/s; T<sub>s</sub> = 623 000/104.7 = 5950 N·m ≈ 6000 N·m.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 7500 N·m would need 785 kW.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 7000 N·m would need 733 kW.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 4500 N·m would need 471 kW.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> T_s = (3EV/X_s)(P/2)(π/180)/ω_s per mechanical degree.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-102", year: 2018, no: 102, subject: "machines", topic: "Reluctance power of a salient pole machine",
q: `The second-harmonic component of the power P versus load angle δ characteristic of a synchronous machine, operating at a terminal voltage V<sub>t</sub> and having the d- and q-axis reactance per phase of X<sub>d</sub> and X<sub>q</sub>, respectively, is`,
opts: ["(V<sub>t</sub>²/2)(X<sub>d</sub>X<sub>q</sub>/(X<sub>d</sub> + X<sub>q</sub>)) sin 2δ", "(V<sub>t</sub>²/2)(1/X<sub>q</sub> − 1/X<sub>d</sub>) sin 2δ", "(V<sub>t</sub>²/2)(X<sub>d</sub>X<sub>q</sub>/(X<sub>d</sub> + X<sub>q</sub>)) cos 2δ", "(V<sub>t</sub>²/2)(1/X<sub>q</sub> − 1/X<sub>d</sub>) cos 2δ"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) (V<sub>t</sub>²/2)(1/X<sub>q</sub> − 1/X<sub>d</sub>) sin 2δ</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The power of a salient pole machine is P = (EV/X<sub>d</sub>) sin δ + (V²/2)(1/X<sub>q</sub> − 1/X<sub>d</sub>) sin 2δ. The first term is the fundamental (excitation) component, and the second, the reluctance power, is the second harmonic with amplitude (V²/2)(1/X<sub>q</sub> − 1/X<sub>d</sub>).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The reactances appear as a product over a sum, which is not the form of the reluctance term.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Same wrong coefficient, and cos 2δ is also wrong.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The coefficient is right but the function of the angle must be sin 2δ.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Reluctance power = (V²/2)(1/Xq − 1/Xd) sin 2δ.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-103", year: 2018, no: 103, subject: "machines", topic: "Synchronous condenser",
q: `The term Synchronous condenser refers to`,
opts: ["A synchronous motor with a capacitor connected across the stator terminals to improve the power factor", "A synchronous motor operating at full-load with leading power factor", "An over-excited synchronous motor partially supplying mechanical load and also improving the power factor of the system to which it is connected", "An over-excited synchronous motor operating at no-load with leading power factor used in large power stations for improvement of power factor"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) an over-excited synchronous motor at no load with leading power factor used in large power stations</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A synchronous condenser (compensator) is a synchronous machine running at no load, with no mechanical load on the shaft, whose field current is varied. Over-excited, it draws leading current (supplies lagging reactive power) and so improves the power factor and regulates the voltage at the receiving end of a line or substation.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> No capacitor is connected; the machine itself supplies the reactive power.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A condenser runs at no load, not at full load.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A condenser carries no mechanical load; one that does is an ordinary synchronous motor used for pf correction.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Synchronous condenser: over-excited, no-load, reactive power source.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-104", year: 2018, no: 104, subject: "machines", topic: "Voltage regulation of an alternator",
q: `Which of the following operating aspects necessitate the computation of Regulation of an alternator? 1. When load is thrown off 2. For designing of an automatic voltage-control equipment 3. For determination of steady-state and transient stability 4. For parallel operation of alternators`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Voltage regulation tells how much the terminal voltage changes between load and no load.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> The voltage rise when the load is thrown off is the regulation.</li><li><b>2</b> The regulator (AVR) design needs the regulation characteristics.</li><li><b>3</b> The reactances that determine the regulation also fix the stability limits.</li><li><b>4</b> Alternators operating in parallel share load and reactive power according to their regulation.</li></ul><p>All four are accepted.</p><p>The regulation of an alternator is found from the open-circuit and short-circuit characteristics by the synchronous impedance, MMF or Potier methods; the synchronous impedance method gives pessimistic values, because it neglects saturation.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Regulation: load rejection, AVR, stability, paralleling.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-105", year: 2018, no: 105, subject: "machines", topic: "Two-phase servomotor",
q: `A 2-phase ac servomotor has a tendency to run as a single-phase induction motor, if the voltage across the control winding becomes zero. To prevent this`,
opts: ["Rotor having high mass and moment of inertia is to be used", "Drag-cup type of light rotor and high resistance is to be used", "A low resistance rotor is to be used", "The number of turns in the control winding is to be kept lesser than in the main reference winding"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) drag-cup type of light rotor and high resistance is to be used</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A single-phase induction motor can continue to run in the direction it was turning, because of the forward-rotating field. With a high-resistance rotor, the torque–speed curve of the single-phase motor has a negative slope everywhere (the maximum torque occurs at a slip above one), so the motor stops quickly when the control voltage is removed. A light drag-cup rotor also gives a low inertia and quick response.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A heavy rotor would keep running by inertia.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A low-resistance rotor makes the single-phase torque positive at speed, so the motor would keep running.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The turns ratio of the windings has no effect on single-phasing.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> High rotor resistance: negative torque slope, no single-phase running.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-106", year: 2018, no: 106, subject: "machines", topic: "Speed of a stepper motor", selfCorrected: true,
q: `A single-stack, 8-phase (stator), multiple-step motor has 6-rotor teeth. The poles are excited one at a time. If excitation frequency is 120 Hz, the speed of the motor is`,
opts: ["3 rps", "5 rps", "10 rps", "15 rps"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 5 rps</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a variable-reluctance stepper with N<sub>s</sub> = 8 stator poles and N<sub>r</sub> = 6 rotor teeth, the step angle is 360(N<sub>s</sub> − N<sub>r</sub>)/(N<sub>s</sub>N<sub>r</sub>) = 360 × 2/48 = 15°.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Steps per second = 120, so the speed is 120 × 15° = 1800°/s.</li><li>1800/360 = 5 rev/s.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 3 rps would result from a step angle of 9°.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 10 rps would need a step angle of 30°.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 15 rps would need 45° steps.</li></ul><p>A variable-reluctance stepper has the step angle 360°/(N<sub>r</sub> × number of phases) in the simplest arrangement; the formula with (N<sub>s</sub> − N<sub>r</sub>) applies to motors where the stator and rotor tooth counts differ.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Speed = (step angle × pulse rate)/360.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-107", year: 2018, no: 107, subject: "power", topic: "Line length compared with wavelength",
q: `An extra high voltage transmission line of length 300 km can be approximated by a lossless line having propagation constant β = 0·00127 rad/km. The percentage ratio of line length to wavelength will nearly be`,
opts: ["24%", "19%", "12%", "6%"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 6%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Wavelength λ = 2π/β = 6.283/0.00127 = 4947 km.</p><div class="formula">λ = 2π/β</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Ratio = 300/4947 = 0.0606 = 6 %.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 24 % would need a wavelength of 1250 km.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 19 % would need a wavelength of 1580 km.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 12 % would need a wavelength of 2500 km.</li></ul><p>A transmission line shorter than about λ/10 (about 500 km at 50 Hz) can be treated with lumped parameters; 6 % of the wavelength is well within that limit, which justifies the nominal π model for this line. The phase shift along the line is βl = 0.00127 × 300 = 0.381 rad = 21.8 electrical degrees, which is small enough for the short-line approximations to be reasonably accurate.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> λ = 2π/β (≈ 5000 km at 50–60 Hz).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-108", year: 2018, no: 108, subject: "power", topic: "Lossy capacitor",
q: `A lossy capacitor C<sub>x</sub>, rated for operation of 5 kV, 50 Hz is represented by an equivalent circuit with an ideal capacitor C<sub>p</sub> in parallel with a resistor R<sub>p</sub>. C<sub>p</sub> is 0·102 μF; and R<sub>p</sub> = 1·25 MΩ. The power loss, and tan δ, of this lossy capacitor when operating at the rated voltage are, respectively`,
opts: ["20 W and 0·04", "10 W and 0·04", "20 W and 0·025", "10 W and 0·025"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 20 W and 0·025</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Loss in the parallel resistor: P = V²/R<sub>p</sub> = (5000)²/1.25 × 10⁶ = 20 W.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>For a parallel model tan δ = 1/(ωC<sub>p</sub>R<sub>p</sub>) = 1/(314.16 × 0.102 × 10⁻⁶ × 1.25 × 10⁶) = 1/40.05 = 0.025.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> tan δ = 0.04 would need ωC<sub>p</sub>R<sub>p</sub> = 25.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Both values are wrong.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 10 W would need R<sub>p</sub> = 2.5 MΩ.</li></ul><p>The dissipation factor tan δ is a figure of merit for dielectric quality; a value of 0.025 is high and shows an unusually lossy capacitor, since good power capacitors have tan δ below 10⁻³.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Parallel RC: tan δ = 1/(ωCR).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-109", year: 2018, no: 109, subject: "power", topic: "Travelling time of a surge",
q: `The time interval needed for a surge to travel to the end of a 600 km long overhead transmission line is`,
opts: ["6 s", "2 s", "20 ms", "2 ms"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 2 ms</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A surge on an overhead line travels at about the speed of light, 3 × 10⁵ km/s.</p><div class="formula">t = l/v, v ≈ 3 × 10⁵ km/s</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>t = 600/(3 × 10⁵) = 2 × 10⁻³ s = 2 ms.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 6 s would be a speed of 100 km/s.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 2 s would be a speed of 300 km/s.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 20 ms would be a speed of 30 000 km/s.</li></ul><p>The surge reaches the end of the line in 2 ms and, after reflection, returns after 4 ms; the wave equation governs how the surge is reflected and attenuated at the line terminations. This is also why the insulation co-ordination of long lines is based on the travel time of a surge: the lightning stroke at one end and the protective gap at the other see each other only after this delay.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Surge speed on overhead lines ≈ c.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-110", year: 2018, no: 110, subject: "power", topic: "Zero regulation of a lossless line",
q: `At what power factor will a lossless line with a reactance of 0·6 pu exhibit zero regulation given that the sending-end voltage is 1·0 pu?`,
opts: ["0·800 lag", "0·800 lead", "0·954 lead", "Unity p.f."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·954 lead</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a lossless line with series reactance X delivering a current I (1 pu) at a leading power factor: V<sub>s</sub>² = (V<sub>r</sub> − XI sin φ)² + (XI cos φ)². Zero regulation means V<sub>s</sub> = V<sub>r</sub> = 1.</p><div class="formula">V_s² = (V_r − XI sin φ)² + (XI cos φ)² (leading)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>1 = (1 − 0.6 sin φ)² + (0.6 cos φ)² = 1 − 1.2 sin φ + 0.36.</li><li>sin φ = 0.36/1.2 = 0.3, so cos φ = 0.954 (leading).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A lagging load makes the receiving-end voltage fall (positive regulation).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.8 lead gives sin φ = 0.6 and V<sub>s</sub> &lt; V<sub>r</sub> (a Ferranti rise).</li><li><b>(d)</b> At unity pf the receiving voltage is lower than the sending voltage (regulation positive).</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Zero regulation needs a leading load with sin φ = XI/2.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-111", year: 2018, no: 111, subject: "power", topic: "Efficiency of a transmission line",
q: `An 11 kV, 3-phase transmission line has resistance of 1·5 Ω and reactance of 4 Ω per phase. The efficiency of the line when supplying the load of 4 MW at 0·8 lagging power factor is nearly`,
opts: ["99%", "95%", "92%", "90%"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 92%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Line current I = P/(√3 V cos φ) = 4 × 10⁶/(1.732 × 11 000 × 0.8) = 262.4 A.</p><div class="formula">I = P/(√3V cos φ)</div><div class="formula">η = P/(P + 3I²R)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Copper loss = 3I²R = 3 × 262.4² × 1.5 = 0.31 MW.</li><li>Efficiency = 4/(4 + 0.31) = 0.928 ≈ 92.8 %, or 92 % when quoted by 1 − 0.31/4 = 92.2 %.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 99 % would need a loss of 40 kW.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 95 % would need a loss of 0.2 MW.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 90 % would need a loss of 0.44 MW.</li></ul><p>The percentage voltage drop in the line is I(R cos φ + X sin φ)/V × √3 = 262.4 × (1.2 + 2.4)/11000 × 1.732 = 14.9 %, so the regulation is poor and the load would normally be supplied at a higher voltage.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> η = P/(P + 3I²R).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-112", year: 2018, no: 112, subject: "power", topic: "Dielectric loss in a cable",
q: `The dielectric loss in the insulation of a lossy underground cable, due to leakage current is (using standard notations)`,
opts: ["ωCV² cos δ", "ωCV tan δ", "ωCV² tan δ", "ωCV sin δ"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) ωCV² tan δ</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The charging current is I<sub>c</sub> = ωCV and the loss current is I<sub>c</sub> tan δ. The loss is P = V × I<sub>c</sub> tan δ = ωCV² tan δ per phase (V the phase voltage).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> cos δ would apply if the loss were taken per unit apparent power.</li><li><b>(b)</b> This is a current, not a power; it lacks one factor of V.</li><li><b>(d)</b> This is also dimensionally a current.</li></ul><p>Dielectric loss increases with the square of the voltage and linearly with frequency and capacitance, which is why it limits the voltage rating of cables to about 66 kV, above which special insulation such as oil-filled or gas-pressurised cables is used.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> P_d = ωCV² tan δ.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-113", year: 2018, no: 113, subject: "power", topic: "Ratio of three-phase to single line-to-ground fault current",
q: `A 3-phase, 100 MVA, 11 kV generator has the following p.u. constants. The generator neutral is solidly grounded. X₁ = X₂ = 3X₀ = 0·15 Ω. The ratio of the fault current due to three-phase dead-short-circuit to that due to L-G fault would be nearly`,
opts: ["0·33", "0·56", "0·78", "1·0"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·78</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> X₁ = X₂ = 0.15 and X₀ = 0.05 (pu). Prefault voltage 1 pu.</p><div class="formula">I_3φ = 1/X₁</div><div class="formula">I_LG = 3/(X₁ + X₂ + X₀)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Three-phase fault current I₃ = 1/X₁ = 6.67 pu.</li><li>L-G fault current I<sub>LG</sub> = 3/(X₁ + X₂ + X₀) = 3/0.35 = 8.57 pu.</li><li>Ratio = 6.67/8.57 = 0.778.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.33 would result from comparing 1/X₁ with 3/(X₁ + X₂ + X₀) taken upside down plus a slip.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.56 would need I_LG ≈ 11.9 pu.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A ratio of 1 would need X₀ = X₁.</li></ul><p>The L-G fault current is greater than the three-phase fault current here because X₀ is smaller than X₁; for a solidly grounded generator with X₀ &lt; X₁ the single line-to-ground fault is the severest fault.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> I_3φ = E/X₁; I_LG = 3E/(X₁ + X₂ + X₀).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-114", year: 2018, no: 114, subject: "power", topic: "Sequence components with an open line",
q: `A balanced 3-phase load is supplied from a 3-phase supply. The contact in line c of the triple-pole switch contactor fails to connect when switched on. If the line-currents in lines a and b record 25 A each, then the positive-sequence component of the current is<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2018-114.png" alt="Figure for question 114">`,
opts: ["14·4∠+30° A", "25·0∠−30° A", "14·4∠−30° A", "25·0∠+30° A"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 14·4∠−30° A</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> With line c open, I<sub>c</sub> = 0, and I<sub>a</sub> + I<sub>b</sub> = 0 so I<sub>b</sub> = −I<sub>a</sub>. Taking I<sub>a</sub> = 25∠0°:</p><div class="formula">I_a1 = (I_a + aI_b + a²I_c)/3</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I<sub>a1</sub> = (I<sub>a</sub> + aI<sub>b</sub> + a²I<sub>c</sub>)/3 = 25(1 − a)/3.</li><li>1 − a = 1.5 − j0.866 = 1.732∠−30°.</li><li>I<sub>a1</sub> = 25 × 1.732∠−30°/3 = 14.4∠−30° A.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The angle must be −30°, as 1 − a lags the reference.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 25 A would be the line current, not one-third of √3 times it.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Both magnitude and angle are wrong.</li></ul><p>The negative sequence current I₂ = (I<sub>a</sub> + a²I<sub>b</sub> + aI<sub>c</sub>)/3 = 14.4∠+30° A has the same magnitude as I₁, and the zero sequence current is zero because the three-wire supply has no neutral path.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> I₁ = (Ia + aIb + a²Ic)/3.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-115", year: 2018, no: 115, subject: "power", topic: "Arc in a circuit breaker",
q: `In a circuit-breaker, the arc is produced due to 1. Thermal emission 2. High temperature of air 3. Field emission. Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> When the contacts part, the arc is started by the emission of electrons from the cathode.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: thermionic (thermal) emission from the heated cathode spot.</li><li><b>2</b> Not counted as a cause: the high temperature is a result of the arc and sustains it through thermal ionisation of the gas.</li><li><b>3</b> True: field emission, because of the very high field across the small gap when the contacts separate.</li></ul><p>So 1 and 3 only.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Arc initiation: field emission + thermionic emission; sustained by thermal ionisation.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-116", year: 2018, no: 116, subject: "power", topic: "Bus impedance matrix",
q: `The line reactances of a power network are: line 1 from bus 0 to bus 1, 0·2 p.u.; line 2 from bus 1 to bus 2, 0·4 p.u. The bus impedance matrix with '0' as ref-bus is`,
opts: ["[0·2 0·4; 0·4 0·6]", "[0·4 0·2; 0·2 0·6]", "[0·2 0·2; 0·2 0·6]", "[0·6 0·2; 0·2 0·4]"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) [0·2 0·2; 0·2 0·6]</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The network is a radial chain: reference 0 — 0.2 — bus 1 — 0.4 — bus 2.</p><div class="formula">Z_ii = impedance from bus i to reference</div><div class="formula">Z_ij = impedance of the common path</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Z₁₁ = impedance from bus 1 to the reference = 0.2.</li><li>Z₂₂ = 0.2 + 0.4 = 0.6.</li><li>Z₁₂ = Z₂₁ = the path common to both buses = 0.2.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Z₁₂ = 0.4 is not the shared path impedance.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Z₁₁ = 0.4 is the impedance of the line, not the driving point impedance of bus 1.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The entries Z₁₁ = 0.6 and Z₂₂ = 0.4 are interchanged.</li></ul><p>The bus impedance matrix is built step by step: adding a branch from a new bus to the reference gives Z₁₁; adding a branch from an existing bus to a new bus adds the branch impedance to the diagonal and copies the row and column.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Z_bus(i,j) = impedance of the path common to i and j to the reference.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-117", year: 2018, no: 117, subject: "machines", topic: "Alternator", selfCorrected: true,
q: `An alternator is`,
opts: ["A polyphase synchronous machine operated with DC exciter", "A polyphase synchronous machine operated with AC exciter", "A three-phase induction machine with prime mover", "Any AC generator"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) a polyphase synchronous machine operated with a DC exciter</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> An alternator is a synchronous generator: a polyphase armature winding on the stator and a field winding on the rotor, which is excited with direct current from a dc exciter (or a rectified static exciter), driven by a prime mover.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The field of a synchronous machine needs direct current; an ac exciter would need a rectifier.</li><li><b>(c)</b> An induction machine driven above synchronous speed is an induction generator, not an alternator.</li><li><b>(d)</b> "Any AC generator" is too general: induction generators are also ac generators.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Alternator = synchronous generator with dc field excitation.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-118", year: 2018, no: 118, subject: "power", topic: "Assessment of power system stability",
q: `The stability of a system, when subjected to a disturbance, is assessable by which of the following methods? 1. Swing curve 2. Equal-area criterion 3. Power-angle diagram 4. Power-circle diagram`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 3 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Transient stability is studied with:</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> The swing curve (δ against t) shows whether the angle settles or grows.</li><li><b>2</b> The equal-area criterion tests stability without solving the swing equation.</li><li><b>3</b> The power-angle diagram shows the pre-fault, fault and post-fault curves.</li><li><b>4</b> The power-circle diagram gives the steady-state power flow of a line, not its stability.</li></ul><p>For steady state stability the power angle must not exceed 90°; for transient stability the equal area criterion states that the accelerating area must equal the decelerating area before the machine loses synchronism.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Stability: swing curve, equal area, power-angle curves.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-119", year: 2018, no: 119, subject: "power", topic: "Increasing the transmission capacity",
q: `Power transmission capacity of a high voltage line can be increased by`,
opts: ["Increasing the resistance of the line", "Increasing the inductive reactance of the line", "Reducing the effective series reactance of the line", "Reducing the shunt admittance of the line"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) reducing the effective series reactance of the line</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The maximum power transferred over a line is P<sub>max</sub> = V<sub>s</sub>V<sub>r</sub>/X. Reducing the series reactance (by series capacitors, bundled conductors or parallel lines) increases the capacity.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> More resistance raises the loss and lowers the capacity.</li><li><b>(b)</b> More reactance lowers P<sub>max</sub> = VsVr/X.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The shunt admittance does not limit the power in this relation.</li></ul><p>The series compensation with capacitors can reduce the effective reactance by as much as 70 % and is a common means of raising the transmission capacity of long EHV lines; typical compensation levels are 30 to 70 %.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> P_max = V_sV_r/X: reduce X.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-120", year: 2018, no: 120, subject: "power", topic: "Acceleration of a turbo-alternator",
q: `A 40 MVA, 11 kV, 3-phase, 50 Hz, 4-pole turbo-alternator has an inertia constant of 15 sec. An input of 20 MW developed 15 MW of output power (Neglecting losses). Then the acceleration is`,
opts: ["60°/s²", "65°/s²", "70°/s²", "75°/s²"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 75°/s²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula:</b> α = 180 f P<sub>a</sub>/(GH) electrical degrees per second², with P<sub>a</sub> the accelerating power.</p><div class="formula">α = 180 f P_a/(GH) °/s²</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>P<sub>a</sub> = 20 − 15 = 5 MW; G = 40 MVA; H = 15 s.</li><li>α = 180 × 50 × 5/(40 × 15) = 45 000/600 = 75°/s².</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 60°/s² would need P<sub>a</sub> = 4 MW.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 65°/s² would need P<sub>a</sub> = 4.33 MW.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 70°/s² would need P<sub>a</sub> = 4.67 MW.</li></ul><p>The inertia constant H is the kinetic energy at rated speed per MVA of rating; for a large turbo-alternator it is typically 4 to 10 s, so the value 15 s here is high, and the acceleration is correspondingly small.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> α = 180 f P_a/(G H).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-121", year: 2018, no: 121, subject: "machines", topic: "Back and front pitch of a lap winding",
q: `In a Progressive Simplex Lap Winding for a 4-pole, 14-slot, 2 coil-sides per slot d.c. armature, the back pitch y<sub>b</sub> and front pitch y<sub>f</sub> will be respectively`,
opts: ["7 and 5", "5 and 5", "7 and 7", "5 and 7"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 7 and 5</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Number of coil sides = 14 × 2 = 28. The pole pitch is 28/4 = 7 coil sides. For a simplex progressive lap winding, y<sub>b</sub> = y<sub>f</sub> + 2 and both are odd, the back pitch being near the pole pitch.</p><div class="formula">Pole pitch = coil sides/P</div><div class="formula">Simplex lap: y_b − y_f = ±2</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>y<sub>b</sub> = 7, y<sub>f</sub> = 5, resultant y = 2 (progressive).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Equal pitches would give a zero resultant pitch.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Equal pitches again.</li><li><b>(d)</b> y<sub>b</sub> &lt; y<sub>f</sub> is the retrogressive winding.</li></ul><p>In a lap winding the number of parallel paths A equals the number of poles P, here 4, so the armature current divides into 4 paths, and 4 brushes are required.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Lap winding: y_b − y_f = ±2, progressive: y_b > y_f.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-122", year: 2018, no: 122, subject: "machines", topic: "Starting torque of an induction motor", selfCorrected: true,
q: `The starting current in an induction motor is 5 times the full-load current while the full load slip is 4%. The ratio of starting torque to full-load torque is`,
opts: ["0·6", "0·8", "1·0", "1·2"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1·0</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> T<sub>st</sub>/T<sub>fl</sub> = (I<sub>st</sub>/I<sub>fl</sub>)² × s<sub>fl</sub> = 5² × 0.04 = 1.0.</p><div class="formula">T_st/T_fl = (I_st/I_fl)² s_fl</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>(I_st/I_fl)² = 25.</li><li>T_st/T_fl = 25 × 0.04 = <b>1.0</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.6 would need s = 0.024.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.8 would need s = 0.032.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1.2 would need s = 0.048.</li></ul><p>This relation neglects the stator impedance and assumes that the rotor resistance and reactance do not change with frequency. With a star–delta starter the starting current is reduced to one-third and the starting torque to one-third. Direct on-line starting thus produces a starting torque equal to the full-load torque with five times the full-load current, an acceptable result for small motors but not for large ones, for which a reduced voltage starter is used.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> T_st/T_fl = (I_st/I_fl)² s_fl.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-123", year: 2018, no: 123, subject: "power", topic: "Bundled conductors", selfCorrected: true,
q: `When bundle of conductors are used in place of single conductors the effective inductance and capacitance will, respectively`,
opts: ["Increase and decrease", "Decrease and increase", "Decrease and remain unaffected", "Increase and remain unaffected"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) decrease and increase</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A bundle has a larger geometric mean radius than a single conductor of the same total cross-section, so L = 2 × 10⁻⁷ ln(GMD/GMR) falls. The effective radius for the capacitance is also larger, so C = 2πε/ln(GMD/r) rises. The surface gradient is reduced, which lowers corona.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The inductance falls and the capacitance rises, never the opposite.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The capacitance is increased, not unaffected.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The inductance decreases.</li></ul><p>Bundling also reduces the corona loss and radio interference by reducing the maximum surface gradient of the conductors, and it increases the current carrying capacity; two-, three- or four-conductor bundles are used at 400, 765 and 1200 kV.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Bundling: lower L, higher C, lower corona.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-124", year: 2018, no: 124, subject: "pe", topic: "Duty cycle of a buck regulator",
q: `A buck regulator has an input voltage of 12 V and the required output voltage is 5 V. What is the duty cycle of the regulator?`,
opts: ["5/12", "12/5", "5/2", "6"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 5/12</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a buck converter V<sub>o</sub> = DV<sub>in</sub>, so D = V<sub>o</sub>/V<sub>in</sub> = 5/12 = 0.417.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 12/5 is greater than 1; a duty cycle cannot exceed 1.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 5/2 is greater than 1.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 6 is greater than 1.</li></ul><p>The buck converter output is always lower than the input, since the duty cycle cannot exceed 1. The ratio of output to input voltage equals the duty cycle in continuous conduction mode, and does not depend on the load. In a boost converter the relation is V<sub>o</sub> = V<sub>in</sub>/(1 − D), and in a buck-boost converter V<sub>o</sub> = −V<sub>in</sub>D/(1 − D), so the type of converter must be identified before the duty cycle is found.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Buck: V_o = D V_in.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-125", year: 2018, no: 125, subject: "basic", topic: "Electrolytic capacitor",
q: `Statement (I): An electrolytic capacitor consists of two electrodes immersed in an electrolyte with a chemical film on one of the electrodes acting as the dielectric. Statement (II): The electrolytic capacitor may be operated with any one of the electrodes as anode positive with respect to the other.`,
opts: ["Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true and Statement (II) is the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are individually true but Statement (II) is not the correct explanation of Statement (I)", "Statement (I) is true, but Statement (II) is false", "Statement (I) is false, but Statement (II) is true"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) I true, II false</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The oxide film is formed on the anode by the electrolytic action.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>I</b> True.</li><li><b>II</b> False: the dielectric film exists on one electrode only, so the capacitor is polarised: that electrode must be kept positive. Reversed voltage destroys the film and the capacitor.</li></ul><p>So (c).</p><p>Electrolytic capacitors reach very large capacitances (up to farads) because the oxide film is only a few nanometres thick, but they have a high leakage current and a limited life, and are used in power supply filters.</p><p><b>Verdict on each option:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> II is false, so (a) is not right.</li><li><b>(b)</b> II is false, so (b) is not right.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> I is true and II is false.</li><li><b>(d)</b> I is true, so (d) is not right.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Electrolytic capacitor: polarised.</p>` }
);

