window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2017-26", year: 2017, no: 26, subject: "circuits", topic: "First Cauer form",
q: `In the first Cauer network, with a pole at infinity, the first element must be`,
opts: ["Series capacitor", "Series inductor", "Shunt capacitor", "Shunt inductor"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) series inductor</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A driving-point impedance Z(s) with a pole at s = ∞ behaves like sL at high frequency; the first Cauer form removes this pole by taking out a <b>series inductor</b> first. The remainder, an admittance with a pole at infinity, is realised by a shunt capacitor, and the process is repeated to give a ladder of series inductors and shunt capacitors (an LC ladder).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A series capacitor corresponds to a pole at s = 0 (the second Cauer form).</li><li><b>(c)</b> A shunt capacitor removes a pole at infinity of the admittance, which is the second step of the ladder, not the first.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A shunt inductor removes a pole at the origin of the admittance (second Cauer form).</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> First Cauer: pole at ∞ of Z → series L; then shunt C.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-27", year: 2017, no: 27, subject: "materials", topic: "Total magnetic moment",
q: `The total magnetic moment: 1. is called saturation magnetization. 2. depends on the number of magnetic dipoles per unit volume, the instant electric current and the area of the current loop. Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) both 1 and 2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The magnetic moment per unit volume, with all dipoles aligned, is the saturation magnetisation M<sub>s</sub>.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: when all the atomic dipoles are aligned the total moment per unit volume reaches the saturation magnetisation.</li><li><b>2</b> True: each dipole has a moment m = I·A (current times loop area), and the total moment per unit volume is the number of dipoles per unit volume times m.</li></ul><p>Both statements are accepted, so (c).</p><p>For ferromagnetic materials the saturation magnetisation M<sub>s</sub> of iron is about 1.7 × 10⁶ A/m. It falls with temperature and vanishes at the Curie point, which is 768 °C for iron, 358 °C for nickel and 1115 °C for cobalt.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> m = IA per loop; M = N m per unit volume.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-28", year: 2017, no: 28, subject: "maths", topic: "Dot product of vectors",
q: `Which of the following statements are correct regarding dot product of vectors? 1. Dot product is less than or equal to the product of magnitudes of two vectors. 2. When two vectors are perpendicular to each other, then their dot product is non-zero. 3. Dot product of two vectors is positive or negative depending whether the angle between the vectors is less than or greater than π/2. 4. Dot product is equal to the product of one vector and the projection of the vector on the first one.`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A·B = |A||B|cos θ.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: since cos θ ≤ 1.</li><li><b>2</b> False: for perpendicular vectors cos 90° = 0, so the dot product is zero.</li><li><b>3</b> True: cos θ is positive for θ &lt; π/2 and negative for θ &gt; π/2.</li><li><b>4</b> True: A·B = |A| times the projection of B on A.</li></ul><p>The dot product is commutative, distributive over addition, and is used to find the work done by a force (F·d) and the component of a vector along a given direction. The cross product, in contrast, is perpendicular to both vectors.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> A·B = 0 for perpendicular vectors.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-29", year: 2017, no: 29, subject: "materials", topic: "Susceptibility of a diamagnetic material",
q: `Susceptibility of a diamagnetic material is: 1. Negative 2. Positive 3. Dependent on the temperature 4. Independent of the temperature`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only", "2 and 4 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Diamagnetism arises from the induced orbital moments opposing the applied field (Lenz's law).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: χ is small and negative (about −10⁻⁵).</li><li><b>2</b> False: positive susceptibility is paramagnetic or ferromagnetic.</li><li><b>3</b> False: the induced moment does not depend on thermal agitation.</li><li><b>4</b> True: χ of a diamagnetic material is independent of temperature (unlike paramagnets, which follow the Curie law).</li></ul><p>Typical values of susceptibility: bismuth −1.7 × 10⁻⁴, copper −10⁻⁵, aluminium +2 × 10⁻⁵, while iron has χ of several thousand. Superconductors are perfect diamagnets with χ = −1.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Diamagnetic: χ < 0, independent of T.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-30", year: 2017, no: 30, subject: "materials", topic: "Susceptibility statements",
q: `Consider the following statements: 1. The susceptibility χ of diamagnetic materials is small and negative. 2. The susceptibility of para and anti ferromagnetic materials is small but positive. 3. The susceptibility has a finite value for free space or air. Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Susceptibility χ<sub>m</sub> = μ<sub>r</sub> − 1.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True.</li><li><b>2</b> True: paramagnetic and antiferromagnetic materials have small positive χ.</li><li><b>3</b> False: for free space μ<sub>r</sub> = 1, so χ<sub>m</sub> = 0 (air is practically the same).</li></ul><p>The relative permeability μ<sub>r</sub> = 1 + χ<sub>m</sub> is almost exactly 1 for non-ferromagnetic materials, so a coil with an air or aluminium core has nearly the same inductance as one in free space. Free space has μ<sub>r</sub> = 1 exactly, so a vacuum is neither para- nor diamagnetic; the small values for air come from its oxygen content, which is slightly paramagnetic.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> χ = μr − 1; free space: 0.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-31", year: 2017, no: 31, subject: "materials", topic: "Reducing eddy-current loss",
q: `Eddy current losses in transformer cores can be reduced by the use of 1. Solid cores 2. Laminated cores 3. Ferrites. Select the correct answer using the codes given below.`,
opts: ["2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Eddy-current loss ∝ f²B²t²/ρ, where t is the thickness of the conducting path and ρ the resistivity.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> Solid cores give the largest eddy loop area and the highest loss.</li><li><b>2</b> Laminated cores cut the loop area, reducing the loss.</li><li><b>3</b> Ferrites have a very high resistivity, so eddy current is negligible (used at high frequency).</li></ul><p>At power frequency, silicon steel laminations about 0.3 to 0.5 mm thick are used; at radio frequencies, powdered iron or ferrite cores are used because the laminations would still carry large eddy currents.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Reduce eddy loss: thin laminations, higher resistivity, ferrites.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-32", year: 2017, no: 32, subject: "materials", topic: "Magnetostriction",
q: `The phenomenon of magnetostriction occurs when a ferromagnetic substance is magnetized resulting in`,
opts: ["Heating", "Small changes in its dimensions", "Small changes in its crystal structure", "Some change in its mechanical properties"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) small changes in its dimensions</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Magnetostriction is the change in length (and volume) of a ferromagnetic material when it is magnetised, because the domains rotate and the lattice strains. It is responsible for the humming of transformers and is used in ultrasonic transducers.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Heating is hysteresis loss, a different effect.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The crystal structure type does not change; only the lattice spacing changes slightly.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Mechanical properties such as strength do not change with magnetisation.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Magnetostriction: dimensional change on magnetising.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-33", year: 2017, no: 33, subject: "materials", topic: "F-centres",
q: `What type of defect causes F-centers in a crystal?`,
opts: ["Stoichiometric defect", "Metal excess defect due to anion vacancies", "Metal excess defect due to extra cations", "Frenkel defect"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) metal excess defect due to anion vacancies</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> An F-centre (Farbe = colour) is an anion vacancy occupied by an electron that balances the charge. It arises in alkali halides heated in excess metal vapour (metal excess defect), and it absorbs visible light and gives the crystal colour.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Stoichiometric defects (Schottky, Frenkel) keep the ratio of ions unchanged.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A metal excess from extra interstitial cations gives other centres, not the F-centre.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Frenkel is a stoichiometric defect in which an ion moves to an interstitial site.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> F-centre = anion vacancy + trapped electron.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-34", year: 2017, no: 34, subject: "materials", topic: "Superconductors",
q: `Consider the following statements: 1. Superconductors exhibit normal conductivity behaviour above a transition temperature T<sub>c</sub>. 2. Superconductors lose their superconducting nature in an external magnetic field, provided the external magnetic field is above a critical value. 3. High T<sub>c</sub> superconductors have T<sub>c</sub> values in the range 1 to 10 K. Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Superconductivity needs T &lt; T<sub>c</sub> and H &lt; H<sub>c</sub>.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: above T<sub>c</sub> the material is in the normal state.</li><li><b>2</b> True: a field above the critical value destroys superconductivity.</li><li><b>3</b> False: high-T<sub>c</sub> ceramics (e.g. YBCO, 92 K) have T<sub>c</sub> far above 10 K; 1–10 K is the range of conventional metals.</li></ul><p>Type-I superconductors (lead, mercury, tin) have a sharp transition at H<sub>c</sub>, while type-II superconductors (Nb₃Sn, NbTi, YBCO) allow flux penetration between H<sub>c1</sub> and H<sub>c2</sub> and are used for high-field magnets.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> High-T_c: above 30 K (YBCO 92 K).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-35", year: 2017, no: 35, subject: "materials", topic: "Superconductivity",
q: `Superconductivity is a material property associated with`,
opts: ["Changing shape by stretching", "Stretching without breaking", "A loss of thermal resistance", "A loss of electrical resistance"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) a loss of electrical resistance</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A superconductor, cooled below its critical temperature, loses all dc electrical resistance and expels magnetic flux (Meissner effect).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Changing shape by stretching is plasticity or elasticity.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Stretching without breaking is ductility.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Superconductors do not lose thermal resistance; heat conduction is not the property.</li></ul><p>The first superconductor discovered was mercury by Kamerlingh Onnes in 1911, with a transition at 4.2 K. Applications include MRI magnets, maglev trains, SQUID magnetometers and lossless power cables.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Superconductor: R = 0 below T_c.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-36", year: 2017, no: 36, subject: "materials", topic: "Vibration frequency of an atom in a crystal",
q: `An atom in a crystal vibrates at a frequency, determined by 1. Crystal heat current 2. Crystal temperature 3. The stiffness of the bonds with neighbour atoms. Select the correct answer using the codes given below.`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Each atom behaves as a harmonic oscillator bound by its neighbours; its natural frequency is ω = √(k/m), determined by the bond stiffness k and the atomic mass. Temperature changes the amplitude (energy) of the vibration, not its frequency in the harmonic approximation, and heat current has no bearing on it.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> Not a factor: heat current describes transport of energy, not the frequency of an atom.</li><li><b>2</b> Not a factor: temperature fixes the amplitude and the number of phonons excited.</li><li><b>3</b> The factor: bond stiffness (and mass) fixes the frequency.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> ω = √(k/m): stiffer bonds, higher frequency.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-37", year: 2017, no: 37, subject: "materials", topic: "Nanomaterials",
q: `Consider the following statements: 1. Nano means 10⁻⁹ so that nano materials have an order of dimension higher than the size of atom and come in the form of rods, tubes, spheres or even thin sheets/films. 2. Nano materials have enhanced or changed structural property. 3. Nano elements lend themselves to mechanical processing like rolling, twisting, positioning. 4. Nano elements show important electrical, magnetic and optical characteristics that are useful in electrical industry.`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4", "3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Nanomaterials have at least one dimension of 1–100 nm.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: 1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m, larger than an atom (~0.1 nm); forms include rods, tubes, spheres and films.</li><li><b>2</b> True: the large surface-to-volume ratio and quantum effects alter structural properties.</li><li><b>3</b> True: nano elements such as nanotubes and sheets can be rolled, twisted and positioned.</li><li><b>4</b> True: they show distinctive electrical, magnetic and optical behaviour used in electrical industry.</li></ul><p>All four are accepted.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Nano: 1–100 nm, size-dependent properties.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-38", year: 2017, no: 38, subject: "circuits", topic: "Identifying a circuit element from waveforms",
q: `The voltage and current waveforms for an element are shown in the figure (current rises linearly from 0 to 2 A in 2 s and stays at 2 A; voltage is a constant 2 V for 0–2 s). The circuit element and its value are<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2017-38.png" alt="Figure for question 38">`,
opts: ["Capacitor, 2 F", "Inductor, 2 H", "Capacitor, 0·5 F", "Inductor, 0·5 H"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) inductor, 2 H</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The voltage is constant while the current changes linearly, and the voltage disappears when the current becomes steady. That is the behaviour of an inductor: v = L di/dt.</p><div class="formula">v = L di/dt</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>di/dt = 2 A/2 s = 1 A/s during 0–2 s.</li><li>L = v/(di/dt) = 2/1 = 2 H.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> For a capacitor i = C dv/dt: a constant voltage would give zero current.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Same reason; and 0.5 F would not fit the data anyway.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The element is an inductor, but L = 2 H, not 0.5 H (that would need v = 0.5 V).</li></ul><p>After 2 s the current is constant (di/dt = 0) and the voltage is zero, consistent with an inductor.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Inductor: v = L di/dt.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-39", year: 2017, no: 39, subject: "circuits", topic: "Links of a co-tree",
q: `In a connected graph, the total number of branches is b and the total number of nodes is n. Then the number of links L of a co-tree is`,
opts: ["b − n", "b − n − 1", "b + n − 1", "b − n + 1"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) b − n + 1</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A tree of a connected graph with n nodes has n − 1 branches. The links (chords) are the remaining branches: L = b − (n − 1) = b − n + 1. This is also the number of independent loops (fundamental loops).</p><div class="formula">Tree branches = n − 1</div><div class="formula">L = b − (n − 1)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>A tree of n nodes uses n − 1 branches.</li><li>Remaining branches are links: L = b − n + 1.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> b − n omits one: it would be the number of links if the tree had n branches.</li><li><b>(b)</b> b − n − 1 subtracts one too many.</li><li><b>(c)</b> b + n − 1 adds the nodes instead of subtracting them.</li></ul><p>The number of independent KVL equations is equal to the number of links (fundamental loops), b − n + 1, and the number of independent KCL equations is n − 1. Together they give b equations, enough with the b element laws to find 2b unknowns.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Tree: n − 1 branches; links: b − n + 1.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-40", year: 2017, no: 40, subject: "circuits", topic: "Thevenin equivalent",
q: `For the circuit shown, Thevenin's open circuit voltage V<sub>oc</sub> and Thevenin's equivalent resistance R<sub>eq</sub> at terminals A–B are, respectively (50 V source, R₁ = 5 Ω in series, R₂ = 5 Ω shunt, R₃ = 2.5 Ω in series, R₄ = 5 Ω shunt across A–B, with R<sub>L</sub> removed)<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2017-40.png" alt="Figure for question 40">`,
opts: ["6·25 V and 2·5 Ω", "12·5 V and 5 Ω", "6·25 V and 5 Ω", "12·5 V and 2·5 Ω"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 12·5 V and 2·5 Ω</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Reduce stage by stage from the source.</p><div class="formula">V_th = V × R_shunt/(R_series + R_shunt)</div><div class="formula">R_th = R₁ ∥ R₂ (source shorted)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Source with R₁ and R₂: V = 50 × 5/(5 + 5) = 25 V, resistance 5 ∥ 5 = 2.5 Ω.</li><li>Add R₃ in series: 25 V behind 2.5 + 2.5 = 5 Ω.</li><li>R₄ = 5 Ω across A–B: V<sub>oc</sub> = 25 × 5/(5 + 5) = 12.5 V; R<sub>eq</sub> = 5 ∥ 5 = 2.5 Ω.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 6.25 V halves the voltage once too often.</li><li><b>(b)</b> R<sub>eq</sub> = 5 Ω is the value before R₄ is added in parallel.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Both values are wrong.</li></ul><p>The Norton equivalent of this source is I<sub>N</sub> = V<sub>oc</sub>/R<sub>eq</sub> = 12.5/2.5 = 5 A in parallel with 2.5 Ω, and the maximum power delivered to a load R<sub>L</sub> = 2.5 Ω is V<sub>oc</sub>²/(4R<sub>eq</sub>) = 15.6 W.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Repeated half-division; Rth = parallel of the last shunt and the series source resistance.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-41", year: 2017, no: 41, subject: "circuits", topic: "Current in the middle branch", selfCorrected: true,
q: `What is the current through the 8 Ω resistance connected across terminals M and N in the circuit? (8 V source with 12 Ω in the left branch, 2 V source with 8 Ω in the right branch)<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2017-41.png" alt="Figure for question 41">`,
opts: ["0·34 A from M to N", "0·29 A from M to N", "0·29 A from N to M", "0·34 A from N to M"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·34 A from N to M</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the figure both batteries have their positive plates (the long lines) towards the bottom node N, so they raise N above the branches' top ends. Take N as reference and let V be the potential of M (the source terminals at the top are at −8 V and −2 V).</p><div class="formula">KCL at M: Σ (V_M − V_k)/R_k = 0</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Node M: (V + 8)/12 + V/8 + (V + 2)/8 = 0.</li><li>V(1/12 + 1/8 + 1/8) = −(8/12 + 2/8) → 0.3333V = −0.9167 → V = −2.75 V.</li><li>The potential of M is below N, so the current in the 8 Ω resistor flows from N to M: 2.75/8 = 0.34 A.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Right magnitude but the opposite direction (M is at the lower potential).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.29 A comes from a wrong node equation; the solved current is 0.34 A from N to M.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.29 A has the right direction but the wrong magnitude.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Nodal analysis at M; negative V means current flows from N to M.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-42", year: 2017, no: 42, subject: "circuits", topic: "Maximum power transfer",
q: `What is the value of resistance R which will allow maximum power dissipation in the circuit? (8 V source, 20 Ω in series, 10 Ω shunt, 5 Ω in series, then R across a–b)<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2017-42.png" alt="Figure for question 42">`,
opts: ["11·66 Ω", "10·33 Ω", "8·33 Ω", "7·66 Ω"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 11·66 Ω</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Maximum power in R occurs when R equals the Thevenin resistance seen from terminals a–b.</p><div class="formula">R_L = R_th for maximum power</div><div class="formula">R_th = (20 ∥ 10) + 5</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Source shorted: the 20 Ω and 10 Ω are in parallel: 20 × 10/30 = 6.667 Ω.</li><li>R<sub>th</sub> = 6.667 + 5 = 11.67 Ω.</li><li>R = R<sub>th</sub> = 11.66 Ω.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 10.33 Ω would need 20 ∥ 10 = 5.33 Ω.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 8.33 Ω would be 20 ∥ 10 plus 1.67 Ω; the series resistor is 5 Ω.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 7.66 Ω forgets part of the 5 Ω series resistor.</li></ul><p>At maximum power transfer the efficiency of the Thevenin source is only 50 %, since the same power is dissipated in the internal resistance. Maximum power transfer is therefore used in communication circuits, not in power systems.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> R_L = R_th for maximum power.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-43", year: 2017, no: 43, subject: "circuits", topic: "Power factor of an RLC series circuit",
q: `Two resistors of 5 Ω and 10 Ω and an inductor L are connected in series across a 50 cos ωt voltage source. If the power consumed by the 5 Ω resistor is 10 W, the power factor of the circuit is`,
opts: ["1·0", "0·8", "0·6", "0·4"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·6</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> P = I²R for the 5 Ω resistor: I² = 10/5 = 2, so I<sub>rms</sub> = √2 A.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Supply rms voltage = 50/√2 = 35.36 V.</li><li>|Z| = V/I = 35.36/1.414 = 25 Ω.</li><li>R = 5 + 10 = 15 Ω; power factor = R/|Z| = 15/25 = 0.6.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Unity would need |Z| = 15 Ω (no inductor).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.8 would need |Z| = 18.75 Ω.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.4 would need |Z| = 37.5 Ω.</li></ul><p>Power in the whole circuit is I²R<sub>total</sub> = 2 × 15 = 30 W (real), apparent power S = VI = 35.36 × 1.414 = 50 VA, so the power factor is 30/50 = 0.6, lagging because the load includes an inductor.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> pf = R/|Z|, with I from P = I²R.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-44", year: 2017, no: 44, subject: "circuits", topic: "Parameters of a two-element series circuit",
q: `A two-element series circuit is connected across an AC source given by e = 200√2 sin(314t + 20) V. The current is then found to be i = 10√2 cos(314t − 25) A. The parameters of the circuit are`,
opts: ["R = 20 Ω and C = 160 μF", "R = 14·14 Ω and C = 225 μF", "L = 45 mH and C = 225 μF", "L = 45 mH and C = 160 μF"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) R = 14·14 Ω and C = 225 μF</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Write the current as a sine: 10√2 cos(314t − 25°) = 10√2 sin(314t + 65°). The voltage phase is +20°, the current phase +65°.</p><div class="formula">|Z| = V_m/I_m</div><div class="formula">R = |Z| cos φ, X_C = |Z| sin φ</div><div class="formula">C = 1/(ωX_C)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>The current leads the voltage by 45°, so the circuit is R–C with phase angle 45°.</li><li>|Z| = 200/10 = 20 Ω; R = 20 cos 45° = 14.14 Ω; X<sub>C</sub> = 20 sin 45° = 14.14 Ω.</li><li>C = 1/(ωX<sub>C</sub>) = 1/(314 × 14.14) = 225 μF.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> R = 20 Ω would leave no reactance; the phase angle is 45°.</li><li><b>(c)</b> L would make the current lag the voltage; it leads here.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Same objection: the current leads, so the reactive element is a capacitor.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Current leading voltage by θ: R–C with tan θ = X_C/R.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-45", year: 2017, no: 45, subject: "ad", topic: "Slew rate",
q: `How fast can the output of an OP Amp change by 10 V, if its slew rate is 1 V/μs?`,
opts: ["5 μs", "10 μs", "15 μs", "20 μs"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 10 μs</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Slew rate SR = ΔV/Δt, the maximum rate of change of the output.</p><div class="formula">Δt = ΔV/SR</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Δt = ΔV/SR = 10 V/(1 V/μs) = 10 μs.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 5 μs would need SR = 2 V/μs.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 15 μs would need SR = 0.67 V/μs.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 20 μs would need SR = 0.5 V/μs.</li></ul><p>The slew rate limits the large-signal bandwidth of an op-amp: for a sine wave of amplitude V<sub>p</sub> the maximum undistorted frequency is f = SR/(2πV<sub>p</sub>). The 741 has a slew rate of about 0.5 V/μs. On a 10 V swing a slower amplifier, such as a 741 with SR = 0.5 V/μs, would need 20 μs, which shows why high-speed op-amps are chosen for pulse and video work.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Minimum time = ΔV/SR.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-46", year: 2017, no: 46, subject: "circuits", topic: "Power factor angle of a star load",
q: `A three-phase star-connected load is operating at a power factor angle Φ, with Φ being the angle between`,
opts: ["Line voltage and line current", "Phase voltage and phase current", "Line voltage and phase current", "Phase voltage and line current"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) phase voltage and phase current</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The power factor angle is the angle between the voltage across a load element and the current through it, that is the phase voltage and phase current. In a star connection the line current equals the phase current, so the angle between phase voltage and line current is also Φ; the standard statement is phase voltage and phase current. The line voltage is displaced by 30° from the phase voltage, so the angle between line voltage and line current is Φ ± 30°.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The angle between line voltage and line current is Φ + 30° (or Φ − 30°).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Line voltage and phase current are likewise displaced by 30° extra.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Numerically equal to Φ in a star connection, but the defining pair is phase voltage and phase current.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> cos Φ is defined per phase.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-47", year: 2017, no: 47, subject: "circuits", topic: "Reciprocal two-port",
q: `For a two-port reciprocal network, the three transmission parameters are A = 4, B = 7 and C = 5. What is the value of D?`,
opts: ["9·5", "9·0", "8·5", "8·0"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 9·0</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a reciprocal network the determinant of the transmission matrix is 1: AD − BC = 1.</p><div class="formula">Reciprocal: AD − BC = 1</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>4D − 7 × 5 = 1 → 4D = 36 → D = 9.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> D = 9.5 gives AD − BC = 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> D = 8.5 gives AD − BC = −1.</li><li><b>(d)</b> D = 8 gives AD − BC = −3.</li></ul><p>For a symmetrical reciprocal network we also have A = D. The inverse relation among the parameters, D = (1 + BC)/A, gives D = (1 + 35)/4 = 9, which is the same result. Equivalent statements of reciprocity are h₁₂ = −h₂₁ and y₁₂ = y₂₁, so any one of these relations tests whether a two-port is reciprocal.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Reciprocity: AD − BC = 1.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-48", year: 2017, no: 48, subject: "circuits", topic: "Reciprocity in z-parameters",
q: `Consider the following as representations of reciprocity in terms of z-parameters: 1. z₁₁ = z₁₂ 2. z₁₂ = z₂₂ 3. z₁₂ = z₂₁. Which of the above representations is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A two-port is reciprocal if the open-circuit voltage ratio is unchanged when source and response are interchanged: V₂/I₁ with port 2 open equals V₁/I₂ with port 1 open, i.e. z₁₂ = z₂₁. (The condition z₁₁ = z₂₂ in addition makes the network symmetrical.)</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: z₁₁ = z₁₂ is not a general network condition.</li><li><b>2</b> False: z₁₂ = z₂₂ is not a general network condition.</li><li><b>3</b> True: z₁₂ = z₂₁ (equivalently y₁₂ = y₂₁, AD − BC = 1, h₁₂ = −h₂₁).</li></ul><p>Reciprocity holds for networks made of linear bilateral passive elements (R, L, C and transformers) and does not hold for networks with active devices such as transistors and gyrators.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Reciprocal: z12 = z21.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-49", year: 2017, no: 49, subject: "circuits", topic: "Surface charge density in a dielectric-filled capacitor",
q: `A parallel-plate capacitor is made of two circular plates separated by a distance of 5 mm with dielectric with dielectric constant of 2·2 between them. When the electric field in the dielectric is 3 × 10⁴ V/m, the charge density of the positive plate will be, nearly`,
opts: ["58·5 × 10⁴ C/m²", "29·5 × 10⁴ C/m²", "29·5 × 10⁻⁴ C/m²", "58·5 × 10⁻⁴ C/m²"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 58·5 × 10⁻⁴ C/m² (as printed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The charge density on the plate equals the electric flux density D = ε₀ε<sub>r</sub>E.</p><div class="formula">ρ_s = D = ε₀ε_r E</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>D = 8.854 × 10⁻¹² × 2.2 × 3 × 10⁴ = 5.84 × 10⁻⁷ C/m².</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 58.5 × 10⁴ has the right digits but a huge positive power.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 29.5 × 10⁴ has half the digits and a wrong power.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 29.5 × 10⁻⁴ has the wrong digits (half the value) and a wrong power.</li></ul><p>The correct magnitude is 5.85 × 10⁻⁷ C/m² (58.5 × 10⁻⁸), so none of the options is numerically exact; the printed exponent seems to be a typesetting error, and (d) has the right digits 58.5.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> σ = D = ε₀ε_r E (value 5.85 × 10⁻⁷ C/m²).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-50", year: 2017, no: 50, subject: "circuits", topic: "Voltage divider",
q: `What is the potential drop across the 80 Ω resistor in the figure? (80 Ω and 20 Ω in series across a 25 V battery)<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2017-50.png" alt="Figure for question 50">`,
opts: ["20 V", "15 V", "10 V", "5 V"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 20 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Voltage divider: V<sub>80</sub> = 25 × 80/(80 + 20) = 20 V. The current is 25/100 = 0.25 A, and 0.25 × 80 = 20 V.</p><div class="formula">V_R = V × R/(R + R_other)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I = 25/(80 + 20) = 0.25 A.</li><li>V₈₀ = 0.25 × 80 = <b>20 V</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 15 V would need a 60 Ω resistor.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 10 V would need a 40 Ω resistor.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 5 V is the drop across the 20 Ω resistor.</li></ul><p>The current is 0.25 A and the total power delivered by the battery is 25 × 0.25 = 6.25 W, of which 5 W is dissipated in the 80 Ω resistor and 1.25 W in the 20 Ω resistor, in proportion to the resistances. Symmetric connections such as this one need no further calculation, because the same current flows in both resistors of the series circuit.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Series divider: share of the larger resistor.</p>` }
);

