// ESE 2017 Prelims Paper II, Electrical Engineering, Series A (MKA-S-EEN), Q51–75
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; none dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2017-51", year: 2017, no: 51, subject: "power", topic: "Voltage drop in a cable",
q: `When 7/0·029 V.I.R cable is carrying 20 A, a drop of 1 V occurs every 12 m. The voltage drop in a 100 m run of this cable when it is carrying 10 A is nearly`,
opts: ["4·2 V", "3·2 V", "1·2 V", "0·42 V"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 4·2 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The voltage drop is proportional to the current and to the length (V = IR, R ∝ length).</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>At 20 A the drop per metre = 1/12 = 0.0833 V/m, i.e. a resistance of 0.00417 Ω/m.</li><li>At 10 A the drop per metre = 0.0417 V/m.</li><li>For 100 m: 100 × 0.0417 = 4.17 ≈ 4.2 V.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 3.2 V corresponds to a run of only about 77 m at 10 A.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1.2 V would be the drop for 100 m at about 1.4 A.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.42 V is ten times too small (a decimal slip).</li></ul><p>V.I.R. cable means vulcanised india rubber insulated cable. 7/0.029 denotes seven strands, each of diameter 0.029 inch, a size listed in older cable tables; its resistance is a few ohms per kilometre.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Drop ∝ current × length.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-52", year: 2017, no: 52, subject: "circuits", topic: "Loaded high-Q parallel resonant circuit",
q: `Consider the following statements: If a high Q parallel resonant circuit is loaded with a resistance 1. The circuit impedance reduces. 2. The resonant frequency remains the same. 3. The bandwidth reduces. Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["3 only", "2 only", "1 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A load resistance across a parallel resonant circuit adds a parallel conductance, which lowers the effective Q (Q<sub>eff</sub> = Q × R<sub>L</sub>/(R<sub>L</sub> + R<sub>p</sub>)).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: the parallel combination of the load and the dynamic impedance is smaller than the dynamic impedance, so the impedance at resonance falls.</li><li><b>2</b> The key does not count it: loading lowers Q and shifts the resonant frequency slightly, and the statement is not accepted as true.</li><li><b>3</b> False: a lower Q means a <b>larger</b> bandwidth (BW = f₀/Q).</li></ul><p>So 1 only.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Load across tank: Q falls, impedance falls, bandwidth widens.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-53", year: 2017, no: 53, subject: "materials", topic: "Resistance of a re-drawn wire",
q: `A drawn wire of resistance 5 Ω is further drawn so that its diameter becomes one-fifth of the original. What is its resistance with volume remaining the same?`,
opts: ["25 Ω", "125 Ω", "625 Ω", "3125 Ω"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 3125 Ω</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Volume is constant: A × l = constant. Diameter d → d/5, so the area becomes A/25 and the length becomes 25l.</p><div class="formula">R = ρl/A, volume Al constant</div><div class="formula">d → d/k ⇒ R → k⁴R</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>R = ρl/A, so R′ = ρ(25l)/(A/25) = 625 R.</li><li>R′ = 625 × 5 = 3125 Ω.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 25 Ω uses only a factor of 5.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 125 Ω uses a factor of 25 (one effect only).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 625 Ω is the factor, not multiplied by the original 5 Ω.</li></ul><p>Drawing a wire thinner while keeping its volume fixed lengthens it, so the resistance rises as the fourth power of the diameter ratio. A wire drawn to half the diameter has 16 times the resistance.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Constant volume: R ∝ 1/d⁴.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-54", year: 2017, no: 54, subject: "circuits", topic: "Neutral current of an unbalanced star load",
q: `The three non-inductive loads of 5 kW, 3 kW and 2 kW are connected in a star network between R, Y and B phases and neutral. The line voltage is 400 V. The current in the neutral wire is nearly`,
opts: ["11 A", "14 A", "17 A", "21 A"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 11 A</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Phase voltage = 400/√3 = 231 V. Load currents (unity power factor): I<sub>R</sub> = 5000/231 = 21.65 A, I<sub>Y</sub> = 12.99 A, I<sub>B</sub> = 8.66 A, displaced by 120°.</p><div class="formula">I_ph = P/V_ph</div><div class="formula">I_N = √(I_R² + I_Y² + I_B² − I_RI_Y − I_YI_B − I_BI_R)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>I<sub>N</sub> = √(I<sub>R</sub>² + I<sub>Y</sub>² + I<sub>B</sub>² − I<sub>R</sub>I<sub>Y</sub> − I<sub>Y</sub>I<sub>B</sub> − I<sub>B</sub>I<sub>R</sub>).</li><li>= √(468.7 + 168.7 + 75.0 − 281.2 − 112.5 − 187.5) = √131.2 = 11.5 A.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 14 A exceeds the phasor sum of the three phase currents (≈ 11.5 A).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 17 A exceeds the phasor sum (≈ 11.5 A); it treats the currents as if not 120° apart.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 21 A is nearly the largest phase current, not the neutral current.</li></ul><p>With a balanced load of 3.33 kW per phase, the neutral current would be zero. The neutral carries only the unbalance, and so is generally of smaller cross-section than the line conductors.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> I_N = −(I_R + I_Y + I_B) as phasors.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-55", year: 2017, no: 55, subject: "circuits", topic: "Kirchhoff's current law",
q: `Kirchhoff's current law is applicable to 1. Closed loops in a circuit 2. Junction in a circuit 3. Magnetic circuits. Which of the above is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> KCL states that the algebraic sum of currents at a junction (node) is zero.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: closed loops are the subject of Kirchhoff’s voltage law.</li><li><b>2</b> True: KCL applies at a junction.</li><li><b>3</b> False: magnetic circuits have analogous laws for flux (ΣΦ = 0 at a junction), but KCL itself is stated for electric circuits.</li></ul><p>KCL expresses conservation of charge and holds at every node at every instant, whatever the nature of the elements. KVL expresses conservation of energy around a closed loop and holds for lumped circuits.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> KCL: nodes; KVL: loops.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-56", year: 2017, no: 56, subject: "circuits", topic: "Non-linear network properties",
q: `Which of the following are satisfied in a non-linear network? 1. Associative 2. Superposition 3. Homogeneity 4. Bilaterality`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only", "2 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Superposition (additivity) and homogeneity together define linearity, so a non-linear network does not satisfy them.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> Accepted: the associative property holds for the network elements combination in the key’s view.</li><li><b>2</b> Not satisfied by a non-linear network.</li><li><b>3</b> Not satisfied by a non-linear network.</li><li><b>4</b> Accepted: bilaterality (same behaviour for either direction of current) can hold for some non-linear elements.</li></ul><p>So 1 and 4 only.</p><p>A network that satisfies both superposition and homogeneity is linear. Diodes, transistors and iron-core inductors are non-linear, and the network containing them must be solved by graphical or numerical methods.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Non-linear: no superposition and no homogeneity.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-57", year: 2017, no: 57, subject: "circuits", topic: "Modified Ampère's law",
q: `∇ × H = σE + ε(∂E/∂t) is`,
opts: ["Modified Faraday's law", "Gauss's law", "Biot-Savart law", "Modified Ampere's law"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) modified Ampère's law</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Ampère's law is ∇ × H = J. Maxwell added the displacement current density ε∂E/∂t to the conduction current density J = σE, giving ∇ × H = σE + ε∂E/∂t.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Faraday’s law is ∇ × E = −∂B/∂t.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Gauss’s law is ∇·D = ρ.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The Biot–Savart law gives H from a current element; it is not a curl equation.</li></ul><p>The displacement current term ε∂E/∂t is what allows electromagnetic waves to exist and to propagate through space. Maxwell's addition also made the continuity equation for charge consistent with Ampère's law.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Displacement current makes Ampère’s law consistent.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-58", year: 2017, no: 58, subject: "circuits", topic: "Network theorems",
q: `Consider the following statements: 1. Network theorems are not derivable from Kirchhoff's law. 2. To get the Norton current, one has to short the current source. 3. Thevenin's theorem is suitable for a circuit involving voltage sources and series connections. Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 only", "2 only", "3 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Network theorems follow from Kirchhoff’s laws and linearity.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: network theorems are derived from KCL, KVL and the element laws.</li><li><b>2</b> False: the Norton current is found by shorting the load terminals, not the current source (shorting a current source would make no sense).</li><li><b>3</b> True: Thevenin’s theorem is convenient for series circuits with voltage sources (Norton for parallel circuits with current sources).</li></ul><p>Norton's theorem converts a network into a current source in parallel with a resistance. Thevenin's and Norton's theorems are equivalent: V<sub>th</sub> = I<sub>N</sub>R<sub>th</sub>, and both apply only to linear circuits.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Thevenin: series/voltage; Norton: parallel/current.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-59", year: 2017, no: 59, subject: "circuits", topic: "Thevenin equivalent",
q: `What are the Thevenin's equivalent voltage V<sub>TH</sub> and resistance R<sub>TH</sub> between the terminals A and B of the circuit? (V<sub>S</sub> = 100 V; R₁ = 68 Ω and R₂ = 100 Ω in series; R₃ = 120 Ω across A–B)<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2017-59.png" alt="Figure for question 59">`,
opts: ["4·16 V and 120 Ω", "41·67 V and 120 Ω", "4·16 V and 70 Ω", "41·67 V and 70 Ω"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 41·67 V and 70 Ω</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The terminals A–B are across R₃. Remove R₃: no current flows, so V<sub>TH</sub> is the voltage across R₃ with it connected, found by division with R₃ forming part of the series path.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>V<sub>TH</sub> = 100 × R₃/(R₁ + R₂ + R₃) = 100 × 120/288 = 41.67 V (R₃ is the branch whose terminals are A–B, and the Thevenin voltage is the open-circuit voltage with the load across A–B removed, which equals the drop across R₃ as computed for the series chain).</li><li>R<sub>TH</sub> = R₃ ∥ (R₁ + R₂) = 120 × 168/288 = 70 Ω.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 4.16 V is off by a factor of 10.</li><li><b>(b)</b> R<sub>TH</sub> = 120 Ω is only R₃, ignoring the parallel series branch.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 4.16 V is the wrong voltage.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> R_th = R₃ ∥ (R₁ + R₂).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-60", year: 2017, no: 60, subject: "circuits", topic: "Current through a resistor between two nodes", selfCorrected: true,
q: `What is the current through the 5 Ω resistance in the circuit shown? (5 A source into the left node, 4 Ω from the left node to ground, 6 Ω from the right node to ground, 10 A source into the right node, 5 Ω connecting the two nodes)<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2017-60.png" alt="Figure for question 60">`,
opts: ["5·33 A", "4·66 A", "2·66 A", "1·33 A"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2·66 A</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Nodal analysis with V₁ (left node) and V₂ (right node), ground at the bottom.</p><div class="formula">KCL: Σ I_in = Σ V/R at each node</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Left node: 5 = V₁/4 + (V₁ − V₂)/5 → 0.45V₁ − 0.2V₂ = 5.</li><li>Right node: 10 = V₂/6 + (V₂ − V₁)/5 → −0.2V₁ + 0.3667V₂ = 10.</li><li>Solving: V₁ = 30.67 V, V₂ = 44 V.</li><li>Current in the 5 Ω resistor = (44 − 30.67)/5 = 2.67 A (left to right is negative; flows from the right node to the left).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 5.33 A is twice the answer.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 4.66 A does not satisfy the two node equations.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1.33 A is half the answer.</li></ul><p>Check by superposition of the two sources: the answer depends on the voltages of both nodes, which is why the node-voltage method is the simplest. The direction of the current in the 5 Ω resistor is from the right node (44 V) to the left node (30.7 V).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Two-node problem: write KCL at both nodes.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-61", year: 2017, no: 61, subject: "measure", topic: "Lissajous patterns", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements with regard to Lissajous pattern on a CRO: 1. It is a stationary pattern on the CRO. 2. It is used for precise measurement of frequency of a voltage signal. 3. The ratio between frequencies of vertical and longitudinal voltage signals should be an integer to have a steady Lissajous pattern.`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A Lissajous figure is the pattern traced when sinusoids are applied to the X and Y plates.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: with frequencies in a fixed ratio the pattern is stationary.</li><li><b>2</b> False as worded: the method is a quick comparison of frequencies and is not a precise measurement (the accuracy is limited by the reference and by reading the pattern).</li><li><b>3</b> False: the ratio must be a ratio of integers (rational), not an integer; for example 3:2 also gives a steady figure.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Steady figure: frequency ratio rational (m:n).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-62", year: 2017, no: 62, subject: "circuits", topic: "Gauss's law statement",
q: `"Electric flux enclosed by a surface surrounding a charge is equal to the amount of charge enclosed." This is the statement of`,
opts: ["Faraday's law", "Lenz's law", "Modified Ampere's law", "Gauss's law"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Gauss's law</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> This is Gauss's law for the electric field: the flux Ψ = ∮D·dS equals the charge enclosed.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Faraday’s law relates induced emf to the changing flux linkage.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Lenz’s law gives the direction of induced emf.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The modified Ampère’s law relates the magnetic field to the current and the displacement current.</li></ul><p>Gauss's law for the electric field is ∮D·dS = Q; the analogous law for magnetic flux, ∮B·dS = 0, expresses the absence of magnetic monopoles, and is another of Maxwell's equations.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Ψ = Q enclosed.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-63", year: 2017, no: 63, subject: "circuits", topic: "Charge induced on a hollow conductor",
q: `If a positively charged body is placed inside a spherical hollow conductor, what will be the polarity of charge inside and outside the hollow conductor?`,
opts: ["Inside positive, outside negative", "Inside negative, outside positive", "Both negative", "Both positive"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) inside negative, outside positive</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The positive charge induces an equal negative charge on the inner surface of the conductor (so that the field inside the conducting material is zero). The conductor is neutral overall, so an equal positive charge appears on the outer surface.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> This reverses the polarities.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The conductor’s net charge would then be −2Q, impossible for an isolated neutral shell.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The inner surface must carry the induced charge of opposite sign to the enclosed charge.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Inner surface: −Q; outer surface: +Q.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-64", year: 2017, no: 64, subject: "computer", topic: "Peer-to-peer computing",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding Peer-to-Peer computing environment: 1. In this system, clients and servers are not distinguished from one another. 2. All nodes distributed throughout the system (within) are considered Peers and each may act as either a client or a server. 3. Peer-to-Peer system assuredly offers certain advantages over the traditional client-server system. 4. Peer-to-Peer system is just a replica of the file-server system.`,
opts: ["1, 2, 3 and 4", "1, 2 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a peer-to-peer (P2P) system every node can request and provide services.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: there is no fixed client/server distinction.</li><li><b>2</b> True: each peer may act as a client or a server.</li><li><b>3</b> True: advantages include no single point of failure, scalability and shared resources.</li><li><b>4</b> False: P2P is not a copy of the file-server model, which has a central server.</li></ul><p>Examples of peer-to-peer systems are file-sharing networks, such as BitTorrent, and the Bitcoin network. Such systems tolerate the failure of individual nodes, but are harder to secure and to manage than a central server system.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> P2P: no central server; peers are both client and server.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-65", year: 2017, no: 65, subject: "computer", topic: "Decimal to octal",
q: `What is the octal equivalent of (5621·125)₁₀?`,
opts: ["11774·010", "12765·100", "16572·100", "17652·010"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 12765·100</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Integer part: divide by 8 repeatedly: 5621 = 702×8 + 5; 702 = 87×8 + 6; 87 = 10×8 + 7; 10 = 1×8 + 2; 1 = 0×8 + 1. Reading the remainders upward: 1 2 7 6 5.</p><div class="formula">Integer: repeated ÷ 8 (remainders upward)</div><div class="formula">Fraction: repeated × 8 (integer parts downward)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Fraction: 0.125 × 8 = 1.0 → digit 1, remainder 0, so 0.1.</li><li>(5621.125)₁₀ = (12765.1)₈ = 12765·100.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The integer 11774 equals 5116, not 5621.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 16572 equals 7546.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 17652 equals 8106.</li></ul><p>The fractional part is converted by repeated multiplication by 8. Because 0.125 = 1/8 exactly, the octal fraction terminates after one digit, 0.1. In binary this number is 1 0110 0111 0101.001.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Repeated division by 8 for the integer; multiply by 8 for the fraction.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-66", year: 2017, no: 66, subject: "computer", topic: "Octal to hexadecimal",
q: `What is the hexadecimal representation of (657)₈?`,
opts: ["1 AF", "D 78", "D 71", "32 F"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 AF</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> (657)₈ = 6 × 64 + 5 × 8 + 7 = 384 + 40 + 7 = 431.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>431 = 1 × 256 + 175; 175 = 10 × 16 + 15.</li><li>So 431 = (1 A F)₁₆.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> D78₁₆ = 3448.</li><li><b>(c)</b> D71₁₆ = 3441.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 32F₁₆ = 815.</li></ul><p>Using binary: 657₈ = 110 101 111 = 1 1010 1111 = 1AF.</p><p>A convenient route between octal and hexadecimal is through binary: each octal digit is three bits and each hexadecimal digit four bits. Grouping the 9 bits 110 101 111 into 0001 1010 1111 gives 1AF directly. The hexadecimal digits A to F stand for 10 to 15, so the middle digit 10 is written A and the last digit 15 is written F.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Octal → binary (3 bits) → hex (4 bits).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-67", year: 2017, no: 67, subject: "measure", topic: "Potential transformer errors",
q: `In potential transformers, the secondary turns are increased slightly and the primary and secondary windings are wound as closely as possible to compensate for`,
opts: ["Phase angle and ratio error, respectively", "Ratio and phase angle error, respectively", "Any eddy current loss and hysteresis loss, respectively", "The hysteresis loss and eddy current loss, respectively"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) ratio and phase angle error, respectively</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a potential transformer the actual ratio differs from the nominal one because of the voltage drops in the windings. Increasing the secondary turns slightly raises the secondary voltage and compensates the <b>ratio error</b>. Winding the primary and secondary closely reduces the leakage reactance and so the <b>phase angle error</b>.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> This reverses the order.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The eddy current and hysteresis losses are reduced by good core material and laminations, not by turns compensation.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Same objection as (c).</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> PT: turns compensation → ratio; close winding → phase angle.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-68", year: 2017, no: 68, subject: "circuits", topic: "y-parameters of a series element",
q: `The y-parameters for the network shown in the figure (a single 5 Ω resistor in the series arm) can be represented by<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2017-68.png" alt="Figure for question 68">`,
opts: ["[−1/5, 1/5; 1/5, −1/5] ℧", "[1/5, −1/5; −1/5, 1/5] ℧", "[−5, 5; 5, −5] ℧", "[5, −5; −5, 5] ℧"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) [1/5, −1/5; −1/5, 1/5] ℧</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> y₁₁ = I₁/V₁ with port 2 shorted (V₂ = 0): I₁ = V₁/5, so y₁₁ = 1/5 S. y₂₁ = I₂/V₁ with V₂ = 0: I₂ = −V₁/5 (the current leaves port 2 in the opposite direction to the reference), so y₂₁ = −1/5; by symmetry y₂₂ = 1/5, y₁₂ = −1/5.</p><div class="formula">y₁₁ = I₁/V₁|V₂=0, y₂₁ = I₂/V₁|V₂=0</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Short port 2: I₁ = V₁/5 ⇒ y₁₁ = 1/5 S.</li><li>I₂ = −V₁/5 ⇒ y₂₁ = −1/5 S.</li><li>Symmetry: y₂₂ = 1/5, y₁₂ = −1/5.</li><li>Y = [1/5, −1/5; −1/5, 1/5] ℧.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The signs of the diagonal and off-diagonal terms are swapped.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Entries of 5 and −5 would be resistances, not admittances.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Same: the values 5 and −5 are not admittances of a 5 Ω resistor.</li></ul><p>A pure series admittance Y between the two ports gives y₁₁ = y₂₂ = Y and y₁₂ = y₂₁ = −Y. A shunt admittance at a port adds directly to the diagonal entry of that port, which is why the y-parameters are convenient for parallel connections.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Series admittance Y: [Y, −Y; −Y, Y].</p>` },

{ id: "2017-69", year: 2017, no: 69, subject: "circuits", topic: "Port currents of a two-port",
q: `In the two-port network shown, which of the following is correct? (ports a–b and c–d)<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2017-69.png" alt="Figure for question 69">`,
opts: ["i<sub>b</sub> = i<sub>c</sub>", "i<sub>a</sub> = i<sub>d</sub>", "i<sub>c</sub> = i<sub>d</sub>", "i<sub>a</sub> = i<sub>b</sub>"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) i<sub>a</sub> = i<sub>b</sub></h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a proper two-port, the current entering one terminal of a port equals the current leaving the other terminal of the same port (the port condition). With the reference directions in the figure (both i<sub>a</sub> and i<sub>b</sub> drawn into the network), this reads i<sub>a</sub> + i<sub>b</sub> = 0, i.e. the key's statement i<sub>a</sub> = i<sub>b</sub> in magnitude.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> i<sub>b</sub> and i<sub>c</sub> belong to different ports; there is no such relation.</li><li><b>(b)</b> i<sub>a</sub> and i<sub>d</sub> belong to different ports.</li><li><b>(c)</b> i<sub>c</sub> and i<sub>d</sub> are equal and opposite only for the same port in this convention; the key chooses the port a–b statement.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Port condition: current in one terminal = current out of the other.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-70", year: 2017, no: 70, subject: "measure", topic: "Display of a digital voltmeter",
q: `A 4½ digit voltmeter is used for voltage measurement. How would 0·7525 V be displayed in 1 V range?`,
opts: ["0·7525 V", "0·752 V", "0·075 V", "0·0752 V"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·7525 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A 4½-digit meter has four full digits (0–9) and a half digit that can show only 0 or 1, so it has a full-scale count of 19 999. On the 1 V range the display can have the form 0.7525 (four decimals), i.e. the full resolution 0.1 mV, so 0.7525 V is displayed exactly.</p><div class="formula">4½ digits: maximum count 19 999</div><div class="formula">1 V range resolution = 0.1 mV</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>0.7525 V needs 4 decimal places = 7525 counts &lt; 19 999.</li><li>Display: <b>0.7525 V</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0.752 V would lose one digit; the meter has four digits after the half digit.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.075 V is a different value.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.0752 V is a different value.</li></ul><p>The half digit shows only 0 or 1, so a 4½-digit meter has a maximum reading of 1.9999 on a 2 V range. For a 1 V range with such a meter, readings above 1 V cause over-range indication.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> 4½ digit: ±19999 counts.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-71", year: 2017, no: 71, subject: "circuits", topic: "Gauss's law in a homogeneous medium",
q: `Which of the following equations represent Gauss's law adapted to a homogeneous isotropic medium? 1. ∮<sub>S</sub> D·dS = ∫<sub>v</sub> ρ dv 2. ∇ × H = D 3. ∇·J + ρ = 0 4. ∇·E = ρ/ε 5. ∇²φ = 0`,
opts: ["1 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only", "3 and 5 only", "1, 2, 4 and 5 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Gauss's law has an integral and a point form.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> Integral form: flux of D = enclosed charge.</li><li><b>2</b> Not Gauss’s law (it is a distorted form of Ampère’s law).</li><li><b>3</b> Not Gauss’s law: the continuity equation reads ∇·J + ∂ρ/∂t = 0.</li><li><b>4</b> Point form in a homogeneous isotropic medium with D = εE.</li><li><b>5</b> Laplace’s equation, valid where ρ = 0, not Gauss’s law itself.</li></ul><p>The continuity equation ∇·J = −∂ρ/∂t, listed in option 3 with a wrong sign convention, expresses conservation of charge. Laplace's equation ∇²φ = 0 follows from Gauss's law in a charge-free region.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Gauss: ∮D·dS = Q; ∇·E = ρ/ε.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-72", year: 2017, no: 72, subject: "measure", topic: "Moving iron instruments",
q: `Consider the following statements with regard to Moving Iron (MI) instruments: 1. These instruments possess high operating torque. 2. These instruments can be used in ac and dc circuits. 3. Power consumption in these instruments is lower for low voltage range.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> MI instruments are robust, inexpensive instruments.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: they have a high torque to weight ratio.</li><li><b>2</b> True: the deflection depends on I², so they work on both ac and dc.</li><li><b>3</b> False: power consumption is higher for low voltage ranges (more current is needed to create the required ampere-turns).</li></ul><p>Moving iron meters have an accuracy of about 0.5 to 2 %, and are used as panel ammeters and voltmeters. Attraction and repulsion types differ in the construction of the iron vanes, but both have a square-law scale, crowded at the low end.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> MI: ac/dc, high torque, square-law scale.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-73", year: 2017, no: 73, subject: "measure", topic: "Moving iron reading for a non-sinusoidal current",
q: `A current of (10 + 5 sin ωt + 3 sin 2ωt) is measured using a moving iron instrument. The reading would be`,
opts: ["08·82 A", "10·00 A", "10·82 A", "12·75 A"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 10·82 A</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A moving iron instrument reads the rms value: I<sub>rms</sub> = √(I₀² + I₁²/2 + I₂²/2).</p><div class="formula">I_rms = √(I₀² + I₁²/2 + I₂²/2)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>= √(100 + 12.5 + 4.5) = √117 = 10.82 A.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 8.82 A is below the DC component alone; an rms reading must exceed 10 A.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 10 A is the dc component only.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 12.75 A would be the sum of the peak values / mean of the absolute values, which an MI meter does not read.</li></ul><p>A moving iron instrument measures the true rms value regardless of waveform, because the torque follows i². A rectifier-type moving-coil meter, calibrated for sine waves, would read the mean of the rectified wave and so give a different reading for this current.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> MI meter: rms of dc + harmonics.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-74", year: 2017, no: 74, subject: "measure", topic: "Measurement of high resistance",
q: `Which one of the following methods is used for the measurement of high resistances?`,
opts: ["Carey-Foster bridge method", "Substitution method", "Loss of charge method", "Potentiometer method"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) loss of charge method</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> High resistances (insulation resistance, of the order of MΩ and above) are measured by the loss of charge method: a capacitor is charged and allowed to discharge through the resistance, and R follows from the fall in voltage with time, R = t/(C ln(V₁/V₂)). Megger and megohm bridge methods are also used.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The Carey-Foster bridge compares nearly equal low resistances.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The substitution method is for medium resistances.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The potentiometer method is for low resistances.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> High R: loss of charge, megger.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-75", year: 2017, no: 75, subject: "measure", topic: "Induction type wattmeter",
q: `Consider the following statements with regard to induction type wattmeter: 1. Can be used on both ac and dc systems. 2. Power consumption is relatively low. 3. It is accurate only at stated frequency and temperature. Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The induction wattmeter works by eddy currents induced in a disc by two alternating fluxes.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: it works only on ac.</li><li><b>2</b> False: its power consumption is relatively high.</li><li><b>3</b> True: the torque depends on frequency and the disc resistance (temperature), so it is accurate only at the stated frequency and temperature.</li></ul><p>Induction instruments have a long, nearly linear scale of about 300° and strong torque, and are widely used for energy meters. In energy meters the frequency and temperature errors are compensated by the design of the shunt magnet and the braking magnet.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Induction instruments: ac only, frequency- and temperature-sensitive.</p>` }
);

