// ESE 2017 Prelims Paper II, Electrical Engineering, Series A (MKA-S-EEN), Q76–100
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; none dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2017-76", year: 2017, no: 76, subject: "computer", topic: "Cache access time",
q: `A computer system has a cache with a cache access time T<sub>C</sub> = 10 ns, a hit ratio of 80% and an average memory access time T<sub>M</sub> = 20 ns. What is the access time for physical memory T<sub>P</sub>?`,
opts: ["90 ns", "80 ns", "60 ns", "20 ns"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 60 ns</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula:</b> T<sub>M</sub> = h·T<sub>C</sub> + (1 − h)·T<sub>P</sub>.</p><div class="formula">T_M = h T_C + (1 − h) T_P</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>20 = 0.8 × 10 + 0.2 × T<sub>P</sub> → 0.2T<sub>P</sub> = 12.</li><li>T<sub>P</sub> = 60 ns.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 90 ns would give an average of 26 ns.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 80 ns would give an average of 24 ns.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 20 ns is the average, not the main memory time.</li></ul><p>The relation is the weighted average of the two access times, with the hit ratio h as the weight. At h = 100 % the average access time equals that of the cache, and at h = 0 % it equals that of main memory. A hit ratio of 80 % is typical of a small cache; real processors reach 95 % or more with multi-level caches, which brings the average access time close to the cache time.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Average access time = h·Tc + (1 − h)·Tp.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-77", year: 2017, no: 77, subject: "computer", topic: "C expression with side effects",
q: `If n has the value 3, then the C language statement a[++n] = n++; assigns`,
opts: ["3 to a[5]", "4 to a[5]", "4 to a[4]", "5 to a[5]"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 3 to a[5] (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The statement modifies n twice between sequence points, so the C standard leaves the order of evaluation undefined. With a typical compiler that evaluates the right-hand side first, n++ yields 3 (n becomes 4), then ++n makes n = 5 and the index is 5: a[5] = 3. This is the reading the key adopts.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Assigning 4 would need the left side to be evaluated first, with n++ then giving 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> a[4] needs the index to be evaluated before the right-hand increment.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 5 would need the value of the post-increment to be the final n, which it never is.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Undefined behaviour in C: avoid modifying a variable twice in one expression.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-78", year: 2017, no: 78, subject: "computer", topic: "Minimum arithmetic operations for a polynomial", selfCorrected: true,
q: `The minimum number of arithmetic operations required to evaluate the polynomial P(X) = X⁵ + 8X³ + X for a given value of X using only one temporary variable is`,
opts: ["8", "7", "6", "5"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 6</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Factorise to reduce the work: P(X) = X(X⁴ + 8X² + 1) = X[X²(X² + 8) + 1].</p><div class="formula">P(X) = X[X²(X² + 8) + 1]</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>T = X × X (one multiplication).</li><li>Using T and X (and one more step to keep X² while forming X² + 8), the evaluation of X²(X² + 8) + 1 and the final multiplication by X together need five further operations in the counting adopted by the key.</li><li>Total: 6 operations.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 8 is the cost of the naive method: four multiplications for X⁵, two for 8X³ and two additions.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 7 operations is more than the key allows.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 5 operations is possible only if a second temporary variable is permitted, which the question does not allow.</li></ul><p>The count depends on how a temporary variable and the original X are allowed to be reused; the key's value is 6.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Factor P as X(X²(X² + 8) + 1) to save multiplications.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-79", year: 2017, no: 79, subject: "pe", topic: "Freewheeling diode",
q: `A freewheeling diode in phase-controlled rectifiers`,
opts: ["enables inverter operation", "is responsible for additional reactive power", "improves the line power factor", "is responsible for additional harmonics"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) improves the line power factor</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A freewheeling diode across an inductive load carries the load current when the output voltage would go negative. It prevents the negative part of the output voltage, so the supply current stops earlier in each cycle: the displacement between supply voltage and current falls, and the input power factor improves. It also prevents the load voltage from going negative, so inverter operation is not possible.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The freewheeling diode prevents the voltage reversal needed for inversion.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Reactive power is reduced, not increased.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The load current ripple and the supply current harmonics are generally reduced, not increased.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Freewheeling: no negative output voltage, better pf, no inversion.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-80", year: 2017, no: 80, subject: "materials", topic: "Electrical conductivity statements",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding electrical conductivity σ: 1. It increases with temperature in semiconductors. 2. Its increase with temperature is exponential. 3. It increases in metals and their alloys, linearly with temperature.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In semiconductors carriers are thermally generated, n<sub>i</sub> ∝ exp(−E<sub>g</sub>/2kT), so conductivity rises exponentially with temperature.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True.</li><li><b>2</b> True: the rise is exponential.</li><li><b>3</b> False: in metals the resistivity increases linearly with temperature, so the <b>conductivity decreases</b>.</li></ul><p>The conductivity of a semiconductor is σ = e(nμ<sub>n</sub> + pμ<sub>p</sub>), with n and p rising as exp(−E<sub>g</sub>/2kT), so a 10 °C rise can double the intrinsic carrier concentration, while the fall of mobility with temperature is a much weaker effect.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Metals: conductivity falls with T; semiconductors: rises exponentially.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-81", year: 2017, no: 81, subject: "control", topic: "Derivative compensation",
q: `What is the effect on the natural frequency (ω<sub>n</sub>) and damping factor (δ) in the control systems when derivative compensation is used?`,
opts: ["ω<sub>n</sub> increases and δ decreases", "ω<sub>n</sub> remains unchanged and δ increases", "ω<sub>n</sub> remains unchanged and δ decreases", "ω<sub>n</sub> decreases and δ increases"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) ω<sub>n</sub> remains unchanged and δ increases</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a second-order system with derivative (rate) feedback or PD control, the characteristic equation becomes s² + (2ζω<sub>n</sub> + K<sub>d</sub>)s + ω<sub>n</sub>² = 0. The constant term, hence ω<sub>n</sub>, is unchanged, while the coefficient of s increases, so the damping factor ζ increases.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> ω<sub>n</sub> does not change, and the damping rises.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Damping rises, not falls.</li><li><b>(d)</b> ω<sub>n</sub> is unchanged.</li></ul><p>Derivative compensation is equivalent to adding a zero at s = −K<sub>p</sub>/K<sub>d</sub> to the open-loop transfer function. It reduces the overshoot and speeds up the response, but increases the sensitivity to high-frequency noise.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Derivative action adds damping and leaves ω_n alone.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-82", year: 2017, no: 82, subject: "basic", topic: "High-frequency response of an R-C coupled amplifier",
q: `Consider the following components in a multi-stage R-C coupled amplifier: 1. Parasitic capacitance of transistor 2. Coupling capacitance 3. Stray capacitance 4. Wiring capacitance. Which of the above components effectively control high frequencies?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1, 2 and 4", "1, 3 and 4", "2, 3 and 4"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1, 3 and 4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At high frequencies the gain of an RC-coupled amplifier falls because of the shunt capacitances at the output of each stage: the transistor’s parasitic (junction) capacitance, the stray capacitance and the wiring capacitance. The coupling capacitor is large and acts as a short circuit at mid and high frequencies; it controls the <b>low</b> frequency response.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> Controls high frequencies.</li><li><b>2</b> Controls low frequencies only.</li><li><b>3</b> Controls high frequencies.</li><li><b>4</b> Controls high frequencies.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Coupling capacitor → low-f cut-off; shunt capacitances → high-f cut-off.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-83", year: 2017, no: 83, subject: "basic", topic: "Wien bridge oscillator",
q: `A Wien Bridge Oscillator is suitable for 1. Audio frequency applications 2. Radio frequency applications 3. Very low frequency applications. Which of the above frequency applications is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Wien bridge oscillator, with f = 1/(2πRC), is a stable RC oscillator for the audio range (tens of Hz to a few hundred kHz). At radio frequencies the required R and C become impractically small and op-amp bandwidth limits it, so LC oscillators are used. At very low frequencies the capacitors become very large.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Radio frequency oscillators use LC tuned circuits or crystals.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Very low frequency would need very large capacitors.</li><li><b>(d)</b> It is suitable for audio only.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> RC oscillators: audio; LC oscillators: radio.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-84", year: 2017, no: 84, subject: "basic", topic: "RC phase-shift oscillator",
q: `In an R-C phase shift oscillator using FET and 3-section R-C phase shift network, the condition for sustained oscillation is (n = R<sub>d</sub>/R)`,
opts: ["β &gt; 6n", "β &gt; 29", "β &gt; 4n + 23 + 29/n", "β &gt; 23 + 29/n"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) β &gt; 29</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a three-section RC phase-shift network the attenuation at the oscillation frequency is 1/29, so the amplifier gain must be at least 29. For a BJT-type amplifier the condition is h<sub>fe</sub> &gt; 4K + 23 + 29/K, while for a FET amplifier the gain g<sub>m</sub>R<sub>d</sub> must exceed 29. The key gives β &gt; 29 for the FET version.</p><div class="formula">RC network attenuation β_net = 1/29</div><div class="formula">Loop gain ≥ 1 ⇒ |A| ≥ 29</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Three-section RC network gives 180° shift with attenuation 1/29.</li><li>Barkhausen: A × 1/29 ≥ 1 ⇒ <b>β &gt; 29</b> (FET version).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> β &gt; 6n is not a relation for this circuit.</li><li><b>(c)</b> This is the transistor (BJT) expression with the term 4K + 23 + 29/K.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Also a BJT-type expression, missing the 4K term.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> 3-section RC network: attenuation 29, so |A| > 29.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-85", year: 2017, no: 85, subject: "basic", topic: "Tuned collector oscillator",
q: `A tuned-collector oscillator has a fixed inductance of 100 μH and has to be tunable over the frequency band of 500 kHz to 1500 kHz. What is the range of variable capacitor to be used?`,
opts: ["115 – 1021 pF", "113 – 1015 pF", "93 – 1015 μF", "110 – 1021 μF"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 113 – 1015 pF</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula:</b> f = 1/(2π√LC), so C = 1/(4π²f²L).</p><div class="formula">C = 1/(4π²f²L)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>At 500 kHz: C = 1/(4π² × 2.5 × 10¹¹ × 10⁻⁴) = 1.013 × 10⁻⁹ F = 1013 ≈ 1015 pF.</li><li>At 1500 kHz: C = 1/(4π² × 2.25 × 10¹² × 10⁻⁴) = 1.126 × 10⁻¹⁰ F = 113 pF.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 115 – 1021 pF is slightly off at both ends.</li><li><b>(c)</b> μF is wrong by 10⁶, and 93 pF would tune to about 1.65 MHz, beyond the band.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Same unit error.</li></ul><p>A tuned-collector oscillator is a variant of the Hartley/Colpitts family in which the tank is in the collector circuit and feedback is taken by a transformer winding. The frequency is f = 1/(2π√LC), and a high-ratio tuning capacitor is needed for the 3:1 frequency range.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> C = 1/(4π²f²L): low f needs large C.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-86", year: 2017, no: 86, subject: "ad", topic: "Boolean expression simplification",
q: `The logical expression, ABC̄ + AB̄C + AB̄C̄ is equivalent to`,
opts: ["Ā(B + C)", "Ā + B̄ + C̄", "ĀB̄C̄", "A(C̄ + B̄)"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) A(C̄ + B̄)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Factor A: A(BC̄ + B̄C + B̄C̄).</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>BC̄ + B̄C̄ = C̄(B + B̄) = C̄, so the bracket becomes C̄ + B̄C.</li><li>C̄ + B̄C = C̄ + B̄ (absorption: X + X̄Y = X + Y).</li><li>Result: A(B̄ + C̄).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Ā(B + C) contains no A term; the function is zero when A = 0.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Ā + B̄ + C̄ is the NAND of ABC, which is 1 when A = 0.</li><li><b>(c)</b> ĀB̄C̄ is 1 only for A = B = C = 0, whereas the given function is 0 there.</li></ul><p>A Karnaugh map of the original function shows three adjacent minterms (110, 101, 100) forming two overlapping groups AB̄ and AC̄, which give A(B̄ + C̄) and confirm the algebraic simplification.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Use X + X̄Y = X + Y.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-87", year: 2017, no: 87, subject: "ad", topic: "R-2R ladder DAC",
q: `What is the analog output for a 4-bit R-2R ladder DAC when input is (1000)₂, for V<sub>ref</sub> = 5 V?`,
opts: ["2·3333 V", "2·4444 V", "2·5556 V", "2·6667 V"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 2·6667 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The key evaluates the output as V<sub>o</sub> = V<sub>ref</sub> × (decimal value)/(2ⁿ − 1) = 5 × 8/15 = 2.6667 V, treating the full scale as 15 steps.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2.3333 V would result from 7/15 of 5 V.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 2.4444 V corresponds to 22/45 of 5 V, not from this ladder.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2.5556 V is not from this input.</li></ul><p>(With the usual R-2R relation V<sub>o</sub> = V<sub>ref</sub>·D/2ⁿ the output would be 2.5 V, which is not among the options.)</p><p>In an R-2R ladder the output is V<sub>o</sub> = V<sub>ref</sub>(b₃/2 + b₂/4 + b₁/8 + b₀/16). With the input 1000 the usual output is 2.5 V. The key's 2.6667 V corresponds to a full-scale of exactly V<sub>ref</sub> with 15 steps.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Key: V = Vref × D/(2ⁿ − 1).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-88", year: 2017, no: 88, subject: "ad", topic: "MUX as an XNOR gate",
q: `Which logic inputs should be given to the input lines I₀, I₁, I₂ and I₃, if the MUX is to behave as two input XNOR gate? (4-to-1 MUX with select lines S₁ = x, S₀ = y)<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2017-88.png" alt="Figure for question 88">`,
opts: ["0110", "1001", "1010", "1111"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1001</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The XNOR output is 1 when the inputs are equal: x y = 00 → 1, 01 → 0, 10 → 0, 11 → 1.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>With x, y as S₁S₀, the selected input is I₀ for 00, I₁ for 01, I₂ for 10 and I₃ for 11.</li><li>So I₀ = 1, I₁ = 0, I₂ = 0, I₃ = 1, i.e. 1001.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0110 realises the XOR gate.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1010 gives the function ȳ (x ignored).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1111 is the constant 1.</li></ul><p>A 4-to-1 multiplexer can realise any two-variable function by connecting the variables to the select lines and the function values (0 or 1) to the data inputs. This is the basis of look-up-table logic in programmable devices.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Put the truth-table outputs on I₀…I₃.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-89", year: 2017, no: 89, subject: "signals", topic: "Dirichlet conditions",
q: `Fourier series of any periodic signal x(t) can be obtained if 1. ∫₀ᵀ |x(t)| dt &lt; ∞ 2. Finite number of discontinuities within finite time interval t 3. Infinite number of discontinuities. Select the correct answer using the codes given below.`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Dirichlet conditions require that, over one period, the signal is absolutely integrable, has a finite number of maxima and minima and a finite number of discontinuities.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> Required: absolute integrability.</li><li><b>2</b> Required: a finite number of discontinuities.</li><li><b>3</b> Not allowed: an infinite number of discontinuities violates the conditions.</li></ul><p>The Dirichlet conditions are sufficient but not necessary. Signals such as a periodic impulse train, which has an infinite value at the impulses, still have a Fourier series representation in the generalised sense.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Dirichlet: absolutely integrable; finite extrema; finite discontinuities.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-90", year: 2017, no: 90, subject: "signals", topic: "Causality of an LTI system",
q: `LTI system is causal`,
opts: ["If and only if its impulse response is non-zero for negative values of n.", "If and only if its impulse response is non-zero for positive values of n.", "If its impulse response is zero for negative values of n.", "If its impulse response is zero for positive values of n."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) if its impulse response is zero for negative values of n</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A causal system's output depends only on present and past inputs. For an LTI system this holds exactly when the impulse response h[n] = 0 for n &lt; 0.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A non-zero response at negative n would mean the output starts before the impulse (non-causal).</li><li><b>(b)</b> Non-zero response at positive n is possible for a causal system but is not the defining condition.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Zero for positive n would make a purely anti-causal system.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Causal LTI: h[n] = 0 for n < 0.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-91", year: 2017, no: 91, subject: "signals", topic: "Discrete Fourier transform",
q: `Consider the following statements with respect to Discrete Fourier Transform (DFT): 1. It is obtained by performing a sampling operation in the time domain. 2. It transforms a finite duration sequence into a discrete frequency spectrum. 3. It is obtained by performing a sampling operation in both time and frequency domains.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 only", "3 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The DFT is the DTFT of a finite sequence sampled in frequency at N equally spaced points.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> Incomplete: sampling in time alone gives the DTFT, a continuous spectrum.</li><li><b>2</b> True: a finite-duration sequence is mapped to a discrete (sampled) spectrum.</li><li><b>3</b> True: both the time and the frequency are sampled in the DFT.</li></ul><p>The DFT of an N-point sequence has N frequency samples spaced 2π/N apart. Sampling the frequency axis of the DTFT makes the time sequence periodic, which is why the DFT assumes a periodic extension of the data.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> DFT: discrete in time and in frequency.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-92", year: 2017, no: 92, subject: "signals", topic: "Laplace transform of a rectangular pulse",
q: `The Laplace transform of the function f(t) = 8 for 0 &lt; t &lt; 1 (zero elsewhere) is<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2017-92.png" alt="Figure for question 92">`,
opts: ["F(s) = 8s(1 − e⁻ˢ)", "F(s) = (8/s)(1 + e⁻ˢ)", "F(s) = 8s(1 + e⁻ˢ)", "F(s) = (8/s)(1 − e⁻ˢ)"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) F(s) = (8/s)(1 − e⁻ˢ)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> F(s) = ∫₀¹ 8e⁻ˢᵗ dt = 8[−e⁻ˢᵗ/s]₀¹ = (8/s)(1 − e⁻ˢ). Equivalently f(t) = 8u(t) − 8u(t − 1), whose transform is 8/s − 8e⁻ˢ/s.</p><div class="formula">F(s) = ∫₀^∞ f(t)e^(−st) dt</div><div class="formula">L{u(t − a)} = e^(−as)/s</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>F(s) = ∫₀¹ 8e^(−st) dt = 8[−e^(−st)/s]₀¹.</li><li>= <b>(8/s)(1 − e^(−s))</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Multiplying by s instead of dividing is dimensionally wrong.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The delayed step is subtracted, not added.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Both the s factor and the sign are wrong.</li></ul><p>Alternatively, using the time-shifting property, L{u(t − 1)} = e⁻ˢ/s, so L{8u(t) − 8u(t − 1)} = 8/s − 8e⁻ˢ/s = (8/s)(1 − e⁻ˢ). The final value theorem gives f(∞) = lim s F(s) = 0, as it should for a pulse.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Pulse = u(t) − u(t − T).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-93", year: 2017, no: 93, subject: "signals", topic: "Complexity of the direct DFT",
q: `The number of complex additions and multiplications in direct DFT are, respectively`,
opts: ["N(N − 1) and N²", "N(N + 1) and N²", "N(N + 1)² and N", "N and N²"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) N(N − 1) and N²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Each of the N output samples is a sum of N products: X[k] = Σ x[n]W<sub>N</sub><sup>nk</sup>. That needs N complex multiplications and N − 1 complex additions per sample, so in all N² multiplications and N(N − 1) additions.</p><div class="formula">X[k] = Σ_{n=0}^{N−1} x[n]W_N^(nk)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Per output sample: N multiplications, N − 1 additions.</li><li>N samples: N² multiplications and <b>N(N − 1)</b> additions.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> N(N + 1) additions overcounts.</li><li><b>(c)</b> N(N + 1)² and N are not the counts.</li><li><b>(d)</b> N additions would be only one sample’s worth.</li></ul><p>For N = 1024, direct evaluation needs about 10⁶ complex multiplications, while an FFT needs only (N/2) log₂N = 5120, a saving of 200 times; this is why the FFT is central to digital signal processing.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Direct DFT: N² complex multiplications.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-94", year: 2017, no: 94, subject: "signals", topic: "Fourier transform of a rectangular pulse",
q: `The Fourier transform of a unit rectangular pulse of height 1 between t = −1 and t = +1 is<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2017-94.png" alt="Figure for question 94">`,
opts: ["ω sin ω", "2 sin ω/ω", "ω/sin ω", "cos ω/2ω"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 sin ω/ω</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> X(ω) = ∫ from −1 to 1 of e⁻ʲωᵗ dt = [e⁻ʲωᵗ/(−jω)] = (e<sup>jω</sup> − e<sup>−jω</sup>)/(jω) = 2 sin ω/ω.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> ω sin ω is zero at ω = 0, but the area of the pulse is 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> ω/sin ω tends to 1 at ω = 0 and grows, unlike a sinc.</li><li><b>(d)</b> cos ω/2ω is infinite at ω = 0.</li></ul><p>At ω = 0 the value is 2, the area of the pulse (width 2 × height 1).</p><p>The Fourier transform of a rectangular pulse is the sinc function, whose first zero occurs at ω = π. The narrower the pulse in time, the wider its spectrum: the time–bandwidth product is constant.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Rect of width 2T: 2 sin(ωT)/ω.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-95", year: 2017, no: 95, subject: "signals", topic: "Complexity of the FFT",
q: `The number of complex additions and multiplications in FFT are, respectively,`,
opts: ["(N/2) log₂N and N log₂N", "N log₂N and (N/2) log₂N", "(N/2) log₂N and log₂N", "log₂N and (N/2) log₂N"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) N log₂N and (N/2) log₂N</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A radix-2 FFT has log₂N stages. Each stage has N/2 butterflies; each butterfly needs one complex multiplication and two complex additions.</p><div class="formula">Stages = log₂N, butterflies/stage = N/2</div><div class="formula">Butterfly: 1 multiplication, 2 additions</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Complex multiplications = (N/2) log₂N.</li><li>Complex additions = N log₂N.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The two quantities are interchanged.</li><li><b>(c)</b> log₂N alone is far too small for a transform of N points.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Same: log₂N additions is only the number of stages.</li></ul><p>The FFT algorithm works by repeatedly splitting an N-point DFT into two N/2-point DFTs (decimation in time or in frequency). The saving is greatest for N a power of 2, when the radix-2 algorithm is applicable.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> FFT: N/2 butterflies per stage × log₂N stages.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-96", year: 2017, no: 96, subject: "circuits", topic: "Positive real functions", doubt: true,
q: `Consider the following driving point impedance functions: Z₁(s) = (s + 2)/(s² + 3s + 5); Z₂(s) = (s + 2)/(s² + 5); Z₃(s) = (s + 2)/(s² + 2s + 1); Z₄(s) = (s + 2)(s + 4)/((s + 1)(s + 3)). Which one of the above is positive real?`,
opts: ["Z₁", "Z₂", "Z₃", "Z₄"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Z₄</h3><p class="note">In doubt (key followed). By the usual test Z₁ is also positive real (poles at −1.5 ± j1.66, Re Z₁(jω) = (10 + ω²)/|D|² &gt; 0). The key's (d) Z₄ is shown.</p><p><b>Concept:</b> A rational function is positive real if it has no poles in the right half plane (and simple poles on the jω-axis with positive residues), and Re Z(jω) ≥ 0 for all ω.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(d) Z₄</b> Poles −1, −3 and zeros −2, −4 alternate on the negative real axis (a pole nearest the origin, then zero, pole, zero): the classic RC/RL pattern. Re Z₄(jω) = (24 + 13ω² + ω⁴)/|D|² &gt; 0. Positive real, the key’s answer.</li><li><b>(b) Z₂</b> Poles at ±j√5 with a zero at −2: the real part changes sign with ω, and the residue condition fails, so it is not PR.</li><li><b>(c) Z₃</b> A double pole at s = −1: the degree-excess is 1, but the interlacing and the real-part check fail at ω = 1 (it is not the form of a driving point function).</li><li><b>(a) Z₁</b> See the note below.</li></ul><p>Z₁ is PR as well by the real-part test (Re Z₁(jω) = (10 + ω²)/|D|² &gt; 0, no right-half-plane poles), but the key accepts only Z₄.</p><p>For a function to be positive real, its poles and zeros must also lie in the left half plane or on the axis, and the degrees of numerator and denominator differ by no more than one. These conditions are necessary for realisability by passive R, L and C elements.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> PR: no RHP poles, Re Z(jω) ≥ 0.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-97", year: 2017, no: 97, subject: "control", topic: "Stability from the closed-loop transfer function",
q: `The closed-loop transfer function of a system is C(s)/R(s) = (s − 2)/(s³ + 8s² + 19s + 12). The system is`,
opts: ["Stable", "Unstable", "Conditionally stable", "Critically stable"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) stable</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Stability depends on the poles, i.e. the roots of the denominator, not on the zero at s = 2.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>s³ + 8s² + 19s + 12 = (s + 1)(s + 3)(s + 4).</li><li>Poles at −1, −3 and −4 are all in the left half plane, so the system is stable.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The right-half-plane zero makes the system non-minimum phase, but does not make it unstable.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Conditional stability concerns gain variation in an open loop system.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Critical stability needs poles on the jω-axis.</li></ul><p>Routh check: 8 × 19 = 152 &gt; 12 and all coefficients are positive.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Zeros do not affect stability; poles do.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-98", year: 2017, no: 98, subject: "control", topic: "High-frequency slope of a Bode plot",
q: `A system has 14 poles and 2 zeros in its open-loop transfer function. The slope of its highest frequency asymptote in its magnitude plot is`,
opts: ["−40 dB/dec", "−240 dB/dec", "+40 dB/dec", "+240 dB/dec"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) −240 dB/dec</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At very high frequency the slope is −20(P − Z) dB/decade.</p><div class="formula">High-frequency slope = −20(P − Z) dB/dec</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>P − Z = 14 − 2 = 12.</li><li>Slope = −20 × 12 = −240 dB/dec.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> −40 dB/dec is the slope for P − Z = 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The slope is negative since poles exceed zeros.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Positive 240 dB/dec would need zeros to exceed poles by 12.</li></ul><p>The same count applies to the phase: each pole contributes −90° at high frequency and each zero +90°, so the net phase tends to −90° × 12 = −1080°. Such a high-order system has a very large phase lag and is generally unstable in closed loop.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> High-frequency slope = −20(P − Z) dB/dec.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-99", year: 2017, no: 99, subject: "control", topic: "Transfer function from a Bode plot",
q: `The open-loop transfer function for the Bode's magnitude plot is (the magnitude plot has a slope of +40 dB/dec up to ω = 5, +20 dB/dec from 5 to 50 and 0 dB/dec above 50)<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2017-99.png" alt="Figure for question 99">`,
opts: ["G(s)H(s) = K/(s²(1 + 0·2s)(1 + 0·02s))", "G(s)H(s) = Ks/((1 + 0·2s)(1 + 0·02s))", "G(s)H(s) = Ks²/((s + 5)(s + 50))", "G(s)H(s) = K/(s²(s + 5)(s + 50))"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) G(s)H(s) = Ks²/((s + 5)(s + 50))</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The initial slope of +40 dB/dec means two zeros at the origin (s²). The slope decreases by 20 dB/dec at each corner frequency, so there are poles at ω = 5 and ω = 50, bringing the slope to +20 and then 0.</p><div class="formula">Initial slope +20n ⇒ sⁿ</div><div class="formula">Each pole corner: −20 dB/dec</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Corners at 5 and 50 → factors (1 + s/5) and (1 + s/50), i.e. (s + 5) and (s + 50).</li><li>G(s)H(s) = Ks²/((s + 5)(s + 50)).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1/s² gives a slope of −40 dB/dec at low frequency.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A single s gives +20 dB/dec initially.</li><li><b>(d)</b> This has −40 dB/dec at low frequency and an overall high-frequency slope of −80.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Slope changes: +40 → +20 → 0: two zeros at origin, two poles.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-100", year: 2017, no: 100, subject: "control", topic: "Row of zeros in the Routh array",
q: `While forming a Routh array, the situation of a row of zeros indicates that the system`,
opts: ["has symmetrically located roots", "is stable", "is insensitive to variations in gain", "has asymmetrically located roots"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) has symmetrically located roots</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A complete row of zeros in the Routh array means that the characteristic polynomial has a factor that is even in s, i.e. roots symmetrically placed about the origin (pairs ±σ or ±jω, or quadruplets ±σ ± jω). The auxiliary equation from the row above gives these roots.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Such a system is not strictly stable: roots on the jω-axis or in the right half plane exist alongside their symmetrical partners.</li><li><b>(c)</b> It is not related to gain insensitivity.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The roots are symmetric, not asymmetric.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Zero row → auxiliary polynomial → symmetric roots.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2017e.js ----
// ESE 2017 Prelims Paper II, Electrical Engineering, Series A (MKA-S-EEN), Q101–125
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; none dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2017-101", year: 2017, no: 101, subject: "control", topic: "Compensation for steady-state error",
q: `A linear time-invariant control system with unsatisfactory steady state error is to be compensated. Which is/are the correct type of cascade compensation to be provided? 1. Lead 2. Lag 3. Lag-lead`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A lag compensator raises the low-frequency gain (the error constants K<sub>p</sub>, K<sub>v</sub>) by a factor α &gt; 1 while leaving the high-frequency gain unchanged, which reduces the steady-state error without upsetting the transient response much.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> A lead network improves the transient response and bandwidth; it does not help the steady-state error.</li><li><b>2</b> The lag network is the one for improving steady-state accuracy.</li><li><b>3</b> The lag-lead network improves both, but the key accepts the lag network alone as the answer for the steady-state error.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Lag: steady state; lead: transient.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-102", year: 2017, no: 102, subject: "control", topic: "Phase-lead network",
q: `A phase-lead network has its transfer function G<sub>C</sub>(s) = (1 + 0·04s)/(1 + 0·01s). What is the frequency at which the maximum phase-lead occurs?`,
opts: ["25 rad/sec", "50 rad/sec", "75 rad/sec", "100 rad/sec"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 50 rad/sec</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For (1 + αTs)/(1 + Ts) the maximum phase lead occurs at ω<sub>m</sub> = 1/(T√α), the geometric mean of the corner frequencies.</p><div class="formula">ω_m = 1/(T√α) = √(ω_zero ω_pole)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Zero corner = 1/0.04 = 25 rad/s; pole corner = 1/0.01 = 100 rad/s.</li><li>ω<sub>m</sub> = √(25 × 100) = 50 rad/s.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 25 rad/s is the zero corner.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 75 rad/s is the arithmetic mean, not the geometric mean.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 100 rad/s is the pole corner.</li></ul><p>The maximum phase lead is φ<sub>m</sub> = sin⁻¹[(α − 1)/(α + 1)] with α = 4, giving φ<sub>m</sub> = 36.9°. The maximum lead is greater when the ratio between zero and pole corners is larger.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> ω_m = √(zero corner × pole corner).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-103", year: 2017, no: 103, subject: "control", topic: "Open-loop transfer function from the characteristic equation",
q: `What is the open-loop transfer function for the system, whose characteristic equation is F(s) = s³ + 3s² + (K + 2)s + 5K = 0?`,
opts: ["G(s)H(s) = 5K/(s(s + 1)(s + 3))", "G(s)H(s) = Ks/(s(s + 1)(s + 2))", "G(s)H(s) = K(s + 5)/(s(s + 1)(s + 2))", "G(s)H(s) = 5K/(s(s + 1)(s + 2))"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) G(s)H(s) = K(s + 5)/(s(s + 1)(s + 2))</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Write the characteristic equation in the form 1 + G(s)H(s) = 0 by separating the terms in K:</p><div class="formula">1 + G(s)H(s) = 0 ⇒ G(s)H(s) = K·N(s)/D(s)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>s³ + 3s² + 2s + K(s + 5) = 0.</li><li>s³ + 3s² + 2s = s(s + 1)(s + 2).</li><li>Divide by s(s + 1)(s + 2): 1 + K(s + 5)/[s(s + 1)(s + 2)] = 0.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The denominator s(s + 1)(s + 3) = s³ + 4s² + 3s does not match s³ + 3s² + 2s.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The numerator Ks would give K s only, not K(s + 5).</li><li><b>(d)</b> A numerator of 5K alone drops the Ks term.</li></ul><p>Rewriting the characteristic equation as 1 + G(s)H(s) = 0 shows the open-loop poles at 0, −1, −2 and a zero at −5. The root locus plot for K from 0 to infinity starts at these poles.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Collect K terms: 1 + K·N(s)/D(s) = 0.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-104", year: 2017, no: 104, subject: "control", topic: "Negative damping",
q: `In a system, the damping coefficient is −2. The system response will be`,
opts: ["Undamped", "Oscillations with decreasing magnitude", "Oscillations with increasing magnitude", "Critically damped"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) oscillations with increasing magnitude</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A negative damping coefficient puts the poles in the right half of the s-plane: the characteristic roots are −ζω<sub>n</sub> ± jω<sub>n</sub>√(1 − ζ²) with a positive real part. The response then grows exponentially with time; the system is unstable. Among the options, the response of increasing magnitude is the one that fits (the key accepts it).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Undamped means ζ = 0, sustained oscillation of constant amplitude.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Decreasing magnitude needs positive damping.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Critically damped means ζ = 1.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> ζ < 0: poles in the right half plane, growing response.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-105", year: 2017, no: 105, subject: "control", topic: "Transfer function from state equations",
q: `A dynamic system is described by ẋ = [0 1; −3 −4]x + [0; 1]u and y = [10 0]x. Then the transfer function relating Y and u is given by`,
opts: ["Y(s)/u(s) = 10s/(s² + 4s + 3)", "Y(s)/u(s) = 10/(s² + 4s + 3)", "Y(s)/u(s) = s/(s² + 2s + 1)", "Y(s)/u(s) = s/(s² + 3s + 1)"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Y(s)/u(s) = 10/(s² + 4s + 3)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula:</b> G(s) = C(sI − A)⁻¹B.</p><div class="formula">G(s) = C(sI − A)⁻¹B</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>sI − A = [s −1; 3 s + 4]; its determinant is s(s + 4) + 3 = s² + 4s + 3.</li><li>(sI − A)⁻¹B = [1; s]/(s² + 4s + 3).</li><li>G(s) = [10 0][1; s]/(s² + 4s + 3) = 10/(s² + 4s + 3).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The numerator 10s would arise from C = [0 10].</li><li><b>(c)</b> The characteristic polynomial s² + 2s + 1 does not match det(sI − A).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Same objection.</li></ul><p>The state-space representation also shows that the poles are the eigenvalues of A: det(sI − A) = s² + 4s + 3 gives s = −1, −3, so the system is stable. The output matrix C picks the first state variable and scales it by 10.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> G(s) = C(sI − A)⁻¹B; the denominator is det(sI − A).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-106", year: 2017, no: 106, subject: "control", topic: "Mode of controller",
q: `The characteristics of a mode of controller are summarized: 1. If error is zero, the output from the controller is zero. 2. If error is constant in time, the output from the controller is zero. 3. For changing error in time, the output from the controller is |K|% for every 1% sec⁻¹ rate of change of error. 4. For positive rate of change of error, the output is also positive. The mode of controller is`,
opts: ["Integral controller", "Derivative controller", "Proportional derivative", "Proportional integral"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) derivative controller</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The output of a derivative controller is u = K<sub>d</sub> de/dt: zero for zero error, zero for a constant error (the derivative of a constant is zero), and proportional to the rate of change of error, positive when the error is rising.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> An integral controller gives a growing output for a constant error.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A PD controller also has a proportional part, which gives an output for a constant error.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A PI controller gives output for a constant error through both modes.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> D mode: output ∝ rate of change of error.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-107", year: 2017, no: 107, subject: "machines", topic: "Short-circuit voltage of a transformer",
q: `A 1000 V/400 V power transformer has a nominal short-circuit voltage V<sub>SC</sub> = 40%. Which one of the following statements is correct?`,
opts: ["A voltage of 400 V appears across the short-circuited secondary terminals.", "A voltage of 16 V appears across the short-circuited secondary terminals.", "When the secondary terminals are short-circuited, the rated current flows at the primary side at a primary voltage of 400 V.", "The primary voltage drops to 400 V, when the secondary terminals are short-circuited."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) when the secondary terminals are short-circuited, the rated current flows on the primary at a primary voltage of 400 V</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The per-unit short-circuit voltage is the fraction of the rated voltage which, applied to the primary with the secondary shorted, drives rated current: V<sub>SC</sub> = 40 % of 1000 V = 400 V.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A shorted secondary has zero voltage across it.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Same: a short circuit holds the secondary voltage at zero.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The primary voltage is the supply voltage; it does not drop to 400 V by itself (with a stiff supply it stays at 1000 V and a very large current flows).</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> V_sc: voltage on the primary giving rated current with the secondary shorted.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-108", year: 2017, no: 108, subject: "machines", topic: "Open-delta connection",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding three-phase transformers in Open-Delta (V-V) connections: 1. Being a temporary remedy when one transformer forms of Delta-Delta system is damaged, and removed from service. 2. The Volt Ampere (VA) supplied by each transformer is half of the total VA, and the system is not overloaded. 3. An important precaution is that load shall be reduced by √3 times in this case.`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the V–V connection two transformers supply a three-phase load.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: it is an emergency arrangement if one transformer of a delta–delta bank fails.</li><li><b>2</b> False: each transformer carries V·I = 1/√3 (57.7 %) of the total load VA, not half; each runs at its full rating while the bank delivers only 57.7 % of the original delta–delta capacity.</li><li><b>3</b> True: the load must be reduced by a factor √3 (to 57.7 %) so that the two remaining transformers are not overloaded.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> V-V bank capacity: 57.7 % of Δ–Δ.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-109", year: 2017, no: 109, subject: "machines", topic: "Torque-speed curve of an induction motor",
q: `On the Torque/Speed curve of an induction motor shown in the figure, four points of operation are marked as A, B, C and D. Which one of them represents the operation at a slip greater than 1?<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2017-109.png" alt="Figure for question 109">`,
opts: ["A", "B", "C", "D"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) A</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Slip s = (N<sub>s</sub> − N)/N<sub>s</sub> is greater than 1 only when the rotor speed N is negative, that is the rotor is driven against the direction of the rotating field (plugging, braking region). On the curve, this is the part to the left of the torque axis (speed &lt; 0).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> B is in the motoring region, with slip between 0 and 1 (near the pull-out torque).</li><li><b>(c)</b> C is in the motoring region on the stable part of the curve.</li><li><b>(d)</b> D is beyond synchronous speed (slip &lt; 0), the generating region.</li></ul><p>Point A lies to the left of the torque axis, at negative speed: slip greater than 1. B and C are in the motoring region (0 &lt; s &lt; 1), and D lies at speed above synchronous speed (negative slip, generating region).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Slip > 1 ⇔ negative speed (plugging).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-110", year: 2017, no: 110, subject: "machines", topic: "Torque angle of a synchronous motor with v/f control",
q: `A 3-phase, 460 V, 6-pole, 60 Hz cylindrical rotor synchronous motor has a synchronous reactance of 2·5 Ω and negligible armature resistance. The load torque, proportional to the square of the speed, is 398 N.m at 1200 rpm. Unity power factor is maintained by excitation control. Keeping the v/f constant, the frequency is reduced to 36 Hz. The torque angle δ is`,
opts: ["9·5°", "12·5°", "25·5°", "30°"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 12·5°</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At 36 Hz the synchronous speed is 1200 × 36/60 = 720 rpm. Keeping V/f constant: V = 460 × 36/60 = 276 V (line), X<sub>s</sub> = 2.5 × 0.6 = 1.5 Ω.</p><div class="formula">V/f constant</div><div class="formula">T ∝ N²</div><div class="formula">tan δ = IX_s/V (unity p.f.)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Torque at 720 rpm = 398 × (0.6)² = 143.3 N·m; ω = 75.4 rad/s; P = 10.8 kW.</li><li>Phase voltage V = 276/√3 = 159.4 V; I = P/(3V cos φ) = 10 800/(3 × 159.4) = 22.6 A (unity pf).</li><li>Voltage drop IX<sub>s</sub> = 22.6 × 1.5 = 33.9 V, at 90° to V.</li><li>tan δ = 33.9/159.4 = 0.2127 → δ = 12.0°, the nearest option being 12.5°.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 9.5° would need an IX<sub>s</sub> of 26.7 V.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 25.5° would need an IX<sub>s</sub> of 76 V.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 30° would need an IX<sub>s</sub> of 92 V.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Unity pf: tan δ = I X_s/V.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-111", year: 2017, no: 111, subject: "power", topic: "Capability curve of a synchronous generator",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding capability curves of a synchronous generator: 1. The MVA loading should not exceed the generator rating. 2. The field current should not be allowed to exceed a specified value determined by field heating. 3. The MW loading should not exceed the rating of the prime mover. 4. The load angle must be more than 90°.`,
opts: ["1, 2, 3 and 4", "1 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 3 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1, 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The capability curve is bounded by three limits: armature heating (the MVA circle), field heating (the field current limit) and the prime mover rating (the MW limit); a stability limit on the leading side is added.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: armature (stator) current limit gives the MVA circle.</li><li><b>2</b> True: the rotor heating limit on the excitation.</li><li><b>3</b> True: the turbine rating limits the active power.</li><li><b>4</b> False: the load angle must stay <b>below</b> 90° (steady-state stability limit), not above it.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Capability curve limits: armature, field, prime mover, stability.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-112", year: 2017, no: 112, subject: "machines", topic: "Torque of a synchronous motor",
q: `A 12-pole, 440 V, 50 Hz, 3-phase synchronous motor takes a line current of 100 A at 0·8 pf leading. Neglecting losses, the torque developed will be`,
opts: ["705 Nm", "1165 Nm", "1058 Nm", "525 Nm"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1165 Nm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Synchronous speed N<sub>s</sub> = 120f/P = 120 × 50/12 = 500 rpm; ω = 2π × 500/60 = 52.36 rad/s.</p><div class="formula">N_s = 120f/P, ω = 2πN_s/60</div><div class="formula">P = √3V_LI_L cos φ, T = P/ω</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>P = √3 V<sub>L</sub>I<sub>L</sub> cos φ = 1.732 × 440 × 100 × 0.8 = 60.97 kW.</li><li>T = P/ω = 60 970/52.36 = 1164 N·m.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 705 N·m would correspond to 36.9 kW.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1058 N·m would correspond to 55.4 kW.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 525 N·m would correspond to 27.5 kW.</li></ul><p>The synchronous motor is also able to supply reactive power: at 0.8 leading power factor it acts as a capacitor to the supply, so synchronous motors are used for power factor correction as synchronous condensers.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> T = P/ω_s with P = √3 VI cos φ.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-113", year: 2017, no: 113, subject: "machines", topic: "Salient pole and cylindrical rotor alternators",
q: `Consider the following statements: 1. Salient pole alternators have small diameters and large axial lengths. 2. Cylindrical rotor alternators have a distributed winding. 3. Cylindrical rotor alternators are wound for large number of poles. 4. Salient pole alternators run at speeds slower than cylindrical rotor machines.`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "2 and 4 only", "1 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Cylindrical rotor (turbo) alternators are high-speed machines with 2 or 4 poles; salient pole alternators are slow-speed machines with many poles.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: salient pole machines have large diameters and short axial lengths; small diameter and long axial length belong to cylindrical rotors.</li><li><b>2</b> True: the field winding of the cylindrical rotor is distributed in slots.</li><li><b>3</b> False: they have a small number of poles (2 or 4).</li><li><b>4</b> True: salient pole machines (hydro) run at low speeds.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Turbo: 2/4 poles, long and thin; hydro: many poles, large diameter.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-114", year: 2017, no: 114, subject: "machines", topic: "Step angle of a permanent magnet stepper motor",
q: `A permanent magnet stepper motor with 8 poles in stator and 6 poles in rotor will have a step angle of`,
opts: ["7·5°", "15°", "30°", "60°"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 15°</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Step angle = 360° × (N<sub>s</sub> − N<sub>r</sub>)/(N<sub>s</sub> × N<sub>r</sub>) = 360 × (8 − 6)/(8 × 6) = 15°.</p><div class="formula">β = 360°(N_s − N_r)/(N_sN_r)</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>β = 360 × (8 − 6)/(8 × 6) = 720/48 = <b>15°</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 7.5° would result from 360/48.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 30° would be 360 × 2/24.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 60° would be 360/6, the rotor tooth pitch.</li></ul><p>The step angle formula for a variable reluctance or permanent magnet stepper is 360°/(number of phases × rotor teeth) in the simplest case. Smaller step angles give a finer resolution, as needed in positioning drives such as printers and robots. In a hybrid or variable reluctance stepper the step angle is smaller still, 1.8° being common for 200 steps per revolution, which gives fine positioning in machine tools.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Step angle = 360(Ns − Nr)/(Ns·Nr).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-115", year: 2017, no: 115, subject: "power", topic: "Symmetrical network",
q: `The transmission line is represented as a two-port network. The sending end voltage and current are expressed in terms of receiving end voltage and current as V<sub>S</sub> = AV<sub>R</sub> + BI<sub>R</sub> and I<sub>S</sub> = CV<sub>R</sub> + DI<sub>R</sub>. The condition for symmetry for the network is<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2017-115.png" alt="Figure for question 115">`,
opts: ["A = C", "A = D", "B = C", "B = D"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) A = D</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A network is symmetrical if the ports can be interchanged without changing the terminal behaviour. In terms of ABCD parameters this means A = D (a reciprocal network also has AD − BC = 1). Transmission lines (nominal π, T and long line) have A = D.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A = C has different dimensions (A is dimensionless, C is in siemens).</li><li><b>(c)</b> B = C has different dimensions (ohms against siemens).</li><li><b>(d)</b> B = D has different dimensions (ohms against a dimensionless D).</li></ul><p>The ABCD parameters of a transmission line are A = D = cosh γl, B = Z<sub>c</sub> sinh γl and C = sinh γl/Z<sub>c</sub>. For a short line A = D = 1, B = Z and C = 0. In every case AD − BC = 1.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Symmetric: A = D; reciprocal: AD − BC = 1.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-116", year: 2017, no: 116, subject: "power", topic: "System frequency with governor characteristics",
q: `A power system has two synchronous generators having governor turbine characteristics as P₁ = 50(50 − f) and P₂ = 100(51 − f) where f represents the system frequency. Assuming a lossless operation of the complete power system, what is the system frequency for a total load of 800 MW?`,
opts: ["55·33 Hz", "50 Hz", "45·33 Hz", "40 Hz"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 45·33 Hz</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Both generators share the load at the common frequency: P₁ + P₂ = 800.</p><div class="formula">P₁ + P₂ = P_load at common f</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>2500 − 50f + 5100 − 100f = 800.</li><li>7600 − 150f = 800 → f = 6800/150 = 45.33 Hz.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 55.33 Hz is above both no-load frequencies (50 and 51 Hz), so the output would be negative.</li><li><b>(b)</b> At 50 Hz, P₁ = 0 and P₂ = 100 MW only.</li><li><b>(d)</b> At 40 Hz the total would be 500 + 1100 = 1600 MW.</li></ul><p>The frequency settles where the total generation equals the load. If the two machines had the same droop, the load would be shared equally; here generator 2 has twice the slope and takes twice the share of the frequency change.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Sum the governor characteristics at the common f.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-117", year: 2017, no: 117, subject: "circuits", topic: "Shunt element in a cascaded network",
q: `Two networks are connected in cascade in the figure (series Z₁ = 50∠30° followed by a shunt Z₂). The equivalent ABCD constants are obtained for the combined network having C = 0·1∠90°. What is the value of Z₂?<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2017-117.png" alt="Figure for question 117">`,
opts: ["500∠−60°", "0·10 j", "−10 j", "50∠−60°"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) −10 j</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a series impedance Z₁ followed by a shunt impedance Z₂, the ABCD matrix is [1 Z₁; 0 1] × [1 0; 1/Z₂ 1], giving C = 1/Z₂.</p><div class="formula">[A B; C D] = [1 Z₁; 0 1][1 0; 1/Z₂ 1]</div><div class="formula">C = 1/Z₂</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>1/Z₂ = 0.1∠90° → Z₂ = 10∠−90° Ω = −j10 Ω.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 500∠−60° would give C = 0.002∠60°.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.10 j is the value of C itself, not Z₂.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 50∠−60° would give C = 0.02∠60°.</li></ul><p>The combined constants of a series Z₁ followed by a shunt Z₂ are A = 1 + Z₁/Z₂, B = Z₁, C = 1/Z₂ and D = 1. Since the network has A ≠ D, it is not symmetrical, which is why it is called an L network.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Shunt Z₂ contributes C = 1/Z₂.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-118", year: 2017, no: 118, subject: "power", topic: "Factors affecting corona",
q: `Which one of the following does not have an effect on corona?`,
opts: ["Spacing between conductors", "Conductor size", "Line voltage", "Length of conductor"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) length of conductor</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The critical disruptive voltage depends on the conductor radius and spacing, the air density factor, the surface condition and weather. The line voltage decides whether corona occurs. The corona loss per unit length depends on all these, but the onset of corona (and the critical voltage) does not depend on the length of the conductor; only the total loss, being per kilometre, scales with the length.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The spacing enters the disruptive voltage V<sub>c</sub> = g₀δ r ln(d/r).</li><li><b>(b)</b> The conductor radius enters the same expression.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The line voltage determines whether the critical voltage is exceeded.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> V_c = m g₀ δ r ln(d/r).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-119", year: 2017, no: 119, subject: "power", topic: "Corona",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding corona: 1. It causes radio interference. 2. It attenuates lightning surges. 3. It causes power loss. 4. It is more prevalent in the middle conductor of a transmission line employing flat conductor configuration.`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4", "3 and 4 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Corona is the ionisation of the air around conductors at high voltage.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: the pulsating discharge produces radio and television interference.</li><li><b>2</b> True: the corona around the conductor absorbs energy and damps travelling surges.</li><li><b>3</b> True: it dissipates real power (corona loss).</li><li><b>4</b> True: in a flat arrangement the middle conductor has higher surface gradients because of the fields of the two outer ones, so corona starts there.</li></ul><p>All four are accepted.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Corona: loss, interference, surge attenuation.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-120", year: 2017, no: 120, subject: "power", topic: "Penalty factor",
q: `The loss formula coefficient matrix for a two-plant system is given by B = [0·001 −0·0001; −0·0001 0·0013] MW⁻¹. The economic schedule for a certain load is given as P₁ = 150 MW and P₂ = 275 MW. What is the penalty factor for plant 1 for this condition?`,
opts: ["1·324", "1·515", "1·575", "1·721"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1·324</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formulae:</b> P<sub>L</sub> = B₁₁P₁² + 2B₁₂P₁P₂ + B₂₂P₂²; penalty factor PF₁ = 1/(1 − ∂P<sub>L</sub>/∂P₁).</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>∂P<sub>L</sub>/∂P₁ = 2B₁₁P₁ + 2B₁₂P₂ = 2(0.001)(150) + 2(−0.0001)(275) = 0.30 − 0.055 = 0.245.</li><li>PF₁ = 1/(1 − 0.245) = 1/0.755 = 1.324.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 1.515 would need ∂P<sub>L</sub>/∂P₁ = 0.34.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1.575 would need ∂P<sub>L</sub>/∂P₁ = 0.365.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1.721 would need ∂P<sub>L</sub>/∂P₁ = 0.419.</li></ul><p>The penalty factor accounts for the transmission loss that an incremental increase of generation at a plant causes. Plants remote from the load centre have a larger ∂P<sub>L</sub>/∂P and so a larger penalty factor, and are scheduled lower.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> PF = 1/(1 − ∂P_L/∂P_i).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-121", year: 2017, no: 121, subject: "power", topic: "Economic load dispatch with limits",
q: `A lossless power system has two generators G₁ and G₂; and total load to be served is 200 MW. The respective cost curves C₁ and C₂ are C₁ = P<sub>G1</sub> + 0·01P<sub>G1</sub>² and C₂ = 5P<sub>G2</sub> + 0·02P<sub>G2</sub>². Assume the minimum loading on any generator to be 30 MW, the most economical loads P<sub>G1</sub> and P<sub>G2</sub> for the two generators are, respectively`,
opts: ["170 MW and 100 MW", "200 MW and 100 MW", "170 MW and 30 MW", "200 MW and 30 MW"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 170 MW and 30 MW</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Equal incremental cost: dC₁/dP₁ = 1 + 0.02P₁ and dC₂/dP₂ = 5 + 0.04P₂, with P₁ + P₂ = 200.</p><div class="formula">dC₁/dP₁ = dC₂/dP₂ (equal incremental cost)</div><div class="formula">P₁ + P₂ = P_D</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>1 + 0.02(200 − P₂) = 5 + 0.04P₂ → 5 − 0.02P₂ = 5 + 0.04P₂ → P₂ = 0.</li><li>P₂ = 0 is below the 30 MW minimum, so G₂ is set at its lower limit of 30 MW and G₁ takes 200 − 30 = 170 MW.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The two outputs add to 270 MW, more than the load of 200 MW.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The two outputs add to 300 MW.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The two outputs add to 230 MW.</li></ul><p>The incremental cost of G₂ (5 + 0.04P) is higher than that of G₁ (1 + 0.02P) even at no load for G₂, so G₂ should be kept at its minimum. At the final dispatch λ = 1 + 0.02 × 170 = 4.4 against G₂'s 5 + 0.04 × 30 = 6.2, confirming that G₂ should not be increased.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Dispatch by equal λ, then clamp to the limits.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-122", year: 2017, no: 122, subject: "power", topic: "Steady-state power limit of synchronous machines",
q: `In a 3-phase ac power transmission system using synchronous generation,`,
opts: ["The steady state power limits of both round rotor and salient pole machines are reached at δ = π/2 of their respective power angle characteristics.", "The steady state power limit of round rotor machines occurs at a much smaller angle δ as compared to that of salient pole machine power angle characteristic.", "The steady state power limit of salient pole machines occurs at smaller angle δ as compared to that of round rotor machine power angle characteristic.", "The transient state power limits of synchronous generators do not depend on initial load just before the large change in load or on 3-phase fault."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) the steady-state power limit of salient pole machines occurs at a smaller angle δ than for a round rotor machine</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a round rotor machine P = (EV/X<sub>s</sub>) sin δ, with the maximum at δ = 90°. A salient pole machine has the extra reluctance power term (V²/2)(1/X<sub>q</sub> − 1/X<sub>d</sub>) sin 2δ, which moves the maximum to an angle <b>below</b> 90° (typically 70–80°).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Only the round rotor limit is at exactly 90°.</li><li><b>(b)</b> It is the salient pole machine that reaches its limit at a smaller angle.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The transient limit depends on the initial operating condition (the pre-fault loading).</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Salient pole: pull-out angle < 90° because of reluctance power.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-123", year: 2017, no: 123, subject: "power", topic: "Long-distance HVDC transmission", doubt: true,
q: `Bulk power transmission over long HVDC lines is preferred because of`,
opts: ["Low cost of HVDC terminal", "No harmonic losses", "Minimum line power losses", "Simple protection"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) no harmonic losses (as keyed)</h3><p class="note">In doubt (key followed). A converter station generates harmonics, so "no harmonic losses" is not a feature of HVDC; the usual reason for choosing HVDC is the lower line power loss, option (c). The key's (b) is shown.</p><p><b>Concept:</b> HVDC is chosen for long distances mainly because the line has no reactive-power problems, lower line losses and needs less right-of-way. The terminals (converter stations) are costly and generate harmonics which need filters.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> HVDC terminal equipment is expensive; the saving is in the line.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Lower line loss is the advantage normally listed, and the answer we chose first.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Protection of HVDC links is complex.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> HVDC: lower line losses, costly terminals, harmonics need filters.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-124", year: 2017, no: 124, subject: "pe", topic: "Turn-off time of a thyristor",
q: `The turn-off time of a thyristor is 30 μs at 50°C. What is its turn-off time at 100°C?`,
opts: ["15 μs", "30 μs", "60 μs", "120 μs"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 60 μs</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The turn-off time of a thyristor increases with temperature, because the stored charge takes longer to recombine at a higher temperature; the key takes it to double for the 50 °C rise: 60 μs.</p><div class="formula">t_q increases with junction temperature</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Temperature rises by 50 °C.</li><li>Key's rule: turn-off time doubles ⇒ 30 × 2 = <b>60 μs</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> It does not fall with temperature.</li><li><b>(b)</b> It does change with temperature.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 120 μs would be a fourfold increase.</li></ul><p>A high junction temperature also reduces the blocking capability and raises the leakage current of a thyristor, so it must be derated at elevated temperature. Fast switching thyristors have a turn-off time of 5–50 μs.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> t_q rises with junction temperature.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-125", year: 2017, no: 125, subject: "pe", topic: "Switching losses of an IGBT",
q: `The IGBT used in the circuit has the following data: t<sub>ON</sub> = 3 μs, t<sub>OFF</sub> = 1·2 μs, Duty cycle (D) = 0·7, V<sub>CE(sat)</sub> = 2 V and f<sub>s</sub> = 1 kHz (supply 200 V, R<sub>L</sub> = 10 Ω). What are the switching power losses during turn-on and turn-off, respectively?<img class="fig" src="img/ee/2017-125.png" alt="Figure for question 125">`,
opts: ["1·98 W and 1·7 W", "2·2 W and 1·7 W", "1·98 W and 0·792 W", "2·2 W and 0·792 W"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1·98 W and 0·792 W</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The on-state collector current is I<sub>C</sub> = (200 − 2)/10 = 19.8 A. With linear voltage and current transitions, the switching loss is P = V<sub>CC</sub>I<sub>C</sub>t f<sub>s</sub>/6.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Turn-on: 200 × 19.8 × 3 × 10⁻⁶ × 1000/6 = 1.98 W.</li><li>Turn-off: 200 × 19.8 × 1.2 × 10⁻⁶ × 1000/6 = 0.792 W.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A 1.7 W turn-off loss would need t_off ≈ 2.6 μs.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 2.2 W would need I_C ≈ 22 A, and 1.7 W would need t_off ≈ 2.6 μs.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A 2.2 W turn-on loss would need t_on ≈ 3.33 μs.</li></ul><p>The conduction loss in the on-state would be V<sub>CE(sat)</sub> × I<sub>C</sub> × D = 2 × 19.8 × 0.7 = 27.7 W, much larger than the switching losses at 1 kHz; switching losses become dominant only at high switching frequencies.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> P_sw = V I t f/6 for linear transitions.</p>` }
);

